(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis).P1_CORPUS_DOCS = {
  n: 4278,
  pairs: [
  {"id":"hq-00001","question":"Should I choose salted or unsalted nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00002","question":"Is being sore after exercise a good sign?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00003","question":"How long can a cough last before worrying?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00004","question":"Why do I get itchy rashes in winter?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00005","question":"Dizziness with chest pain - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00006","question":"Does tongue scraping help bad breath?","answer":"The tongue's rough surface traps bacteria and food debris, and that coating is a major source of bad breath. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00007","question":"Are sugary drinks bad for me?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00008","question":"My child's ear hurts and she has a fever - when do we go in?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Tenderness and swelling behind the ear is the one earache sign that means go now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00009","question":"How often should I get up from my desk?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00010","question":"How long should I wash my hands?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00011","question":"What does a good breakfast actually do for me?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00012","question":"What are healthy snacks?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. Many afternoon cravings are really thirst, boredom or a low-protein lunch in disguise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00013","question":"Is honey healthier than sugar?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00014","question":"How long does jet lag last?","answer":"Jet lag is your body clock still running on the time zone you left, so sleep and wake signals arrive at the wrong local hours. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00015","question":"Does coffee count towards my daily water intake?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. The idea that coffee leaves you dehydrated is outdated: for regular drinkers it is a net-hydrating drink. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00016","question":"How much salmon counts as a portion?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00017","question":"My doctor refused antibiotics for my cold - why?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00018","question":"Is a handful of grapes really a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00019","question":"Should I cut down on red meat?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Simple swaps make that easy: a couple of meat-free days a week, chicken or fish some nights, or smaller portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00020","question":"How long should a home strength workout be?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00021","question":"Why do older antihistamines make you drowsy?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00022","question":"What foods should I avoid with reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00023","question":"Why do I feel weaker if I train hard every day?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00024","question":"When should I worry about dizziness?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00025","question":"How much dairy should I have a day?","answer":"You do not strictly need dairy, but milk, yogurt and cheese are convenient sources of calcium, protein and iodine. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00026","question":"How much caffeine is in tea compared to coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00027","question":"Which allergy medicine will not make me sleepy?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00028","question":"How do I relieve muscle soreness after exercise?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00029","question":"Is an all-nighter ever worth it?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. A tired brain both encodes less during the day and files less overnight, so fatigue hits memory twice. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00030","question":"How do I know if I stop breathing in my sleep?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00031","question":"The splinter broke off under my skin - what do I do?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Deep, near an eye, or showing spreading redness are the times a splinter needs professional removal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00032","question":"What is a tension headache?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00033","question":"How fast should I try to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00034","question":"Is the snooze button bad for you?","answer":"Each snooze starts a new too-short sleep cycle and then interrupts it, which is why you wake up feeling drugged rather than rested. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00035","question":"How long does it take for exercise to become a habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00036","question":"Why do my eyes and nose itch when I am near cats?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00037","question":"What symptoms always need medical attention?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00038","question":"I sliced my finger on a knife and it will not stop bleeding - what do I do?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00039","question":"How long can a cough last before worrying?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00040","question":"My child cannot keep fluids down with a stomach bug - what now?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00041","question":"Do I need antibiotics for pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00042","question":"Why do I need to take antibiotics exactly as prescribed?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00043","question":"Should you hit a choking person on the back?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00044","question":"What does dehydrated pee look like?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00045","question":"What is hands-only CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00046","question":"What is the difference between a normal allergic reaction and anaphylaxis?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00047","question":"Does climbing stairs instead of the elevator really help?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00048","question":"How much sugar is in a can of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00049","question":"My bowel habits changed suddenly and will not change back - should I worry?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00050","question":"Can my baby sleep on their tummy?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00051","question":"When should I use sanitizer instead of soap?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. After the toilet, after handling food, or whenever hands look dirty, soap and water is the better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00052","question":"What does fiber do for your body?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. People who eat more fiber tend to have lower rates of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00053","question":"My nose is bleeding - should I tilt my head back?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00054","question":"Should I put oil in my ear?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00055","question":"I feel lightheaded - is it serious?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00056","question":"Do kids need structured exercise or just play?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00057","question":"Can napping at 7 pm ruin my night?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Long naps pull you into deep sleep stages, and waking from those produces that heavy, hungover feeling. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00058","question":"What counts as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00059","question":"I got shampoo in my baby's eyes - is that an emergency?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00060","question":"How do I treat a minor burn?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00061","question":"Is plant protein complete?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Eating a variety across the day covers all the building blocks; perfect combinations at every single meal are not required. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00062","question":"Is it normal to wake up in the middle of the night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. The waking itself is normal sleep architecture; the second wake-up is anxiety switching the lights on upstairs. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00063","question":"What should I do for chest pain?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00064","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I stand up?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00065","question":"Why does a consistent sleep schedule matter?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. A fixed wake time is the strongest lever you have - it sets the pressure for sleep that evening more than anything else. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00066","question":"Is coughing up blood serious?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00067","question":"How short can an exercise session be and still count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00068","question":"My grandmother fainted and was confused afterward - is that normal?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00069","question":"Someone collapsed after a wasp sting - is this an emergency?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00070","question":"What is the first thing to do for a bleeding cut?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00071","question":"Is my snacking actually dehydration?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. True hunger is gradual and open to any food; a sudden longing for one specific snack is usually a craving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00072","question":"Does hot water dry out your skin?","answer":"The post-shower itch is evaporating water pulling more moisture from skin whose oils the hot water already stripped. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00073","question":"Why does benadryl-style medicine stop making me sleepy?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. Tolerance builds within days - the sleepiness fades while the grogginess the next morning does not. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00074","question":"How long can a cough last before worrying?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00075","question":"Does a drink before bed ruin sleep quality?","answer":"A drink may knock you out faster, but it fragments your sleep later in the night and cuts REM. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00076","question":"What causes sudden dizziness?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00077","question":"What is the best way to build an exercise habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00078","question":"Where does the 10,000 steps number come from?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00079","question":"What actually helps when you have a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00080","question":"What is the 150 minutes a week guideline?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00081","question":"Is dizziness a sign of a stroke?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00082","question":"What is a good cool-down?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00083","question":"Why is balance training important for older adults?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00084","question":"How hard should I be breathing during exercise?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00085","question":"My kid has a nosebleed at bedtime - what should I do?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00086","question":"How do I treat a minor burn?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00087","question":"I rolled my ankle playing basketball - what should I do tonight?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Heat, alcohol, running and massage are the four things to skip in the first couple of days. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00088","question":"Is butter or olive oil better for you?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fats - butter, ghee, coconut oil, fatty processed meat and pastry - raise cholesterol, so keep them to a smaller share of your diet. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00089","question":"Can I reheat food more than once?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Leftovers should be reheated until steaming hot all the way through, and only reheated once. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00090","question":"Does skipping breakfast make you gain weight?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00091","question":"Are protein bars and cereals ultra-processed?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00092","question":"What are the signs of a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00093","question":"What is the difference between a viral and bacterial infection?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00094","question":"Why do we cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00095","question":"Why does a double spirit count as 2 units?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A standard pint of lager carries roughly 2.5 units, so the 14-unit weekly limit is about 5-6 pints spread across the week. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00096","question":"When should I take an earache to the doctor?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Tenderness and swelling behind the ear is the one earache sign that means go now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00097","question":"How do I stop heartburn without medicine?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00098","question":"Is the sugar in fruit bad for you?","answer":"The natural sugar in whole fruit arrives packaged with fiber, water, vitamins and antioxidants, so it is absorbed slowly and is not a worry for almost everyone. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00099","question":"How much sleep do adults need?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00100","question":"Why do potatoes not count as a vegetable portion?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00101","question":"Why is everything worse when I am tired?","answer":"Sleep loss amplifies emotional reactions: the brain's alarm centers run hotter and the calming, rational control from the prefrontal cortex weakens. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00102","question":"My coworker is choking, turning red and cannot talk - what should I do right now?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00103","question":"Can kids use adult toothpaste?","answer":"Children need adult help with brushing until about age 7 or 8, when their hand control is good enough to do it alone under supervision. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00104","question":"Why are trans fats worse than saturated fat?","answer":"Trans fats are the unhealthiest fats there are - worse than saturated fat - which is why most countries have banned artificial ones. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00105","question":"Why do I feel dizzy if I stop exercising suddenly?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00106","question":"Do fried foods contain trans fats?","answer":"Artificial trans fats are made when liquid oils are hardened into solids, and they raise heart disease risk more than any other kind of fat. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00107","question":"Should I take medicine for a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00108","question":"Can cold weather set off asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00109","question":"What is the right way to stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00110","question":"How long does it take for exercise to become a habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00111","question":"My cough has lasted a month - is that normal?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00112","question":"Why can't babies have honey?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00113","question":"How many back blows do you give a choking person?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00114","question":"Do step counts even matter?","answer":"Ten thousand is a nice round marketing number; the science says most of the benefit arrives well before it. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00115","question":"Should I call 911 before starting CPR?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00116","question":"How can I cool my bedroom down for sleep?","answer":"Your core temperature needs to drop a little to fall asleep, so a cool room works with your biology rather than against it. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00117","question":"How do I avoid injury when starting a new sport?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00118","question":"I cut my knee on the pavement - what should I do once the bleeding stops?","answer":"Running water is the best cleanser for everyday cuts; scrubbing with alcohol only adds pain and tissue damage. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00119","question":"Does honey work for coughs?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. The honest evidence review: honey performs about as well as the syrups and costs far less. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00120","question":"Is milk good for you?","answer":"You do not strictly need dairy, but milk, yogurt and cheese are convenient sources of calcium, protein and iodine. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00121","question":"Is coffee as hydrating as water?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. The idea that coffee leaves you dehydrated is outdated: for regular drinkers it is a net-hydrating drink. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00122","question":"Is raw honey better for you?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00123","question":"What foods are high in calcium?","answer":"Adults need around 700 milligrams of calcium a day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00124","question":"Should pregnant women eat fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00125","question":"What should I do after hitting my head?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Feeling fine right away does not rule out a delayed problem, which is why the 48-hour watch matters. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00126","question":"Why am I hungrier when I sleep badly?","answer":"After short sleep, hunger hormones tilt toward eating more, and cravings lean toward sugary, high-calorie food. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00127","question":"When should I see a doctor about sleep?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00128","question":"Why does my body react to pollen?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00129","question":"My baby is choking on a bit of apple - what should I do first?","answer":"Support the baby's head, keep it lower than the body, and give up to 5 back blows between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00130","question":"Are frozen vegetables as healthy as fresh?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00131","question":"When should I take an earache to the doctor?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00132","question":"How deep do compressions need to be?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00133","question":"How long does an ear infection last?","answer":"Comfort care plus 3 days of patience resolves the majority of earaches. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00134","question":"Dizziness with chest pain - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00135","question":"When should I see a doctor for a burn?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Anything bigger than the person's palm belongs in front of a clinician. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00136","question":"I always quit exercising after a few weeks - what am I doing wrong?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00137","question":"Is it too late to start exercising at 60?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00138","question":"What does dehydration feel like?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00139","question":"Is strength training really that important?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00140","question":"Should I drive myself to the hospital with chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00141","question":"What actually causes allergy symptoms?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00142","question":"Does snoring mean sleep apnea?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. Snoring becomes a medical question when breathing pauses or daytime sleepiness join it, not because of the noise alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00143","question":"What happens if I do not get enough vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00144","question":"Does honey count as added sugar?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00145","question":"The sting site is still swollen the next day - is that normal?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00146","question":"How often should I brush my teeth?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Once in the morning and once last thing before bed - the bedtime brush matters most, because saliva flow drops while you sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00147","question":"How do I stay motivated to exercise?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00148","question":"My baby fell off the bed onto the carpet - how do I know if he is okay?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00149","question":"What should you never do during a nosebleed?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00150","question":"Why does exercise make me wheeze?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00151","question":"When is dehydration serious?","answer":"No urine in 8 hours, sunken eyes or new confusion are the stop-waiting signs. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00152","question":"Is red meat bad for you?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Simple swaps make that easy: a couple of meat-free days a week, chicken or fish some nights, or smaller portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00153","question":"Why are trans fats worse than saturated fat?","answer":"Artificial trans fats are made when liquid oils are hardened into solids, and they raise heart disease risk more than any other kind of fat. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00154","question":"Is washing hands with water alone enough?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00155","question":"Can I use the same cutting board for meat and vegetables?","answer":"Keep raw meat and its juices away from food that is eaten raw: use separate cutting boards and utensils, or wash them thoroughly between uses. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00156","question":"How do I treat conjunctivitis at home?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00157","question":"What should I put on a burn after cooling it?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. After cooling, cover it loosely with cling film or a clean lint-free cloth and leave the skin alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00158","question":"How do I clean my phone safely?","answer":"Your phone travels to the bathroom, the kitchen and everywhere your hands go, picking up whatever they touch, and is cleaned far less often than your hands. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00159","question":"What drinks can I have instead of coffee when pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00160","question":"Why do I crave junk food when I am tired?","answer":"After short sleep, hunger hormones tilt toward eating more, and cravings lean toward sugary, high-calorie food. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00161","question":"Is flossing or interdental brushes better?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Bleeding at first usually means the gums are already inflamed; daily cleaning reverses that within a week or two, and the bleeding stops. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00162","question":"How do I relieve constipation naturally?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00163","question":"Can I eat too much protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00164","question":"Is it bad to have a cat if you have asthma?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00165","question":"Can lack of sleep make me sick?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. During deep sleep the immune system ramps up defensive activity, which is one reason rest is prescribed for illness. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00166","question":"Why do people say caffeine dehydrates you?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. The idea that coffee leaves you dehydrated is outdated: for regular drinkers it is a net-hydrating drink. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00167","question":"Is sleep apnea dangerous?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00168","question":"Should I splash water on someone who fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00169","question":"Why does my chest burn after eating?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00170","question":"Why did I go grey and wobbly standing in line?","answer":"Heat, pallor, tunnel vision and a cold sweat are the classic pre-faint signs. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00171","question":"Headache with a stiff neck and fever - what could it be?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00172","question":"Do potatoes count towards five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00173","question":"Why do kids have bad dreams?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00174","question":"I have fever and body aches - is this the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00175","question":"Why am I wide awake after evening workouts?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Evening hard sessions keep some people wired; others sleep fine - your own response is the test. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00176","question":"Does a 5 minute walk do anything?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00177","question":"I never took a CPR class - what should I do if someone collapses and is not breathing?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00178","question":"How long after soda can I brush?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acid temporarily softens enamel, and brushing during that window wears it; the fluoride-and-saliva repair needs time. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00179","question":"How do I wash my hands properly?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00180","question":"Are naps good or bad for you?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Long naps pull you into deep sleep stages, and waking from those produces that heavy, hungover feeling. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00181","question":"What is jet lag?","answer":"Jet lag is your body clock still running on the time zone you left, so sleep and wake signals arrive at the wrong local hours. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00182","question":"Why does the room spin when I lie down or turn over?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00183","question":"What happens if I drink litres of water very quickly?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00184","question":"Should I wash raw chicken before cooking?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. The splash zone from rinsing chicken is a classic source of food poisoning outbreaks traced back to home kitchens. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00185","question":"Why am I constipated all the time?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00186","question":"What is pink eye?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00187","question":"How do you do the Heimlich maneuver?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00188","question":"Are electric toothbrushes better?","answer":"The brush is the tool; the 2 minutes of coverage is the treatment. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00189","question":"Why should I eat five portions of fruit and veg every day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00190","question":"When should I take an earache to the doctor?","answer":"Three days, discharge, or a genuinely unwell child are the standard referral points. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00191","question":"What should I do if cleaning fluid gets in my eye?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00192","question":"What is the first thing to do for a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00193","question":"Do I need to lift weights to be healthy?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00194","question":"How do I stop a bee sting from itching?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00195","question":"Will extra protein turn into muscle?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00196","question":"Is there treatment for norovirus?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00197","question":"My toddler fell and bumped her head - what should I do?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00198","question":"What should I put on a scrape?","answer":"Petroleum jelly alone keeps minor wounds moist just as well for most people, without the allergy risk. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00199","question":"What is the best mattress for back pain?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. Replace a mattress when it sags, lumps, or you wake stiff and sleep better elsewhere, which is often around 7 to 8 years. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00200","question":"How do I fix my sleep schedule?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. Big weekend lie-ins feel like repayment but create 'social jet lag', leaving Monday mornings feeling like jet lag after a long flight. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00201","question":"Can you shower too much?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. Daily showers are fine too, but very hot water and harsh scrubbing strip the skin's oils and can trigger dryness and itch. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00202","question":"Someone is not breathing - what comes first, calling or compressions?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00203","question":"How do I relieve muscle soreness after exercise?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00204","question":"How many grams of salt is too much?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00205","question":"When does a fever become an emergency for adults?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever with a stiff neck or an unexplained rash can signal meningitis - that is a same-hour call. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00206","question":"Why do labels list syrup instead of sugar?","answer":"Sugar hides under many names: glucose, dextrose, fructose, maltose, corn syrup, rice syrup, honey, agave and fruit juice concentrate. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00207","question":"Is a cold caused by bacteria?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00208","question":"My nose is bleeding - should I tilt my head back?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00209","question":"What is hands-only CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00210","question":"The sting site is still swollen the next day - is that normal?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00211","question":"Is my child getting enough sleep?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Children who regularly sleep less than their range are more likely to be irritable, struggle with learning, and behave as if hyperactive rather than sleepy. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00212","question":"Why can't I sleep after a night shift?","answer":"Shift work fights your body clock, so the goal is defending one solid sleep block: blackout curtains, a cool quiet room, phones off, and telling household members you are off-limits. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00213","question":"How much fruit juice should I drink a day?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00214","question":"Does hand sanitizer kill norovirus?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. After the toilet, after handling food, or whenever hands look dirty, soap and water is the better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00215","question":"Why does my mind race when I wake at night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. It becomes a problem when you notice the waking, check the time, and start worrying, which re-activates the brain. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00216","question":"How do I treat a minor burn?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00217","question":"Should I have alcohol-free days?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00218","question":"Why do I get headaches at my desk?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00219","question":"What is the healthiest way to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00220","question":"How do I know if my ankle is broken or just sprained?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. A joint that will not take weight may be fractured - get it looked at. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00221","question":"Is antiperspirant bad for you?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Neither has been shown to cause cancer or health harm in the decades they have been studied; 'aluminum causes breast cancer' is a myth without evidence. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00222","question":"I got the worst headache of my life very suddenly - what should I do?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00223","question":"Is fish a good source of protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and whole grains add up to plenty of protein over a day, with extra fiber as a bonus. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00224","question":"Are sleep trackers accurate?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. They are useful for spotting trends - short nights, late bedtimes - but not for diagnosing anything; that needs a medical sleep study. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00225","question":"Is pink eye serious?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00226","question":"When should I see a doctor for a burn?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. White, leathery or charred-looking skin means a deep burn, and chemical or electrical burns always need professional assessment. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00227","question":"Why should you not put ice on a burn?","answer":"Ice and folk remedies like butter can damage the skin further or seal heat in - plain running water is the only first step that helps. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00228","question":"What happens if I drink too much caffeine?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00229","question":"How many grams of added sugar is too much?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00230","question":"What are the signs that need an ambulance immediately?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00231","question":"What helps a sore throat fast?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00232","question":"Is coughing good for you?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00233","question":"How do I stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00234","question":"Why can I not stick to an exercise routine?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00235","question":"Should I eat breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00236","question":"What actually causes allergy symptoms?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00237","question":"Do I need a vitamin D supplement?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00238","question":"Is dancing moderate or vigorous activity?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00239","question":"Is a headache a sign I need water?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00240","question":"Does climbing stairs instead of the elevator really help?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00241","question":"The sting site is still swollen the next day - is that normal?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00242","question":"How do I treat athlete's foot?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. Reinfection usually comes from the shoes, socks or bathroom floor, so treat the environment along with the feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00243","question":"Is coughing up blood serious?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00244","question":"Why is my asthma inhaler blue?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00245","question":"My grandmother fainted and was confused afterward - is that normal?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00246","question":"How do I know my inhaler technique is right?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00247","question":"Is sleepwalking dangerous?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Do not try to wake the person; gently guide them back to bed. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00248","question":"Should I cool down after exercise?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00249","question":"Can my baby sleep on their tummy?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00250","question":"When is insomnia considered chronic?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00251","question":"Why compare foods per 100 grams?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00252","question":"What should I eat for breakfast to stay full until lunch?","answer":"A filling breakfast combines fiber with protein: oatmeal with fruit and nuts, eggs on wholegrain toast, or plain yogurt with berries. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00253","question":"How do I know my inhaler technique is right?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00254","question":"Why is my throat worse in the morning?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00255","question":"How fast should I increase my exercise?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00256","question":"How long should I keep doing CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00257","question":"How do I stop a blister hurting when I walk?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00258","question":"Which foods are high in iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00259","question":"There is glass in my hand - should I pull it out?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Pad around anything stuck in a wound and leave it for the professionals to remove. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00260","question":"How much water is too much in one day?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00261","question":"Can walking help me lose weight?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00262","question":"Why should I swap soda for water?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00263","question":"Is it okay to take someone else's antibiotics?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00264","question":"When should I see a doctor about migraines?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00265","question":"Why do I regain weight after dieting?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00266","question":"Are eggs healthy?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00267","question":"Do babies and children need less salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00268","question":"How do I know my inhaler technique is right?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00269","question":"How many cups of coffee can I drink a day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00270","question":"Are canned vegetables nutritious?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00271","question":"Is alcohol mouthwash bad for you?","answer":"Mouthwash cannot replace brushing and interdental cleaning, because it cannot remove plaque - only mechanical cleaning does that. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00272","question":"Is 30 minutes of activity enough for a child?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00273","question":"How long should I wash my hands?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00274","question":"Does juice count towards my five a day?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00275","question":"How often should I wash my hands during the day?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. Germs reach your hands mainly at these moments, which is why targeting them beats constant frantic washing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00276","question":"Why not cover coughs with your hand?","answer":"Cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue - or the crook of your elbow if there isn't one - then bin the tissue and wash your hands. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00277","question":"What triggers asthma attacks?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00278","question":"What cough signs mean urgent care?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00279","question":"Should I eat with diarrhea and vomiting?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00280","question":"Is plain water the only drink that keeps me hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00281","question":"Should I avoid all processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00282","question":"How do I clean my tongue properly?","answer":"Cleaning the tongue removes the bacterial film that brushing teeth leaves untouched. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00283","question":"Why does my mind race when I wake at night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. The waking itself is normal sleep architecture; the second wake-up is anxiety switching the lights on upstairs. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00284","question":"Is tongue scraping safe?","answer":"Cleaning the tongue removes the bacterial film that brushing teeth leaves untouched. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00285","question":"Is decaf a good evening substitute?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00286","question":"How do I get vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00287","question":"What is the difference between moderate and vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00288","question":"How many nuts should I eat a day?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00289","question":"How long do you pinch your nose for a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00290","question":"Does washing chicken remove bacteria?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. The splash zone from rinsing chicken is a classic source of food poisoning outbreaks traced back to home kitchens. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00291","question":"Why is washing chicken bad?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. Washing does not remove bacteria anyway - only proper cooking does. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00292","question":"What should I do if someone faints in front of me?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00293","question":"How short can an exercise session be and still count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00294","question":"When is vertigo an emergency?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00295","question":"Which foods are high in iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00296","question":"Why is vitamin D important?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00297","question":"Is snoring bad for your health?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. It matters when it is loud and paired with pauses, gasping or choking during sleep and heavy daytime sleepiness - that pattern suggests sleep apnea and deserves a doctor's attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00298","question":"What are head injury warning signs in children?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00299","question":"What is social jet lag?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. Big weekend lie-ins feel like repayment but create 'social jet lag', leaving Monday mornings feeling like jet lag after a long flight. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00300","question":"Is meal prepping worth it?","answer":"Home cooking puts the salt shaker and the sugar bowl back in your hands, so you decide how much goes in. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00301","question":"Are eggs bad for your cholesterol?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00302","question":"Should I remove contact lenses after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00303","question":"Should I take medicine for a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00304","question":"How can I tell if I have a cold or the flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. The speed of onset is the best clue: colds creep up, flu drops on you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00305","question":"Why compare foods per 100 grams?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00306","question":"Is sea salt healthier than table salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00307","question":"How do I know if chest pain is a heart attack?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00308","question":"Should sick family members have their own towel?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. Most respiratory viruses spread by hands and close contact, so these small habits cut the chain better than disinfecting every surface blindly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00309","question":"What if I am alone when someone collapses?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00310","question":"Is coughing good for you?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00311","question":"Is a glass of wine a day good for me?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00312","question":"Why do doctors recommend walking so much?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00313","question":"What are the signs of a severe allergic reaction to a sting?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. Lip or tongue swelling after a sting is not a normal local reaction - treat it as an emergency. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00314","question":"What does a meningitis rash look like?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00315","question":"My blister burst on its own - what now?","answer":"Resist popping it; the fluid is sterile and the skin over it does the job of a dressing. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00316","question":"How fast should I increase my exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00317","question":"Is a headache a sign I need water?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00318","question":"Should I see a doctor before starting to exercise?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00319","question":"What is RICE for a sprain?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00320","question":"What actually helps when you have a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00321","question":"Is it dangerous to drive when tired?","answer":"Being awake for 17-19 hours impairs driving about as much as being over the legal alcohol limit in many countries. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00322","question":"Does hand sanitizer kill norovirus?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. Sanitizer is a convenience, not a substitute: soap physically removes what gel only partially deactivates.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00323","question":"Is pink eye serious?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00324","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I have not had enough to drink?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00325","question":"Why do I feel dizzy if I stop exercising suddenly?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00326","question":"Is dark urine a sign that I need more water?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00327","question":"When should I see a doctor about constipation?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00328","question":"How should I warm up before exercise?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00329","question":"Should I cool down after exercise?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00330","question":"Why do most people need more fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00331","question":"How high is too high for a fever in adults?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00332","question":"How do I clean after a stomach bug?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. Most respiratory viruses spread by hands and close contact, so these small habits cut the chain better than disinfecting every surface blindly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00333","question":"Can my kid go to school with pink eye?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Rubbing is the self-spreading mechanism; washcloths and makeup are the household vectors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00334","question":"There is dust in my eye - how do I get it out?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00335","question":"What should I do if someone is choking?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00336","question":"What are the signs of dehydration?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00337","question":"Is one soda a day really a problem?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00338","question":"Is everything processed unhealthy?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00339","question":"Can I exercise every day?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00340","question":"Should I trust my smartwatch sleep data?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. A wristwatch guesses at stages from movement and heart rate; only a clinic study with brain sensors can measure sleep properly. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00341","question":"Why does my body feel better when I warm up first?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00342","question":"Does drinking water before eating help you eat less?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. It is a harmless habit with modest benefits: helpful, but not a magic weight-loss trick.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00343","question":"Should someone with heat exhaustion keep drinking water?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. Confusion, hot dry skin or collapse means heatstroke - an emergency that needs an immediate call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00344","question":"Is a fruit smoothie a good replacement for breakfast fruit?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00345","question":"Should I floss?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Gums that bleed with flossing are gums that need more flossing, not less - the inflammation is what causes the blood. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00346","question":"Why can I not stick to an exercise routine?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00347","question":"Can older adults still build muscle?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00348","question":"What should I do if someone is choking?","answer":"Coughing is the body's own first aid for choking, so let it work while it is working. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00349","question":"How much protein do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00350","question":"Why is my kid so sedentary and what do I do?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00351","question":"Which electric toothbrush should I buy?","answer":"A manual brush used properly for 2 minutes cleans just as well as many electric ones. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00352","question":"Is coughing good for you?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00353","question":"What is pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00354","question":"How long should I ice a sprain?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00355","question":"How much water should I drink every day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00356","question":"How can I prevent nightmares in children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00357","question":"How much sleep does a school-age child need?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Overtired children often look wired rather than worn out, so bedtime battles can be a sign of too little sleep. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00358","question":"What should I do if my child swallowed something harmful?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00359","question":"How much sleep do adults need?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00360","question":"What are hidden names for sugar on labels?","answer":"Sugar hides under many names: glucose, dextrose, fructose, maltose, corn syrup, rice syrup, honey, agave and fruit juice concentrate. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00361","question":"Why is vitamin D important?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00362","question":"How does salt affect my heart?","answer":"Salt makes your body retain water, and that extra fluid raises the pressure inside your blood vessels. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00363","question":"When is dehydration serious?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00364","question":"Should night workers take melatonin?","answer":"Daytime sleep is lighter and shorter by nature, so blackout darkness, cool air and ear protection are doing real work, not just comfort. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00365","question":"I got shampoo in my baby's eyes - is that an emergency?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00366","question":"What foods should I avoid with reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00367","question":"What does asthma feel like?","answer":"Asthma is inflamed airways that narrow further with triggers, producing wheeze and breathlessness. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00368","question":"What is norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00369","question":"Why does sugary cereal leave me hungry again so fast?","answer":"Build breakfast around fiber plus protein - for example oatmeal with berries and nuts, or eggs with wholegrain toast. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00370","question":"What causes skin rashes?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00371","question":"How deep do compressions need to be?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00372","question":"What is the poison control number?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00373","question":"Should I cut all fat from my diet?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fats - butter, ghee, coconut oil, fatty processed meat and pastry - raise cholesterol, so keep them to a smaller share of your diet. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00374","question":"How much caffeine is in tea compared to coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00375","question":"How quickly do you need to take Tamiflu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00376","question":"Is antibacterial soap better than regular soap?","answer":"Antibacterial soap has never been shown to outperform ordinary soap and water in real-world handwashing. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00377","question":"When is fainting an emergency?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00378","question":"How much exercise do children need?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00379","question":"How much fiber do I need for regular bowel movements?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00380","question":"I feel dizzy when I stand up - could it be dehydration?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00381","question":"Is 6 hours of sleep enough?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00382","question":"Do I need dairy for calcium?","answer":"You do not strictly need dairy, but milk, yogurt and cheese are convenient sources of calcium, protein and iodine. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00383","question":"Can I wear a mask on public transport with a cold?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. The mask's main job when you are sick is source control - protecting everyone else - not protecting you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00384","question":"How much fish should I eat a week?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00385","question":"Why do I feel weaker if I train hard every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00386","question":"Why do kids have bad dreams?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00387","question":"When should I call 911 instead of driving to the hospital?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00388","question":"Should I have alcohol-free days?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00389","question":"Why is my asthma inhaler blue?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00390","question":"Does drinking water really help constipation?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00391","question":"What is the daily salt limit for adults?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00392","question":"Should I wrap a swollen ankle?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Heat, alcohol, running and massage are the four things to skip in the first couple of days. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00393","question":"Is chili good for your metabolism?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Ulcers are almost always caused by a stomach bacterium or by painkillers like ibuprofen, not by spice; hot food can sting if you already have one, though. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00394","question":"What temperature kills food poisoning bacteria?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Pink can linger in safely cooked chicken and white-looking meat can still be unsafe, which is why the 74 °C number matters. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00395","question":"Does walking count as moderate exercise?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00396","question":"What is the best way to prevent foot fungus?","answer":"Fungus thrives in warm, damp, dark places - exactly what sweaty, enclosed toes provide when left damp. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00397","question":"Why is school so early for teenagers?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Later start times give back the hours the late-shifted teen clock steals, and studies show teens use them to sleep more. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00398","question":"How long does a cold last?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00399","question":"Why did advice about eggs change?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00400","question":"How should I cover a sneeze?","answer":"Cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue - or the crook of your elbow if there isn't one - then bin the tissue and wash your hands. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00401","question":"Which fish are oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00402","question":"Should I trust my smartwatch sleep data?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. They are useful for spotting trends - short nights, late bedtimes - but not for diagnosing anything; that needs a medical sleep study. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00403","question":"Is it OK to practice intermittent fasting and skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00404","question":"How do I stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00405","question":"How do I know if my cut needs stitches?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00406","question":"Is alcohol good for cleaning wounds?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. The old advice to let wounds 'breathe' has been overturned: a clean, moist, covered wound heals measurably faster.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00407","question":"Does fruit have too much sugar?","answer":"Whole fruit is one of the healthiest foods there is; its sugar is wrapped in fiber and water, so it does not act like candy. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00408","question":"How do I teach a child to wash hands properly?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00409","question":"How many teaspoons of sugar are in a can of cola?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00410","question":"How long should you monitor someone after a head injury?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Watch the person for two days: any repeated vomiting, growing confusion or unusual sleepiness means emergency care. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00411","question":"How do I know if a rash is something serious?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00412","question":"Is being sore after exercise a good sign?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00413","question":"I keep getting dizzy every day - should I see a doctor?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00414","question":"My child cannot keep fluids down with a stomach bug - what now?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00415","question":"How do I know if chest pain is a heart attack?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00416","question":"Do I need to lift weights to be healthy?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00417","question":"What is the difference between moderate and vigorous activity?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00418","question":"What is the difference between a dry and chesty cough?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00419","question":"What is an allergic reaction doing in my body?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00420","question":"What is a good cool-down?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00421","question":"Does skipping breakfast make you gain weight?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00422","question":"Can you drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00423","question":"What cough signs mean urgent care?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00424","question":"Cough with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00425","question":"What mouthwash should children use?","answer":"Mouthwash cannot replace brushing and interdental cleaning, because it cannot remove plaque - only mechanical cleaning does that. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00426","question":"Should I raise my bed for heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00427","question":"Headache with a stiff neck and fever - what could it be?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00428","question":"Does walking count as moderate exercise?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00429","question":"Is washing hands with water alone enough?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00430","question":"Does a higher fever mean a worse illness?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00431","question":"What is RICE for a sprain?","answer":"Rest, wrapped ice, a compression bandage and elevation are the four first-day moves for a sprain. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00432","question":"How long should I cool down for?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00433","question":"I keep getting dizzy every day - should I see a doctor?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00434","question":"How do I make someone with a fever comfortable?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00435","question":"Can walking help me lose weight?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00436","question":"How do I know if a bread is really wholegrain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00437","question":"My head feels like a tight band - what is that?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00438","question":"Can you build muscle on a vegetarian diet?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Different plants are low in different amino acids, so variety over the day - not one magic food pairing - does the job. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00439","question":"Do kids need structured exercise or just play?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00440","question":"What is the safe way to get a splinter out?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Deep, near an eye, or showing spreading redness are the times a splinter needs professional removal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00441","question":"Why won't the doctor give me antibiotics?","answer":"Cold, flu and most sore throats are viral; antibiotics do nothing for them. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00442","question":"Is snoring bad for your health?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. Snoring becomes a medical question when breathing pauses or daytime sleepiness join it, not because of the noise alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00443","question":"Do I need antibacterial soap at home?","answer":"For everyday handwashing, plain soap is just as effective as 'antibacterial' varieties. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00444","question":"How hard should I be breathing during exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00445","question":"Should I scrub my nails when washing hands?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Keep nails trimmed and scrub your fingertips against your palms while washing so the nails get attention too. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00446","question":"How many cups of fluid should I have each day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00447","question":"When does a stomach bug need a doctor?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00448","question":"Can I eat as much honey as I want?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00449","question":"What causes the common cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00450","question":"Someone collapsed after a wasp sting - is this an emergency?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. Lip or tongue swelling after a sting is not a normal local reaction - treat it as an emergency. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00451","question":"Why do I wake up exhausted despite 8 hours in bed?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00452","question":"Is ham a healthy lunch meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00453","question":"Why am I hungrier when I sleep badly?","answer":"Short sleep disturbs appetite hormones: ghrelin (hunger) rises and leptin (fullness) falls, so you feel hungrier the next day. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00454","question":"How is restless legs treated?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Low iron stores are sometimes involved, so it is worth seeing a doctor - several treatments work well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00455","question":"Are nuts good for you?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00456","question":"Is 6,000 steps a day okay?","answer":"Ten thousand is a nice round marketing number; the science says most of the benefit arrives well before it. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00457","question":"Is it dangerous to swallow blood from a nosebleed?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00458","question":"I have fever and body aches - is this the flu?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00459","question":"Can I take antihistamines every night for sleep?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. After a few nights, antihistamine-based aids stop helping sleep but keep causing heavy-headed mornings. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00460","question":"Why do doctors recommend walking so much?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00461","question":"How do I clean a small cut?","answer":"Running water is the best cleanser for everyday cuts; scrubbing with alcohol only adds pain and tissue damage. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00462","question":"Is a fever bad for you?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00463","question":"What are the signs of low iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00464","question":"Why do coughs last so long after a cold?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00465","question":"Can my child sleep after a head bump?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00466","question":"How much water do adults need daily?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00467","question":"Someone is not breathing - what comes first, calling or compressions?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00468","question":"Should I set multiple alarms?","answer":"Each snooze starts a new too-short sleep cycle and then interrupts it, which is why you wake up feeling drugged rather than rested. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00469","question":"What should vegans eat for protein and B12?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Eating a variety across the day covers all the building blocks; perfect combinations at every single meal are not required. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00470","question":"How active should my 8-year-old be?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00471","question":"How do I tell the difference between soreness and injury?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00472","question":"Why does my body feel better when I warm up first?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00473","question":"Where does the 10,000 steps number come from?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. Roughly 7,000-8,000 steps captures a large share of the health benefit for most adults. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00474","question":"I went from fine to flattened in one morning - is that flu?","answer":"Gradual sniffles point to a cold; sudden fever and aches point to flu. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00475","question":"What is a healthy amount of red meat?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Simple swaps make that easy: a couple of meat-free days a week, chicken or fish some nights, or smaller portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00476","question":"Will extra protein turn into muscle?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00477","question":"Is cold water better for your skin?","answer":"The post-shower itch is evaporating water pulling more moisture from skin whose oils the hot water already stripped. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00478","question":"Why do kids get cavities even with brushing?","answer":"Most young children cannot brush their own teeth well - an adult's hand finishing the job nightly is the standard advice. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00479","question":"Is sitting all day bad for you even if I exercise?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00480","question":"Should I stuff a tissue up my nose to stop the bleeding?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. Blood thinners turn a routine nosebleed into one that deserves prompt medical help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00481","question":"What are the risks of sitting too long?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00482","question":"Is it dangerous to drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00483","question":"What is the first thing to do after burning yourself?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00484","question":"I feel lightheaded - is it serious?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00485","question":"Why does handwashing take 20 seconds?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00486","question":"Can stroke symptoms go away on their own?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00487","question":"Are protein bars and cereals ultra-processed?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00488","question":"Should I call 911 before starting CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00489","question":"Why does oat milk have so little protein?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Two things to check on any plant milk: calcium fortification and added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00490","question":"Do I need rest days from exercise?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00491","question":"How long should I hold a stretch?","answer":"Hold, breathe, release - 15 to 30 seconds per stretch on warm muscles. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00492","question":"How do I sleep during the day after working nights?","answer":"Shift work fights your body clock, so the goal is defending one solid sleep block: blackout curtains, a cool quiet room, phones off, and telling household members you are off-limits. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00493","question":"What should I eat after a workout for protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Plant proteins arrive with fiber and less saturated fat, so mixing beans, lentils, nuts and whole grains through the day works very well. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00494","question":"Why do I feel so much better after a walk?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00495","question":"Do kids need structured exercise or just play?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00496","question":"Is cooking at home healthier?","answer":"Home cooking puts the salt shaker and the sugar bowl back in your hands, so you decide how much goes in. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00497","question":"What counts as strength exercise?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00498","question":"What should I do after using a tissue?","answer":"Cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue - or the crook of your elbow if there isn't one - then bin the tissue and wash your hands. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00499","question":"How do you do CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00500","question":"What is the difference between a reliever and preventer inhaler?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00501","question":"My toddler has a fever but is playing - should I worry?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. Trust the pattern: drinking, crying appropriately and responding normally are reassuring; floppiness, refusal to drink or a shrill cry are not. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00502","question":"What is a good cool-down?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00503","question":"When should I see a doctor about sleep?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00504","question":"Why does my body react to pollen?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00505","question":"What is vertigo exactly?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00506","question":"How should I store leftover rice?","answer":"The danger with rice is not reheating itself - it is the hours it spent warm in between cooking and reheating. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00507","question":"Does a higher fever mean a worse illness?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00508","question":"Are nuts good for you?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00509","question":"Is it normal to wake up in the middle of the night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. It becomes a problem when you notice the waking, check the time, and start worrying, which re-activates the brain. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00510","question":"How much fluoride should adult toothpaste have?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Not rinsing sounds odd, but the fluoride residue is the active ingredient; rinsing washes away the protection you just applied.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00511","question":"How can I tell if a product is full of sugar without a nutrition panel?","answer":"Manufacturers often spread sugar across several names - glucose, dextrose, maltose, syrups, honey - so no single one tops the ingredients list. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00512","question":"Someone cannot breathe or speak because they are choking - what do I do?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00513","question":"What is the best cough medicine?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. Warmth, moisture and honey address the irritated airway directly, which is where most cough medicine evidence points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00514","question":"What should I do if cleaning fluid gets in my eye?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00515","question":"Is my sore throat bacterial or viral?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. White tonsil patches alone are not conclusive, but combined with fever and no cough they warrant a check. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00516","question":"What are the benefits of walking daily?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00517","question":"Does water dilute stomach acid if I drink while eating?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. Water with food is harmless and can add to fullness; the 'bad for digestion' claim is a myth.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00518","question":"Why do I feel dizzy if I stop exercising suddenly?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00519","question":"How many nuts should I eat a day?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00520","question":"Do later school start times help teens?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Later start times give back the hours the late-shifted teen clock steals, and studies show teens use them to sleep more. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00521","question":"What is the difference between a reliever and preventer inhaler?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00522","question":"Why do I feel so much better after a walk?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00523","question":"Does sleeping in repay my sleep debt?","answer":"Extra weekend sleep helps somewhat, but you cannot fully bank sleep on Saturday for a week of 5-hour nights. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00524","question":"How do I avoid injury when starting a new sport?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00525","question":"What happens if I drink litres of water very quickly?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00526","question":"Is dark urine a sign that I need more water?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00527","question":"Should I put ice or butter on a burn?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. After cooling, cover it loosely with cling film or a clean lint-free cloth and leave the skin alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00528","question":"How do I avoid germs at the gym?","answer":"Gym equipment is touched by hundreds of hands a day, so wiping machines and mats before and after use is the polite and healthy move. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00529","question":"Do short bursts of exercise count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00530","question":"What is deep sleep?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. The first half of the night is deep-sleep-heavy for body repair; the second half is REM-heavy for dreams and emotional sorting. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00531","question":"What counts towards my daily fluid intake?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00532","question":"I always quit exercising after a few weeks - what am I doing wrong?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00533","question":"When do I need a new toothbrush?","answer":"Replace your toothbrush - or electric brush head - about every 3 months. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00534","question":"What should I look for on a nutrition label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00535","question":"What should I drink during a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00536","question":"How much salt should I eat a day?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00537","question":"Is ham a healthy lunch meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00538","question":"How do you do CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00539","question":"Is it bad to have a cat if you have asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00540","question":"Should I remove contact lenses after a chemical splash?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00541","question":"How many cups of fluid should I have each day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00542","question":"Does exercise still help if you start late in life?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00543","question":"How long does conjunctivitis last?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00544","question":"What lowers the risk of SIDS?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00545","question":"Can I do strength exercises at home without equipment?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00546","question":"When is a mask useful for illness?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. Masks work best combined with the basics: distance where possible, tissues, and handwashing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00547","question":"I get throbbing headaches with nausea - could they be migraines?","answer":"One-sided throbbing pain with nausea and light sensitivity is the classic migraine pattern. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00548","question":"How long should I cool down for?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00549","question":"What is DOMS?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00550","question":"Should night workers take melatonin?","answer":"Shift work fights your body clock, so the goal is defending one solid sleep block: blackout curtains, a cool quiet room, phones off, and telling household members you are off-limits. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00551","question":"How is a migraine different from a normal headache?","answer":"One-sided throbbing pain with nausea and light sensitivity is the classic migraine pattern. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00552","question":"Something is stuck in my eyelid area - what do I do?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00553","question":"Is a fruit smoothie a good replacement for breakfast fruit?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00554","question":"Is athlete's foot contagious?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. Reinfection usually comes from the shoes, socks or bathroom floor, so treat the environment along with the feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00555","question":"Can I drink coffee in the evening?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00556","question":"Should I avoid all processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00557","question":"Should I see a doctor for a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00558","question":"If I only drink tea and coffee all day, am I still hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00559","question":"Constipation with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00560","question":"Why does my towel smell after two days?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Weekly-hot washing of bedding also kills the dust mites that allergy sufferers react to. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00561","question":"Should I put oil in my ear?","answer":"Comfort care plus 3 days of patience resolves the majority of earaches. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00562","question":"What are trans fats?","answer":"Artificial trans fats are made when liquid oils are hardened into solids, and they raise heart disease risk more than any other kind of fat. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00563","question":"How long before bed should I turn off screens?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. People who use phones in bed tend to fall asleep later and sleep less overall. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00564","question":"What is the best way to build an exercise habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00565","question":"Is dancing moderate or vigorous activity?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00566","question":"Is it normal for a cold to last two weeks?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00567","question":"Why do I always overeat at dinner?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Eat slowly and pause partway through - your brain needs about 20 minutes to register fullness. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00568","question":"How are night terrors different from nightmares?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. Unlike nightmares, the child usually has no memory of it and settles back to sleep; they are harmless and typically fade with age. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00569","question":"Why do guidelines say warm or cold water?","answer":"Studies comparing water temperatures find no meaningful difference in germs removed - the soap and scrubbing do the job. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00570","question":"Why does my skin itch after a hot shower?","answer":"The post-shower itch is evaporating water pulling more moisture from skin whose oils the hot water already stripped. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00571","question":"Should I bundle up when I have a fever?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00572","question":"What does five a day actually mean?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00573","question":"I keep getting dizzy every day - should I see a doctor?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00574","question":"Should I take antihistamines every day during allergy season?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. Nasal steroid sprays added to antihistamines control stubborn seasonal allergies better than either alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00575","question":"Do masks protect others from my cough?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. The mask's main job when you are sick is source control - protecting everyone else - not protecting you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00576","question":"When should I see a doctor about sleep?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00577","question":"What is a good warm-up before running?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00578","question":"Is teeth grinding linked to stress?","answer":"Nighttime grinding (bruxism) often comes with stress, and can also travel with sleep apnea, alcohol and some medications. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00579","question":"I have fever and body aches - is this the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00580","question":"Does exercise help with stress and anxiety?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00581","question":"What should I look for on a nutrition label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00582","question":"Why does the room spin when I lie down or turn over?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00583","question":"Should I exercise through sharp pain?","answer":"Both sides, dull, fading = DOMS; one spot, sharp, swelling = get it looked at. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00584","question":"Is bed-sharing safe with a newborn?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00585","question":"Are frozen vegetables as healthy as fresh?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00586","question":"What signs mean a feverish child needs urgent care?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. A rash that does not blanch under pressure plus fever needs emergency assessment. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00587","question":"What counts as processed meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00588","question":"Are potatoes unhealthy then?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00589","question":"Should I splash water on someone who fainted?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00590","question":"Do I need to use my preventer inhaler when I feel fine?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00591","question":"Can I eat chili every day?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Hot peppers irritate an existing ulcer or bad heartburn, but they do not create ulcers - bacteria and anti-inflammatory painkillers do that. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00592","question":"Should I nap when I land after a long flight?","answer":"Your internal clock adjusts only slowly to a new time zone, so the fix is to feed it the right light and meal cues, not to force sleep. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00593","question":"How long should I hold a stretch?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00594","question":"Is five a day enough, or should I aim higher?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00595","question":"How many nuts should I eat a day?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00596","question":"Should you squeeze a stinger out?","answer":"The stinger still pumps venom after the bee is gone, so scrape it out sideways as fast as you can. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00597","question":"Does coffee count towards my daily water intake?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. Caffeine has a mild diuretic effect, but habitual drinkers build tolerance, so the overall effect is still hydration. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00598","question":"Should I suppress my cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00599","question":"What does asthma feel like?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Good control is the norm with modern treatment - frequent symptoms mean the plan needs review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00600","question":"How are night terrors different from nightmares?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. In a night terror the child is stuck between sleep stages, so waking them usually prolongs the confusion rather than helping. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00601","question":"What should I put on a burn after cooling it?","answer":"Ice and folk remedies like butter can damage the skin further or seal heat in - plain running water is the only first step that helps. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00602","question":"How long should I keep doing CPR?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00603","question":"How much exercise do I need for mental health benefits?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00604","question":"Does vitamin C help absorb iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00605","question":"Should I call an ambulance for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00606","question":"Why does my heartburn get worse at night?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00607","question":"Should I let a wound air out?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. Cover it with a clean dressing; slightly moist, protected wounds heal faster and scar less than ones left to scab in open air.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00608","question":"What should I do on rest days?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00609","question":"How do I read a food label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00610","question":"What do I do during an asthma attack?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00611","question":"Are energy drinks worse than coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00612","question":"How long do you pinch your nose for a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00613","question":"How do I know if I should see a doctor or wait it out?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00614","question":"Does flu come with a fever?","answer":"Gradual sniffles point to a cold; sudden fever and aches point to flu. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00615","question":"Is it bad to have a cat if you have asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00616","question":"How do I soothe a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00617","question":"Does exercise help with stress and anxiety?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00618","question":"Do wounds heal faster covered or uncovered?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. Cover it with a clean dressing; slightly moist, protected wounds heal faster and scar less than ones left to scab in open air.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00619","question":"How do I know if a rash is something serious?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00620","question":"My kid has a nosebleed at bedtime - what should I do?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00621","question":"Are eggs healthy?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00622","question":"What is the best treatment for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00623","question":"Do scabs help healing?","answer":"A scab is your body's natural bandage, and picking it reopens the wound and invites infection and scars. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00624","question":"What is asthma?","answer":"Asthma is inflamed airways that narrow further with triggers, producing wheeze and breathlessness. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00625","question":"Something is stuck in my eyelid area - what do I do?","answer":"Rubbing can turn a speck into a corneal scratch, which is far worse than the speck. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00626","question":"How often should I do strength training?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00627","question":"What actually helps when you have a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00628","question":"How often should I stretch to get more flexible?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00629","question":"Why do I wake up exhausted despite 8 hours in bed?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00630","question":"Should I put a burn under water right away?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00631","question":"Can the flu shot give you the flu?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00632","question":"How long should you monitor someone after a head injury?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00633","question":"How much water should I drink every day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00634","question":"When is the best time to get a flu shot?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00635","question":"Does eating before bed make you gain weight?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. A good rule of thumb is to finish a big meal 2-3 hours before you lie down, mainly to protect sleep and digestion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00636","question":"What does a severe asthma attack look like?","answer":"Upright posture, one puff every 30-60 seconds up to 10, then reassess - that is the core sequence. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00637","question":"How quickly do you need to act on a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00638","question":"What are the signs of tonsillitis?","answer":"High fever plus pus spots plus tender glands plus no cough is the strep pattern. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00639","question":"How long should you run a burn under cold water?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00640","question":"What strength exercises can I do at home for beginners?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00641","question":"How do I care for a burn blister?","answer":"Leave the blister intact; breaking it opens the door to infection. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00642","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I have not had enough to drink?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00643","question":"Dizziness with chest pain - what should I do?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00644","question":"Can I give my child melatonin?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. Melatonin is a timing signal, not a knockout drop, so 'more' does not mean 'more asleep'. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00645","question":"How much fluoride should adult toothpaste have?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Once in the morning and once last thing before bed - the bedtime brush matters most, because saliva flow drops while you sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00646","question":"Why won't the doctor give me antibiotics?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Every unnecessary antibiotic course trains bacteria to survive the next real one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00647","question":"Do older people need less sleep?","answer":"Older adults still need around 7 to 8 hours; what changes is that sleep becomes lighter, more fragmented, and shifted earlier. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00648","question":"What causes skin rashes?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00649","question":"How active should my 8-year-old be?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00650","question":"Does fiber help with cholesterol?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. People who eat more fiber tend to have lower rates of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00651","question":"What is the difference between a cold and flu?","answer":"Gradual sniffles point to a cold; sudden fever and aches point to flu. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00652","question":"When should I worry about my child's fever?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. Trust the pattern: drinking, crying appropriately and responding normally are reassuring; floppiness, refusal to drink or a shrill cry are not. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00653","question":"My knee hurts after running - is that just DOMS?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Pain that changes your stride or form is the clearest dividing line between soreness and injury. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00654","question":"Should children have less sugar than adults?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00655","question":"Is it okay to take someone else's antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00656","question":"What is a fever, actually?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00657","question":"What should I eat and drink with a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00658","question":"How much sleep does a school-age child need?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Children who regularly sleep less than their range are more likely to be irritable, struggle with learning, and behave as if hyperactive rather than sleepy. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00659","question":"What are the signs a cut is infected?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Pad around anything stuck in a wound and leave it for the professionals to remove. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00660","question":"Do vegetarians need protein powder?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Eating a variety across the day covers all the building blocks; perfect combinations at every single meal are not required. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00661","question":"Does a nightcap improve sleep quality?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. But as your body processes it, sleep fragments: more awakenings in the second half of the night, less REM, and often a bathroom trip. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00662","question":"How do I stop conjunctivitis spreading in my house?","answer":"Very contagious by touch - hygiene beats any drops for containment. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00663","question":"Does beer make you gain weight?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Calories you drink barely register as food to your appetite, which makes alcohol calories easy to overdo.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00664","question":"How much water should I drink every day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00665","question":"How do I tell the difference between hunger and thirst?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. True hunger is gradual and open to any food; a sudden longing for one specific snack is usually a craving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00666","question":"Why doesn't hand sanitizer work on norovirus?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00667","question":"What is the difference between moderate and vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00668","question":"Why does flu make your whole body ache?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00669","question":"Is beef healthy in moderation?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Cutting back is easier than it sounds: smaller portions, chicken or fish on some days, and a few meat-free meals a week all help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00670","question":"The sting site is still swollen the next day - is that normal?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00671","question":"How should I brush my child's teeth?","answer":"Most young children cannot brush their own teeth well - an adult's hand finishing the job nightly is the standard advice. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00672","question":"Which fish are oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00673","question":"Someone cannot breathe or speak because they are choking - what do I do?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00674","question":"Why do my hamstrings feel so tight?","answer":"Hold, breathe, release - 15 to 30 seconds per stretch on warm muscles. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00675","question":"Someone collapsed and will not wake up - what do I do?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00676","question":"Does lack of sleep make you gain weight?","answer":"Short sleep disturbs appetite hormones: ghrelin (hunger) rises and leptin (fullness) falls, so you feel hungrier the next day. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00677","question":"Why is processed meat worse than fresh meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00678","question":"Does calculating how much sleep I have left make insomnia worse?","answer":"Every glance at the clock triggers mental arithmetic and worry, both of which are wake-up signals to the brain. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00679","question":"When does a splinter need a doctor?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Deep, near an eye, or showing spreading redness are the times a splinter needs professional removal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00680","question":"Is a nosebleed while on blood thinners an emergency?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Deep-packed tissues tear the clot away when removed - keep it to external pressure only. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00681","question":"Do screens before bed ruin sleep?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. The stimulation matters as much as the blue light: a tense show or an endless feed keeps your nervous system alert. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00682","question":"Why is ORS better than water for a stomach bug?","answer":"ORS packets rehydrate fastest and restore the salts lost with diarrhea and vomiting. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00683","question":"What time do teenage brains want to sleep?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Meanwhile school often starts before 8:30 am, meaning chronic sleep loss across the school week. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00684","question":"Is sleeping in on weekends bad for you?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. A fixed wake time is the strongest lever you have - it sets the pressure for sleep that evening more than anything else. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00685","question":"Where do abdominal thrusts go?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00686","question":"Is full-fat or low-fat dairy better?","answer":"You do not strictly need dairy, but milk, yogurt and cheese are convenient sources of calcium, protein and iodine. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00687","question":"What helps constipation fast?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00688","question":"How much sleep does a 70 year old need?","answer":"Older adults still need around 7 to 8 hours; what changes is that sleep becomes lighter, more fragmented, and shifted earlier. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00689","question":"What lifestyle changes help acid reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00690","question":"Do sweet potatoes count as one of five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00691","question":"How should I recover from a week of short sleep?","answer":"A weekend lie-in feels great and repays a little of the debt, but only part of it - and at the cost of shifting your body clock later. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00692","question":"How do I start exercising after years of being sedentary?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00693","question":"What foods have the most fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00694","question":"Can I stop antibiotics when I feel better?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00695","question":"My toddler drank from a cleaning bottle - what do I do first?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00696","question":"Are potatoes unhealthy then?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00697","question":"Do canned and frozen vegetables count towards five a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00698","question":"Why am I tired all the time if I sleep 6 hours?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00699","question":"How long do you pinch your nose for a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00700","question":"What is the best way to bring a fever down?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00701","question":"How do I tell the difference between soreness and injury?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Pain that changes your stride or form is the clearest dividing line between soreness and injury. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00702","question":"Why do doctors keep telling me to eat more fiber?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. Diets rich in fiber are linked with a lower risk of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00703","question":"What happens if I do not get enough vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00704","question":"How much protein does the average person need?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00705","question":"How long does a stomach bug last?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00706","question":"Is it bad to skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00707","question":"Does melatonin help with jet lag?","answer":"Jet lag is your body clock still running on the time zone you left, so sleep and wake signals arrive at the wrong local hours. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00708","question":"Is 150 minutes of exercise really necessary?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00709","question":"Should I eat breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00710","question":"What about chemical or electrical burns?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00711","question":"Why do I keep eating after I am actually full?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Because fullness signals lag roughly 20 minutes behind your plate, slowing down genuinely helps you eat less. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00712","question":"How does an EpiPen work?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. Lip or tongue swelling after a sting is not a normal local reaction - treat it as an emergency. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00713","question":"There is dust in my eye - how do I get it out?","answer":"Rubbing can turn a speck into a corneal scratch, which is far worse than the speck. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00714","question":"Why does late pizza give me heartburn?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. A good rule of thumb is to finish a big meal 2-3 hours before you lie down, mainly to protect sleep and digestion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00715","question":"How long do you pinch your nose for a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00716","question":"Why am I constipated all the time?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00717","question":"What should I do if a cut gapes open?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. The gaping test is simple: if the edges will not fall together on their own, the wound usually needs closing. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00718","question":"Can my body fight a bacterial infection without antibiotics?","answer":"Cold, flu and most sore throats are viral; antibiotics do nothing for them. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00719","question":"What triggers asthma attacks?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00720","question":"Is there treatment for norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00721","question":"How fast should you do chest compressions?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00722","question":"Does honey work for coughs?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00723","question":"Should I worry if my child cries a lot after hitting their head?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00724","question":"How do I know if I am working hard enough?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00725","question":"What is orthosomnia?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. They are useful for spotting trends - short nights, late bedtimes - but not for diagnosing anything; that needs a medical sleep study. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00726","question":"Is ultra-processed food bad for you?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00727","question":"Is pink salt better for me than regular salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00728","question":"What should I drink during a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00729","question":"How long do sore throats last?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00730","question":"Should I wrap a swollen ankle?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00731","question":"What is the 48-hour rule for vomiting bugs?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00732","question":"Why do I grind my teeth at night?","answer":"Stress is the most common driver of grinding, but a blocked airway and apnea are worth ruling out when it is severe. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00733","question":"Should I stuff a tissue up my nose to stop the bleeding?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00734","question":"My grandmother fainted and was confused afterward - is that normal?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00735","question":"Why do I sleep badly after drinking?","answer":"A drink may knock you out faster, but it fragments your sleep later in the night and cuts REM. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00736","question":"How can I lose weight without a fad diet?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00737","question":"What is the daily salt limit for adults?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00738","question":"Why is my skin worse in winter?","answer":"The post-shower itch is evaporating water pulling more moisture from skin whose oils the hot water already stripped. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00739","question":"What should I do with leftover antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00740","question":"Is pink salt better for me than regular salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00741","question":"What is a night guard for?","answer":"Stress is the most common driver of grinding, but a blocked airway and apnea are worth ruling out when it is severe. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00742","question":"Is dark urine a sign that I need more water?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00743","question":"Should I use hydrogen peroxide on a cut?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. The old advice to let wounds 'breathe' has been overturned: a clean, moist, covered wound heals measurably faster.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00744","question":"How deep do compressions need to be?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00745","question":"What are head injury warning signs in children?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00746","question":"Does climbing stairs instead of the elevator really help?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00747","question":"What causes skin rashes?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00748","question":"Can stroke symptoms go away on their own?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00749","question":"How do I treat a mild rash at home?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00750","question":"How many minutes of exercise do adults need?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00751","question":"Someone cannot breathe or speak because they are choking - what do I do?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00752","question":"Are OTC cough syrups worth buying?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00753","question":"How is restless legs treated?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Because iron deficiency is one known contributor, a simple blood test is part of the standard work-up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00754","question":"My dad has chest pain and is sweating - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00755","question":"Why is the second day after a workout worse than the first?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00756","question":"Should I drink tea with my meals?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00757","question":"How do I stop tossing and turning?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Lying there frustrated teaches your brain that bed equals stress, which deepens the problem. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00758","question":"How many steps a day is actually enough?","answer":"Ten thousand is a nice round marketing number; the science says most of the benefit arrives well before it. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00759","question":"What symptoms always need medical attention?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00760","question":"My elderly mother will not drink and seems confused - is that dehydration?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. In babies: dry diapers, a sunken soft spot and listlessness are the red flags to act on. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00761","question":"Can athlete's foot spread to my nails?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. Reinfection usually comes from the shoes, socks or bathroom floor, so treat the environment along with the feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00762","question":"Why do I feel weaker if I train hard every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00763","question":"How long is one sleep cycle?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night and handles physical repair and immune housekeeping; REM clusters toward morning and handles emotion and memory processing. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00764","question":"Does using a smaller plate help you eat less?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Eat slowly and pause partway through - your brain needs about 20 minutes to register fullness. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00765","question":"How do I relieve constipation naturally?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00766","question":"What if I am alone when someone collapses?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00767","question":"When should my cold be getting better?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00768","question":"Why do doctors recommend walking so much?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00769","question":"Should I use alcohol or antiseptic on a cut?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. A fresh dressing once a day keeps the wound clean while it closes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00770","question":"Why do I keep catching colds?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00771","question":"Is it bad to share a razor?","answer":"Razor nicks and gum bleeding make those two the most important items to keep personal. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00772","question":"Does washing chicken remove bacteria?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. Washing does not remove bacteria anyway - only proper cooking does. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00773","question":"Does exercise still help if you start late in life?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00774","question":"What happens in anaphylaxis?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00775","question":"Should I call an ambulance for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00776","question":"Is a nosebleed while on blood thinners an emergency?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. Blood thinners turn a routine nosebleed into one that deserves prompt medical help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00777","question":"How can I eat smaller portions?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Because fullness signals lag roughly 20 minutes behind your plate, slowing down genuinely helps you eat less. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00778","question":"When should I worry about snoring?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. Snoring becomes a medical question when breathing pauses or daytime sleepiness join it, not because of the noise alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00779","question":"Can I eat fruit on a low-sugar diet?","answer":"Whole fruit is one of the healthiest foods there is; its sugar is wrapped in fiber and water, so it does not act like candy. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00780","question":"How much exercise should I get each week?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00781","question":"Does honey help a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00782","question":"Do I need to drink eight glasses of water a day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00783","question":"How do I stop worrying about lost sleep?","answer":"Checking the clock at night turns a small wake-up into math homework: 'if I sleep now I still get 5 hours' - and that pressure blocks sleep. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00784","question":"How do I treat a blister on my heel?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00785","question":"How do I remove a splinter?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00786","question":"What is the 48-hour rule for vomiting bugs?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00787","question":"Should I rub my eye when something is in it?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00788","question":"What does dehydrated pee look like?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00789","question":"What about chips - do they count as vegetables?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00790","question":"How do I treat heat exhaustion?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00791","question":"Does airing out rooms help against germs?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. Most respiratory viruses spread by hands and close contact, so these small habits cut the chain better than disinfecting every surface blindly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00792","question":"How do I stay motivated to exercise?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00793","question":"Can my child sleep after a head bump?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00794","question":"Is a manual toothbrush enough?","answer":"The brush is the tool; the 2 minutes of coverage is the treatment. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00795","question":"Should I put cream on a rash?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00796","question":"What counts as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00797","question":"When is the best time to nap?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Naps past about 30 minutes tip you into deep sleep, so you wake groggy - and late-afternoon or evening naps eat into the sleep pressure you need at bedtime. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00798","question":"What are the benefits of walking daily?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00799","question":"My child has a fever and purple spots - what do I do?","answer":"The glass test is simple: spots that do not fade under pressure plus fever means call emergency services now. Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00800","question":"What are the signs a cut is infected?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Redness, swelling, warmth or pus in the days after a cut mean it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00801","question":"What should I put on a scrape?","answer":"For minor cuts and scrapes, keeping the wound moist is the goal - a thin layer of petroleum jelly does that fine. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00802","question":"How often can I eat bacon and sausages?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00803","question":"When is the best time to get a flu shot?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00804","question":"Something chemical splashed in my eye and it burns - first step?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00805","question":"When should I see a doctor about constipation?","answer":"Three weeks, blood, weight loss or severe pain are the standard thresholds for a GP visit. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00806","question":"Headache with a stiff neck and fever - what could it be?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00807","question":"What strength exercises can I do at home for beginners?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00808","question":"How long can you go without peeing before worrying?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. In babies: dry diapers, a sunken soft spot and listlessness are the red flags to act on. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00809","question":"What about chips - do they count as vegetables?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00810","question":"Why do we cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00811","question":"What does a migraine feel like?","answer":"One-sided throbbing pain with nausea and light sensitivity is the classic migraine pattern. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00812","question":"What is a fever, actually?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00813","question":"How many baby carrots make one portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00814","question":"Why does my child scream in his sleep?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. Unlike nightmares, the child usually has no memory of it and settles back to sleep; they are harmless and typically fade with age. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00815","question":"Does drinking water really help constipation?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00816","question":"When should a rash go to the emergency room?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Painful, blistering or fast-spreading rashes are urgent dermatology territory, not home-care territory. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00817","question":"What are the key times to wash hands?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. Germs reach your hands mainly at these moments, which is why targeting them beats constant frantic washing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00818","question":"What is pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00819","question":"What is the difference between a cold and flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00820","question":"Why do my muscles hurt 2 days after working out?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00821","question":"When should I worry about my child's nightmares?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00822","question":"How much alcohol is safe to drink?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00823","question":"Why do I snore?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. It matters when it is loud and paired with pauses, gasping or choking during sleep and heavy daytime sleepiness - that pattern suggests sleep apnea and deserves a doctor's attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00824","question":"How long does a stomach bug last?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00825","question":"How do I get rid of a stress headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00826","question":"I am out of shape - where do I begin?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00827","question":"How much fruit juice should I drink a day?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00828","question":"Is a fever bad for you?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00829","question":"Should I wear a mask when I am sick?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. Masks work best combined with the basics: distance where possible, tissues, and handwashing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00830","question":"When does a cut need stitches?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. The gaping test is simple: if the edges will not fall together on their own, the wound usually needs closing. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00831","question":"How do I know chicken is fully cooked?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Pink can linger in safely cooked chicken and white-looking meat can still be unsafe, which is why the 74 °C number matters. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00832","question":"Why is homemade food better than takeout?","answer":"When you cook at home you control the salt, sugar, fat and portion size, which is why people who cook more tend to eat better overall. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00833","question":"Is my snacking actually dehydration?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. Real hunger builds gradually and almost any food sounds good; a sudden urge for one specific treat is usually a craving, not hunger.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00834","question":"Is coughing up blood serious?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00835","question":"Is it normal for a cold to last two weeks?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00836","question":"I feel dizzy when I stand up - could it be dehydration?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00837","question":"Is it safe to eat food past the use-by date?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. The dangerous bacteria are the quiet ones: no smell, no taste, no visible change - which is why dates, not sniffing, keep you safe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00838","question":"How do I know if a bread is really wholegrain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00839","question":"How many units is a glass of wine?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A pint of average-strength beer (4-5%) holds about 2.3 to 2.8 units, and a 175 ml glass of wine is about 2.3 units. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00840","question":"Is breakfast the most important meal of the day?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00841","question":"Is it safe to start exercising at my age?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00842","question":"What counts as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00843","question":"Why do most people need more fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00844","question":"How much salad counts as a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00845","question":"Should I take medicine for a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00846","question":"Do I need to exercise for 10 minutes straight for it to matter?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00847","question":"Is a cold caused by bacteria?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00848","question":"When does dehydration need a hospital?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00849","question":"How long should I wash my hands?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00850","question":"How long does an ear infection last?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Nothing goes into an aching ear without advice; drops in a perforated eardrum can cause damage. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00851","question":"Is peanut butter as healthy as whole nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00852","question":"What counts as activity for children?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00853","question":"Why is reheated rice risky?","answer":"Uncooked rice can carry spores of a bacterium that survives cooking; if cooked rice sits warm on the counter, those spores multiply and make toxins. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00854","question":"Is there treatment for norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00855","question":"How do I clear a choking infant?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00856","question":"What does the traffic light label mean?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00857","question":"Dizziness with chest pain - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00858","question":"Do I need mouthwash?","answer":"Mouthwash cannot replace brushing and interdental cleaning, because it cannot remove plaque - only mechanical cleaning does that. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00859","question":"What are examples of muscle-strengthening activities?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00860","question":"Why is my kid so sedentary and what do I do?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00861","question":"Is sleeping in on weekends bad for you?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. Big weekend lie-ins feel like repayment but create 'social jet lag', leaving Monday mornings feeling like jet lag after a long flight. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00862","question":"Should I take medicine to bring a fever down?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00863","question":"Why am I wide awake after evening workouts?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00864","question":"What is the difference between a viral and bacterial infection?","answer":"Cold, flu and most sore throats are viral; antibiotics do nothing for them. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00865","question":"How many grams of salt is too much?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00866","question":"What are the benefits of walking daily?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00867","question":"Is day-old rice from a takeaway safe?","answer":"The danger with rice is not reheating itself - it is the hours it spent warm in between cooking and reheating. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00868","question":"Is banana too sugary to eat every day?","answer":"The natural sugar in whole fruit arrives packaged with fiber, water, vitamins and antioxidants, so it is absorbed slowly and is not a worry for almost everyone. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00869","question":"When is the most important time to brush?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acid temporarily softens enamel, and brushing during that window wears it; the fluoride-and-saliva repair needs time. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00870","question":"Does honey help a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00871","question":"What are the signs of dehydration?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00872","question":"Are OTC cough syrups worth buying?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. Warmth, moisture and honey address the irritated airway directly, which is where most cough medicine evidence points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00873","question":"The whole family is vomiting - what is going around?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00874","question":"Is sharing nail clippers safe?","answer":"Razor nicks and gum bleeding make those two the most important items to keep personal. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00875","question":"Is pink eye contagious?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Rubbing is the self-spreading mechanism; washcloths and makeup are the household vectors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00876","question":"Is sleep apnea dangerous?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00877","question":"What is histamine?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00878","question":"When should an adult with a fever see a doctor?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00879","question":"Is walking really good exercise?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00880","question":"The blister on my burn popped on its own - now what?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00881","question":"Why should I swap soda for water?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00882","question":"Who gets sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00883","question":"I get throbbing headaches with nausea - could they be migraines?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. Frequent or disabling migraines are very treatable now - no one should simply endure them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00884","question":"Are smoothies a good breakfast?","answer":"Build breakfast around fiber plus protein - for example oatmeal with berries and nuts, or eggs with wholegrain toast. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00885","question":"What helps a sore throat fast?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00886","question":"Is heartburn the same as a heart problem?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Cardiac chest pain and reflux pain can feel similar - when unsure, treat it as urgent.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00887","question":"Do naps count towards my nightly sleep?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Naps past about 30 minutes tip you into deep sleep, so you wake groggy - and late-afternoon or evening naps eat into the sleep pressure you need at bedtime. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00888","question":"How many minutes of exercise do adults need?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00889","question":"How are choking steps different for babies?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00890","question":"How should I wash a knife after raw meat?","answer":"Cross-contamination is the most common way kitchen germs reach people: raw meat touching a salad board, then lunch. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00891","question":"Is the flu vaccine worth it?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00892","question":"How much fiber do I need for regular bowel movements?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00893","question":"My nose is bleeding - should I tilt my head back?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00894","question":"How do you do the Heimlich maneuver?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00895","question":"When should I see a doctor about a cough?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00896","question":"When should I call 911 instead of driving to the hospital?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00897","question":"My toddler drank from a cleaning bottle - what do I do first?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00898","question":"Why is ultra-processed food so easy to overeat?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00899","question":"When is dehydration serious?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. In babies: dry diapers, a sunken soft spot and listlessness are the red flags to act on. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00900","question":"What cough signs mean urgent care?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00901","question":"How much dairy should I have a day?","answer":"Dairy is an easy calcium-and-protein package, but you can get the same nutrients from fortified plant drinks, leafy greens, tofu and fish with soft bones. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00902","question":"What does the traffic light label mean?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00903","question":"Do animal bites need medical attention?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. The gaping test is simple: if the edges will not fall together on their own, the wound usually needs closing. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00904","question":"Is fluid coming from my ear serious?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00905","question":"Why does flu make your whole body ache?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00906","question":"How do I know if a burn is serious?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. White, leathery or charred-looking skin means a deep burn, and chemical or electrical burns always need professional assessment. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00907","question":"Is canned soup healthy?","answer":"Salt is the catch with store-bought soup: many brands pack a large share of your daily limit into one serving. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00908","question":"How long should a sprain take to get better?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. A joint that will not take weight may be fractured - get it looked at. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00909","question":"Why do I get itchy rashes in winter?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00910","question":"Why do doctors recommend honey over cough syrup?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. Warmth, moisture and honey address the irritated airway directly, which is where most cough medicine evidence points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00911","question":"Is it safe to start exercising at my age?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00912","question":"How much caffeine is in tea compared to coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00913","question":"Why is my eye red and sticky?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00914","question":"Do crash diets work?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00915","question":"How long does caffeine stay in your body?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00916","question":"How many puffs can I take in an asthma attack?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00917","question":"How many nuts should I eat a day?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00918","question":"My child's ear hurts and she has a fever - when do we go in?","answer":"Three days, discharge, or a genuinely unwell child are the standard referral points. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00919","question":"Which foods are high in protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and whole grains add up to plenty of protein over a day, with extra fiber as a bonus. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00920","question":"How do I know if a bread is really wholegrain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00921","question":"What should I eat after a workout for protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and whole grains add up to plenty of protein over a day, with extra fiber as a bonus. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00922","question":"What about chips - do they count as vegetables?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00923","question":"Can I brush my teeth straight after drinking orange juice?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acid temporarily softens enamel, and brushing during that window wears it; the fluoride-and-saliva repair needs time. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00924","question":"There is glass in my hand - should I pull it out?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00925","question":"How short can an exercise session be and still count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00926","question":"Why does handwashing take 20 seconds?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00927","question":"How much salmon counts as a portion?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00928","question":"How long after a stomach bug am I contagious?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed norovirus for two days after they feel fine - and it takes only a few particles to infect someone. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00929","question":"Is a cold caused by bacteria?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00930","question":"Should I suppress my cough?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00931","question":"How long does the flu last?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00932","question":"How do I treat an earache at home?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00933","question":"Do I need rest days from exercise?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00934","question":"What drinks can I have instead of coffee when pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00935","question":"Does a standing desk count as being active?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00936","question":"How can I tell a craving from real hunger?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. Real hunger builds gradually and almost any food sounds good; a sudden urge for one specific treat is usually a craving, not hunger.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00937","question":"What does a meningitis rash look like?","answer":"The glass test is simple: spots that do not fade under pressure plus fever means call emergency services now. Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00938","question":"Should I drink tea with my meals?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00939","question":"Why is my throat worse in the morning?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00940","question":"Cough with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00941","question":"Should I keep my bedroom warm or cold?","answer":"Your core temperature needs to drop a little to fall asleep, so a cool room works with your biology rather than against it. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00942","question":"Why is my eye red and sticky?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00943","question":"How are choking steps different for babies?","answer":"Support the baby's head, keep it lower than the body, and give up to 5 back blows between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00944","question":"How do I stop a cut from bleeding?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00945","question":"Are eggs bad for your cholesterol?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00946","question":"Can athlete's foot spread to my nails?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. It spreads through damp floors and shared towels, so dry feet well, change socks daily, and don't share footwear.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00947","question":"What is fried rice syndrome?","answer":"The danger with rice is not reheating itself - it is the hours it spent warm in between cooking and reheating. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00948","question":"When should I see a doctor for a sprain?","answer":"Inability to bear weight, visible deformity or numbness all mean it needs an examination, not home care. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00949","question":"My baby fell off the bed onto the carpet - how do I know if he is okay?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00950","question":"What cleaner kills norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed norovirus for two days after they feel fine - and it takes only a few particles to infect someone. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00951","question":"How many sugar cubes a day is considered OK?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00952","question":"How much fiber do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00953","question":"Why do I get itchy rashes in winter?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00954","question":"Is fluid coming from my ear serious?","answer":"Three days, discharge, or a genuinely unwell child are the standard referral points. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00955","question":"How big is one of my five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00956","question":"I bumped my head and feel fine - should I still watch for something?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Watch the person for two days: any repeated vomiting, growing confusion or unusual sleepiness means emergency care. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00957","question":"How fast should I increase my exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00958","question":"Will extra protein turn into muscle?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00959","question":"How do I stop heartburn without medicine?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00960","question":"Does airing out rooms help against germs?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. The high-traffic touchpoints matter more than doorknobs nobody touches - focus cleaning where hands actually go.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00961","question":"Do I need to wash hands after touching my phone?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. Germs reach your hands mainly at these moments, which is why targeting them beats constant frantic washing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00962","question":"What are trans fats?","answer":"Trans fats are the unhealthiest fats there are - worse than saturated fat - which is why most countries have banned artificial ones. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00963","question":"Can you do the Heimlich maneuver on a baby?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Abdominal thrusts are for adults and older children only - for babies they cause real harm. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00964","question":"What should I put on a burn after cooling it?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00965","question":"Can being dehydrated give you a headache?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00966","question":"How much alcohol is safe to drink?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00967","question":"How do I avoid injury when starting a new sport?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00968","question":"Is fruit juice as healthy as whole fruit?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00969","question":"Can a 5 pm coffee ruin my night?","answer":"Half the caffeine from your 3 pm coffee is still circulating around 8 pm - right when your body should be winding down. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00970","question":"Is washing hands with water alone enough?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00971","question":"When does a fever become an emergency for adults?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00972","question":"How do I spot sugar in an ingredients list?","answer":"Manufacturers often spread sugar across several names - glucose, dextrose, maltose, syrups, honey - so no single one tops the ingredients list. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00973","question":"Can low iron make me tired?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00974","question":"How do I stay hydrated with a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00975","question":"Is exercise good for depression?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00976","question":"Should I raise my bed for heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00977","question":"Can my kid go to school with pink eye?","answer":"Very contagious by touch - hygiene beats any drops for containment. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00978","question":"Do I need to use my preventer inhaler when I feel fine?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00979","question":"How do I stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00980","question":"Should I floss before or after brushing?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Gums that bleed with flossing are gums that need more flossing, not less - the inflammation is what causes the blood. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00981","question":"How many rest days should I take a week?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00982","question":"Do eggs cause heart disease?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00983","question":"Why do I need a flu shot every year?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00984","question":"Should I choose salted or unsalted nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00985","question":"What does dehydration feel like?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00986","question":"How do I keep my feet healthy?","answer":"Fungus thrives in warm, damp, dark places - exactly what sweaty, enclosed toes provide when left damp. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00987","question":"What is oral rehydration solution?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00988","question":"I always quit exercising after a few weeks - what am I doing wrong?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00989","question":"Does exercise help with stress and anxiety?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00990","question":"Why do my muscles hurt 2 days after working out?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00991","question":"How often should I wash gym clothes?","answer":"Gym equipment is touched by hundreds of hands a day, so wiping machines and mats before and after use is the polite and healthy move. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00992","question":"Why is my eye red and sticky?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00993","question":"What are the signs of a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00994","question":"Why is oily fish good for you?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00995","question":"How long does caffeine stay in your system before bed?","answer":"Half the caffeine from your 3 pm coffee is still circulating around 8 pm - right when your body should be winding down. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00996","question":"Why can I not keep my legs still when trying to sleep?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Because iron deficiency is one known contributor, a simple blood test is part of the standard work-up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00997","question":"Why is it bad to check the time at night?","answer":"Every glance at the clock triggers mental arithmetic and worry, both of which are wake-up signals to the brain. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00998","question":"Will my food taste bland if I cut salt?","answer":"Eating too much salt makes your body hold on to extra water, which pushes blood pressure up. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-00999","question":"Does caffeine dehydrate you?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. Caffeine has a mild diuretic effect, but habitual drinkers build tolerance, so the overall effect is still hydration. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01000","question":"My grandmother fainted and was confused afterward - is that normal?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01001","question":"What is the first thing to do after burning yourself?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01002","question":"Why do I dream more before waking?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night and handles physical repair and immune housekeeping; REM clusters toward morning and handles emotion and memory processing. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01003","question":"Is full-fat or low-fat dairy better?","answer":"Dairy is an easy calcium-and-protein package, but you can get the same nutrients from fortified plant drinks, leafy greens, tofu and fish with soft bones. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01004","question":"Headache with a stiff neck and fever - what could it be?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01005","question":"Why can I not stick to an exercise routine?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01006","question":"What counts as activity for children?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01007","question":"What should I drink instead of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01008","question":"What makes asthma worse in winter?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01009","question":"Should you squeeze a stinger out?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. After the stinger is out, soap and water plus a wrapped cold pack are all most stings need. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01010","question":"Is 150 minutes of exercise really necessary?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01011","question":"Does caffeine count from tea and chocolate in pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01012","question":"Is coconut oil healthy?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fat pushes LDL cholesterol up, so trading butter for olive or rapeseed oil is a heart-friendly deal. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01013","question":"How long should I cool down for?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01014","question":"Do wounds heal faster covered or uncovered?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. The old advice to let wounds 'breathe' has been overturned: a clean, moist, covered wound heals measurably faster.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01015","question":"Why are nuts recommended if they are so high in calories?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01016","question":"When is a headache an emergency?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01017","question":"How can I quickly judge if a food is healthy?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01018","question":"When should I see a doctor about a cough?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01019","question":"How do I start exercising after years of being sedentary?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01020","question":"When should I call an ambulance for asthma?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. Speech in single words, lips tinged blue, or no response to 10 puffs all mean an emergency call now. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01021","question":"How should I recover from a week of short sleep?","answer":"Extra weekend sleep helps somewhat, but you cannot fully bank sleep on Saturday for a week of 5-hour nights. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01022","question":"Should I raise the cut above my heart?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01023","question":"Should I rinse after brushing my teeth?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Once in the morning and once last thing before bed - the bedtime brush matters most, because saliva flow drops while you sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01024","question":"How much salt should I eat a day?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01025","question":"What should I look for in a milk alternative?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Two things to check on any plant milk: calcium fortification and added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01026","question":"Can you drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01027","question":"How far do sneezes travel?","answer":"Cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue - or the crook of your elbow if there isn't one - then bin the tissue and wash your hands. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01028","question":"Why do I feel dizzy if I stop exercising suddenly?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01029","question":"What are the signs of heat exhaustion?","answer":"Sweating plus dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps in a hot setting is the classic heat exhaustion picture. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01030","question":"Why does calcium matter as I get older?","answer":"Aim for about 700 mg daily - roughly three portions of dairy or fortified alternatives spread across the day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01031","question":"Why is drying between my toes important?","answer":"Fungus thrives in warm, damp, dark places - exactly what sweaty, enclosed toes provide when left damp. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01032","question":"Does sleeping more help you recover from illness?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. During deep sleep the immune system ramps up defensive activity, which is one reason rest is prescribed for illness. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01033","question":"What causes skin rashes?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01034","question":"Does a drink before bed ruin sleep quality?","answer":"Alcohol makes you drowsy and can help you fall asleep faster, but it disrupts the second half of the night. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01035","question":"Which allergy medicine will not make me sleepy?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. Nasal steroid sprays added to antihistamines control stubborn seasonal allergies better than either alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01036","question":"Why does my ear hurt after a cold?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01037","question":"What actually causes allergy symptoms?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01038","question":"What causes the common cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01039","question":"Does honey work for coughs?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. Warmth, moisture and honey address the irritated airway directly, which is where most cough medicine evidence points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01040","question":"What should vegans eat for protein and B12?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Different plants are low in different amino acids, so variety over the day - not one magic food pairing - does the job. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01041","question":"Can I stop antibiotics when I feel better?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01042","question":"Is a headache a sign I need water?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01043","question":"Why do my eyes and nose itch when I am near cats?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01044","question":"How often should I do strength training?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01045","question":"My grandma says put butter on burns - is that right?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01046","question":"How often should I get up from my desk?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01047","question":"Do you need to stretch after a workout?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01048","question":"Should I cut all fat from my diet?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fat pushes LDL cholesterol up, so trading butter for olive or rapeseed oil is a heart-friendly deal. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01049","question":"Is raw honey better for you?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01050","question":"Can you do the Heimlich maneuver on a baby?","answer":"Support the baby's head, keep it lower than the body, and give up to 5 back blows between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01051","question":"How does drinking affect REM sleep?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. In the second half of the night, as alcohol wears off, sleep becomes shallow and broken - that is the rebound. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01052","question":"Which fats are healthy?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fats - butter, ghee, coconut oil, fatty processed meat and pastry - raise cholesterol, so keep them to a smaller share of your diet. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01053","question":"Can I exercise every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01054","question":"Is sleep apnea dangerous?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01055","question":"What is asthma?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Good control is the norm with modern treatment - frequent symptoms mean the plan needs review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01056","question":"Should I splash water on someone who fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01057","question":"What actually helps a night-time cough?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01058","question":"When does dehydration need a hospital?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. In babies: dry diapers, a sunken soft spot and listlessness are the red flags to act on. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01059","question":"What makes meat 'processed'?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01060","question":"How long will a sting stay swollen?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01061","question":"Why does caffeine matter when you are expecting?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01062","question":"Why is it hard to breathe out when you have asthma?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Good control is the norm with modern treatment - frequent symptoms mean the plan needs review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01063","question":"How do I treat an earache at home?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Nothing goes into an aching ear without advice; drops in a perforated eardrum can cause damage. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01064","question":"When should I call 911 instead of driving to the hospital?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01065","question":"Can I eat food past its best-before date?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. The dangerous bacteria are the quiet ones: no smell, no taste, no visible change - which is why dates, not sniffing, keep you safe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01066","question":"How do I remove earwax safely?","answer":"Earwax is protective - it traps dust, moisturizes the canal and has antibacterial properties - and ears clean themselves as old wax works out naturally. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01067","question":"Does climbing stairs instead of the elevator really help?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01068","question":"What is a healthy breakfast?","answer":"Build breakfast around fiber plus protein - for example oatmeal with berries and nuts, or eggs with wholegrain toast. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01069","question":"Can I eat as much honey as I want?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01070","question":"How many eggs is a day's worth of protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01071","question":"Do potatoes count towards five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01072","question":"How dirty is my phone?","answer":"Your phone travels to the bathroom, the kitchen and everywhere your hands go, picking up whatever they touch, and is cleaned far less often than your hands. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01073","question":"What lowers the risk of SIDS?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01074","question":"My cold is on day 8 - is that normal?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01075","question":"Should I drink water before meals?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. It is a harmless habit with modest benefits: helpful, but not a magic weight-loss trick.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01076","question":"Do I really need to warm up?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01077","question":"Is dancing moderate or vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01078","question":"Why do long naps make me groggy?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Naps past about 30 minutes tip you into deep sleep, so you wake groggy - and late-afternoon or evening naps eat into the sleep pressure you need at bedtime. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01079","question":"Can low iron cause restless legs?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Because iron deficiency is one known contributor, a simple blood test is part of the standard work-up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01080","question":"Can I do all my exercise on the weekend?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01081","question":"Can I give my child melatonin?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. It is not a classic sleeping pill - it nudges timing rather than forcing sedation - and evidence for ordinary insomnia is mixed. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01082","question":"Should I eat with diarrhea and vomiting?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01083","question":"How do I take the stinger out of a sting?","answer":"The stinger still pumps venom after the bee is gone, so scrape it out sideways as fast as you can. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01084","question":"Why do my hamstrings feel so tight?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. The injury-prevention case for pre-exercise stretching is not well supported; the flexibility case for regular stretching is.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01085","question":"Is it too late to start exercising at 60?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01086","question":"Can thirst feel like hunger?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. Real hunger builds gradually and almost any food sounds good; a sudden urge for one specific treat is usually a craving, not hunger.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01087","question":"What causes the common cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01088","question":"Should I suppress my cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01089","question":"Should I pop a blister from new shoes?","answer":"Resist popping it; the fluid is sterile and the skin over it does the job of a dressing. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01090","question":"Is a fruit smoothie a good replacement for breakfast fruit?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01091","question":"Why did advice about eggs change?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01092","question":"When should a rash go to the emergency room?","answer":"The glass test is simple: spots that do not fade under pressure plus fever means call emergency services now. Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01093","question":"Can older adults still build muscle?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01094","question":"Is 6,000 steps a day okay?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01095","question":"Why compare foods per 100 grams?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01096","question":"What is norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01097","question":"How do you treat a jellyfish sting at the beach?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01098","question":"How often should I do strength training?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01099","question":"Is bacon bad for you?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01100","question":"What time of day is best to exercise for sleep?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01101","question":"What happens if I eat too little fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01102","question":"What is the daily limit for added sugar?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01103","question":"What is heartburn?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01104","question":"When should a headache after hitting my head worry me?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01105","question":"How long before bed should I stop eating?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. Leaving about 2 to 3 hours between a large meal and bed is a comfortable rule of thumb.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01106","question":"How short can an exercise session be and still count?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01107","question":"How can I reduce scarring?","answer":"A scab is your body's natural bandage, and picking it reopens the wound and invites infection and scars. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01108","question":"Can I drink juice instead of eating fruit?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01109","question":"My doctor refused antibiotics for my cold - why?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01110","question":"The splinter broke off under my skin - what do I do?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01111","question":"Is it bad to have a cat if you have asthma?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01112","question":"Are nuts good for you?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01113","question":"Is it bad to have a cat if you have asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01114","question":"What temperature should handwashing water be?","answer":"Studies comparing water temperatures find no meaningful difference in germs removed - the soap and scrubbing do the job. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01115","question":"Someone cannot breathe or speak because they are choking - what do I do?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01116","question":"Is it bad to skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01117","question":"When should I see a doctor about migraines?","answer":"One-sided throbbing pain with nausea and light sensitivity is the classic migraine pattern. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01118","question":"I touched a hot pan and my fingers hurt - what should I do first?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01119","question":"Is a fever bad for you?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01120","question":"How long does a stomach bug last?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01121","question":"How much fruit juice should I drink a day?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01122","question":"How long after a stomach bug am I contagious?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. Stay off work, school and visits until 48 hours after the last episode of vomiting or diarrhea, because that is how long you keep shedding the virus. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01123","question":"Is bacon bad for you?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01124","question":"Can exercise improve my mood?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01125","question":"What does a severe asthma attack look like?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01126","question":"Is milk good for you?","answer":"Dairy is an easy calcium-and-protein package, but you can get the same nutrients from fortified plant drinks, leafy greens, tofu and fish with soft bones. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01127","question":"Someone swallowed pills - when is it an emergency?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01128","question":"What are the signs that need an ambulance immediately?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01129","question":"What is the first thing to do after burning yourself?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01130","question":"Why use a preventer inhaler every day?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01131","question":"What helps a headache from staring at screens?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01132","question":"Why do I sleep badly after drinking?","answer":"Alcohol makes you drowsy and can help you fall asleep faster, but it disrupts the second half of the night. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01133","question":"Can my phone carry germs?","answer":"Phones meet your face and hands dozens of times a day, which makes them a highway for whatever germs you picked up last. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01134","question":"How do I avoid trans fats?","answer":"Trans fats are the unhealthiest fats there are - worse than saturated fat - which is why most countries have banned artificial ones. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01135","question":"Does freezing destroy vitamins in vegetables?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01136","question":"Is pink eye serious?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01137","question":"Why can't babies have honey?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01138","question":"Why do I get headaches at my desk?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01139","question":"Do antibiotics help a cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01140","question":"What should I do if I get stung by a bee?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01141","question":"How do I relieve constipation naturally?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01142","question":"When is fainting an emergency?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01143","question":"What helps a sore throat fast?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01144","question":"How do I stop a cut from bleeding?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01145","question":"Does drinking water really help constipation?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01146","question":"Why do I wake up at night after drinking?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. But as your body processes it, sleep fragments: more awakenings in the second half of the night, less REM, and often a bathroom trip. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01147","question":"What causes skin rashes?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01148","question":"What is the safe way to get a splinter out?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01149","question":"What is the difference between deodorant and antiperspirant?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Claims linking antiperspirant ingredients to cancer have been tested and do not hold up; the safety record is long and clean. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01150","question":"Why is tilting your head back during a nosebleed wrong?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. Blood thinners turn a routine nosebleed into one that deserves prompt medical help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01151","question":"Does freezing destroy vitamins in vegetables?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01152","question":"What triggers migraines?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01153","question":"Do sleep apps help or hurt sleep?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. A wristwatch guesses at stages from movement and heart rate; only a clinic study with brain sensors can measure sleep properly. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01154","question":"Can I reheat rice safely?","answer":"The danger with rice is not reheating itself - it is the hours it spent warm in between cooking and reheating. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01155","question":"I feel lightheaded - is it serious?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01156","question":"Should I stretch before or after exercise?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01157","question":"How can I stop snoring?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. It matters when it is loud and paired with pauses, gasping or choking during sleep and heavy daytime sleepiness - that pattern suggests sleep apnea and deserves a doctor's attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01158","question":"When should I call 911 instead of driving to the hospital?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01159","question":"What does a migraine feel like?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. Frequent or disabling migraines are very treatable now - no one should simply endure them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01160","question":"Do I really need to warm up?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01161","question":"Should I see a doctor before starting to exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01162","question":"Are nightmares normal for children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01163","question":"Do canned and frozen vegetables count towards five a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01164","question":"Why is earwax good?","answer":"The only thing you should ever clean with a bud is the outer ear; inside the canal, the bud is the problem. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01165","question":"Does vitamin C cure a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01166","question":"Should I drive myself to the hospital with chest pain?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01167","question":"What is pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01168","question":"My toddler fell and bumped her head - what should I do?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01169","question":"Can the flu shot give you the flu?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01170","question":"Why do experts say plain soap is fine?","answer":"For everyday handwashing, plain soap is just as effective as 'antibacterial' varieties. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01171","question":"Should I put a burn under water right away?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01172","question":"How fast should I increase my exercise?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01173","question":"Does exercise help with stress and anxiety?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01174","question":"Is honey healthier than sugar?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01175","question":"How fast should I increase my exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01176","question":"Why is frozen spinach cheaper but still good for me?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01177","question":"How do you do the Heimlich maneuver?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01178","question":"What counts towards my daily fluid intake?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01179","question":"How much salad counts as a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01180","question":"Why should I not scratch a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01181","question":"Why use a preventer inhaler every day?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01182","question":"Can exercise cancel out a sedentary job?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01183","question":"Why do I need a flu shot every year?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01184","question":"Why wait to brush after eating acidic food?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acidic food and drink soften enamel for a while, so brushing immediately after juice, soda or fruit can scrub the softened layer away. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01185","question":"What causes sudden dizziness?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01186","question":"How do I make someone with a fever comfortable?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01187","question":"Is it lazy or actually fine to rely on frozen veg?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01188","question":"What is jet lag?","answer":"Your internal clock adjusts only slowly to a new time zone, so the fix is to feed it the right light and meal cues, not to force sleep. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01189","question":"Why do I get headaches at my desk?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01190","question":"How much caffeine is safe during pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01191","question":"How do I stop worrying about lost sleep?","answer":"Every glance at the clock triggers mental arithmetic and worry, both of which are wake-up signals to the brain. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01192","question":"Is almond milk healthy?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Choose drinks fortified with calcium - and ideally vitamin D and B12 - and check the label for added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01193","question":"I always quit exercising after a few weeks - what am I doing wrong?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01194","question":"Do over-the-counter sleep aids work?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. Tolerance builds within days - the sleepiness fades while the grogginess the next morning does not. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01195","question":"What should I do if my child swallowed something harmful?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01196","question":"What should I do if my child swallowed something harmful?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01197","question":"Is 150 minutes of exercise really necessary?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01198","question":"Should I see a doctor before starting to exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01199","question":"Can cold weather set off asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01200","question":"Why do long naps make me groggy?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Long naps pull you into deep sleep stages, and waking from those produces that heavy, hungover feeling. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01201","question":"Does stretching prevent injury?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. The injury-prevention case for pre-exercise stretching is not well supported; the flexibility case for regular stretching is.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01202","question":"Why is drowsy driving compared to drunk driving?","answer":"Being awake for 17-19 hours impairs driving about as much as being over the legal alcohol limit in many countries. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01203","question":"Why is wholegrain bread better than white bread?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01204","question":"How do I know if my cut needs stitches?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Redness, swelling, warmth or pus in the days after a cut mean it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01205","question":"Does fruit have too much sugar?","answer":"The natural sugar in whole fruit arrives packaged with fiber, water, vitamins and antioxidants, so it is absorbed slowly and is not a worry for almost everyone. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01206","question":"How do I clean a wound?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. Cover it with a clean dressing; slightly moist, protected wounds heal faster and scar less than ones left to scab in open air.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01207","question":"Do vegetarians need protein powder?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Different plants are low in different amino acids, so variety over the day - not one magic food pairing - does the job. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01208","question":"How quickly do you need to act on a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01209","question":"How short can an exercise session be and still count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01210","question":"How do I know if I am not drinking enough?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01211","question":"What are the key times to wash hands?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. These moments are where germs actually travel onto your hands and then to your mouth, eyes and food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01212","question":"Why is it easier to sleep in a cool room?","answer":"Your core temperature needs to drop a little to fall asleep, so a cool room works with your biology rather than against it. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01213","question":"I feel dizzy when I stand up - could it be dehydration?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01214","question":"What is sleep hygiene?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. If you have been awake for what feels like 20 minutes, get up, do something calm in dim light, and return when sleepy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01215","question":"What signs mean a feverish child needs urgent care?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01216","question":"What does acid reflux feel like?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Cardiac chest pain and reflux pain can feel similar - when unsure, treat it as urgent.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01217","question":"Why is homemade food better than takeout?","answer":"Home cooking puts the salt shaker and the sugar bowl back in your hands, so you decide how much goes in. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01218","question":"Do I need sleeping pills for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01219","question":"How do I clean after norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01220","question":"Is pink chicken safe to eat?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Pink can linger in safely cooked chicken and white-looking meat can still be unsafe, which is why the 74 °C number matters. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01221","question":"Can a fan help me sleep better?","answer":"Steady background sound does not lull you to sleep directly - it prevents the sudden noises that snap you back awake. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01222","question":"When is vertigo an emergency?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01223","question":"When does a cut need medical attention?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Pad around anything stuck in a wound and leave it for the professionals to remove. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01224","question":"Do antibiotics interfere with the pill?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01225","question":"Can I wear a mask on public transport with a cold?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. Masks work best combined with the basics: distance where possible, tissues, and handwashing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01226","question":"Why do I regain weight after dieting?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01227","question":"Can babies have honey for a cough?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. Warmth, moisture and honey address the irritated airway directly, which is where most cough medicine evidence points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01228","question":"Should I sponge someone with a fever with cold water?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01229","question":"Is alcohol good for cleaning wounds?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. Cover it with a clean dressing; slightly moist, protected wounds heal faster and scar less than ones left to scab in open air.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01230","question":"Which foods count as whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01231","question":"Are potatoes unhealthy then?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01232","question":"Can I do all my exercise on the weekend?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01233","question":"Is it bad to let a wound scab?","answer":"A scab is your body's natural bandage, and picking it reopens the wound and invites infection and scars. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01234","question":"Does cooking from scratch save money?","answer":"When you cook at home you control the salt, sugar, fat and portion size, which is why people who cook more tend to eat better overall. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01235","question":"Do antibiotics interfere with the pill?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01236","question":"There is dust in my eye - how do I get it out?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Anything actually embedded in the eyeball stays there until a professional removes it. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01237","question":"Why is the second day after a workout worse than the first?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01238","question":"How much water is too much in one day?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01239","question":"When should I use mouthwash?","answer":"Mouthwash cannot replace brushing and interdental cleaning, because it cannot remove plaque - only mechanical cleaning does that. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01240","question":"How many baby carrots make one portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01241","question":"Does a nightcap improve sleep quality?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. In the second half of the night, as alcohol wears off, sleep becomes shallow and broken - that is the rebound. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01242","question":"Is dancing moderate or vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01243","question":"What should I do with leftover antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01244","question":"When should I worry about my child's fever?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. A rash that does not blanch under pressure plus fever needs emergency assessment. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01245","question":"Can I do all my exercise on the weekend?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01246","question":"What are good sources of protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Plant proteins arrive with fiber and less saturated fat, so mixing beans, lentils, nuts and whole grains through the day works very well. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01247","question":"My child's ear hurts and she has a fever - when do we go in?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01248","question":"How many eggs is a day's worth of protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01249","question":"Do I need to cover a cut with a plaster?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01250","question":"Can my baby sleep on their tummy?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01251","question":"What actually causes allergy symptoms?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01252","question":"Why does afternoon coffee keep me awake?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01253","question":"My friend is choking on a piece of steak but can still talk a little - what now?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01254","question":"How much exercise do I need for mental health benefits?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01255","question":"How fast does walking need to be to count as exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01256","question":"Can I drink coffee while pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01257","question":"Is there a link between sleep and getting colds?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. During deep sleep the immune system ramps up defensive activity, which is one reason rest is prescribed for illness. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01258","question":"Where should I store raw meat in the fridge?","answer":"Cross-contamination is the most common way kitchen germs reach people: raw meat touching a salad board, then lunch. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01259","question":"What should I do if cleaning fluid gets in my eye?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01260","question":"Is bleeding gums normal?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Bleeding at first usually means the gums are already inflamed; daily cleaning reverses that within a week or two, and the bleeding stops. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01261","question":"How do I start exercising after years of being sedentary?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01262","question":"Someone swallowed pills - when is it an emergency?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01263","question":"I got the worst headache of my life very suddenly - what should I do?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01264","question":"My dad has chest pain and is sweating - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01265","question":"Is an all-nighter ever worth it?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. Skimping on sleep after study measurably erases some of the benefit of that study. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01266","question":"How do I know if my sore throat is strep?","answer":"High fever plus pus spots plus tender glands plus no cough is the strep pattern. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01267","question":"When should I worry about dizziness?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01268","question":"Is being sore after exercise a good sign?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01269","question":"Why do I get headaches at my desk?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01270","question":"How do I treat a mild rash at home?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01271","question":"Does salt raise blood pressure?","answer":"Salt makes your body retain water, and that extra fluid raises the pressure inside your blood vessels. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01272","question":"When is a headache an emergency?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01273","question":"Someone is choking but still coughing - what should I do?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01274","question":"What lifestyle changes help acid reflux?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01275","question":"Should I drive myself to the hospital with chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01276","question":"Can daylight exposure fix insomnia?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Getting outside for even 10-20 minutes in the morning makes you more alert by day and sleepier on schedule at night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01277","question":"Does stretching prevent injury?","answer":"Hold, breathe, release - 15 to 30 seconds per stretch on warm muscles. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01278","question":"How do I remove a bee stinger?","answer":"The stinger still pumps venom after the bee is gone, so scrape it out sideways as fast as you can. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01279","question":"How do I get rid of a stress headache?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01280","question":"Will eating a lot of fruit make me gain weight?","answer":"The natural sugar in whole fruit arrives packaged with fiber, water, vitamins and antioxidants, so it is absorbed slowly and is not a worry for almost everyone. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01281","question":"Should I clean my phone screen?","answer":"Phones meet your face and hands dozens of times a day, which makes them a highway for whatever germs you picked up last. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01282","question":"How do I care for a burn blister?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Growing redness, swelling or pus means it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01283","question":"Is watching TV before bed bad?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. The stimulation matters as much as the blue light: a tense show or an endless feed keeps your nervous system alert. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01284","question":"How often should I change my toothbrush?","answer":"A splayed toothbrush is a sign it has done its job; past that point it scrubs less and scratches more. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01285","question":"What is the difference between a reliever and preventer inhaler?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01286","question":"Is carrying heavy shopping bags exercise?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01287","question":"Is cheese unhealthy?","answer":"Dairy is an easy calcium-and-protein package, but you can get the same nutrients from fortified plant drinks, leafy greens, tofu and fish with soft bones. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01288","question":"What cough signs mean urgent care?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01289","question":"How does the flu vaccine work?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01290","question":"I am out of shape - where do I begin?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01291","question":"Why did I go grey and wobbly standing in line?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. Getting horizontal - or at least head-down seated - before you hit the floor prevents most faint-related injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01292","question":"I feel dizzy when I stand up - could it be dehydration?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01293","question":"Is there a medicine that treats the flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For most healthy people the small benefit rarely outweighs side effects; for high-risk groups it can matter a lot. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01294","question":"How do I stop my skin from being dry after showering?","answer":"The post-shower itch is evaporating water pulling more moisture from skin whose oils the hot water already stripped. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01295","question":"Why doesn't hand sanitizer work on norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01296","question":"Should pregnant women eat fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01297","question":"When is a headache an emergency?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01298","question":"How do I stop my skin from being dry after showering?","answer":"Very hot water dissolves the skin's protective oil layer, leaving it tight, dry and itchy. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01299","question":"What makes asthma worse in winter?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01300","question":"Is brushing once a day enough?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Not rinsing sounds odd, but the fluoride residue is the active ingredient; rinsing washes away the protection you just applied.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01301","question":"How do I treat a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01302","question":"My child poked her eye - when do we see a doctor?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Anything actually embedded in the eyeball stays there until a professional removes it. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01303","question":"How long should a warm-up take?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01304","question":"How many back blows do you give a choking person?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01305","question":"Why do my eyes and nose itch when I am near cats?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01306","question":"How much salmon counts as a portion?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01307","question":"How do you help a person who has fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01308","question":"What is in OTC sleep medicine?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. After a few nights, antihistamine-based aids stop helping sleep but keep causing heavy-headed mornings. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01309","question":"Why do I keep catching colds?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01310","question":"Can I take an antihistamine for a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01311","question":"How much exercise do children need?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01312","question":"How long should I wash my hands?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01313","question":"Is strength training really that important?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01314","question":"Should I see a doctor for a sore throat?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01315","question":"Is my sore throat bacterial or viral?","answer":"High fever plus pus spots plus tender glands plus no cough is the strep pattern. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01316","question":"What is the difference between a reliever and preventer inhaler?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01317","question":"Is it bad to skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01318","question":"Do antibiotics interfere with the pill?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01319","question":"Why do I get heartburn when I lie down?","answer":"Acid escaping upward onto the unprotected esophageal lining is the entire mechanism of heartburn. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01320","question":"Why is it easier to sleep in a cool room?","answer":"Falling asleep is tied to a small drop in core body temperature, which a cool room makes easier. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01321","question":"Why do I get itchy rashes in winter?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01322","question":"I always quit exercising after a few weeks - what am I doing wrong?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01323","question":"How can I lose weight without a fad diet?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01324","question":"Is athlete's foot contagious?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. It spreads through damp floors and shared towels, so dry feet well, change socks daily, and don't share footwear.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01325","question":"Is a headache a sign I need water?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01326","question":"Do I need to shower daily?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. Daily showers are fine too, but very hot water and harsh scrubbing strip the skin's oils and can trigger dryness and itch. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01327","question":"Should pregnant women eat fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01328","question":"Should I eat with diarrhea and vomiting?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01329","question":"What are the risks of sitting too long?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01330","question":"Why do we cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01331","question":"Can mouthwash replace brushing?","answer":"Think of mouthwash as a supplement to the brush, never a substitute for it. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01332","question":"What are the signs of anaphylaxis?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01333","question":"How does an EpiPen work?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01334","question":"What is the best way to build an exercise habit?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01335","question":"Is exercise good for depression?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01336","question":"Should I raise my bed for heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01337","question":"Is it safe to start exercising at my age?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01338","question":"Does heart attack pain always feel severe?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01339","question":"How often should I do strength training?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01340","question":"How can I stop myself from fainting once I feel it coming?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. Getting horizontal - or at least head-down seated - before you hit the floor prevents most faint-related injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01341","question":"How do I clear a choking infant?","answer":"Support the baby's head, keep it lower than the body, and give up to 5 back blows between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01342","question":"Should I put oil in my ear?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Nothing goes into an aching ear without advice; drops in a perforated eardrum can cause damage. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01343","question":"How much exercise should I get each week?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01344","question":"Is 20 degrees too warm for sleep?","answer":"Your core temperature needs to drop a little to fall asleep, so a cool room works with your biology rather than against it. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01345","question":"Is beef healthy in moderation?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Simple swaps make that easy: a couple of meat-free days a week, chicken or fish some nights, or smaller portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01346","question":"Can I get enough vitamin D from food alone?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01347","question":"Is snacking bad for your teeth?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. Many afternoon cravings are really thirst, boredom or a low-protein lunch in disguise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01348","question":"Can infections spread through shared razors?","answer":"Razor nicks and gum bleeding make those two the most important items to keep personal. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01349","question":"Should I stretch before or after exercise?","answer":"Hold, breathe, release - 15 to 30 seconds per stretch on warm muscles. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01350","question":"What is ultra-processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01351","question":"Is it bad to share a razor?","answer":"Razors, toothbrushes, towels and clippers can carry blood and skin germs, so sharing them can pass on infections including hepatitis B and C. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01352","question":"When is the best time to nap?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Long naps pull you into deep sleep stages, and waking from those produces that heavy, hungover feeling. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01353","question":"Is coughing good for you?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01354","question":"Can you smell food poisoning bacteria?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. Your nose can detect spoilage, but it cannot detect the bugs that actually poison you, so the use-by date is the one to trust.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01355","question":"How often should I wash my hands during the day?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. These moments are where germs actually travel onto your hands and then to your mouth, eyes and food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01356","question":"How hard should I be breathing during exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01357","question":"Can my newborn sleep with a pillow or blanket?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01358","question":"What should I do when I can't fall asleep?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Lying there frustrated teaches your brain that bed equals stress, which deepens the problem. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01359","question":"Is spicy food bad for you?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Ulcers are almost always caused by a stomach bacterium or by painkillers like ibuprofen, not by spice; hot food can sting if you already have one, though. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01360","question":"What exercise is best for over 70s?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01361","question":"How do I know if a cut is serious?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. Cuts on the face, puncture wounds, and any animal or human bite should be checked - infection and scarring risks are higher there. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01362","question":"How do I stop a blister hurting when I walk?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. Pus, spreading redness or heat around a blister means infection - and foot blisters with diabetes always need review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01363","question":"Should I see a doctor before starting to exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01364","question":"Does dried fruit count towards five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01365","question":"Why is tilting your head back during a nosebleed wrong?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01366","question":"How can I stop myself from fainting once I feel it coming?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01367","question":"Do I need to shower daily?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. If you do shower daily, keep it short and lukewarm, and moisturize afterwards to replace what the water washes away. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01368","question":"Should I stuff a tissue up my nose to stop the bleeding?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Deep-packed tissues tear the clot away when removed - keep it to external pressure only. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01369","question":"Is it OK to sleep 10 hours on Saturday?","answer":"Extra weekend sleep helps somewhat, but you cannot fully bank sleep on Saturday for a week of 5-hour nights. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01370","question":"What does white noise actually do?","answer":"Steady background sound does not lull you to sleep directly - it prevents the sudden noises that snap you back awake. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01371","question":"Can fiber help prevent constipation?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. People who eat more fiber tend to have lower rates of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01372","question":"When should a child first see a dentist?","answer":"Children need adult help with brushing until about age 7 or 8, when their hand control is good enough to do it alone under supervision. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01373","question":"How do I soothe a sore throat?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01374","question":"I never took a CPR class - what should I do if someone collapses and is not breathing?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01375","question":"How many minutes of exercise do adults need?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01376","question":"My child poked her eye - when do we see a doctor?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01377","question":"What causes sudden dizziness?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01378","question":"What do I do during an asthma attack?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. Speech in single words, lips tinged blue, or no response to 10 puffs all mean an emergency call now. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01379","question":"Why wash hands after touching raw chicken?","answer":"Keep raw meat and its juices away from food that is eaten raw: use separate cutting boards and utensils, or wash them thoroughly between uses. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01380","question":"Should I let a wound air out?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. The old advice to let wounds 'breathe' has been overturned: a clean, moist, covered wound heals measurably faster.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01381","question":"How long before bed should I stop drinking caffeine?","answer":"Half the caffeine from your 3 pm coffee is still circulating around 8 pm - right when your body should be winding down. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01382","question":"How do I remove a bee stinger?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. After the stinger is out, soap and water plus a wrapped cold pack are all most stings need. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01383","question":"How do you do CPR?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01384","question":"How do I know if I am working hard enough?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01385","question":"Does alcohol help you sleep better?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. In the second half of the night, as alcohol wears off, sleep becomes shallow and broken - that is the rebound. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01386","question":"How many cups of coffee can I drink a day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01387","question":"My child cannot keep fluids down with a stomach bug - what now?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01388","question":"Can my child sleep after a head bump?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01389","question":"Is five a day enough, or should I aim higher?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01390","question":"When does a cut need medical attention?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01391","question":"Should I make someone throw up after swallowing a poison?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01392","question":"When does a stomach bug need a doctor?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01393","question":"Can sleep apnea be treated?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01394","question":"Should I raise the cut above my heart?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01395","question":"How much water is too much in one day?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01396","question":"How do I know if I stop breathing in my sleep?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01397","question":"How much sugar can I eat a day?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01398","question":"Do sweet potatoes count as one of five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01399","question":"Is canned tuna an oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01400","question":"Is 400 mg of caffeine a hard limit?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01401","question":"What is a good warm-up before running?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01402","question":"I keep getting dizzy every day - should I see a doctor?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01403","question":"Should I avoid all processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01404","question":"What is the 150 minutes a week guideline?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01405","question":"Do marathon runners ever drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01406","question":"How much sugar is in a can of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01407","question":"Why is processed meat worse than fresh meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01408","question":"What is a healthy breakfast?","answer":"A filling breakfast combines fiber with protein: oatmeal with fruit and nuts, eggs on wholegrain toast, or plain yogurt with berries. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01409","question":"Do antacids help heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01410","question":"What do I do if a baby is choking?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Abdominal thrusts are for adults and older children only - for babies they cause real harm. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01411","question":"Are plant milks as good as cow's milk?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Choose drinks fortified with calcium - and ideally vitamin D and B12 - and check the label for added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01412","question":"Should I see a doctor before starting to exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01413","question":"What counts as activity for children?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01414","question":"Why do women need more iron than men?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01415","question":"What are the basic rules for good sleep?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. Lying in bed frustrated trains your brain to associate the bed with wakefulness - getting up briefly breaks that link.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01416","question":"When is the best time to get a flu shot?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01417","question":"How much fiber do I need for regular bowel movements?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01418","question":"When should I apply deodorant?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Neither has been shown to cause cancer or health harm in the decades they have been studied; 'aluminum causes breast cancer' is a myth without evidence. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01419","question":"Do I need antibiotics for my infection?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01420","question":"Should I use hydrogen peroxide on a cut?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. Cover it with a clean dressing; slightly moist, protected wounds heal faster and scar less than ones left to scab in open air.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01421","question":"Can being dehydrated give you a headache?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01422","question":"How much water do adults need daily?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01423","question":"How does salt affect my heart?","answer":"Eating too much salt makes your body hold on to extra water, which pushes blood pressure up. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01424","question":"Why does flu make your whole body ache?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01425","question":"Do fevers always need medicine?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01426","question":"How do I use a nasal rinse safely?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Clean and dry the rinse bottle between uses so it does not grow its own problems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01427","question":"How do I know if my ankle is broken or just sprained?","answer":"Inability to bear weight, visible deformity or numbness all mean it needs an examination, not home care. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01428","question":"Can you build muscle on a vegetarian diet?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Eating a variety across the day covers all the building blocks; perfect combinations at every single meal are not required. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01429","question":"How long after soda can I brush?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acidic food and drink soften enamel for a while, so brushing immediately after juice, soda or fruit can scrub the softened layer away. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01430","question":"How much exercise do I need for mental health benefits?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01431","question":"Why do my gums bleed when I floss?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Bleeding at first usually means the gums are already inflamed; daily cleaning reverses that within a week or two, and the bleeding stops. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01432","question":"What happens if I eat too little fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01433","question":"Does alcohol help you sleep better?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. But as your body processes it, sleep fragments: more awakenings in the second half of the night, less REM, and often a bathroom trip. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01434","question":"What lifestyle changes help acid reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01435","question":"Is white noise safe for babies?","answer":"White noise works by masking sudden sounds - traffic, snoring, household noise - that would otherwise punch through light sleep. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01436","question":"What are the danger signs of dehydration in babies?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01437","question":"Can I get enough vitamin D from food alone?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01438","question":"Which foods are high in iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01439","question":"Why use a preventer inhaler every day?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01440","question":"Does vitamin C help absorb iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01441","question":"Why do I need vitamin D in winter?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01442","question":"Why can I not stick to an exercise routine?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01443","question":"I feel lightheaded - is it serious?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01444","question":"Is alcohol mouthwash bad for you?","answer":"Think of mouthwash as a supplement to the brush, never a substitute for it. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01445","question":"Can my child sleep after a head bump?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01446","question":"How much red meat is safe to eat?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Simple swaps make that easy: a couple of meat-free days a week, chicken or fish some nights, or smaller portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01447","question":"What counts as activity for children?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01448","question":"Why does eating slowly help with weight?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Fullness signals take around 20 minutes to catch up, so fast eaters overshoot easily. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01449","question":"How many minutes of exercise do adults need?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01450","question":"How long will a sting stay swollen?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01451","question":"Are potatoes unhealthy then?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01452","question":"Does dried fruit count towards five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01453","question":"Is a glass of wine before bed a good idea?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. But as your body processes it, sleep fragments: more awakenings in the second half of the night, less REM, and often a bathroom trip. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01454","question":"Why do potatoes not count as a vegetable portion?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01455","question":"How long do I press on a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01456","question":"Are eggs bad for your cholesterol?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01457","question":"Why is ultra-processed food so easy to overeat?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01458","question":"Can I catch something from gym mats?","answer":"Staph bacteria live happily on shared mats and benches; the wipe-and-wash routine is what keeps them away from your skin. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01459","question":"Do I need to cover a cut with a plaster?","answer":"Running water is the best cleanser for everyday cuts; scrubbing with alcohol only adds pain and tissue damage. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01460","question":"How do I rehydrate after vomiting?","answer":"ORS packets rehydrate fastest and restore the salts lost with diarrhea and vomiting. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01461","question":"How do I know I have influenza and not a cold?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01462","question":"My baby fell off the bed onto the carpet - how do I know if he is okay?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01463","question":"I never know when to call the doctor - any rules of thumb?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01464","question":"How long is too long to be constipated?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01465","question":"Can being dehydrated give you a headache?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01466","question":"The blister on my burn popped on its own - now what?","answer":"Leave the blister intact; breaking it opens the door to infection. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01467","question":"What is pink eye?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01468","question":"Which plant milk has the most protein?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Two things to check on any plant milk: calcium fortification and added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01469","question":"What can I do at 2 am instead of staring at the ceiling?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Staying in bed awake and annoyed builds a learned link between bed and frustration, so a brief calm reset elsewhere works better. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01470","question":"Do antibiotics help a cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01471","question":"Why is balance training important for older adults?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01472","question":"How do I take the stinger out of a sting?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. After the stinger is out, soap and water plus a wrapped cold pack are all most stings need. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01473","question":"What is ultra-processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01474","question":"Do I need rest days from exercise?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01475","question":"What happens if I eat too little fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01476","question":"Does exercise help you sleep better?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Evening hard sessions keep some people wired; others sleep fine - your own response is the test. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01477","question":"What is the difference between moderate and vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01478","question":"Why is balance training important for older adults?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01479","question":"What headache symptoms should I never ignore?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01480","question":"What are the symptoms of the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01481","question":"How do I soothe a sore throat?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01482","question":"Is dizziness a sign of a stroke?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01483","question":"Do antacids help heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01484","question":"Can I take an antihistamine for a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01485","question":"What should I eat and drink with a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01486","question":"Why should I swap soda for water?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01487","question":"Does honey count as added sugar?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01488","question":"How do I clean after norovirus?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01489","question":"What helps ear pain fast?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Nothing goes into an aching ear without advice; drops in a perforated eardrum can cause damage. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01490","question":"Can I eat too much protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01491","question":"What are the signs of anaphylaxis?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. The rule is simple: adrenaline first, emergency call always - even after successful adrenaline use. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01492","question":"Why should you not put ice on a burn?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01493","question":"Should I stay home with a cold?","answer":"The awkward truth is that you are most contagious right when the cold feels worst, and somewhat contagious even before it starts. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01494","question":"How do I stop a blister hurting when I walk?","answer":"Resist popping it; the fluid is sterile and the skin over it does the job of a dressing. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01495","question":"Is a blister on a burn a bad sign?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01496","question":"When is insomnia considered chronic?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01497","question":"Do potatoes count towards five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01498","question":"What is histamine?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01499","question":"Why do I panic when I see it's 3 am?","answer":"Checking the clock at night turns a small wake-up into math homework: 'if I sleep now I still get 5 hours' - and that pressure blocks sleep. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01500","question":"What should I do on rest days?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01501","question":"How do I treat a blister on my heel?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. Pus, spreading redness or heat around a blister means infection - and foot blisters with diabetes always need review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01502","question":"My dad was gardening in the heat and feels dizzy and sick - what should I do?","answer":"Sweating plus dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps in a hot setting is the classic heat exhaustion picture. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01503","question":"Do I need to exercise for 10 minutes straight for it to matter?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01504","question":"When do I need a new toothbrush?","answer":"A splayed toothbrush is a sign it has done its job; past that point it scrubs less and scratches more. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01505","question":"How do I know if a rash is something serious?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01506","question":"Why is frozen spinach cheaper but still good for me?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01507","question":"Is sleep apnea dangerous?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01508","question":"My knee hurts after running - is that just DOMS?","answer":"Both sides, dull, fading = DOMS; one spot, sharp, swelling = get it looked at. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01509","question":"Do fevers always need medicine?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01510","question":"I am out of shape - where do I begin?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01511","question":"Can I do strength exercises at home without equipment?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01512","question":"Is 20 degrees too warm for sleep?","answer":"Falling asleep is tied to a small drop in core body temperature, which a cool room makes easier. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01513","question":"Do I really need 8 hours of sleep?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01514","question":"I got stung by a bee on my arm - what now?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01515","question":"Is coughing up blood serious?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01516","question":"Should I use alcohol or antiseptic on a cut?","answer":"Running water is the best cleanser for everyday cuts; scrubbing with alcohol only adds pain and tissue damage. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01517","question":"Is exercise good for depression?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01518","question":"Should someone with heat exhaustion keep drinking water?","answer":"Sweating plus dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps in a hot setting is the classic heat exhaustion picture. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01519","question":"What is an allergic reaction doing in my body?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01520","question":"Is brown rice better than white rice?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01521","question":"Is it normal for a cold to last two weeks?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01522","question":"What happens if I eat too little fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01523","question":"Is pink salt better for me than regular salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01524","question":"Does salt raise blood pressure?","answer":"Eating too much salt makes your body hold on to extra water, which pushes blood pressure up. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01525","question":"How many back blows do you give a choking person?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01526","question":"How do I get vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01527","question":"How long do you rinse an eye after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01528","question":"When is vertigo an emergency?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01529","question":"How much exercise do I need for mental health benefits?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01530","question":"What are head injury warning signs in children?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01531","question":"How should I warm up before exercise?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01532","question":"How long does a cold last?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01533","question":"What helps constipation fast?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01534","question":"How often should I replace my mattress?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. For most people with simple backache, a medium-firm mattress is a reasonable default, but personal comfort trumps the label. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01535","question":"How do I know my inhaler technique is right?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01536","question":"What do I do if a baby is choking?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01537","question":"Is dizziness a sign of a stroke?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01538","question":"How much sleep do adults need?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01539","question":"How many teaspoons of sugar are in a can of cola?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01540","question":"Is it safe to take melatonin every night?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. It is not a classic sleeping pill - it nudges timing rather than forcing sedation - and evidence for ordinary insomnia is mixed. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01541","question":"How do I stop a bee sting from itching?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01542","question":"What happens if I drink too much caffeine?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01543","question":"How do I stop waking up at night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. It becomes a problem when you notice the waking, check the time, and start worrying, which re-activates the brain. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01544","question":"What if I just do not like milk?","answer":"Dairy is an easy calcium-and-protein package, but you can get the same nutrients from fortified plant drinks, leafy greens, tofu and fish with soft bones. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01545","question":"Should I take my child to the doctor for a fever?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01546","question":"Do short bursts of exercise count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01547","question":"Is plain water the only drink that keeps me hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01548","question":"Can poor sleep cause anxiety?","answer":"After a sleepless night, the emotional centers of the brain overreact and the rational brakes work less well. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01549","question":"Should I rub my eye when something is in it?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Anything actually embedded in the eyeball stays there until a professional removes it. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01550","question":"Why won't the doctor give me antibiotics?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01551","question":"I get throbbing headaches with nausea - could they be migraines?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01552","question":"Which alcoholic drinks have the fewest calories?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Calories you drink barely register as food to your appetite, which makes alcohol calories easy to overdo.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01553","question":"What are the warning signs after a head bump?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Feeling fine right away does not rule out a delayed problem, which is why the 48-hour watch matters. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01554","question":"Should I pop a blister from new shoes?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01555","question":"Why does late pizza give me heartburn?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. Leaving about 2 to 3 hours between a large meal and bed is a comfortable rule of thumb.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01556","question":"How long do you pinch your nose for a nosebleed?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01557","question":"How fast does walking need to be to count as exercise?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01558","question":"How long does caffeine stay in your body?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01559","question":"How many cups of fluid should I have each day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01560","question":"What about chemical or electrical burns?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Anything bigger than the person's palm belongs in front of a clinician. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01561","question":"Why does wine make me wake up at 3 am?","answer":"A drink may knock you out faster, but it fragments your sleep later in the night and cuts REM. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01562","question":"Does walking count as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01563","question":"Why is fiber good for you?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. Diets rich in fiber are linked with a lower risk of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01564","question":"Does a 5 minute walk do anything?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01565","question":"How do I know if someone is allergic to a bee sting?","answer":"Anaphylaxis signs are breathing difficulty, swollen lips or tongue, faintness and widespread hives. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01566","question":"How do you treat a jellyfish sting at the beach?","answer":"Seawater rinse, gentle tentacle removal and a hot-water soak are the mainstream steps. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01567","question":"How do I relieve muscle soreness after exercise?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01568","question":"What are the symptoms of the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01569","question":"Why do we cough?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01570","question":"Why is balance training important for older adults?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01571","question":"My child has a fever and purple spots - what do I do?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01572","question":"What does the traffic light label mean?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01573","question":"There is glass in my hand - should I pull it out?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Redness, swelling, warmth or pus in the days after a cut mean it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01574","question":"How do I treat a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01575","question":"Someone swallowed pills - when is it an emergency?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01576","question":"Why is my cough still here after the cold ended?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01577","question":"Is bleeding gums normal?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Gums that bleed with flossing are gums that need more flossing, not less - the inflammation is what causes the blood. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01578","question":"Can I take antihistamines every night for sleep?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. Tolerance builds within days - the sleepiness fades while the grogginess the next morning does not. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01579","question":"How do I do the glass test for a rash?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01580","question":"My toddler drank from a cleaning bottle - what do I do first?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01581","question":"Why do experts recommend olive oil?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fat pushes LDL cholesterol up, so trading butter for olive or rapeseed oil is a heart-friendly deal. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01582","question":"Why should I keep my nails short?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Keep nails trimmed and scrub your fingertips against your palms while washing so the nails get attention too. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01583","question":"What are the signs of dehydration?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01584","question":"How do I clean a graze properly?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. Cover it with a clean dressing; slightly moist, protected wounds heal faster and scar less than ones left to scab in open air.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01585","question":"Does honey help a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01586","question":"Why does afternoon coffee keep me awake?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01587","question":"Why do I feel worse after snoozing?","answer":"Snoozing chops the last stretch of sleep into 9-minute scraps of the lightest, least refreshing kind. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01588","question":"What is more important for weight loss - diet or exercise?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01589","question":"Why do guidelines say warm or cold water?","answer":"The water temperature barely matters for germ removal - comfortable water works as well as scalding hot. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01590","question":"What is the right way to stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01591","question":"Why is drinking called 'drinking your calories'?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Cutting back on alcohol is one of the easiest ways to trim 'invisible' calories.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01592","question":"How do I stay hydrated with a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01593","question":"How often can I eat bacon and sausages?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01594","question":"What does a good breakfast actually do for me?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01595","question":"What if I am alone when someone collapses?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01596","question":"What is the difference between a reliever and preventer inhaler?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01597","question":"What should I do if cleaning fluid gets in my eye?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01598","question":"What lowers the risk of SIDS?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01599","question":"What is an allergic reaction doing in my body?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01600","question":"Should I put cream on a rash?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01601","question":"How do antihistamines work?","answer":"They block the histamine signal rather than treating the underlying sensitivity. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01602","question":"Do crash diets work?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01603","question":"Do short bursts of exercise count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01604","question":"What is the difference between saturated and unsaturated fat?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fat pushes LDL cholesterol up, so trading butter for olive or rapeseed oil is a heart-friendly deal. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01605","question":"What helps a sore throat fast?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01606","question":"How do I know if chest pain is a heart attack?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01607","question":"Why do my hamstrings feel so tight?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01608","question":"Is day-old rice from a takeaway safe?","answer":"Uncooked rice can carry spores of a bacterium that survives cooking; if cooked rice sits warm on the counter, those spores multiply and make toxins. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01609","question":"What counts towards my daily fluid intake?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01610","question":"Should I go to the doctor for a deep cut?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. Cuts on the face, puncture wounds, and any animal or human bite should be checked - infection and scarring risks are higher there. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01611","question":"Why do I need a flu shot every year?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01612","question":"Is it normal for a cold to last two weeks?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01613","question":"What is the best way to bring a fever down?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01614","question":"Does stretching prevent injury?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01615","question":"Should I eat with diarrhea and vomiting?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01616","question":"Why are nuts recommended if they are so high in calories?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01617","question":"Should I squeeze a splinter out?","answer":"Clean tweezers, steady pull along the entry angle, then wash - that is the whole job for most splinters. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01618","question":"How do I flush something out of my eye safely?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Anything actually embedded in the eyeball stays there until a professional removes it. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01619","question":"Is ultra-processed food bad for you?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01620","question":"Do vegans need to worry about protein?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Eating a variety across the day covers all the building blocks; perfect combinations at every single meal are not required. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01621","question":"I got shampoo in my baby's eyes - is that an emergency?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01622","question":"When does a splinter need a doctor?","answer":"Clean tweezers, steady pull along the entry angle, then wash - that is the whole job for most splinters. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01623","question":"How fast does walking need to be to count as exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01624","question":"Why do my eyes and nose itch when I am near cats?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01625","question":"Should I take medicine to bring a fever down?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01626","question":"Is it bad to have a cat if you have asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01627","question":"Is it dangerous to drive when tired?","answer":"Sleep deprivation slows reaction time and attention much like alcohol, without the smell that warns anyone. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01628","question":"Does juice count towards my five a day?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01629","question":"What should I do with leftover antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01630","question":"How many baby carrots make one portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01631","question":"Why do I sleep better after a day outdoors?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Getting outside for even 10-20 minutes in the morning makes you more alert by day and sleepier on schedule at night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01632","question":"What should I do if I get stung by a bee?","answer":"The stinger still pumps venom after the bee is gone, so scrape it out sideways as fast as you can. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01633","question":"How do I count my weekly units?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A pint of average-strength beer (4-5%) holds about 2.3 to 2.8 units, and a 175 ml glass of wine is about 2.3 units. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01634","question":"My baby fell off the bed onto the carpet - how do I know if he is okay?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01635","question":"Should I floss before or after brushing?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Bleeding at first usually means the gums are already inflamed; daily cleaning reverses that within a week or two, and the bleeding stops. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01636","question":"My son ate peanuts and is having trouble breathing - what do I do?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. The rule is simple: adrenaline first, emergency call always - even after successful adrenaline use. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01637","question":"How do I stay motivated to exercise?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01638","question":"Is there treatment for norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01639","question":"I have fever and body aches - is this the flu?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01640","question":"What are microsleeps?","answer":"Being awake for 17-19 hours impairs driving about as much as being over the legal alcohol limit in many countries. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01641","question":"My friend is having an asthma attack and the inhaler is not working - what now?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. Speech in single words, lips tinged blue, or no response to 10 puffs all mean an emergency call now. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01642","question":"Should I use alcohol or antiseptic on a cut?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01643","question":"Do I need sleeping pills for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01644","question":"What does a spacer do?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01645","question":"Should I worry if my child cries a lot after hitting their head?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01646","question":"How long should I supervise my child's brushing?","answer":"Most young children cannot brush their own teeth well - an adult's hand finishing the job nightly is the standard advice. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01647","question":"How long do sore throats last?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01648","question":"How often should you wash your hair?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. If you do shower daily, keep it short and lukewarm, and moisturize afterwards to replace what the water washes away. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01649","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I stand up?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01650","question":"What is a good warm-up before running?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01651","question":"What should I drink during a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01652","question":"How do I start cooking more at home?","answer":"Home cooking puts the salt shaker and the sugar bowl back in your hands, so you decide how much goes in. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01653","question":"What are the risks of sitting too long?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01654","question":"How do I know if my ankle is broken or just sprained?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01655","question":"How many cups of coffee can I drink a day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01656","question":"Why do the elderly wake up so early?","answer":"The need for sleep barely changes with age - what changes is how easily the brain holds deep sleep. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01657","question":"Is canned soup healthy?","answer":"Canned and instant soups can be surprisingly salty - some contain close to half a day's salt limit in a single bowl. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01658","question":"How do I know if I am working hard enough?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01659","question":"Is melatonin a sleeping pill?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. It is not a classic sleeping pill - it nudges timing rather than forcing sedation - and evidence for ordinary insomnia is mixed. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01660","question":"Should I pop a burn blister?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01661","question":"How do I stop a stomach bug spreading at home?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed norovirus for two days after they feel fine - and it takes only a few particles to infect someone. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01662","question":"Are energy drinks worse than coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01663","question":"What should I do after hitting my head?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Watch the person for two days: any repeated vomiting, growing confusion or unusual sleepiness means emergency care. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01664","question":"What is a fever, actually?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01665","question":"Do I really need 10,000 steps a day?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. Roughly 7,000-8,000 steps captures a large share of the health benefit for most adults. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01666","question":"Do white spots on tonsils mean I need antibiotics?","answer":"High fever plus pus spots plus tender glands plus no cough is the strep pattern. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01667","question":"Is it normal for one spot to hurt much more after exercise?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01668","question":"What does a good breakfast actually do for me?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01669","question":"Should I drink sports drinks when sick?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01670","question":"Should I look at the clock when I wake at night?","answer":"Checking the clock at night turns a small wake-up into math homework: 'if I sleep now I still get 5 hours' - and that pressure blocks sleep. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01671","question":"What is the right way to stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01672","question":"How do I start strength training with no equipment?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01673","question":"Which fish are oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01674","question":"How should I warm up before exercise?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01675","question":"Why do teenagers stay up so late?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. Their body clocks naturally shift about two hours later during adolescence, which is why they are not sleepy at 10 pm but exhausted at 7 am. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01676","question":"Should I brush before or after breakfast?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acidic food and drink soften enamel for a while, so brushing immediately after juice, soda or fruit can scrub the softened layer away. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01677","question":"My cough has lasted a month - is that normal?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01678","question":"Can I take an antihistamine for a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01679","question":"Does honey count as added sugar?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01680","question":"How many eggs can I eat a week?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01681","question":"My coworker is choking, turning red and cannot talk - what should I do right now?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01682","question":"How do I know if someone is allergic to a bee sting?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. Lip or tongue swelling after a sting is not a normal local reaction - treat it as an emergency. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01683","question":"Should I bundle up when I have a fever?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01684","question":"What does five a day actually mean?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01685","question":"Why do I need to take antibiotics exactly as prescribed?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01686","question":"What are whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01687","question":"When does workout soreness mean something is torn?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01688","question":"The sting site is still swollen the next day - is that normal?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01689","question":"Should I cool down after exercise?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01690","question":"What is athlete's foot?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. It spreads through damp floors and shared towels, so dry feet well, change socks daily, and don't share footwear.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01691","question":"I never know when to call the doctor - any rules of thumb?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01692","question":"Why do I feel so much better after a walk?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01693","question":"What foods should I avoid with reflux?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01694","question":"How do you help a person who has fainted?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01695","question":"How does sleep deprivation affect the brain?","answer":"After a sleepless night, the emotional centers of the brain overreact and the rational brakes work less well. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01696","question":"Does flu come with a fever?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. The speed of onset is the best clue: colds creep up, flu drops on you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01697","question":"How long should a meal take to eat?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Your fullness lag is about 20 minutes, which is exactly why wolfing food down leads to eating more. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01698","question":"What is a tension headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01699","question":"Why is my phone dirtier than a toilet seat?","answer":"Your phone travels to the bathroom, the kitchen and everywhere your hands go, picking up whatever they touch, and is cleaned far less often than your hands. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01700","question":"Why do older antihistamines make you drowsy?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. Nasal steroid sprays added to antihistamines control stubborn seasonal allergies better than either alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01701","question":"How do I stop tossing and turning?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Staying in bed awake and annoyed builds a learned link between bed and frustration, so a brief calm reset elsewhere works better. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01702","question":"How do I get rid of a stress headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01703","question":"Can I stop antibiotics when I feel better?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01704","question":"What is the healthiest amount of alcohol?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01705","question":"How should a baby sleep safely?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01706","question":"Why is reheated rice risky?","answer":"The danger with rice is not reheating itself - it is the hours it spent warm in between cooking and reheating. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01707","question":"Should I drive myself to the hospital with chest pain?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01708","question":"How long is too long to be constipated?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Bleeding, weight loss or alternating constipation and diarrhea shift this from pharmacy to clinic. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01709","question":"Should I take medicine to bring a fever down?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01710","question":"I touched a hot pan and my fingers hurt - what should I do first?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01711","question":"Cough with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01712","question":"How should I dress a minor wound at home?","answer":"Running water is the best cleanser for everyday cuts; scrubbing with alcohol only adds pain and tissue damage. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01713","question":"How quickly will my taste buds adjust to less salt?","answer":"Eating too much salt makes your body hold on to extra water, which pushes blood pressure up. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01714","question":"What helps a sore throat fast?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01715","question":"What actually helps when you have a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01716","question":"Why does sugary cereal leave me hungry again so fast?","answer":"A filling breakfast combines fiber with protein: oatmeal with fruit and nuts, eggs on wholegrain toast, or plain yogurt with berries. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01717","question":"Why does caffeine matter when you are expecting?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01718","question":"How often can I eat bacon and sausages?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01719","question":"How do I clean a small cut?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. A fresh dressing once a day keeps the wound clean while it closes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01720","question":"Why do kids have bad dreams?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01721","question":"What does swelling behind the ear mean?","answer":"Three days, discharge, or a genuinely unwell child are the standard referral points. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01722","question":"How do I stop waking up at night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. The waking itself is normal sleep architecture; the second wake-up is anxiety switching the lights on upstairs. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01723","question":"Is fluid coming from my ear serious?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Tenderness and swelling behind the ear is the one earache sign that means go now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01724","question":"Why should I get sunlight early in the day?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Getting outside for even 10-20 minutes in the morning makes you more alert by day and sleepier on schedule at night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01725","question":"Why is my cough still here after the cold ended?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01726","question":"Do adults with earache need a doctor?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01727","question":"What is hands-only CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01728","question":"Does exercise help you sleep?","answer":"Regular exercise is one of the most reliable sleep improvers - people fall asleep faster and spend more time in deep sleep. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01729","question":"How does the flu vaccine work?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01730","question":"Does heart attack pain always feel severe?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01731","question":"Do I need rest days from exercise?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01732","question":"Why should I only use my bed for sleeping?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. If you have been awake for what feels like 20 minutes, get up, do something calm in dim light, and return when sleepy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01733","question":"Is almond milk healthy?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Two things to check on any plant milk: calcium fortification and added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01734","question":"Do ear infections need antibiotics?","answer":"Comfort care plus 3 days of patience resolves the majority of earaches. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01735","question":"When am I most contagious with a cold?","answer":"You are contagious from about a day before symptoms start, and most contagious in the first 2-3 days of the cold. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01736","question":"Should I pee on a jellyfish sting?","answer":"Seawater rinse, gentle tentacle removal and a hot-water soak are the mainstream steps. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01737","question":"Are naps good or bad for you?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Naps past about 30 minutes tip you into deep sleep, so you wake groggy - and late-afternoon or evening naps eat into the sleep pressure you need at bedtime. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01738","question":"Is it bad to bounce while stretching?","answer":"Hold, breathe, release - 15 to 30 seconds per stretch on warm muscles. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01739","question":"How long does it take for exercise to become a habit?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01740","question":"Do I really need to warm up?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01741","question":"Why is it bad to check the time at night?","answer":"Checking the clock at night turns a small wake-up into math homework: 'if I sleep now I still get 5 hours' - and that pressure blocks sleep. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01742","question":"Is 150 minutes of exercise really necessary?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01743","question":"My coworker is choking, turning red and cannot talk - what should I do right now?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01744","question":"How do I treat conjunctivitis at home?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01745","question":"What are head injury warning signs in children?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01746","question":"What should I do if I get stung by a bee?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. After the stinger is out, soap and water plus a wrapped cold pack are all most stings need. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01747","question":"Should I put a burn under water right away?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01748","question":"How do I stay hydrated with a stomach bug?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01749","question":"What should I do during a migraine?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01750","question":"How long can a cough last before worrying?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01751","question":"Can I exercise every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01752","question":"Why does my tongue have a white coating?","answer":"The tongue's rough surface traps bacteria and food debris, and that coating is a major source of bad breath. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01753","question":"Is it normal for a baby to wake every 2 hours?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Waking often is normal and healthy for the first months; 'sleeping through the night' arrives gradually, often somewhere after 4-6 months. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01754","question":"Is the flu vaccine worth it?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01755","question":"How do I clean a small cut?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01756","question":"Why do I wake up at 3 am every night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. It becomes a problem when you notice the waking, check the time, and start worrying, which re-activates the brain. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01757","question":"Does cooking from scratch save money?","answer":"Home cooking puts the salt shaker and the sugar bowl back in your hands, so you decide how much goes in. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01758","question":"Is brown rice better than white rice?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01759","question":"Is ultra-processed food bad for you?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01760","question":"Does red wine protect the heart?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01761","question":"My dad has chest pain and is sweating - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01762","question":"What headache symptoms should I never ignore?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01763","question":"What counts as processed meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01764","question":"How many sugar cubes a day is considered OK?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01765","question":"My doctor refused antibiotics for my cold - why?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01766","question":"How do I stop heartburn without medicine?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01767","question":"How long does the flu last?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01768","question":"How much caffeine is safe during pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01769","question":"Cough with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01770","question":"How long should I ice a sprain?","answer":"Rest, wrapped ice, a compression bandage and elevation are the four first-day moves for a sprain. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01771","question":"What are the symptoms of the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01772","question":"How do I get rid of a stress headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01773","question":"I never know when to call the doctor - any rules of thumb?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01774","question":"How do you help a person who has fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01775","question":"Why do people sleepwalk?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Waking a sleepwalker is hard and can cause confusion and distress - steering them calmly back to bed is the better move. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01776","question":"Why do doctors recommend walking so much?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01777","question":"When is fainting an emergency?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01778","question":"Should I sponge someone with a fever with cold water?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01779","question":"Why is my cough still here after the cold ended?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01780","question":"Is it normal for my teen to sleep until noon?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. During puberty the internal clock drifts later, so teens genuinely are not tired at bedtime - biology, not laziness. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01781","question":"Do I have to exercise every day to be healthy?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01782","question":"How do I do the glass test for a rash?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Painful, blistering or fast-spreading rashes are urgent dermatology territory, not home-care territory. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01783","question":"How do I know if I am dehydrated?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01784","question":"Should I drive myself to the hospital with chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01785","question":"Why am I constipated all the time?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01786","question":"What does sleep do for learning?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. A tired brain both encodes less during the day and files less overnight, so fatigue hits memory twice. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01787","question":"Does tongue scraping help bad breath?","answer":"Cleaning the tongue removes the bacterial film that brushing teeth leaves untouched. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01788","question":"What mouthwash should children use?","answer":"Think of mouthwash as a supplement to the brush, never a substitute for it. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01789","question":"What should I do if I feel sleepy on the motorway?","answer":"Being awake for 17-19 hours impairs driving about as much as being over the legal alcohol limit in many countries. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01790","question":"Is breakfast the most important meal of the day?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01791","question":"How is a migraine different from a normal headache?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01792","question":"What does a spacer do?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01793","question":"Can you drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01794","question":"How often should I get up from my desk?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01795","question":"Do I need an expensive mattress to sleep well?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. For most people with simple backache, a medium-firm mattress is a reasonable default, but personal comfort trumps the label. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01796","question":"Is 30 minutes of activity enough for a child?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01797","question":"Should I squeeze a splinter out?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01798","question":"My daughter was stung and her lip is swelling - what should I do?","answer":"Anaphylaxis signs are breathing difficulty, swollen lips or tongue, faintness and widespread hives. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01799","question":"How should I warm up before exercise?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01800","question":"Can cold weather set off asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01801","question":"What is DOMS?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01802","question":"How do I know if chest pain is a heart attack?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01803","question":"What is the fastest way to heal a blister?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01804","question":"How do I treat a blister on my heel?","answer":"Resist popping it; the fluid is sterile and the skin over it does the job of a dressing. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01805","question":"How do I get vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01806","question":"How do I know if I am not drinking enough?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01807","question":"How often should I change my socks?","answer":"Wash feet daily and dry carefully between the toes, where trapped moisture lets fungus grow. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01808","question":"How long should I keep doing CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01809","question":"Why do I always want sugar at 4 pm?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. Many afternoon cravings are really thirst, boredom or a low-protein lunch in disguise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01810","question":"Does calculating how much sleep I have left make insomnia worse?","answer":"Checking the clock at night turns a small wake-up into math homework: 'if I sleep now I still get 5 hours' - and that pressure blocks sleep. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01811","question":"Should I wash hands before eating even at home?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. These moments are where germs actually travel onto your hands and then to your mouth, eyes and food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01812","question":"How should a baby sleep safely?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01813","question":"Why should I only use my bed for sleeping?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. Lying in bed frustrated trains your brain to associate the bed with wakefulness - getting up briefly breaks that link.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01814","question":"Do I have to exercise every day to be healthy?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01815","question":"Does heart attack pain always feel severe?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01816","question":"What helps constipation fast?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01817","question":"Does a 5 minute walk do anything?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01818","question":"Does exercise help with stress and anxiety?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01819","question":"Should I call an ambulance for chest pain?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01820","question":"Why do older antihistamines make you drowsy?","answer":"They block the histamine signal rather than treating the underlying sensitivity. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01821","question":"Is nail biting bad for health?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Fingertips and nail beds are among the dirtiest spots on the hand, which is why the 20-second routine includes them. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01822","question":"Is walking really good exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01823","question":"Are nuts fattening?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01824","question":"Can I stop antibiotics when I feel better?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01825","question":"Is strength training really that important?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01826","question":"How do I stop a bee sting from itching?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01827","question":"How many calories are in alcohol?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Calories you drink barely register as food to your appetite, which makes alcohol calories easy to overdo.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01828","question":"How many minutes of exercise do adults need?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01829","question":"Should I wake up at the same time every day?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. Big weekend lie-ins feel like repayment but create 'social jet lag', leaving Monday mornings feeling like jet lag after a long flight. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01830","question":"What should I do when my child has a nightmare?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01831","question":"Why is wholegrain bread better than white bread?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01832","question":"Does a standing desk count as being active?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01833","question":"Can I catch up on sleep at the weekend?","answer":"Extra weekend sleep helps somewhat, but you cannot fully bank sleep on Saturday for a week of 5-hour nights. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01834","question":"How much sleep is too little for an adult?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01835","question":"How often should I shower?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. Daily showers are fine too, but very hot water and harsh scrubbing strip the skin's oils and can trigger dryness and itch. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01836","question":"Should I splash water on someone who fainted?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01837","question":"How do I wash my hands properly?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01838","question":"How much exercise should I get each week?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01839","question":"Why do my muscles hurt 2 days after working out?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01840","question":"Why does the stomach flu hit so fast?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01841","question":"How many fruits and vegetables should I eat a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01842","question":"When should my cold be getting better?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01843","question":"What is the difference between a dry and chesty cough?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01844","question":"Should I stop using my phone in bed?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. The stimulation matters as much as the blue light: a tense show or an endless feed keeps your nervous system alert. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01845","question":"What are the signs that need an ambulance immediately?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01846","question":"How much fish should I eat a week?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01847","question":"Do antibiotics help a cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01848","question":"Is flossing or interdental brushes better?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Gums that bleed with flossing are gums that need more flossing, not less - the inflammation is what causes the blood. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01849","question":"Do I need a vitamin D supplement?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01850","question":"Do ear infections need antibiotics?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01851","question":"Are trans fats banned?","answer":"Trans fats are the unhealthiest fats there are - worse than saturated fat - which is why most countries have banned artificial ones. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01852","question":"When should an adult with a fever see a doctor?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever with a stiff neck or an unexplained rash can signal meningitis - that is a same-hour call. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01853","question":"Why do I need vitamin D in winter?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01854","question":"Are nuts fattening?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01855","question":"How do I stop a cut from bleeding?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01856","question":"Should I raise my bed for heartburn?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01857","question":"How many teaspoons of sugar are in a can of cola?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01858","question":"How can I prevent nightmares in children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01859","question":"Can a walk in the evening help me sleep?","answer":"Exercise raises sleep pressure and lowers stress hormones, which is why active people generally sleep deeper. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01860","question":"My mom's face is drooping on one side - what should I do?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01861","question":"What is a fever, actually?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01862","question":"Does a higher fever mean a worse illness?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01863","question":"How do I make someone with a fever comfortable?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01864","question":"Do I need an electric toothbrush?","answer":"A manual brush used properly for 2 minutes cleans just as well as many electric ones. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01865","question":"How can I lose weight without a fad diet?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01866","question":"How long is pink eye contagious?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Rubbing is the self-spreading mechanism; washcloths and makeup are the household vectors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01867","question":"How hard should I be breathing during exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01868","question":"Is 6,000 steps a day okay?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. Roughly 7,000-8,000 steps captures a large share of the health benefit for most adults. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01869","question":"Does gulping food down make you gain weight?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Fullness signals take around 20 minutes to catch up, so fast eaters overshoot easily. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01870","question":"Does a 5 minute walk do anything?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01871","question":"How can I get calcium without drinking milk?","answer":"Adults need around 700 milligrams of calcium a day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01872","question":"How often should I get up from my desk?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01873","question":"How do I treat a mild rash at home?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01874","question":"I never took a CPR class - what should I do if someone collapses and is not breathing?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01875","question":"What counts as one portion of fruit or vegetables?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01876","question":"How do I read a food label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01877","question":"Is five a day enough, or should I aim higher?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01878","question":"Should I put a burn under water right away?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01879","question":"What should I do after hitting my head?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01880","question":"Why do dispatchers ask you to put the phone on speaker during CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01881","question":"Do I need a vitamin D supplement?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01882","question":"Is gel sanitizer enough after the toilet?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. Sanitizer is a convenience, not a substitute: soap physically removes what gel only partially deactivates.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01883","question":"My 2-month-old has a fever - what do I do?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01884","question":"My kid got stung by a jellyfish and it really hurts - what helps?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01885","question":"Why is my cough still here after the cold ended?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01886","question":"What does FAST stand for?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01887","question":"Does the sugar limit include fruit and milk?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01888","question":"When does workout soreness mean something is torn?","answer":"Both sides, dull, fading = DOMS; one spot, sharp, swelling = get it looked at. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01889","question":"What counts towards my daily fluid intake?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01890","question":"Why does my ear hurt after a cold?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Nothing goes into an aching ear without advice; drops in a perforated eardrum can cause damage. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01891","question":"Is it bad to eat too fast?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Your fullness lag is about 20 minutes, which is exactly why wolfing food down leads to eating more. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01892","question":"Is sitting all day bad for you even if I exercise?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01893","question":"Should I put cream on a rash?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01894","question":"How sleepy is too sleepy to drive?","answer":"Sleep deprivation slows reaction time and attention much like alcohol, without the smell that warns anyone. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01895","question":"When should I worry about dizziness?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01896","question":"Why is earwax good?","answer":"Earwax is protective - it traps dust, moisturizes the canal and has antibacterial properties - and ears clean themselves as old wax works out naturally. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01897","question":"Why do my muscles hurt 2 days after working out?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01898","question":"Can my newborn sleep with a pillow or blanket?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01899","question":"Should I see a doctor for a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01900","question":"When is bleeding heavy enough to call for help?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01901","question":"Why should I eat five portions of fruit and veg every day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01902","question":"Why did advice about eggs change?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01903","question":"Does sleeping help you lose weight?","answer":"After short sleep, hunger hormones tilt toward eating more, and cravings lean toward sugary, high-calorie food. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01904","question":"Is sea salt healthier than table salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01905","question":"Is 400 mg of caffeine a hard limit?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01906","question":"What is more important for weight loss - diet or exercise?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01907","question":"Does an afternoon coffee affect sleep?","answer":"Half the caffeine from your 3 pm coffee is still circulating around 8 pm - right when your body should be winding down. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01908","question":"Are nuts fattening?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01909","question":"Does juice count towards my five a day?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01910","question":"Can being dehydrated give you a headache?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01911","question":"I am out of shape - where do I begin?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01912","question":"Why do I need to take antibiotics exactly as prescribed?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01913","question":"Why does deodorant stop working?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Claims linking antiperspirant ingredients to cancer have been tested and do not hold up; the safety record is long and clean. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01914","question":"Why does my newborn wake up so much?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Night waking in young babies is developmental, not a problem to fix - feeding needs and immature body clocks drive it. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01915","question":"Why does my asthma get worse at night?","answer":"Asthma is inflamed airways that narrow further with triggers, producing wheeze and breathlessness. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01916","question":"I sliced my finger on a knife and it will not stop bleeding - what do I do?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01917","question":"Is dizziness a sign of a stroke?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01918","question":"Do I need protein shakes or supplements?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01919","question":"My cough has lasted a month - is that normal?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01920","question":"What counts as processed meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01921","question":"Where do abdominal thrusts go?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01922","question":"How do I teach a child to wash hands properly?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01923","question":"Should I study all night or sleep before an exam?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. A tired brain both encodes less during the day and files less overnight, so fatigue hits memory twice. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01924","question":"Why do my muscles hurt 2 days after working out?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01925","question":"Can my kid go to school with pink eye?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01926","question":"How do I keep my feet healthy?","answer":"Wash feet daily and dry carefully between the toes, where trapped moisture lets fungus grow. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01927","question":"How active should my 8-year-old be?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01928","question":"Why does my body react to pollen?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01929","question":"What do I do if a jellyfish stings me?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01930","question":"My baby is choking on a bit of apple - what should I do first?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Abdominal thrusts are for adults and older children only - for babies they cause real harm. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01931","question":"Is ultra-processed food bad for you?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01932","question":"Why does my child scream in his sleep?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. In a night terror the child is stuck between sleep stages, so waking them usually prolongs the confusion rather than helping. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01933","question":"Should you hit a choking person on the back?","answer":"Coughing is the body's own first aid for choking, so let it work while it is working. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01934","question":"What is the safe way to get a splinter out?","answer":"Clean tweezers, steady pull along the entry angle, then wash - that is the whole job for most splinters. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01935","question":"How do I tell the difference between hunger and thirst?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. Real hunger builds gradually and almost any food sounds good; a sudden urge for one specific treat is usually a craving, not hunger.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01936","question":"What counts as one portion of fruit or vegetables?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01937","question":"Can I eat fruit on a low-sugar diet?","answer":"The natural sugar in whole fruit arrives packaged with fiber, water, vitamins and antioxidants, so it is absorbed slowly and is not a worry for almost everyone. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01938","question":"What does fiber do for your body?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. Diets rich in fiber are linked with a lower risk of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01939","question":"Why does caffeine matter when you are expecting?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01940","question":"Why does exercise make me wheeze?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01941","question":"Should I eat breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01942","question":"Is one soda a day really a problem?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01943","question":"How many hours of sleep should I get a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01944","question":"Do I need antibiotics for pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01945","question":"Should I raise the cut above my heart?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01946","question":"What exercise is best for over 70s?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01947","question":"Should I look at the clock when I wake at night?","answer":"Every glance at the clock triggers mental arithmetic and worry, both of which are wake-up signals to the brain. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01948","question":"Should I wake up at the same time every day?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. A fixed wake time is the strongest lever you have - it sets the pressure for sleep that evening more than anything else. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01949","question":"What is the difference between a dry and chesty cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01950","question":"Should I remove contact lenses after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01951","question":"Does honey help a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01952","question":"Does a standing desk count as being active?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01953","question":"What is the right way to stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01954","question":"Should I take medicine for a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01955","question":"My kid has a nosebleed at bedtime - what should I do?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01956","question":"Why can I not stick to an exercise routine?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01957","question":"Do I need to dry my hands after washing?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01958","question":"What actually causes allergy symptoms?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01959","question":"Should I have alcohol-free days?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01960","question":"Does fiber lower the risk of bowel cancer?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. People who eat more fiber tend to have lower rates of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01961","question":"When should I worry about my child's nightmares?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01962","question":"When should a headache after hitting my head worry me?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01963","question":"How long does conjunctivitis last?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01964","question":"I got the worst headache of my life very suddenly - what should I do?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01965","question":"Is it normal for one spot to hurt much more after exercise?","answer":"Both sides, dull, fading = DOMS; one spot, sharp, swelling = get it looked at. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01966","question":"Why does the stomach flu hit so fast?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01967","question":"Is raw honey better for you?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01968","question":"What is a fever, actually?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01969","question":"Does dried fruit count towards five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01970","question":"Is a firm or soft mattress better?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. For most people with simple backache, a medium-firm mattress is a reasonable default, but personal comfort trumps the label. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01971","question":"What are the signs of a severe allergic reaction to a sting?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01972","question":"Do crash diets work?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01973","question":"How do I stop afternoon snack cravings?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. Many afternoon cravings are really thirst, boredom or a low-protein lunch in disguise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01974","question":"What does the traffic light label mean?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01975","question":"What does white noise actually do?","answer":"White noise works by masking sudden sounds - traffic, snoring, household noise - that would otherwise punch through light sleep. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01976","question":"Is sitting all day bad for you even if I exercise?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01977","question":"How much fiber do I need for regular bowel movements?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01978","question":"Can I drink coffee while pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01979","question":"How much sleep do children need?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Overtired children often look wired rather than worn out, so bedtime battles can be a sign of too little sleep. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01980","question":"How do I know if my infection is bacterial?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01981","question":"How long do I press on a bleeding cut?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01982","question":"Does morning light help sleep?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Getting outside for even 10-20 minutes in the morning makes you more alert by day and sleepier on schedule at night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01983","question":"How can I slow down when I eat?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Your fullness lag is about 20 minutes, which is exactly why wolfing food down leads to eating more. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01984","question":"I felt dizzy and sweaty and nearly blacked out - what happened?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. Getting horizontal - or at least head-down seated - before you hit the floor prevents most faint-related injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01985","question":"What counts as moderate exercise?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01986","question":"Why does my nose get more blocked when I blow hard?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Straight tap water in nasal rinses carries a tiny but real risk of rare amoebic infections, which is why sterile or boiled water is the rule.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01987","question":"Is bed-sharing safe with a newborn?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01988","question":"What is the difference between a normal allergic reaction and anaphylaxis?","answer":"Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, faintness and widespread hives together mean anaphylaxis. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01989","question":"Is napping normal for seniors?","answer":"Older adults still need around 7 to 8 hours; what changes is that sleep becomes lighter, more fragmented, and shifted earlier. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01990","question":"Is walking really good exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01991","question":"What is ultra-processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01992","question":"What are the signs of a severe allergic reaction to a sting?","answer":"Anaphylaxis signs are breathing difficulty, swollen lips or tongue, faintness and widespread hives. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01993","question":"Why do dentists like electric brushes for kids?","answer":"A manual brush used properly for 2 minutes cleans just as well as many electric ones. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01994","question":"How much salt should I eat a day?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01995","question":"Is fragmented morning sleep restful?","answer":"Each snooze starts a new too-short sleep cycle and then interrupts it, which is why you wake up feeling drugged rather than rested. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01996","question":"What is a good cool-down?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01997","question":"Why do I forget things when I am tired?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. Skimping on sleep after study measurably erases some of the benefit of that study. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01998","question":"How long should I supervise my child's brushing?","answer":"Children need adult help with brushing until about age 7 or 8, when their hand control is good enough to do it alone under supervision. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-01999","question":"Why is my grandparent's sleep so light?","answer":"The need for sleep barely changes with age - what changes is how easily the brain holds deep sleep. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02000","question":"When is the best time to get a flu shot?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02001","question":"Is five a day enough, or should I aim higher?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02002","question":"Why is frozen spinach cheaper but still good for me?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02003","question":"Why is the second day after a workout worse than the first?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02004","question":"How do I relieve constipation naturally?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02005","question":"Where should I store raw meat in the fridge?","answer":"Keep raw meat and its juices away from food that is eaten raw: use separate cutting boards and utensils, or wash them thoroughly between uses. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02006","question":"How long can you go without peeing before worrying?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02007","question":"Why do dispatchers ask you to put the phone on speaker during CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02008","question":"How do I know if I am dehydrated?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02009","question":"Why does milk go off early sometimes?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. The dangerous bacteria are the quiet ones: no smell, no taste, no visible change - which is why dates, not sniffing, keep you safe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02010","question":"Why do experts recommend olive oil?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fats - butter, ghee, coconut oil, fatty processed meat and pastry - raise cholesterol, so keep them to a smaller share of your diet. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02011","question":"How long should a warm-up take?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02012","question":"When should an adult with a fever see a doctor?","answer":"For adults: above 39.4C, beyond 3 days, or plus stiff neck, rash or confusion are the thresholds for care. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02013","question":"Is washing hands with water alone enough?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02014","question":"Can teeth grinding damage your teeth?","answer":"Nighttime grinding (bruxism) often comes with stress, and can also travel with sleep apnea, alcohol and some medications. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02015","question":"Does melatonin help with jet lag?","answer":"Your internal clock adjusts only slowly to a new time zone, so the fix is to feed it the right light and meal cues, not to force sleep. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02016","question":"What are head injury warning signs in children?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02017","question":"Is popcorn a whole grain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02018","question":"Does walking count as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02019","question":"My child spilled boiling water on their arm - should I take them in?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02020","question":"What are the signs of sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02021","question":"Is carrying heavy shopping bags exercise?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02022","question":"Why is my grandparent's sleep so light?","answer":"Older adults still need around 7 to 8 hours; what changes is that sleep becomes lighter, more fragmented, and shifted earlier. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02023","question":"Why do coughs last so long after a cold?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02024","question":"Is a fruit smoothie a good replacement for breakfast fruit?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02025","question":"Should I call an ambulance for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02026","question":"Should I wake my child during a night terror?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. Unlike nightmares, the child usually has no memory of it and settles back to sleep; they are harmless and typically fade with age. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02027","question":"Is it bad to drink water with meals?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. It is a harmless habit with modest benefits: helpful, but not a magic weight-loss trick.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02028","question":"Is it normal for a cold to last two weeks?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02029","question":"Is dextrose the same as sugar?","answer":"Sugar hides under many names: glucose, dextrose, fructose, maltose, corn syrup, rice syrup, honey, agave and fruit juice concentrate. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02030","question":"What should I do for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02031","question":"Do I need sleeping pills for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02032","question":"How long do I press on a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02033","question":"Why do I dream more before waking?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. The first half of the night is deep-sleep-heavy for body repair; the second half is REM-heavy for dreams and emotional sorting. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02034","question":"What makes meat 'processed'?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02035","question":"I rolled my ankle playing basketball - what should I do tonight?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02036","question":"What is a tension headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02037","question":"When should my cold be getting better?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02038","question":"My bowel habits changed suddenly and will not change back - should I worry?","answer":"Three weeks, blood, weight loss or severe pain are the standard thresholds for a GP visit. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02039","question":"Is sharing nail clippers safe?","answer":"Razors, toothbrushes, towels and clippers can carry blood and skin germs, so sharing them can pass on infections including hepatitis B and C. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02040","question":"Can I catch up on sleep at the weekend?","answer":"A weekend lie-in feels great and repays a little of the debt, but only part of it - and at the cost of shifting your body clock later. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02041","question":"Why doesn't hand sanitizer work on norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02042","question":"What is the first thing to do for a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02043","question":"How many eggs can I eat a week?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02044","question":"How quickly will my taste buds adjust to less salt?","answer":"Salt makes your body retain water, and that extra fluid raises the pressure inside your blood vessels. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02045","question":"Why wash hands after handling raw meat or pets?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. These moments are where germs actually travel onto your hands and then to your mouth, eyes and food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02046","question":"How do I treat conjunctivitis at home?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02047","question":"Does alcohol help you sleep?","answer":"Alcohol makes you drowsy and can help you fall asleep faster, but it disrupts the second half of the night. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02048","question":"Someone is not breathing - what comes first, calling or compressions?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02049","question":"How much exercise do I need for mental health benefits?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02050","question":"How much fiber do I need for regular bowel movements?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02051","question":"How do I know if I am working hard enough?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02052","question":"Does caffeine count from tea and chocolate in pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02053","question":"How many sugar cubes a day is considered OK?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02054","question":"What cleaner kills norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. Stay off work, school and visits until 48 hours after the last episode of vomiting or diarrhea, because that is how long you keep shedding the virus. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02055","question":"What should I do if I feel like I am going to faint?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02056","question":"Do I need calcium supplements?","answer":"Adults need around 700 milligrams of calcium a day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02057","question":"Why does afternoon coffee keep me awake?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02058","question":"Is a firm or soft mattress better?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. Replace a mattress when it sags, lumps, or you wake stiff and sleep better elsewhere, which is often around 7 to 8 years. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02059","question":"What lifestyle changes help acid reflux?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02060","question":"What causes sudden dizziness?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02061","question":"Can I do all my exercise on the weekend?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02062","question":"What is REM sleep for?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night and handles physical repair and immune housekeeping; REM clusters toward morning and handles emotion and memory processing. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02063","question":"What drinks can I have instead of coffee when pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02064","question":"How do I avoid injury when starting a new sport?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02065","question":"What is athlete's foot?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. Reinfection usually comes from the shoes, socks or bathroom floor, so treat the environment along with the feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02066","question":"How long should I keep doing CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02067","question":"Do fevers always need medicine?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02068","question":"I never took a CPR class - what should I do if someone collapses and is not breathing?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02069","question":"How much exercise should I get each week?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02070","question":"How do you help a person who has fainted?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02071","question":"Is fish a good source of protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Plant proteins arrive with fiber and less saturated fat, so mixing beans, lentils, nuts and whole grains through the day works very well. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02072","question":"Can I drink coffee in the evening?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02073","question":"Should I eat with diarrhea and vomiting?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02074","question":"Are OTC cough syrups worth buying?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. The honest evidence review: honey performs about as well as the syrups and costs far less. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02075","question":"Do I need to lift weights to be healthy?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02076","question":"Do antibiotics help a cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02077","question":"Should I make someone throw up after swallowing a poison?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02078","question":"How do I start strength training with no equipment?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02079","question":"Why is my asthma inhaler blue?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02080","question":"How do I read a food label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02081","question":"What makes asthma worse in winter?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02082","question":"Is a cold caused by bacteria?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02083","question":"Does hand sanitizer work as well as washing?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. After the toilet, after handling food, or whenever hands look dirty, soap and water is the better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02084","question":"How long does the flu last?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02085","question":"Do I need rest days from exercise?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02086","question":"How long should I cool down for?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02087","question":"Does lack of sleep make you gain weight?","answer":"After short sleep, hunger hormones tilt toward eating more, and cravings lean toward sugary, high-calorie food. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02088","question":"How much fiber do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02089","question":"What is segmented sleep?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. The waking itself is normal sleep architecture; the second wake-up is anxiety switching the lights on upstairs. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02090","question":"What foods should I avoid with reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02091","question":"Do vegans need to worry about protein?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Different plants are low in different amino acids, so variety over the day - not one magic food pairing - does the job. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02092","question":"How do I use a nasal rinse safely?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Straight tap water in nasal rinses carries a tiny but real risk of rare amoebic infections, which is why sterile or boiled water is the rule.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02093","question":"How many eggs can I eat a week?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02094","question":"How do I treat heat exhaustion?","answer":"Sweating plus dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps in a hot setting is the classic heat exhaustion picture. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02095","question":"My dad was gardening in the heat and feels dizzy and sick - what should I do?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. Confusion, hot dry skin or collapse means heatstroke - an emergency that needs an immediate call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02096","question":"How high is too high for a fever in adults?","answer":"For adults: above 39.4C, beyond 3 days, or plus stiff neck, rash or confusion are the thresholds for care. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02097","question":"Can I drink coffee while pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02098","question":"Is peanut butter as healthy as whole nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02099","question":"How do you do the Heimlich maneuver?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02100","question":"Does lack of sleep affect your mood?","answer":"After a sleepless night, the emotional centers of the brain overreact and the rational brakes work less well. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02101","question":"Can I eat chili every day?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Ulcers are almost always caused by a stomach bacterium or by painkillers like ibuprofen, not by spice; hot food can sting if you already have one, though. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02102","question":"Can I share a toothbrush with my partner?","answer":"Razors, toothbrushes, towels and clippers can carry blood and skin germs, so sharing them can pass on infections including hepatitis B and C. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02103","question":"Is it OK to sleep 10 hours on Saturday?","answer":"A weekend lie-in feels great and repays a little of the debt, but only part of it - and at the cost of shifting your body clock later. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02104","question":"Do short bursts of exercise count?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02105","question":"How do I relieve muscle soreness after exercise?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02106","question":"Should I clean my tongue?","answer":"Cleaning the tongue removes the bacterial film that brushing teeth leaves untouched. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02107","question":"How do I clear a choking infant?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Abdominal thrusts are for adults and older children only - for babies they cause real harm. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02108","question":"What happens if I do not get enough vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02109","question":"How big is one of my five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02110","question":"Why does exercise make me wheeze?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02111","question":"Is carrying heavy shopping bags exercise?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02112","question":"My doctor refused antibiotics for my cold - why?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02113","question":"Why do my legs feel creepy and crawly at night?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Low iron stores are sometimes involved, so it is worth seeing a doctor - several treatments work well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02114","question":"Do antivirals help with flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. The 48-hour window is the key fact: antivirals lose most of their benefit after it closes. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02115","question":"Why do experts say plain soap is fine?","answer":"Antibacterial soap has never been shown to outperform ordinary soap and water in real-world handwashing. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02116","question":"When is bleeding heavy enough to call for help?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02117","question":"Does vinegar help jellyfish stings?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Whether vinegar helps depends on the species, which is why local advice beats folklore.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02118","question":"How do I stop a bee sting from itching?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02119","question":"Should I rinse after brushing my teeth?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Not rinsing sounds odd, but the fluoride residue is the active ingredient; rinsing washes away the protection you just applied.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02120","question":"Why do we get fevers when we are sick?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02121","question":"Should I sponge someone with a fever with cold water?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02122","question":"How much alcohol is safe to drink?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02123","question":"Why do potatoes not count as a vegetable portion?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02124","question":"Is exercise good for depression?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02125","question":"Do adults with earache need a doctor?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Tenderness and swelling behind the ear is the one earache sign that means go now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02126","question":"Do I need antibacterial soap at home?","answer":"Antibacterial soap has never been shown to outperform ordinary soap and water in real-world handwashing. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02127","question":"What happens if I sleep 5 hours a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02128","question":"How can I add more fiber to my diet?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02129","question":"What happens in anaphylaxis?","answer":"Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, faintness and widespread hives together mean anaphylaxis. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02130","question":"What about chips - do they count as vegetables?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02131","question":"I keep getting dizzy every day - should I see a doctor?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02132","question":"Why is my kid so sedentary and what do I do?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02133","question":"Can a cold knock you out like the flu?","answer":"Gradual sniffles point to a cold; sudden fever and aches point to flu. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02134","question":"How many units is a glass of wine?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A standard pint of lager carries roughly 2.5 units, so the 14-unit weekly limit is about 5-6 pints spread across the week. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02135","question":"My nose is bleeding - should I tilt my head back?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02136","question":"Is gel sanitizer enough after the toilet?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. After the toilet, after handling food, or whenever hands look dirty, soap and water is the better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02137","question":"How do I know if I should see a doctor or wait it out?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02138","question":"Can I stop antibiotics when I feel better?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02139","question":"Can exercise cancel out a sedentary job?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02140","question":"What helps a headache from staring at screens?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02141","question":"Can my phone carry germs?","answer":"Your phone travels to the bathroom, the kitchen and everywhere your hands go, picking up whatever they touch, and is cleaned far less often than your hands. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02142","question":"When should I wash my hands?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. These moments are where germs actually travel onto your hands and then to your mouth, eyes and food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02143","question":"I got shampoo in my baby's eyes - is that an emergency?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02144","question":"Can infections spread through shared razors?","answer":"Razors, toothbrushes, towels and clippers can carry blood and skin germs, so sharing them can pass on infections including hepatitis B and C. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02145","question":"Why can't my teenager fall asleep before midnight?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Later start times give back the hours the late-shifted teen clock steals, and studies show teens use them to sleep more. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02146","question":"Is tap water safe for a neti pot?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Clean and dry the rinse bottle between uses so it does not grow its own problems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02147","question":"Is butter or olive oil better for you?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fat pushes LDL cholesterol up, so trading butter for olive or rapeseed oil is a heart-friendly deal. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02148","question":"How far do sneezes travel?","answer":"The elbow works because you rarely shake hands with it or rub your eyes with it. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02149","question":"What is vertigo exactly?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02150","question":"Why do coughs last so long after a cold?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02151","question":"What are the signs of sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02152","question":"What should I do for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02153","question":"Do I need protein shakes or supplements?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02154","question":"Should I call 911 before starting CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02155","question":"What is oral rehydration solution?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Small, frequent sips are the technique that works when the stomach is irritated. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02156","question":"Why do I snore?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. Snoring becomes a medical question when breathing pauses or daytime sleepiness join it, not because of the noise alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02157","question":"How much calcium do I need a day?","answer":"Aim for about 700 mg daily - roughly three portions of dairy or fortified alternatives spread across the day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02158","question":"Is sitting all day bad for you even if I exercise?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02159","question":"How do I stay motivated to exercise?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02160","question":"How should I cover a sneeze?","answer":"The elbow works because you rarely shake hands with it or rub your eyes with it. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02161","question":"Will extra protein turn into muscle?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02162","question":"Is strength training really that important?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02163","question":"What should you never do during a nosebleed?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. Blood thinners turn a routine nosebleed into one that deserves prompt medical help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02164","question":"How quickly do you need to take Tamiflu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. The 48-hour window is the key fact: antivirals lose most of their benefit after it closes. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02165","question":"Should I remove contact lenses after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02166","question":"Where do abdominal thrusts go?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02167","question":"Why do I need vitamin D in winter?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02168","question":"How do I treat a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02169","question":"Does exercise help with stress and anxiety?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02170","question":"How long should a warm-up take?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02171","question":"Do scabs help healing?","answer":"Every time you pick a scab you reset part of the healing clock. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02172","question":"Should towels be washed hot?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Weekly-hot washing of bedding also kills the dust mites that allergy sufferers react to. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02173","question":"What soap should I buy for handwashing?","answer":"Antibacterial soap has never been shown to outperform ordinary soap and water in real-world handwashing. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02174","question":"Why am I constipated all the time?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02175","question":"What is a tension headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02176","question":"When should I use an adrenaline auto-injector?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02177","question":"What are the signs that need an ambulance immediately?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02178","question":"My 2-month-old has a fever - what do I do?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. A rash that does not blanch under pressure plus fever needs emergency assessment. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02179","question":"Why am I so irritable when tired?","answer":"Sleep loss amplifies emotional reactions: the brain's alarm centers run hotter and the calming, rational control from the prefrontal cortex weakens. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02180","question":"What is DOMS?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02181","question":"Are frozen vegetables as healthy as fresh?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02182","question":"How long does caffeine stay in your system before bed?","answer":"Caffeine lingers: about half of it is still in your body roughly 5 hours after you drink it. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02183","question":"How long does a stomach bug last?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02184","question":"Do wounds need ointment to heal?","answer":"Petroleum jelly alone keeps minor wounds moist just as well for most people, without the allergy risk. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02185","question":"How do I know if a rash is something serious?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02186","question":"Why did I go grey and wobbly standing in line?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02187","question":"Should I suppress my cough?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02188","question":"What are the benefits of walking daily?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02189","question":"Someone is not breathing - what comes first, calling or compressions?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02190","question":"Why is eating an orange better than drinking orange juice?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02191","question":"What should I look for on a nutrition label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02192","question":"What is histamine?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02193","question":"What is asthma?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02194","question":"How long should you run a burn under cold water?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02195","question":"How do I make someone with a fever comfortable?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02196","question":"How do I relieve muscle soreness after exercise?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02197","question":"Why does calcium matter as I get older?","answer":"Adults need around 700 milligrams of calcium a day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02198","question":"How long do sore throats last?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02199","question":"What helps ear pain fast?","answer":"Comfort care plus 3 days of patience resolves the majority of earaches. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02200","question":"When should a headache after hitting my head worry me?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02201","question":"Do antihistamines work better if I take them before symptoms start?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02202","question":"When is vertigo an emergency?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02203","question":"Do I have to exercise every day to be healthy?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02204","question":"Should I cool down after exercise?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02205","question":"Why is my asthma inhaler blue?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02206","question":"What is an allergic reaction doing in my body?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02207","question":"Do teenagers need more sleep than adults?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. During puberty the internal clock drifts later, so teens genuinely are not tired at bedtime - biology, not laziness. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02208","question":"How do I know my mattress is worn out?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. Replace a mattress when it sags, lumps, or you wake stiff and sleep better elsewhere, which is often around 7 to 8 years. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02209","question":"How can I add more fiber to my diet?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02210","question":"How do I wash my hands properly?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02211","question":"Do marathon runners ever drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02212","question":"Can a 5 pm coffee ruin my night?","answer":"Caffeine lingers: about half of it is still in your body roughly 5 hours after you drink it. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02213","question":"Is brushing once a day enough?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Once in the morning and once last thing before bed - the bedtime brush matters most, because saliva flow drops while you sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02214","question":"Should I clean my tongue?","answer":"The tongue's rough surface traps bacteria and food debris, and that coating is a major source of bad breath. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02215","question":"My mom's face is drooping on one side - what should I do?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02216","question":"How long can a cough last before worrying?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02217","question":"Why do I feel so much better after a walk?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02218","question":"Is there treatment for norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02219","question":"My blister burst on its own - what now?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. Pus, spreading redness or heat around a blister means infection - and foot blisters with diabetes always need review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02220","question":"Is pink eye serious?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02221","question":"Why does flu make your whole body ache?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02222","question":"How can I add more fiber to my diet?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02223","question":"How do I teach a child to wash hands properly?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02224","question":"How do I take the stinger out of a sting?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02225","question":"Is coughing up blood serious?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02226","question":"Does exercise still help if you start late in life?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02227","question":"Can I reheat rice safely?","answer":"Uncooked rice can carry spores of a bacterium that survives cooking; if cooked rice sits warm on the counter, those spores multiply and make toxins. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02228","question":"How can my teenager get enough sleep before school?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. Their body clocks naturally shift about two hours later during adolescence, which is why they are not sleepy at 10 pm but exhausted at 7 am. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02229","question":"When should I wash my hands?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. Germs reach your hands mainly at these moments, which is why targeting them beats constant frantic washing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02230","question":"Do I have to exercise every day to be healthy?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02231","question":"How long do you rinse an eye after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02232","question":"Is there a link between sleep and obesity?","answer":"After short sleep, hunger hormones tilt toward eating more, and cravings lean toward sugary, high-calorie food. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02233","question":"How long should a meal take to eat?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Fullness signals take around 20 minutes to catch up, so fast eaters overshoot easily. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02234","question":"How many hours should a 5 year old sleep?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Overtired children often look wired rather than worn out, so bedtime battles can be a sign of too little sleep. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02235","question":"My kid has a nosebleed at bedtime - what should I do?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02236","question":"Do marathon runners ever drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02237","question":"How do I know if a head injury is serious?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02238","question":"Can you do the Heimlich maneuver on a baby?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02239","question":"Should I drive myself to the hospital with chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02240","question":"How do I relieve muscle soreness after exercise?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02241","question":"How long should I cool down for?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02242","question":"How quickly do you need to act on a stroke?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02243","question":"Why am I tired all the time if I sleep 6 hours?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02244","question":"Is the sugar in fruit bad for you?","answer":"Whole fruit is one of the healthiest foods there is; its sugar is wrapped in fiber and water, so it does not act like candy. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02245","question":"How much caffeine is safe during pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02246","question":"Who gets sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02247","question":"How much sleep is too little for an adult?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02248","question":"Why does the room spin when I lie down or turn over?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02249","question":"Why is too much salt bad for you?","answer":"Eating too much salt makes your body hold on to extra water, which pushes blood pressure up. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02250","question":"What should I do instead of washing chicken?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. Washing does not remove bacteria anyway - only proper cooking does. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02251","question":"How long will a sting stay swollen?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02252","question":"Should I call an ambulance for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02253","question":"Why do I always want sugar at 4 pm?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. If afternoon cravings hit, check the basics first: a proper lunch with protein and enough water prevents many 'hunger' episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02254","question":"Does hot sauce cause ulcers?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Hot peppers irritate an existing ulcer or bad heartburn, but they do not create ulcers - bacteria and anti-inflammatory painkillers do that. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02255","question":"When should I see a doctor about a cough?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02256","question":"Can I count tea and coffee as fluids?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. The idea that coffee leaves you dehydrated is outdated: for regular drinkers it is a net-hydrating drink. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02257","question":"How much protein do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02258","question":"Is plain water the only drink that keeps me hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02259","question":"Should I see a doctor before starting to exercise?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02260","question":"What if I am alone when someone collapses?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02261","question":"What can I eat for breakfast when I am in a hurry?","answer":"A filling breakfast combines fiber with protein: oatmeal with fruit and nuts, eggs on wholegrain toast, or plain yogurt with berries. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02262","question":"How do I stop a cut from bleeding?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02263","question":"My child cannot keep fluids down with a stomach bug - what now?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02264","question":"How do I know if I am working hard enough?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02265","question":"Do I need to use my preventer inhaler when I feel fine?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02266","question":"Why do I feel hungry right after eating?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. Real hunger builds gradually and almost any food sounds good; a sudden urge for one specific treat is usually a craving, not hunger.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02267","question":"How much melatonin should I take?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. Melatonin is a timing signal, not a knockout drop, so 'more' does not mean 'more asleep'. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02268","question":"Do you need to stretch after a workout?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02269","question":"Do older people need less sleep?","answer":"The need for sleep barely changes with age - what changes is how easily the brain holds deep sleep. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02270","question":"How much salad counts as a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02271","question":"What is the healthiest way to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02272","question":"What actually causes allergy symptoms?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02273","question":"What are the signs of a stroke?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02274","question":"What counts as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02275","question":"Do I really need 8 hours of sleep?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02276","question":"Should I brush before or after breakfast?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acid temporarily softens enamel, and brushing during that window wears it; the fluoride-and-saliva repair needs time. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02277","question":"How do I treat a sprained ankle?","answer":"Rest, wrapped ice, a compression bandage and elevation are the four first-day moves for a sprain. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02278","question":"Do animal bites need medical attention?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. Cuts on the face, puncture wounds, and any animal or human bite should be checked - infection and scarring risks are higher there. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02279","question":"Should children have less sugar than adults?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02280","question":"Should I sponge someone with a fever with cold water?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02281","question":"How big is one of my five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02282","question":"My toddler drank from a cleaning bottle - what do I do first?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02283","question":"Do masks stop you spreading colds?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. Masks work best combined with the basics: distance where possible, tissues, and handwashing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02284","question":"Does eating before bed make you gain weight?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. Leaving about 2 to 3 hours between a large meal and bed is a comfortable rule of thumb.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02285","question":"How quickly do you need to act on a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02286","question":"What exercise is best for over 70s?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02287","question":"Why do I get itchy rashes in winter?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02288","question":"How do I know I have influenza and not a cold?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02289","question":"How do I tell the difference between soreness and injury?","answer":"Both sides, dull, fading = DOMS; one spot, sharp, swelling = get it looked at. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02290","question":"How much exercise do I need for better sleep?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02291","question":"How do I count my weekly units?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A standard pint of lager carries roughly 2.5 units, so the 14-unit weekly limit is about 5-6 pints spread across the week. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02292","question":"Should I exercise through sharp pain?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02293","question":"Can stroke symptoms go away on their own?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02294","question":"Why do I feel weaker if I train hard every day?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02295","question":"My kid has a nosebleed at bedtime - what should I do?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02296","question":"Why is my throat worse in the morning?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02297","question":"What is a good warm-up before running?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02298","question":"How long should brushing take?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Not rinsing sounds odd, but the fluoride residue is the active ingredient; rinsing washes away the protection you just applied.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02299","question":"Do I need to cover a cut with a plaster?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. A fresh dressing once a day keeps the wound clean while it closes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02300","question":"When does workout soreness mean something is torn?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Pain that changes your stride or form is the clearest dividing line between soreness and injury. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02301","question":"What should I do during a migraine?","answer":"One-sided throbbing pain with nausea and light sensitivity is the classic migraine pattern. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02302","question":"Why does my heartburn get worse at night?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02303","question":"What are examples of muscle-strengthening activities?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02304","question":"Why does wine make me wake up at 3 am?","answer":"Alcohol makes you drowsy and can help you fall asleep faster, but it disrupts the second half of the night. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02305","question":"What is fried rice syndrome?","answer":"Uncooked rice can carry spores of a bacterium that survives cooking; if cooked rice sits warm on the counter, those spores multiply and make toxins. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02306","question":"How long will a sting stay swollen?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02307","question":"What should I do on rest days?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02308","question":"Do I need an electric toothbrush?","answer":"The brush is the tool; the 2 minutes of coverage is the treatment. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02309","question":"Does climbing stairs instead of the elevator really help?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02310","question":"How do I treat heat exhaustion?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. Confusion, hot dry skin or collapse means heatstroke - an emergency that needs an immediate call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02311","question":"How long do sore throats last?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02312","question":"What soap should I buy for handwashing?","answer":"For everyday handwashing, plain soap is just as effective as 'antibacterial' varieties. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02313","question":"My toddler drank from a cleaning bottle - what do I do first?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02314","question":"My burn is bigger than my palm - should I get it checked?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02315","question":"What should I do if someone sleepwalks?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Do not try to wake the person; gently guide them back to bed. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02316","question":"Do antacids help heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02317","question":"What are the signs of tonsillitis?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02318","question":"Is 6 hours of sleep enough?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02319","question":"Should I make someone throw up after swallowing a poison?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02320","question":"Why does my body feel better when I warm up first?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02321","question":"Is tap water safe for a neti pot?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Straight tap water in nasal rinses carries a tiny but real risk of rare amoebic infections, which is why sterile or boiled water is the rule.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02322","question":"How much sleep is too little for an adult?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02323","question":"Is cold water as good as hot for handwashing?","answer":"Studies comparing water temperatures find no meaningful difference in germs removed - the soap and scrubbing do the job. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02324","question":"Do adults with earache need a doctor?","answer":"Three days, discharge, or a genuinely unwell child are the standard referral points. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02325","question":"Is asthma a permanent condition?","answer":"Asthma is inflamed airways that narrow further with triggers, producing wheeze and breathlessness. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02326","question":"How often should sheets be washed for dust mites?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Bedding is comfortable to wash every 1-2 weeks - more often if you sweat heavily, share a bed with pets, or have allergies. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02327","question":"Is wine less fattening than beer?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Cutting back on alcohol is one of the easiest ways to trim 'invisible' calories.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02328","question":"What foods are high in calcium?","answer":"Aim for about 700 mg daily - roughly three portions of dairy or fortified alternatives spread across the day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02329","question":"Is a nightcap a good idea?","answer":"Alcohol makes you drowsy and can help you fall asleep faster, but it disrupts the second half of the night. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02330","question":"How many units of alcohol a week is OK?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02331","question":"How often should I floss?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Bleeding at first usually means the gums are already inflamed; daily cleaning reverses that within a week or two, and the bleeding stops. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02332","question":"Why does one eye turn into two with pink eye?","answer":"Very contagious by touch - hygiene beats any drops for containment. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02333","question":"Why does benadryl-style medicine stop making me sleepy?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. After a few nights, antihistamine-based aids stop helping sleep but keep causing heavy-headed mornings. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02334","question":"What happens in anaphylaxis?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. The rule is simple: adrenaline first, emergency call always - even after successful adrenaline use. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02335","question":"What does it feel like right before you faint?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02336","question":"My toddler fell and bumped her head - what should I do?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02337","question":"What does a spacer do?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02338","question":"Which fish are oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02339","question":"What does a migraine feel like?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02340","question":"Is popcorn a whole grain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02341","question":"Can exercise improve my mood?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02342","question":"Is it OK to practice intermittent fasting and skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02343","question":"How fast should I try to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02344","question":"Can low iron make me tired?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02345","question":"Do animal bites need a doctor?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02346","question":"Is white noise safe for babies?","answer":"Steady background sound does not lull you to sleep directly - it prevents the sudden noises that snap you back awake. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02347","question":"Does spicy food damage your stomach?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Ulcers are almost always caused by a stomach bacterium or by painkillers like ibuprofen, not by spice; hot food can sting if you already have one, though. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02348","question":"Do antibiotics help a cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02349","question":"How do I know if I should see a doctor or wait it out?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02350","question":"Should I get antivirals for the flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. The 48-hour window is the key fact: antivirals lose most of their benefit after it closes. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02351","question":"Does caffeine dehydrate you?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. The idea that coffee leaves you dehydrated is outdated: for regular drinkers it is a net-hydrating drink. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02352","question":"How do I flush something out of my eye safely?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02353","question":"How long should a sprain take to get better?","answer":"Inability to bear weight, visible deformity or numbness all mean it needs an examination, not home care. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02354","question":"Does a 5 minute walk do anything?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02355","question":"How do I know if a rash is something serious?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02356","question":"Can I take an antihistamine for a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02357","question":"Does caffeine affect sleep?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02358","question":"Does a 5 minute walk do anything?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02359","question":"How often should I floss?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Gums that bleed with flossing are gums that need more flossing, not less - the inflammation is what causes the blood. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02360","question":"Why does the room spin when I lie down or turn over?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02361","question":"Why do people sleepwalk?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Do not try to wake the person; gently guide them back to bed. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02362","question":"What does FAST stand for?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02363","question":"Can I drink coffee in the evening?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02364","question":"How do I clean a wound?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. The old advice to let wounds 'breathe' has been overturned: a clean, moist, covered wound heals measurably faster.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02365","question":"Should I rub my eye when something is in it?","answer":"Rubbing can turn a speck into a corneal scratch, which is far worse than the speck. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02366","question":"How often should I do strength training?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02367","question":"Does using a smaller plate help you eat less?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Because fullness signals lag roughly 20 minutes behind your plate, slowing down genuinely helps you eat less. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02368","question":"How much protein does the average person need?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02369","question":"Should I drink sports drinks when sick?","answer":"ORS packets rehydrate fastest and restore the salts lost with diarrhea and vomiting. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02370","question":"Is snacking bad for your teeth?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. If afternoon cravings hit, check the basics first: a proper lunch with protein and enough water prevents many 'hunger' episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02371","question":"When is the latest I can drink coffee?","answer":"Half the caffeine from your 3 pm coffee is still circulating around 8 pm - right when your body should be winding down. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02372","question":"Why do I catch colds when I am run down?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. Short sleep dents immune defenses, which is one reason the run-down-and-sick pattern is so common. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02373","question":"Why do kids get cavities even with brushing?","answer":"Children need adult help with brushing until about age 7 or 8, when their hand control is good enough to do it alone under supervision. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02374","question":"How do I soothe a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02375","question":"Why is processed meat worse than fresh meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02376","question":"When should my cold be getting better?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02377","question":"What should I eat and drink with a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02378","question":"Is melatonin a sleeping pill?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. Melatonin is a timing signal, not a knockout drop, so 'more' does not mean 'more asleep'. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02379","question":"How long does the flu last?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02380","question":"Does exercise help you sleep?","answer":"Exercise raises sleep pressure and lowers stress hormones, which is why active people generally sleep deeper. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02381","question":"What is the best way to prevent foot fungus?","answer":"Wash feet daily and dry carefully between the toes, where trapped moisture lets fungus grow. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02382","question":"Why does my tongue have a white coating?","answer":"Cleaning the tongue removes the bacterial film that brushing teeth leaves untouched. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02383","question":"What does a meningitis rash look like?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Painful, blistering or fast-spreading rashes are urgent dermatology territory, not home-care territory. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02384","question":"Why does the dentist say not to rinse after brushing?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Not rinsing sounds odd, but the fluoride residue is the active ingredient; rinsing washes away the protection you just applied.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02385","question":"Does hot water kill more germs when washing hands?","answer":"The water temperature barely matters for germ removal - comfortable water works as well as scalding hot. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02386","question":"What is a unit of alcohol?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A pint of average-strength beer (4-5%) holds about 2.3 to 2.8 units, and a 175 ml glass of wine is about 2.3 units. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02387","question":"Is it bad to bounce while stretching?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02388","question":"Something is stuck in my eyelid area - what do I do?","answer":"Whatever is in the eye, rubbing can scratch the cornea, so blink a few times to let tears wash the object out. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Anything actually embedded in the eyeball stays there until a professional removes it. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02389","question":"Is 30 minutes of activity enough for a child?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02390","question":"What helps constipation fast?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02391","question":"Can my newborn sleep with a pillow or blanket?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02392","question":"What should I look for when buying canned fruit?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02393","question":"How many eggs can I eat a week?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02394","question":"How long should I ice a sprain?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Heat, alcohol, running and massage are the four things to skip in the first couple of days. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02395","question":"How many eggs is a day's worth of protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02396","question":"What is the best mattress for back pain?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. For most people with simple backache, a medium-firm mattress is a reasonable default, but personal comfort trumps the label. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02397","question":"Is canned tuna an oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02398","question":"How do I stop conjunctivitis spreading in my house?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02399","question":"What are hidden names for sugar on labels?","answer":"Manufacturers often spread sugar across several names - glucose, dextrose, maltose, syrups, honey - so no single one tops the ingredients list. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02400","question":"Why do I feel weaker if I train hard every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02401","question":"How are choking steps different for babies?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Abdominal thrusts are for adults and older children only - for babies they cause real harm. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02402","question":"I sliced my finger on a knife and it will not stop bleeding - what do I do?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02403","question":"Why should I get sunlight early in the day?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Morning daylight tells the clock 'morning is here', which times evening melatonin release properly that night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02404","question":"Can I get enough vitamin D from food alone?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02405","question":"How do I remove a splinter?","answer":"Clean tweezers, steady pull along the entry angle, then wash - that is the whole job for most splinters. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02406","question":"Why do gyms ask you to bring a towel?","answer":"Gym equipment is touched by hundreds of hands a day, so wiping machines and mats before and after use is the polite and healthy move. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02407","question":"Why is my eye red and sticky?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02408","question":"When is the best time to get a flu shot?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02409","question":"How do you help a person who has fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02410","question":"My grandma says put butter on burns - is that right?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. After cooling, cover it loosely with cling film or a clean lint-free cloth and leave the skin alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02411","question":"How much toothpaste should a toddler use?","answer":"Most young children cannot brush their own teeth well - an adult's hand finishing the job nightly is the standard advice. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02412","question":"My son cannot put weight on his ankle after a fall - should I take him in?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. A joint that will not take weight may be fractured - get it looked at. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02413","question":"Do fevers always need medicine?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02414","question":"Is popcorn a whole grain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02415","question":"How do I know if I should see a doctor or wait it out?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02416","question":"What is norovirus?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02417","question":"Are sugary drinks bad for me?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02418","question":"Does freezing destroy vitamins in vegetables?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02419","question":"How do I avoid injury when starting a new sport?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02420","question":"How do I clean after norovirus?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02421","question":"Should I put cream on a rash?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02422","question":"Should I see a doctor for a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02423","question":"What should I eat and drink with a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02424","question":"Do I need antibiotics for pink eye?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02425","question":"How much sugar is in a can of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02426","question":"My dad was gardening in the heat and feels dizzy and sick - what should I do?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02427","question":"What is the best treatment for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02428","question":"What should I do when my child has a nightmare?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02429","question":"Why do we cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02430","question":"My burn is bigger than my palm - should I get it checked?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Anything bigger than the person's palm belongs in front of a clinician. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02431","question":"Is it possible to eat too much fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02432","question":"What counts as strength exercise?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02433","question":"Does drinking water before eating help you eat less?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. Water with food is harmless and can add to fullness; the 'bad for digestion' claim is a myth.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02434","question":"What are sleep cycles?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night and handles physical repair and immune housekeeping; REM clusters toward morning and handles emotion and memory processing. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02435","question":"What should I do on rest days?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02436","question":"What is the 150 minutes a week guideline?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02437","question":"Can I catch something from gym mats?","answer":"Gym equipment is touched by hundreds of hands a day, so wiping machines and mats before and after use is the polite and healthy move. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02438","question":"My toddler has a fever but is playing - should I worry?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02439","question":"Why do dispatchers ask you to put the phone on speaker during CPR?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02440","question":"Should I pop a burn blister?","answer":"Leave the blister intact; breaking it opens the door to infection. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02441","question":"How many back blows do you give a choking person?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02442","question":"What strength exercises can I do at home for beginners?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02443","question":"My fever has lasted 4 days - is that okay?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02444","question":"What does swelling behind the ear mean?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Frequent ear infections (several in a season) also deserve a doctor's review of the pattern.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02445","question":"Should I avoid egg yolks?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02446","question":"Should I choose salted or unsalted nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02447","question":"Does blue light really affect sleep?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. People who use phones in bed tend to fall asleep later and sleep less overall. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02448","question":"Why do I feel hungry right after eating?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. True hunger is gradual and open to any food; a sudden longing for one specific snack is usually a craving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02449","question":"How long should a home strength workout be?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02450","question":"Should I get antivirals for the flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For most healthy people the small benefit rarely outweighs side effects; for high-risk groups it can matter a lot. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02451","question":"Is dancing moderate or vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02452","question":"Does walking count as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02453","question":"Do sleep apps help or hurt sleep?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. They are useful for spotting trends - short nights, late bedtimes - but not for diagnosing anything; that needs a medical sleep study. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02454","question":"What is DOMS?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02455","question":"How do I stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02456","question":"Does vitamin C cure a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02457","question":"Does honey help a sore throat?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02458","question":"How do I stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. Still bleeding after two 10-minute rounds, or bleeding heavily, means it needs medical attention. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02459","question":"Is brown rice better than white rice?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02460","question":"Is coconut oil healthy?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fats - butter, ghee, coconut oil, fatty processed meat and pastry - raise cholesterol, so keep them to a smaller share of your diet. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02461","question":"Does melatonin work?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. It is not a classic sleeping pill - it nudges timing rather than forcing sedation - and evidence for ordinary insomnia is mixed. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02462","question":"What are the symptoms of the flu?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02463","question":"What time of day is best for exercise and sleep?","answer":"Exercise raises sleep pressure and lowers stress hormones, which is why active people generally sleep deeper. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02464","question":"Should I wake a sleepwalker?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Waking a sleepwalker is hard and can cause confusion and distress - steering them calmly back to bed is the better move. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02465","question":"What helps a headache from staring at screens?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02466","question":"How much protein do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02467","question":"How do I stop a bee sting from itching?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02468","question":"What does dehydrated pee look like?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02469","question":"Does vinegar help jellyfish stings?","answer":"Seawater rinse, gentle tentacle removal and a hot-water soak are the mainstream steps. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02470","question":"What is pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02471","question":"Can babies have honey for a cough?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. The honest evidence review: honey performs about as well as the syrups and costs far less. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02472","question":"What is the daily salt limit for adults?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02473","question":"Should towels be washed hot?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Bedding is comfortable to wash every 1-2 weeks - more often if you sweat heavily, share a bed with pets, or have allergies. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02474","question":"What does dehydrated pee look like?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02475","question":"How do I know I have influenza and not a cold?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02476","question":"Should I put ice or butter on a burn?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02477","question":"Are eggs bad for your cholesterol?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02478","question":"How do I stop hitting snooze?","answer":"Each snooze starts a new too-short sleep cycle and then interrupts it, which is why you wake up feeling drugged rather than rested. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02479","question":"How many fruits and vegetables should I eat a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02480","question":"Does hot water dry out your skin?","answer":"Very hot water dissolves the skin's protective oil layer, leaving it tight, dry and itchy. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02481","question":"Fever with a stiff neck - is that serious?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02482","question":"What should I do for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02483","question":"How should I store leftover rice?","answer":"Uncooked rice can carry spores of a bacterium that survives cooking; if cooked rice sits warm on the counter, those spores multiply and make toxins. Cool leftover rice quickly - within an hour - store it in the fridge, reheat it until steaming, and eat it within about 24 hours. Rice that sat out overnight should be thrown out even if it smells fine, because the toxins do not smell or taste of anything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02484","question":"Can babies have honey for a cough?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02485","question":"The splinter broke off under my skin - what do I do?","answer":"Clean tweezers, steady pull along the entry angle, then wash - that is the whole job for most splinters. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02486","question":"When am I most contagious with a cold?","answer":"The awkward truth is that you are most contagious right when the cold feels worst, and somewhat contagious even before it starts. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02487","question":"What time of day is best to exercise for sleep?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Evening hard sessions keep some people wired; others sleep fine - your own response is the test. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02488","question":"What is a night guard for?","answer":"Nighttime grinding (bruxism) often comes with stress, and can also travel with sleep apnea, alcohol and some medications. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02489","question":"How do I know if a bread is really wholegrain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02490","question":"What does swelling behind the ear mean?","answer":"Get medical advice if the pain lasts more than about 3 days, there is discharge, bleeding or fluid from the ear, or hearing is notably reduced. A child who is drowsy, vomiting, inconsolable, or under 2 with both ears affected should be seen sooner. Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear with a headache can signal a serious spread of infection - urgent care. Tenderness and swelling behind the ear is the one earache sign that means go now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02491","question":"Can I count tea and coffee as fluids?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. Caffeine has a mild diuretic effect, but habitual drinkers build tolerance, so the overall effect is still hydration. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02492","question":"Do I have to exercise every day to be healthy?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02493","question":"Is fruit juice as healthy as whole fruit?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02494","question":"Should I put antibiotic ointment on a cut?","answer":"For minor cuts and scrapes, keeping the wound moist is the goal - a thin layer of petroleum jelly does that fine. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02495","question":"Does fiber lower the risk of bowel cancer?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. Diets rich in fiber are linked with a lower risk of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02496","question":"Should I wash hands before eating even at home?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. Germs reach your hands mainly at these moments, which is why targeting them beats constant frantic washing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02497","question":"How deep do compressions need to be?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02498","question":"What are examples of muscle-strengthening activities?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02499","question":"My head feels like a tight band - what is that?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02500","question":"My daughter was stung and her lip is swelling - what should I do?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. Lip or tongue swelling after a sting is not a normal local reaction - treat it as an emergency. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02501","question":"Do electric toothbrush heads need replacing?","answer":"A splayed toothbrush is a sign it has done its job; past that point it scrubs less and scratches more. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02502","question":"Can I drink coffee in the evening?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02503","question":"How long should a warm-up take?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02504","question":"Is it too late to start exercising at 60?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02505","question":"My coworker is choking, turning red and cannot talk - what should I do right now?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02506","question":"How do I know if chest pain is a heart attack?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02507","question":"Is sitting all day bad for you even if I exercise?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02508","question":"Should I bundle up when I have a fever?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02509","question":"What does sleep do for learning?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. Skimping on sleep after study measurably erases some of the benefit of that study. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02510","question":"How long before bed should I turn off screens?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. The stimulation matters as much as the blue light: a tense show or an endless feed keeps your nervous system alert. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02511","question":"Is it OK to practice intermittent fasting and skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02512","question":"Is everything processed unhealthy?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02513","question":"Are nightmares normal for children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02514","question":"Is it too late to start exercising at 60?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02515","question":"What are the signs of dehydration?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02516","question":"How many sugar cubes a day is considered OK?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02517","question":"Someone swallowed pills - when is it an emergency?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02518","question":"How do I know if a cut is serious?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. The gaping test is simple: if the edges will not fall together on their own, the wound usually needs closing. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02519","question":"My child cannot keep fluids down with a stomach bug - what now?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02520","question":"Do white spots on tonsils mean I need antibiotics?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. White tonsil patches alone are not conclusive, but combined with fever and no cough they warrant a check. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02521","question":"Do kids grow out of night terrors?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. In a night terror the child is stuck between sleep stages, so waking them usually prolongs the confusion rather than helping. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02522","question":"How long will a sting stay swollen?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02523","question":"Can exercise improve my mood?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02524","question":"Should someone with heat exhaustion keep drinking water?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02525","question":"Can lack of sleep make me sick?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. Short sleep dents immune defenses, which is one reason the run-down-and-sick pattern is so common. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02526","question":"Why is too much salt bad for you?","answer":"Salt makes your body retain water, and that extra fluid raises the pressure inside your blood vessels. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02527","question":"When should I go to the doctor for a minor illness?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02528","question":"Why is my asthma inhaler blue?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02529","question":"How can I pick a healthier soup?","answer":"Canned and instant soups can be surprisingly salty - some contain close to half a day's salt limit in a single bowl. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02530","question":"My nose is bleeding - should I tilt my head back?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02531","question":"Does hot sauce cause ulcers?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Ulcers are almost always caused by a stomach bacterium or by painkillers like ibuprofen, not by spice; hot food can sting if you already have one, though. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02532","question":"When is insomnia considered chronic?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02533","question":"What temperature should my fridge be?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Leftovers should be reheated until steaming hot all the way through, and only reheated once. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02534","question":"Why doesn't hand sanitizer work on norovirus?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02535","question":"Is exercise good for depression?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02536","question":"When does a stomach bug need a doctor?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02537","question":"Why cough into your elbow?","answer":"Cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue - or the crook of your elbow if there isn't one - then bin the tissue and wash your hands. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02538","question":"How should I blow my nose?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Clean and dry the rinse bottle between uses so it does not grow its own problems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02539","question":"What counts as insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02540","question":"Can I eat too much protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02541","question":"Is banana too sugary to eat every day?","answer":"Whole fruit is one of the healthiest foods there is; its sugar is wrapped in fiber and water, so it does not act like candy. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02542","question":"Does heart attack pain always feel severe?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02543","question":"My mom's face is drooping on one side - what should I do?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02544","question":"Is a glass of wine a day good for me?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02545","question":"How many grams of added sugar is too much?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02546","question":"How do I do the glass test for a rash?","answer":"The glass test is simple: spots that do not fade under pressure plus fever means call emergency services now. Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02547","question":"What is the poison control number?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02548","question":"How long should I keep doing CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02549","question":"What is histamine?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02550","question":"How do I stop grinding my teeth in my sleep?","answer":"Stress is the most common driver of grinding, but a blocked airway and apnea are worth ruling out when it is severe. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02551","question":"Is nail biting bad for health?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Keep nails trimmed and scrub your fingertips against your palms while washing so the nails get attention too. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02552","question":"What are the risks of sitting too long?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02553","question":"Should I use a white noise machine?","answer":"Steady background sound does not lull you to sleep directly - it prevents the sudden noises that snap you back awake. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02554","question":"Should I take medicine for a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02555","question":"Can I eat food past its best-before date?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. Your nose can detect spoilage, but it cannot detect the bugs that actually poison you, so the use-by date is the one to trust.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02556","question":"Should I floss?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Bleeding at first usually means the gums are already inflamed; daily cleaning reverses that within a week or two, and the bleeding stops. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02557","question":"Why does spicy food burn my mouth?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Ulcers are almost always caused by a stomach bacterium or by painkillers like ibuprofen, not by spice; hot food can sting if you already have one, though. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02558","question":"Why should I not scratch a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02559","question":"When is the best time to get a flu shot?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02560","question":"Does dried fruit count towards five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02561","question":"Why is the second day after a workout worse than the first?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02562","question":"Why use a preventer inhaler every day?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02563","question":"Should I see a doctor for a sore throat?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02564","question":"How long should I keep doing CPR?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02565","question":"Why do my muscles hurt 2 days after working out?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02566","question":"Can I make my own rehydration drink?","answer":"ORS packets rehydrate fastest and restore the salts lost with diarrhea and vomiting. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02567","question":"If I only drink tea and coffee all day, am I still hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02568","question":"Do later school start times help teens?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Meanwhile school often starts before 8:30 am, meaning chronic sleep loss across the school week. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02569","question":"What time of day is best for exercise and sleep?","answer":"Regular exercise is one of the most reliable sleep improvers - people fall asleep faster and spend more time in deep sleep. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02570","question":"Do antihistamines work better if I take them before symptoms start?","answer":"They block the histamine signal rather than treating the underlying sensitivity. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02571","question":"What triggers asthma attacks?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02572","question":"How much calcium do I need a day?","answer":"Adults need around 700 milligrams of calcium a day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02573","question":"What is histamine?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02574","question":"What is the best temperature for sleeping?","answer":"Falling asleep is tied to a small drop in core body temperature, which a cool room makes easier. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02575","question":"Something chemical splashed in my eye and it burns - first step?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02576","question":"How big is one of my five a day?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02577","question":"How fast should you do chest compressions?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02578","question":"How do I know if chest pain is a heart attack?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02579","question":"What does FAST stand for?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02580","question":"Can walking help me lose weight?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02581","question":"How long is a cold contagious?","answer":"The awkward truth is that you are most contagious right when the cold feels worst, and somewhat contagious even before it starts. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02582","question":"How fast should I try to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02583","question":"How long do sore throats last?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02584","question":"Is dancing moderate or vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking that raises your breathing is the classic example of moderate intensity. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02585","question":"Do I need antibiotics for pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02586","question":"Is triclosan banned in soap?","answer":"For everyday handwashing, plain soap is just as effective as 'antibacterial' varieties. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02587","question":"My cold is on day 8 - is that normal?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02588","question":"Why is school so early for teenagers?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Meanwhile school often starts before 8:30 am, meaning chronic sleep loss across the school week. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02589","question":"What is the best way to bring a fever down?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02590","question":"The whole family is vomiting - what is going around?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02591","question":"I cut my knee on the pavement - what should I do once the bleeding stops?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. A fresh dressing once a day keeps the wound clean while it closes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02592","question":"What are examples of muscle-strengthening activities?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02593","question":"Can I exercise every day?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02594","question":"My son cannot put weight on his ankle after a fall - should I take him in?","answer":"Inability to bear weight, visible deformity or numbness all mean it needs an examination, not home care. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02595","question":"How can I stop myself from fainting once I feel it coming?","answer":"Heat, pallor, tunnel vision and a cold sweat are the classic pre-faint signs. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02596","question":"Do I really need to warm up?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02597","question":"What does acid reflux feel like?","answer":"Acid escaping upward onto the unprotected esophageal lining is the entire mechanism of heartburn. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02598","question":"Can teeth grinding damage your teeth?","answer":"Stress is the most common driver of grinding, but a blocked airway and apnea are worth ruling out when it is severe. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02599","question":"When can I go back to work after a stomach bug?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02600","question":"What headache symptoms should I never ignore?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02601","question":"Do kids need structured exercise or just play?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02602","question":"Is exercise good for depression?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02603","question":"How much exercise do children need?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02604","question":"Is asthma a permanent condition?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Good control is the norm with modern treatment - frequent symptoms mean the plan needs review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02605","question":"Is there a medicine that treats the flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02606","question":"What are the danger signs of dehydration in babies?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. In babies: dry diapers, a sunken soft spot and listlessness are the red flags to act on. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02607","question":"Can walking help me lose weight?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02608","question":"Should I call 911 before starting CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02609","question":"What do I do if a jellyfish stings me?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Whether vinegar helps depends on the species, which is why local advice beats folklore.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02610","question":"What should I drink instead of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02611","question":"Can older adults still build muscle?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02612","question":"What is heartburn?","answer":"Acid escaping upward onto the unprotected esophageal lining is the entire mechanism of heartburn. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02613","question":"Why does the dentist say not to rinse after brushing?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Once in the morning and once last thing before bed - the bedtime brush matters most, because saliva flow drops while you sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02614","question":"How often can I eat bacon and sausages?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02615","question":"When can I go back to work after vomiting and diarrhea?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed norovirus for two days after they feel fine - and it takes only a few particles to infect someone. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02616","question":"What is vertigo exactly?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02617","question":"How can vegetarians get enough protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Plant proteins arrive with fiber and less saturated fat, so mixing beans, lentils, nuts and whole grains through the day works very well. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02618","question":"Why is ORS better than water for a stomach bug?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02619","question":"How can I add more fiber to my diet?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02620","question":"What is the first thing to do for a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02621","question":"Should I raise my bed for heartburn?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02622","question":"Can I brush my teeth straight after drinking orange juice?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acidic food and drink soften enamel for a while, so brushing immediately after juice, soda or fruit can scrub the softened layer away. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02623","question":"Why does my phone keep me awake at night?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. People who use phones in bed tend to fall asleep later and sleep less overall. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02624","question":"How do I treat a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02625","question":"Should sick family members have their own towel?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. The high-traffic touchpoints matter more than doorknobs nobody touches - focus cleaning where hands actually go.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02626","question":"Is teeth grinding linked to stress?","answer":"Stress is the most common driver of grinding, but a blocked airway and apnea are worth ruling out when it is severe. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02627","question":"What counts as processed meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02628","question":"What lifestyle changes help acid reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02629","question":"Can you be allergic to antibiotic ointment?","answer":"For minor cuts and scrapes, keeping the wound moist is the goal - a thin layer of petroleum jelly does that fine. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02630","question":"How do I treat a minor burn?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02631","question":"Why does late gym keep me awake?","answer":"Regular exercise is one of the most reliable sleep improvers - people fall asleep faster and spend more time in deep sleep. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02632","question":"How should I wash a knife after raw meat?","answer":"Keep raw meat and its juices away from food that is eaten raw: use separate cutting boards and utensils, or wash them thoroughly between uses. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02633","question":"Why do I regain weight after dieting?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02634","question":"When is a mask useful for illness?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. The mask's main job when you are sick is source control - protecting everyone else - not protecting you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02635","question":"Can I spread a cold before I have symptoms?","answer":"The awkward truth is that you are most contagious right when the cold feels worst, and somewhat contagious even before it starts. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02636","question":"Why does spicy food burn my mouth?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Hot peppers irritate an existing ulcer or bad heartburn, but they do not create ulcers - bacteria and anti-inflammatory painkillers do that. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02637","question":"Someone collapsed and will not wake up - what do I do?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02638","question":"How long does a stomach bug last?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02639","question":"When does a stomach bug need a doctor?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02640","question":"How do colds spread?","answer":"The awkward truth is that you are most contagious right when the cold feels worst, and somewhat contagious even before it starts. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02641","question":"What should I do if cleaning fluid gets in my eye?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02642","question":"Do I need to exercise for 10 minutes straight for it to matter?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02643","question":"Why do we get fevers when we are sick?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02644","question":"How can I stop snoring?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. Snoring becomes a medical question when breathing pauses or daytime sleepiness join it, not because of the noise alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02645","question":"Why should I not scratch a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02646","question":"What is the poison control number?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02647","question":"When should a headache after hitting my head worry me?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02648","question":"What cough signs mean urgent care?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02649","question":"What are the signs that need an ambulance immediately?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02650","question":"How do I rehydrate after vomiting?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Small, frequent sips are the technique that works when the stomach is irritated. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02651","question":"Is a cold caused by bacteria?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02652","question":"How quickly do you need to act on a stroke?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02653","question":"Do I need sleeping pills for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02654","question":"How long before bed should I stop drinking coffee?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02655","question":"Why do most people need more fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02656","question":"Should I snack between meals?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. If afternoon cravings hit, check the basics first: a proper lunch with protein and enough water prevents many 'hunger' episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02657","question":"I got shampoo in my baby's eyes - is that an emergency?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02658","question":"What does a serious rash look like?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02659","question":"Why does my heartburn get worse at night?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02660","question":"What are whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02661","question":"Can I do strength exercises at home without equipment?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02662","question":"My friend is choking on a piece of steak but can still talk a little - what now?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. A silent cough or total inability to speak means the airway is truly blocked - act now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02663","question":"How long is pink eye contagious?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02664","question":"How do I stop germs spreading at home when someone is sick?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. Most respiratory viruses spread by hands and close contact, so these small habits cut the chain better than disinfecting every surface blindly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02665","question":"Is it bad to let a wound scab?","answer":"Every time you pick a scab you reset part of the healing clock. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02666","question":"How many rest days should I take a week?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02667","question":"Can exercise cancel out a sedentary job?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02668","question":"Why does the stomach flu hit so fast?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02669","question":"What actually helps when you have a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02670","question":"Is 30 minutes of activity enough for a child?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02671","question":"Do I need antibiotics for pink eye?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02672","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I stand up?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02673","question":"How much fruit juice should I drink a day?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02674","question":"How many rest days should I take a week?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02675","question":"What actually helps a night-time cough?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. Warmth, moisture and honey address the irritated airway directly, which is where most cough medicine evidence points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02676","question":"I rolled my ankle playing basketball - what should I do tonight?","answer":"Rest, wrapped ice, a compression bandage and elevation are the four first-day moves for a sprain. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02677","question":"Do masks protect others from my cough?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. Masks work best combined with the basics: distance where possible, tissues, and handwashing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02678","question":"Do babies and children need less salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02679","question":"What should I do if my child swallowed something harmful?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02680","question":"My nose is bleeding - should I tilt my head back?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02681","question":"What alcohol percentage should hand sanitizer be?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. Sanitizer is a convenience, not a substitute: soap physically removes what gel only partially deactivates.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02682","question":"Should I wipe down gym equipment?","answer":"Staph bacteria live happily on shared mats and benches; the wipe-and-wash routine is what keeps them away from your skin. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02683","question":"How long can a cough last before worrying?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02684","question":"Does exercise still help if you start late in life?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02685","question":"Why is soup so salty?","answer":"Canned and instant soups can be surprisingly salty - some contain close to half a day's salt limit in a single bowl. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02686","question":"My son ate peanuts and is having trouble breathing - what do I do?","answer":"Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, faintness and widespread hives together mean anaphylaxis. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02687","question":"Why does milk go off early sometimes?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. Your nose can detect spoilage, but it cannot detect the bugs that actually poison you, so the use-by date is the one to trust.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02688","question":"How long before bed should I stop drinking coffee?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02689","question":"How many grams of added sugar is too much?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02690","question":"What is a good cool-down?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02691","question":"Should I avoid egg yolks?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02692","question":"Do marathon runners ever drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02693","question":"Why do I feel worse after snoozing?","answer":"Each snooze starts a new too-short sleep cycle and then interrupts it, which is why you wake up feeling drugged rather than rested. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02694","question":"My kid got stung by a jellyfish and it really hurts - what helps?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Whether vinegar helps depends on the species, which is why local advice beats folklore.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02695","question":"Why am I tired all the time if I sleep 6 hours?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02696","question":"Why does handwashing take 20 seconds?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02697","question":"What time of day is best to exercise for sleep?","answer":"Consistent daytime activity deepens sleep for most people within a few weeks. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02698","question":"Is decaf a good evening substitute?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02699","question":"How do I relieve constipation naturally?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02700","question":"Is pink salt better for me than regular salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02701","question":"Are eggs healthy?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02702","question":"How do I get rid of a stress headache?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02703","question":"Is it too late to start exercising at 60?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02704","question":"How long does caffeine stay in your body?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02705","question":"How do I know my inhaler technique is right?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02706","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I stand up?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02707","question":"Do I need to drink eight glasses of water a day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02708","question":"Can I do all my exercise on the weekend?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02709","question":"When is vertigo an emergency?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02710","question":"Should I suppress my cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02711","question":"How many hours of sleep should I get a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02712","question":"Is chili good for your metabolism?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Hot peppers irritate an existing ulcer or bad heartburn, but they do not create ulcers - bacteria and anti-inflammatory painkillers do that. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02713","question":"How long does it take for exercise to become a habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02714","question":"Is meal prepping worth it?","answer":"When you cook at home you control the salt, sugar, fat and portion size, which is why people who cook more tend to eat better overall. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02715","question":"I never know when to call the doctor - any rules of thumb?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02716","question":"Can older adults still build muscle?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02717","question":"Is cooking at home healthier?","answer":"When you cook at home you control the salt, sugar, fat and portion size, which is why people who cook more tend to eat better overall. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02718","question":"Why do dispatchers ask you to put the phone on speaker during CPR?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02719","question":"What drinks can I have instead of coffee when pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total. Decaf coffee, plain tea in moderation and water are easy swaps while you are expecting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02720","question":"What time should kids go to bed?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Overtired children often look wired rather than worn out, so bedtime battles can be a sign of too little sleep. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02721","question":"Do short bursts of exercise count?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02722","question":"How do I start exercising after years of being sedentary?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02723","question":"What does five a day actually mean?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02724","question":"What is the poison control number?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02725","question":"Is it normal for a cold to last two weeks?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02726","question":"How do I treat a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02727","question":"What is a good warm-up before running?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02728","question":"Is the flu vaccine worth it?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02729","question":"What is the best way to build an exercise habit?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02730","question":"Headache with a stiff neck and fever - what could it be?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02731","question":"Can I drink coffee while pregnant?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02732","question":"Can exercise improve my mood?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02733","question":"What is CBT for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02734","question":"Do wounds need ointment to heal?","answer":"For minor cuts and scrapes, keeping the wound moist is the goal - a thin layer of petroleum jelly does that fine. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02735","question":"Is it safe to start exercising at my age?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02736","question":"Do I need to dry my hands after washing?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02737","question":"Should I take medicine for a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02738","question":"Is blood in my stool from constipation serious?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Bleeding, weight loss or alternating constipation and diarrhea shift this from pharmacy to clinic. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02739","question":"Is triclosan banned in soap?","answer":"Antibacterial soap has never been shown to outperform ordinary soap and water in real-world handwashing. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02740","question":"Why do I need to take antibiotics exactly as prescribed?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02741","question":"When should I use sanitizer instead of soap?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. Sanitizer is a convenience, not a substitute: soap physically removes what gel only partially deactivates.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02742","question":"What happens if I sleep 5 hours a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02743","question":"Why does my nose get more blocked when I blow hard?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Clean and dry the rinse bottle between uses so it does not grow its own problems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02744","question":"Do saline nasal rinses work?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Straight tap water in nasal rinses carries a tiny but real risk of rare amoebic infections, which is why sterile or boiled water is the rule.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02745","question":"Is honey healthier than sugar?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02746","question":"How do I treat conjunctivitis at home?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02747","question":"Do sweet potatoes count as one of five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips. Chips are usually fried in a lot of oil, so they count even less as a healthy vegetable portion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02748","question":"My cough has lasted a month - is that normal?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02749","question":"Why does exercise make me wheeze?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02750","question":"What symptoms always need medical attention?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02751","question":"How do I help a choking adult at dinner?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02752","question":"How much fish should I eat a week?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02753","question":"Why does flu make your whole body ache?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02754","question":"Why use a preventer inhaler every day?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02755","question":"How do I use an EpiPen?","answer":"Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, faintness and widespread hives together mean anaphylaxis. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02756","question":"Should I pee on a jellyfish sting?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02757","question":"Why does my chest burn after eating?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Cardiac chest pain and reflux pain can feel similar - when unsure, treat it as urgent.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02758","question":"What foods should I avoid with reflux?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02759","question":"How many minutes of exercise do adults need?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02760","question":"How often should I brush my teeth?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Not rinsing sounds odd, but the fluoride residue is the active ingredient; rinsing washes away the protection you just applied.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02761","question":"Why do coughs last so long after a cold?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02762","question":"How do I clean my tongue properly?","answer":"The tongue's rough surface traps bacteria and food debris, and that coating is a major source of bad breath. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02763","question":"How do I clean after norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02764","question":"How long should you monitor someone after a head injury?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Feeling fine right away does not rule out a delayed problem, which is why the 48-hour watch matters. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02765","question":"How do I stop conjunctivitis spreading in my house?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Rubbing is the self-spreading mechanism; washcloths and makeup are the household vectors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02766","question":"Is ham a healthy lunch meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02767","question":"How much caffeine is safe per day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02768","question":"Someone cannot breathe or speak because they are choking - what do I do?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02769","question":"Why do kids have bad dreams?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02770","question":"Are canned vegetables nutritious?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02771","question":"Does juice count towards my five a day?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02772","question":"Are smoothies a good breakfast?","answer":"A filling breakfast combines fiber with protein: oatmeal with fruit and nuts, eggs on wholegrain toast, or plain yogurt with berries. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02773","question":"How many cups of coffee can I drink a day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02774","question":"Should I drink sports drinks when sick?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Small, frequent sips are the technique that works when the stomach is irritated. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02775","question":"What is the difference between a cold and flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. The speed of onset is the best clue: colds creep up, flu drops on you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02776","question":"Why do coughs last so long after a cold?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02777","question":"Should I pop a burn blister?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Growing redness, swelling or pus means it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02778","question":"When should my cold be getting better?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02779","question":"Why do labels list syrup instead of sugar?","answer":"Manufacturers often spread sugar across several names - glucose, dextrose, maltose, syrups, honey - so no single one tops the ingredients list. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02780","question":"When should I see a doctor about constipation?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Bleeding, weight loss or alternating constipation and diarrhea shift this from pharmacy to clinic. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02781","question":"Is it bad to eat late at night?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. Leaving about 2 to 3 hours between a large meal and bed is a comfortable rule of thumb.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02782","question":"Something chemical splashed in my eye and it burns - first step?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02783","question":"What should I do if I feel sleepy on the motorway?","answer":"Sleep deprivation slows reaction time and attention much like alcohol, without the smell that warns anyone. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02784","question":"Should I call 911 before starting CPR?","answer":"Call first, compressions second - the dispatcher becomes your coach the moment you connect. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02785","question":"What are the signs of a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02786","question":"What counts as one portion of fruit or vegetables?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02787","question":"What about chemical or electrical burns?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. White, leathery or charred-looking skin means a deep burn, and chemical or electrical burns always need professional assessment. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02788","question":"How many hours of sleep should I get a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02789","question":"Should I take medicine to bring a fever down?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02790","question":"Is sea salt healthier than table salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02791","question":"How long should brushing take?","answer":"Brush twice a day for about 2 minutes with fluoride toothpaste - for adults, 1350 to 1500 parts per million fluoride. Spit out the foam but do not rinse with water: leaving a thin layer of fluoride on your teeth is what strengthens enamel overnight. Once in the morning and once last thing before bed - the bedtime brush matters most, because saliva flow drops while you sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02792","question":"Why is my cough still here after the cold ended?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02793","question":"Why do toothbrush bristles matter?","answer":"Replace your toothbrush - or electric brush head - about every 3 months. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02794","question":"My grandmother fainted and was confused afterward - is that normal?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02795","question":"How long should a nap be?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Long naps pull you into deep sleep stages, and waking from those produces that heavy, hungover feeling. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02796","question":"My elderly mother will not drink and seems confused - is that dehydration?","answer":"No urine in 8 hours, sunken eyes or new confusion are the stop-waiting signs. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02797","question":"Should I put cream on a rash?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02798","question":"Why should I keep my nails short?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Fingertips and nail beds are among the dirtiest spots on the hand, which is why the 20-second routine includes them. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02799","question":"What does dehydrated pee look like?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02800","question":"Why does caffeine matter when you are expecting?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02801","question":"I bumped my head and feel fine - should I still watch for something?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02802","question":"My bowel habits changed suddenly and will not change back - should I worry?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Bleeding, weight loss or alternating constipation and diarrhea shift this from pharmacy to clinic. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02803","question":"Is my sore throat bacterial or viral?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02804","question":"When does a cut need stitches?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. Cuts on the face, puncture wounds, and any animal or human bite should be checked - infection and scarring risks are higher there. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02805","question":"Should I take my child to the doctor for a fever?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. A rash that does not blanch under pressure plus fever needs emergency assessment. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02806","question":"How do I know if my infection is bacterial?","answer":"Cold, flu and most sore throats are viral; antibiotics do nothing for them. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02807","question":"How fast should you do chest compressions?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02808","question":"Should I put antibiotic ointment on a cut?","answer":"Petroleum jelly alone keeps minor wounds moist just as well for most people, without the allergy risk. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02809","question":"Should I take antihistamines every day during allergy season?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02810","question":"Does a higher fever mean a worse illness?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02811","question":"Do antibiotics interfere with the pill?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02812","question":"Why do I always overeat at dinner?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Because fullness signals lag roughly 20 minutes behind your plate, slowing down genuinely helps you eat less. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02813","question":"Can walking help me lose weight?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02814","question":"How do I know if a burn is serious?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Anything bigger than the person's palm belongs in front of a clinician. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02815","question":"How much salt is in a bowl of canned soup?","answer":"Salt is the catch with store-bought soup: many brands pack a large share of your daily limit into one serving. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02816","question":"Is it bad to work out at night?","answer":"Regular exercise is one of the most reliable sleep improvers - people fall asleep faster and spend more time in deep sleep. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02817","question":"Can walking help me lose weight?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02818","question":"How many rest days should I take a week?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02819","question":"Why does a consistent sleep schedule matter?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. Big weekend lie-ins feel like repayment but create 'social jet lag', leaving Monday mornings feeling like jet lag after a long flight. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02820","question":"Does beer make you gain weight?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Cutting back on alcohol is one of the easiest ways to trim 'invisible' calories.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02821","question":"What counts as insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02822","question":"How do I treat a sprained ankle?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02823","question":"How long should a warm-up take?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02824","question":"Is antibacterial soap better than regular soap?","answer":"For everyday handwashing, plain soap is just as effective as 'antibacterial' varieties. Regulators have found no proof that antibacterial soaps protect people better, and some ingredients in them (like triclosan) have been restricted over safety questions. Technique - 20 seconds, all surfaces, thorough drying - is what cleans hands, not the marketing on the bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02825","question":"The whole family is vomiting - what is going around?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02826","question":"Is breakfast the most important meal of the day?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02827","question":"How do I read a food label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02828","question":"Do eggs cause heart disease?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02829","question":"What triggers asthma attacks?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02830","question":"What is the 48-hour rule for vomiting bugs?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02831","question":"Why is snoring worse after alcohol?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. Snoring becomes a medical question when breathing pauses or daytime sleepiness join it, not because of the noise alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02832","question":"How much red meat is safe to eat?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Cutting back is easier than it sounds: smaller portions, chicken or fish on some days, and a few meat-free meals a week all help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02833","question":"Is decaf a good evening substitute?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02834","question":"What temperature should handwashing water be?","answer":"The water temperature barely matters for germ removal - comfortable water works as well as scalding hot. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02835","question":"What should I do if someone faints in front of me?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02836","question":"Do sweet potatoes count as one of five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02837","question":"Can older adults still build muscle?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02838","question":"Is sleepwalking dangerous?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Waking a sleepwalker is hard and can cause confusion and distress - steering them calmly back to bed is the better move. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02839","question":"What should I look for when buying canned fruit?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02840","question":"Does vitamin C cure a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02841","question":"What causes the common cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02842","question":"Is ham a healthy lunch meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02843","question":"Do antibiotics help a cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02844","question":"Is it OK to practice intermittent fasting and skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02845","question":"What is the best way to bring a fever down?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02846","question":"Why do I need a flu shot every year?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02847","question":"How do I make someone with a fever comfortable?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02848","question":"Is it normal for a baby to wake every 2 hours?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Night waking in young babies is developmental, not a problem to fix - feeding needs and immature body clocks drive it. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02849","question":"Why does my body feel better when I warm up first?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02850","question":"Do I have to exercise every day to be healthy?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02851","question":"My cold is on day 8 - is that normal?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02852","question":"Why can't babies have honey?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02853","question":"What time do teenage brains want to sleep?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Later start times give back the hours the late-shifted teen clock steals, and studies show teens use them to sleep more. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02854","question":"How long do you rinse an eye after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02855","question":"Are plant milks as good as cow's milk?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Two things to check on any plant milk: calcium fortification and added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02856","question":"Can poor sleep cause anxiety?","answer":"Sleep loss amplifies emotional reactions: the brain's alarm centers run hotter and the calming, rational control from the prefrontal cortex weakens. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02857","question":"My coworker is choking, turning red and cannot talk - what should I do right now?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02858","question":"How do I soothe a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02859","question":"Will eating a lot of fruit make me gain weight?","answer":"Whole fruit is one of the healthiest foods there is; its sugar is wrapped in fiber and water, so it does not act like candy. Health guidance limits added and free sugars, not the sugar in whole fruit - fruit counts towards your five a day, not against it. Only people with specific medical instructions, such as some people with diabetes counting carbohydrates, need to track fruit portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02860","question":"How high is too high for a fever in adults?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever with a stiff neck or an unexplained rash can signal meningitis - that is a same-hour call. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02861","question":"When should I see a doctor about a cough?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02862","question":"How do I know if I am not drinking enough?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02863","question":"Do antacids help heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02864","question":"Do you need to stretch after a workout?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02865","question":"I have fever and body aches - is this the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02866","question":"What does five a day actually mean?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02867","question":"Why is snoring worse after alcohol?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. It matters when it is loud and paired with pauses, gasping or choking during sleep and heavy daytime sleepiness - that pattern suggests sleep apnea and deserves a doctor's attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02868","question":"What helps a headache from staring at screens?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02869","question":"When should I go to urgent care instead of the ER?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02870","question":"When does a splinter need a doctor?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Do not dig aggressively; splinters that are deep, near the eye, or causing spreading redness belong with a doctor, and a deep dirty splinter may raise the tetanus question.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02871","question":"Do I need to dry my hands after washing?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02872","question":"Why does my asthma get worse at night?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02873","question":"Are electric toothbrushes better?","answer":"A manual brush used properly for 2 minutes cleans just as well as many electric ones. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02874","question":"How much sleep is too little for an adult?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02875","question":"Dizziness with chest pain - what should I do?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02876","question":"When does a cut need medical attention?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Redness, swelling, warmth or pus in the days after a cut mean it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02877","question":"What is the best way to build an exercise habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02878","question":"Why do I get itchy rashes in winter?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02879","question":"Are nightmares normal for children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02880","question":"My son ate peanuts and is having trouble breathing - what do I do?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02881","question":"When will my baby sleep through the night?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Waking often is normal and healthy for the first months; 'sleeping through the night' arrives gradually, often somewhere after 4-6 months. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02882","question":"What fever temperature is dangerous for kids?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02883","question":"Why cough into your elbow?","answer":"The elbow works because you rarely shake hands with it or rub your eyes with it. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02884","question":"Why is my cough still here after the cold ended?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02885","question":"Can sleep apnea be treated?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02886","question":"How do you treat a jellyfish sting at the beach?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Whether vinegar helps depends on the species, which is why local advice beats folklore.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02887","question":"What counts as moderate exercise?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02888","question":"What does a serious rash look like?","answer":"The glass test is simple: spots that do not fade under pressure plus fever means call emergency services now. Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02889","question":"Why is wholegrain bread better than white bread?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02890","question":"What is the healthiest amount of alcohol?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02891","question":"When should I call 911 instead of driving to the hospital?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02892","question":"How do I make someone with a fever comfortable?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02893","question":"Does sleep help memory?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. Skimping on sleep after study measurably erases some of the benefit of that study. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02894","question":"What exercise is best for over 70s?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02895","question":"Why do I feel dizzy if I stop exercising suddenly?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02896","question":"When should I go to urgent care instead of the ER?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02897","question":"Why does my phone keep me awake at night?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. The stimulation matters as much as the blue light: a tense show or an endless feed keeps your nervous system alert. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02898","question":"Should I put a burn under water right away?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02899","question":"Do screens before bed ruin sleep?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. People who use phones in bed tend to fall asleep later and sleep less overall. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02900","question":"Why is my throat worse in the morning?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02901","question":"Why should you not put ice on a burn?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. After cooling, cover it loosely with cling film or a clean lint-free cloth and leave the skin alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02902","question":"How much sleep do adults need?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02903","question":"Why does eating slowly help with weight?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Your fullness lag is about 20 minutes, which is exactly why wolfing food down leads to eating more. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02904","question":"When is fainting an emergency?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02905","question":"My coworker is choking, turning red and cannot talk - what should I do right now?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02906","question":"Do teenagers need more sleep than adults?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. Their body clocks naturally shift about two hours later during adolescence, which is why they are not sleepy at 10 pm but exhausted at 7 am. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02907","question":"Should I sponge someone with a fever with cold water?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02908","question":"What are the signs of a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02909","question":"What counts as strength exercise?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02910","question":"Something chemical splashed in my eye and it burns - first step?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02911","question":"How much protein do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02912","question":"When can I go back to work after a stomach bug?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02913","question":"How do I avoid trans fats?","answer":"Artificial trans fats are made when liquid oils are hardened into solids, and they raise heart disease risk more than any other kind of fat. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02914","question":"What if I just do not like milk?","answer":"You do not strictly need dairy, but milk, yogurt and cheese are convenient sources of calcium, protein and iodine. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02915","question":"What is the best way to bring a fever down?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02916","question":"Why is my eye red and sticky?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02917","question":"How do I avoid cross-contamination in the kitchen?","answer":"Cross-contamination is the most common way kitchen germs reach people: raw meat touching a salad board, then lunch. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02918","question":"Why does the room spin when I lie down or turn over?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02919","question":"Should I cool down after exercise?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02920","question":"Can thirst feel like hunger?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. True hunger is gradual and open to any food; a sudden longing for one specific snack is usually a craving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02921","question":"Should I stay home with a cold?","answer":"You are contagious from about a day before symptoms start, and most contagious in the first 2-3 days of the cold. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02922","question":"Why wait to brush after eating acidic food?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acid temporarily softens enamel, and brushing during that window wears it; the fluoride-and-saliva repair needs time. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02923","question":"Do I need to dry my hands after washing?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02924","question":"When should I worry about snoring?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. It matters when it is loud and paired with pauses, gasping or choking during sleep and heavy daytime sleepiness - that pattern suggests sleep apnea and deserves a doctor's attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02925","question":"How much sugar is in a can of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02926","question":"Should I set multiple alarms?","answer":"Snoozing chops the last stretch of sleep into 9-minute scraps of the lightest, least refreshing kind. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02927","question":"Can I drink juice instead of eating fruit?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02928","question":"What makes asthma worse in winter?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02929","question":"What is a good warm-up before running?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02930","question":"Why does my heartburn get worse at night?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02931","question":"Is it bad to exercise at night?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02932","question":"Do saline nasal rinses work?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Clean and dry the rinse bottle between uses so it does not grow its own problems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02933","question":"When should I use an adrenaline auto-injector?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. The rule is simple: adrenaline first, emergency call always - even after successful adrenaline use. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02934","question":"When should I see a doctor about migraines?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. Frequent or disabling migraines are very treatable now - no one should simply endure them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02935","question":"How long does a cold last?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02936","question":"Is it safe to start exercising at my age?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02937","question":"Should I clean my phone screen?","answer":"Your phone travels to the bathroom, the kitchen and everywhere your hands go, picking up whatever they touch, and is cleaned far less often than your hands. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02938","question":"Should I cut down on red meat?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Cutting back is easier than it sounds: smaller portions, chicken or fish on some days, and a few meat-free meals a week all help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02939","question":"My knee hurts after running - is that just DOMS?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Never train through sharp or joint pain - adapted activity while a muscle is mildly sore is fine, but pain with a specific address is a stop sign. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02940","question":"Why is everything worse when I am tired?","answer":"After a sleepless night, the emotional centers of the brain overreact and the rational brakes work less well. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02941","question":"Do fevers always need medicine?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02942","question":"My doctor refused antibiotics for my cold - why?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02943","question":"Is 6 hours of sleep enough?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02944","question":"How often should I get up from my desk?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02945","question":"Does heart attack pain always feel severe?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02946","question":"How fast does walking need to be to count as exercise?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02947","question":"Should I worry if my child cries a lot after hitting their head?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02948","question":"Someone is choking but still coughing - what should I do?","answer":"Coughing is the body's own first aid for choking, so let it work while it is working. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02949","question":"Why does my body react to pollen?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02950","question":"Do antacids help heartburn?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02951","question":"What is deep sleep?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. Deep sleep dominates the first half of the night and handles physical repair and immune housekeeping; REM clusters toward morning and handles emotion and memory processing. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02952","question":"Do electric toothbrush heads need replacing?","answer":"Replace your toothbrush - or electric brush head - about every 3 months. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02953","question":"Where does the 10,000 steps number come from?","answer":"Ten thousand is a nice round marketing number; the science says most of the benefit arrives well before it. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02954","question":"Why do we get fevers when we are sick?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02955","question":"How do I get over jet lag faster?","answer":"Your internal clock adjusts only slowly to a new time zone, so the fix is to feed it the right light and meal cues, not to force sleep. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02956","question":"What are the risks of sitting too long?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02957","question":"I went from fine to flattened in one morning - is that flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. The speed of onset is the best clue: colds creep up, flu drops on you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02958","question":"What exercise is best for over 70s?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02959","question":"What signs mean a feverish child needs urgent care?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. Trust the pattern: drinking, crying appropriately and responding normally are reassuring; floppiness, refusal to drink or a shrill cry are not. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02960","question":"What is the 150 minutes a week guideline?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02961","question":"Is it bad to work out at night?","answer":"Exercise raises sleep pressure and lowers stress hormones, which is why active people generally sleep deeper. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02962","question":"What is sleep hygiene?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. Lying in bed frustrated trains your brain to associate the bed with wakefulness - getting up briefly breaks that link.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02963","question":"Does a white or charred burn need medical care?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02964","question":"Which foods count as whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02965","question":"How many units of alcohol a week is OK?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02966","question":"Is carrying heavy shopping bags exercise?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02967","question":"How do I treat athlete's foot?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. It spreads through damp floors and shared towels, so dry feet well, change socks daily, and don't share footwear.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02968","question":"Does gulping food down make you gain weight?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Your fullness lag is about 20 minutes, which is exactly why wolfing food down leads to eating more. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02969","question":"Do antihistamines work better if I take them before symptoms start?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. Nasal steroid sprays added to antihistamines control stubborn seasonal allergies better than either alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02970","question":"What happens if I drink litres of water very quickly?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02971","question":"I touched a hot pan and my fingers hurt - what should I do first?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02972","question":"Can I take an antihistamine for a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02973","question":"Why is oily fish good for you?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02974","question":"When should I apply deodorant?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Claims linking antiperspirant ingredients to cancer have been tested and do not hold up; the safety record is long and clean. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02975","question":"How active should my 8-year-old be?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02976","question":"How do I sleep during the day after working nights?","answer":"Daytime sleep is lighter and shorter by nature, so blackout darkness, cool air and ear protection are doing real work, not just comfort. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02977","question":"What are the signs of heat exhaustion?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. Confusion, hot dry skin or collapse means heatstroke - an emergency that needs an immediate call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02978","question":"How do I treat a sprained ankle?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Heat, alcohol, running and massage are the four things to skip in the first couple of days. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02979","question":"Why is my throat worse in the morning?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps. See a doctor if it is severe, lasts over a week, or comes with high fever, pus on the tonsils or difficulty swallowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02980","question":"Are canned vegetables nutritious?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02981","question":"Where do abdominal thrusts go?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02982","question":"Does water dilute stomach acid if I drink while eating?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. It is a harmless habit with modest benefits: helpful, but not a magic weight-loss trick.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02983","question":"What are the symptoms of the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02984","question":"Can the flu shot give you the flu?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02985","question":"Can kids use adult toothpaste?","answer":"Most young children cannot brush their own teeth well - an adult's hand finishing the job nightly is the standard advice. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02986","question":"Why do toothbrush bristles matter?","answer":"A splayed toothbrush is a sign it has done its job; past that point it scrubs less and scratches more. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02987","question":"When is a headache an emergency?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02988","question":"How do I stop a cut from bleeding?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02989","question":"How long is one sleep cycle?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. The first half of the night is deep-sleep-heavy for body repair; the second half is REM-heavy for dreams and emotional sorting. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02990","question":"Why is eating an orange better than drinking orange juice?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02991","question":"Why does athlete's foot keep coming back?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. Reinfection usually comes from the shoes, socks or bathroom floor, so treat the environment along with the feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02992","question":"Constipation with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"Three weeks, blood, weight loss or severe pain are the standard thresholds for a GP visit. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02993","question":"Why does my heartburn get worse at night?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02994","question":"How do I remove a splinter?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Deep, near an eye, or showing spreading redness are the times a splinter needs professional removal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02995","question":"How much sleep do teenagers need?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. Their body clocks naturally shift about two hours later during adolescence, which is why they are not sleepy at 10 pm but exhausted at 7 am. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02996","question":"How do I know if my sore throat is strep?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02997","question":"What is DOMS?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02998","question":"How do I reset my body clock?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Getting outside for even 10-20 minutes in the morning makes you more alert by day and sleepier on schedule at night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-02999","question":"How do I treat a mild rash at home?","answer":"Cool, moisturized, unscratched skin heals most mild rashes without intervention. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03000","question":"Why do my gums bleed when I floss?","answer":"Yes - clean between your teeth once a day with floss or interdental brushes, because a toothbrush cannot reach those surfaces. Gums that bleed with flossing are gums that need more flossing, not less - the inflammation is what causes the blood. Persistent bleeding, loose teeth or bad breath that will not quit deserve a dental check.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03001","question":"Why does my body feel better when I warm up first?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03002","question":"Can I stop antibiotics when I feel better?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03003","question":"What helps with shift work sleep problems?","answer":"Shift work fights your body clock, so the goal is defending one solid sleep block: blackout curtains, a cool quiet room, phones off, and telling household members you are off-limits. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03004","question":"How do I know my mattress is worn out?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. For most people with simple backache, a medium-firm mattress is a reasonable default, but personal comfort trumps the label. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03005","question":"Is it safe to start exercising at my age?","answer":"Begin at half the effort you feel capable of and add about 10 percent a week. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03006","question":"My toddler fell and bumped her head - what should I do?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03007","question":"Why shouldn't I share towels?","answer":"Razor nicks and gum bleeding make those two the most important items to keep personal. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03008","question":"How do I stop afternoon snack cravings?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. If afternoon cravings hit, check the basics first: a proper lunch with protein and enough water prevents many 'hunger' episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03009","question":"Why is my kid so sedentary and what do I do?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03010","question":"How hard should I be breathing during exercise?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03011","question":"When should I go to the doctor for a minor illness?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03012","question":"Is it okay to take someone else's antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03013","question":"Can sleep apnea be treated?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03014","question":"How many rest days should I take a week?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03015","question":"What is the difference between a viral and bacterial infection?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Every unnecessary antibiotic course trains bacteria to survive the next real one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03016","question":"Can my body fight a bacterial infection without antibiotics?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Every unnecessary antibiotic course trains bacteria to survive the next real one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03017","question":"What is the best temperature for sleeping?","answer":"Your core temperature needs to drop a little to fall asleep, so a cool room works with your biology rather than against it. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03018","question":"Is numbness after a sprain serious?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03019","question":"Can a walk in the evening help me sleep?","answer":"Regular exercise is one of the most reliable sleep improvers - people fall asleep faster and spend more time in deep sleep. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03020","question":"Should I raise my bed for heartburn?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03021","question":"I feel lightheaded - is it serious?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03022","question":"What foods have the most fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03023","question":"What does a good breakfast actually do for me?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03024","question":"Is numbness after a sprain serious?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. A joint that will not take weight may be fractured - get it looked at. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03025","question":"Someone swallowed pills - when is it an emergency?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03026","question":"Does vitamin C help absorb iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03027","question":"Why do dentists like electric brushes for kids?","answer":"The brush is the tool; the 2 minutes of coverage is the treatment. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03028","question":"How can I slow down when I eat?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Fullness signals take around 20 minutes to catch up, so fast eaters overshoot easily. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03029","question":"Someone collapsed and will not wake up - what do I do?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03030","question":"I touched a hot pan and my fingers hurt - what should I do first?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03031","question":"What helps ear pain fast?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03032","question":"How do I stop a cut from bleeding?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03033","question":"I cut my knee on the pavement - what should I do once the bleeding stops?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03034","question":"Can I reheat food more than once?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Pink can linger in safely cooked chicken and white-looking meat can still be unsafe, which is why the 74 °C number matters. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03035","question":"When is insomnia considered chronic?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03036","question":"When is a headache an emergency?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03037","question":"How long should a sprain take to get better?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03038","question":"Does sleeping help you lose weight?","answer":"Short sleep disturbs appetite hormones: ghrelin (hunger) rises and leptin (fullness) falls, so you feel hungrier the next day. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03039","question":"What is an allergic reaction doing in my body?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03040","question":"When should I call an ambulance for asthma?","answer":"Upright posture, one puff every 30-60 seconds up to 10, then reassess - that is the core sequence. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03041","question":"How many units of alcohol a week is OK?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03042","question":"What should I eat and drink with a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03043","question":"What happens if I drink too much caffeine?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03044","question":"Is toothpaste good for burns?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. After cooling, cover it loosely with cling film or a clean lint-free cloth and leave the skin alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03045","question":"Why is soup so salty?","answer":"Salt is the catch with store-bought soup: many brands pack a large share of your daily limit into one serving. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03046","question":"Is blood in my stool from constipation serious?","answer":"Three weeks, blood, weight loss or severe pain are the standard thresholds for a GP visit. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03047","question":"What actually helps a night-time cough?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. The honest evidence review: honey performs about as well as the syrups and costs far less. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03048","question":"How often should I change my socks?","answer":"Fungus thrives in warm, damp, dark places - exactly what sweaty, enclosed toes provide when left damp. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03049","question":"When is bleeding heavy enough to call for help?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03050","question":"Is it dangerous to drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03051","question":"Someone collapsed and will not wake up - what do I do?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03052","question":"Can adults sleepwalk?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Do not try to wake the person; gently guide them back to bed. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03053","question":"What is the healthiest amount of alcohol?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03054","question":"Headache with a stiff neck and fever - what could it be?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03055","question":"Why do teenagers stay up so late?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. During puberty the internal clock drifts later, so teens genuinely are not tired at bedtime - biology, not laziness. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03056","question":"Something chemical splashed in my eye and it burns - first step?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03057","question":"Why do I keep catching colds?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03058","question":"When does a stomach bug need a doctor?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03059","question":"What strength exercises can I do at home for beginners?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03060","question":"My cut is still bleeding after 20 minutes of pressure - what now?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Pad around anything stuck in a wound and leave it for the professionals to remove. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03061","question":"Why do I get headaches at my desk?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03062","question":"Why does my body feel better when I warm up first?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03063","question":"Does sleep help memory?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. A tired brain both encodes less during the day and files less overnight, so fatigue hits memory twice. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03064","question":"Do you need to stretch after a workout?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03065","question":"Should I call an ambulance for chest pain?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03066","question":"What should I do if I cannot fall asleep after 20 minutes?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. Lying in bed frustrated trains your brain to associate the bed with wakefulness - getting up briefly breaks that link.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03067","question":"Do I need to exercise for 10 minutes straight for it to matter?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03068","question":"What is the daily limit for added sugar?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03069","question":"Can cotton buds damage your ears?","answer":"The only thing you should ever clean with a bud is the outer ear; inside the canal, the bud is the problem. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03070","question":"Can stroke symptoms go away on their own?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03071","question":"What do I do if a baby is choking?","answer":"Support the baby's head, keep it lower than the body, and give up to 5 back blows between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03072","question":"My mom's face is drooping on one side - what should I do?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03073","question":"Does hand sanitizer work as well as washing?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. Sanitizer is a convenience, not a substitute: soap physically removes what gel only partially deactivates.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03074","question":"Is it dangerous to swallow blood from a nosebleed?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Deep-packed tissues tear the clot away when removed - keep it to external pressure only. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03075","question":"Can I get enough vitamin D from food alone?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03076","question":"Does the sugar limit include fruit and milk?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03077","question":"Why wash hands after touching raw chicken?","answer":"Cross-contamination is the most common way kitchen germs reach people: raw meat touching a salad board, then lunch. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03078","question":"Is toothpaste good for burns?","answer":"Ice and folk remedies like butter can damage the skin further or seal heat in - plain running water is the only first step that helps. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03079","question":"What lifestyle changes help acid reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03080","question":"How long does the flu last?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03081","question":"What should I do on rest days?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03082","question":"How long do you pinch your nose for a nosebleed?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03083","question":"Can you shower too much?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. If you do shower daily, keep it short and lukewarm, and moisturize afterwards to replace what the water washes away. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03084","question":"How many rest days should I take a week?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03085","question":"Is the snooze button bad for you?","answer":"Snoozing chops the last stretch of sleep into 9-minute scraps of the lightest, least refreshing kind. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03086","question":"What is the 150 minutes a week guideline?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03087","question":"Can low iron cause restless legs?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Low iron stores are sometimes involved, so it is worth seeing a doctor - several treatments work well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03088","question":"Is plant protein complete?","answer":"Vegans and vegetarians can comfortably meet protein needs with beans, lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Different plants are low in different amino acids, so variety over the day - not one magic food pairing - does the job. Vegans do need a reliable source of vitamin B12 - fortified foods or a supplement - because B12 occurs naturally only in animal foods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03089","question":"What is the 48-hour rule for vomiting bugs?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03090","question":"Are canned vegetables nutritious?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03091","question":"Why is balance training important for older adults?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03092","question":"Why do wounds itch when healing?","answer":"A scab is your body's natural bandage, and picking it reopens the wound and invites infection and scars. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03093","question":"Is the flu vaccine worth it?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03094","question":"Do I need antibiotics for my infection?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Every unnecessary antibiotic course trains bacteria to survive the next real one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03095","question":"Where do abdominal thrusts go?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03096","question":"What is a tension headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03097","question":"Can I do strength exercises at home without equipment?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03098","question":"How long does jet lag last?","answer":"Your internal clock adjusts only slowly to a new time zone, so the fix is to feed it the right light and meal cues, not to force sleep. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03099","question":"Why does a double spirit count as 2 units?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A pint of average-strength beer (4-5%) holds about 2.3 to 2.8 units, and a 175 ml glass of wine is about 2.3 units. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03100","question":"How much salt should I eat a day?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03101","question":"When does flu treatment with antivirals work best?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03102","question":"Does morning light help sleep?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Morning daylight tells the clock 'morning is here', which times evening melatonin release properly that night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03103","question":"Does melatonin work?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. Melatonin is a timing signal, not a knockout drop, so 'more' does not mean 'more asleep'. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03104","question":"When should I go to urgent care instead of the ER?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03105","question":"Are sleep trackers accurate?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. A wristwatch guesses at stages from movement and heart rate; only a clinic study with brain sensors can measure sleep properly. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03106","question":"Does a white or charred burn need medical care?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Anything bigger than the person's palm belongs in front of a clinician. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03107","question":"Is red meat bad for you?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Cutting back is easier than it sounds: smaller portions, chicken or fish on some days, and a few meat-free meals a week all help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03108","question":"Should I see a doctor for a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03109","question":"How long does a cold last?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03110","question":"When does a stomach bug need a doctor?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03111","question":"My grandmother fainted and was confused afterward - is that normal?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03112","question":"How can I get calcium without drinking milk?","answer":"Aim for about 700 mg daily - roughly three portions of dairy or fortified alternatives spread across the day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03113","question":"When can I go back to work after a stomach bug?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03114","question":"My child has a fever and purple spots - what do I do?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Painful, blistering or fast-spreading rashes are urgent dermatology territory, not home-care territory. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03115","question":"Can a cold knock you out like the flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. The speed of onset is the best clue: colds creep up, flu drops on you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03116","question":"What should you never do during a nosebleed?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Deep-packed tissues tear the clot away when removed - keep it to external pressure only. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03117","question":"Do I need to lift weights to be healthy?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03118","question":"Are eggs healthy?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03119","question":"I feel dizzy when I stand up - could it be dehydration?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03120","question":"Why does the stomach flu hit so fast?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03121","question":"I felt dizzy and sweaty and nearly blacked out - what happened?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03122","question":"How can I cool my bedroom down for sleep?","answer":"Falling asleep is tied to a small drop in core body temperature, which a cool room makes easier. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03123","question":"Is a fever bad for you?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03124","question":"How many fruits and vegetables should I eat a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03125","question":"Do I really need 10,000 steps a day?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03126","question":"What does it feel like right before you faint?","answer":"Heat, pallor, tunnel vision and a cold sweat are the classic pre-faint signs. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03127","question":"Why do we get fevers when we are sick?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03128","question":"What is more important for weight loss - diet or exercise?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03129","question":"How much red meat should I eat a week?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Cutting back is easier than it sounds: smaller portions, chicken or fish on some days, and a few meat-free meals a week all help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03130","question":"How long is pink eye contagious?","answer":"Very contagious by touch - hygiene beats any drops for containment. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03131","question":"When will my baby sleep through the night?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Night waking in young babies is developmental, not a problem to fix - feeding needs and immature body clocks drive it. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03132","question":"How do I flush something out of my eye safely?","answer":"Rubbing can turn a speck into a corneal scratch, which is far worse than the speck. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03133","question":"Why does my ear hurt after a cold?","answer":"Comfort care plus 3 days of patience resolves the majority of earaches. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03134","question":"Do I need to use my preventer inhaler when I feel fine?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03135","question":"When can I go back to work after vomiting and diarrhea?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. Stay off work, school and visits until 48 hours after the last episode of vomiting or diarrhea, because that is how long you keep shedding the virus. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03136","question":"Is carrying heavy shopping bags exercise?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03137","question":"My toddler drank from a cleaning bottle - what do I do first?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03138","question":"Do I need protein shakes or supplements?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03139","question":"How often should sheets be washed for dust mites?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Weekly-hot washing of bedding also kills the dust mites that allergy sufferers react to. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03140","question":"How many back blows do you give a choking person?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03141","question":"Is 30 minutes of activity enough for a child?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03142","question":"My baby is choking on a bit of apple - what should I do first?","answer":"Lay the baby face-down along your forearm, head lower than chest, and give up to 5 back blows with the heel of your hand between the shoulder blades. If that fails, turn them face-up and give up to 5 chest thrusts with two fingers on the breastbone. Never use abdominal thrusts on a baby under one - they can injure internal organs. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear quickly, and keep alternating until help arrives or the object comes out.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03143","question":"Does climbing stairs instead of the elevator really help?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03144","question":"When should I go to urgent care instead of the ER?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03145","question":"What helps a headache from staring at screens?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03146","question":"When should I worry about my child's nightmares?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03147","question":"When should I worry about my child's fever?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03148","question":"How do I know if a burn is serious?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03149","question":"Does caffeine count from tea and chocolate in pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Higher caffeine intakes are linked to a greater risk of low birth weight and miscarriage. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03150","question":"Do deodorants cause cancer?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Neither has been shown to cause cancer or health harm in the decades they have been studied; 'aluminum causes breast cancer' is a myth without evidence. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03151","question":"Should I study all night or sleep before an exam?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. Skimping on sleep after study measurably erases some of the benefit of that study. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03152","question":"What symptoms always need medical attention?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03153","question":"My friend is having an asthma attack and the inhaler is not working - what now?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03154","question":"How often should I get up from my desk?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03155","question":"Should I wake my child during a night terror?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. In a night terror the child is stuck between sleep stages, so waking them usually prolongs the confusion rather than helping. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03156","question":"Is wine less fattening than beer?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Calories you drink barely register as food to your appetite, which makes alcohol calories easy to overdo.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03157","question":"Does exercise still help if you start late in life?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03158","question":"Can fiber help prevent constipation?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. Diets rich in fiber are linked with a lower risk of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03159","question":"Why do I keep eating after I am actually full?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Eat slowly and pause partway through - your brain needs about 20 minutes to register fullness. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03160","question":"What are the basic rules for good sleep?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. If you have been awake for what feels like 20 minutes, get up, do something calm in dim light, and return when sleepy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03161","question":"How fast does walking need to be to count as exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03162","question":"How do I treat conjunctivitis at home?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03163","question":"Should I put cream on a rash?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03164","question":"My baby fell off the bed onto the carpet - how do I know if he is okay?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03165","question":"How much salt is in a bowl of canned soup?","answer":"Canned and instant soups can be surprisingly salty - some contain close to half a day's salt limit in a single bowl. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03166","question":"How long should a home strength workout be?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03167","question":"Should I choose salted or unsalted nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03168","question":"How does the flu vaccine work?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03169","question":"What are the signs of heat exhaustion?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03170","question":"Can stroke symptoms go away on their own?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03171","question":"Is it okay to take someone else's antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03172","question":"How many hours of sleep should I get a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03173","question":"Do I need to exercise for 10 minutes straight for it to matter?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03174","question":"Does fiber help with cholesterol?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. Diets rich in fiber are linked with a lower risk of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03175","question":"How do I treat a minor burn?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03176","question":"What triggers migraines?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. Frequent or disabling migraines are very treatable now - no one should simply endure them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03177","question":"Does sleep affect your immune system?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. During deep sleep the immune system ramps up defensive activity, which is one reason rest is prescribed for illness. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03178","question":"What headache symptoms should I never ignore?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03179","question":"Why do I forget things when I am tired?","answer":"Sleep is when the brain replays and files the day's learning, moving fragile short-term memories into longer-term storage. A tired brain both encodes less during the day and files less overnight, so fatigue hits memory twice. An all-nighter trades the memory consolidation you need for a few extra hours of tired reading - a bad deal before an exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03180","question":"Why does oat milk have so little protein?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Choose drinks fortified with calcium - and ideally vitamin D and B12 - and check the label for added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03181","question":"Why do potatoes not count as a vegetable portion?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. They are still perfectly fine to eat - just count them as your carbohydrate, not as a vegetable. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03182","question":"Is there a link between sleep and obesity?","answer":"Short sleep disturbs appetite hormones: ghrelin (hunger) rises and leptin (fullness) falls, so you feel hungrier the next day. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03183","question":"Does a white or charred burn need medical care?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. White, leathery or charred-looking skin means a deep burn, and chemical or electrical burns always need professional assessment. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03184","question":"What should I do if my child swallowed something harmful?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03185","question":"My 2-month-old has a fever - what do I do?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. Trust the pattern: drinking, crying appropriately and responding normally are reassuring; floppiness, refusal to drink or a shrill cry are not. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03186","question":"What should I do with leftover antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03187","question":"Why do my shoes smell?","answer":"Wash feet daily and dry carefully between the toes, where trapped moisture lets fungus grow. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03188","question":"Do I need to drink eight glasses of water a day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03189","question":"Should I have alcohol-free days?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03190","question":"Which foods count as whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03191","question":"When is bleeding heavy enough to call for help?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03192","question":"Should I avoid egg yolks?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03193","question":"Is it normal for my teen to sleep until noon?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. Their body clocks naturally shift about two hours later during adolescence, which is why they are not sleepy at 10 pm but exhausted at 7 am. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03194","question":"Does red wine protect the heart?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03195","question":"How much protein does the average person need?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03196","question":"Why do I need a flu shot every year?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03197","question":"Do I really need to warm up?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03198","question":"Is everything processed unhealthy?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03199","question":"Can stroke symptoms go away on their own?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03200","question":"What is ultra-processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03201","question":"How long can you go without peeing before worrying?","answer":"No urine in 8 hours, sunken eyes or new confusion are the stop-waiting signs. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03202","question":"How much fish should I eat a week?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03203","question":"What triggers asthma attacks?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03204","question":"Something chemical splashed in my eye and it burns - first step?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03205","question":"What should I drink instead of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03206","question":"What does FAST stand for?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03207","question":"Should I pop a blister from new shoes?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. Pus, spreading redness or heat around a blister means infection - and foot blisters with diabetes always need review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03208","question":"Can I do strength exercises at home without equipment?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03209","question":"How long is a cold contagious?","answer":"You are contagious from about a day before symptoms start, and most contagious in the first 2-3 days of the cold. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03210","question":"My kid got stung by a jellyfish and it really hurts - what helps?","answer":"Seawater rinse, gentle tentacle removal and a hot-water soak are the mainstream steps. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03211","question":"How do I know if I stop breathing in my sleep?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03212","question":"Why does my ear feel blocked?","answer":"The only thing you should ever clean with a bud is the outer ear; inside the canal, the bud is the problem. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03213","question":"Should I wear a mask when I am sick?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. The mask's main job when you are sick is source control - protecting everyone else - not protecting you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03214","question":"Why do I get heartburn when I lie down?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Cardiac chest pain and reflux pain can feel similar - when unsure, treat it as urgent.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03215","question":"I got stung by a bee on my arm - what now?","answer":"The stinger still pumps venom after the bee is gone, so scrape it out sideways as fast as you can. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03216","question":"Do I need antibiotics for my infection?","answer":"Cold, flu and most sore throats are viral; antibiotics do nothing for them. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03217","question":"How fast should I try to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03218","question":"Does heart attack pain always feel severe?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Discomfort that feels like indigestion can still be a heart attack, particularly in people with risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03219","question":"My cough has lasted a month - is that normal?","answer":"Three weeks is the standard 'get it looked at' line for a cough. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03220","question":"Can I do all my exercise on the weekend?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03221","question":"Is pink chicken safe to eat?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Leftovers should be reheated until steaming hot all the way through, and only reheated once. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03222","question":"How do I relieve constipation naturally?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03223","question":"How deep do compressions need to be?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03224","question":"How much caffeine is in tea compared to coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03225","question":"Is strength training really that important?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03226","question":"How can vegetarians get enough protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and whole grains add up to plenty of protein over a day, with extra fiber as a bonus. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03227","question":"How do I know if I should see a doctor or wait it out?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03228","question":"Someone is not breathing - what comes first, calling or compressions?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03229","question":"Are granola bars a healthy snack?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. If afternoon cravings hit, check the basics first: a proper lunch with protein and enough water prevents many 'hunger' episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03230","question":"Is it bad to drink water with meals?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. Water with food is harmless and can add to fullness; the 'bad for digestion' claim is a myth.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03231","question":"Should children have less sugar than adults?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03232","question":"My fever has lasted 4 days - is that okay?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever with a stiff neck or an unexplained rash can signal meningitis - that is a same-hour call. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03233","question":"Why do I keep catching colds?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03234","question":"What should I do if I feel like I am going to faint?","answer":"Heat, pallor, tunnel vision and a cold sweat are the classic pre-faint signs. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03235","question":"Why is wholegrain bread better than white bread?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03236","question":"How do you do the Heimlich maneuver?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03237","question":"Why do wounds itch when healing?","answer":"Every time you pick a scab you reset part of the healing clock. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03238","question":"Why do most people need more fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03239","question":"How can I pick a healthier soup?","answer":"Salt is the catch with store-bought soup: many brands pack a large share of your daily limit into one serving. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03240","question":"How long do I press on a bleeding cut?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03241","question":"Does an afternoon coffee affect sleep?","answer":"Caffeine lingers: about half of it is still in your body roughly 5 hours after you drink it. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03242","question":"What is the daily limit for added sugar?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03243","question":"How do I get vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03244","question":"Does white noise help you sleep?","answer":"Steady background sound does not lull you to sleep directly - it prevents the sudden noises that snap you back awake. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03245","question":"Why is the second day after a workout worse than the first?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03246","question":"How long do I press on a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03247","question":"My burn is bigger than my palm - should I get it checked?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. White, leathery or charred-looking skin means a deep burn, and chemical or electrical burns always need professional assessment. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03248","question":"Does sleeping more help you recover from illness?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. Short sleep dents immune defenses, which is one reason the run-down-and-sick pattern is so common. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03249","question":"Should I squeeze a splinter out?","answer":"Wash your hands and the area, then grip the exposed end with clean tweezers and pull it out at the same angle it went in. If it is buried under the skin, a sterilized needle can gently lift the skin over the end before you tweeze it out. Wash the area again afterward, cover it, and watch for redness or pus over the next few days. Deep, near an eye, or showing spreading redness are the times a splinter needs professional removal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03250","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I have not had enough to drink?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more. Young children and older adults can dehydrate faster than healthy adults, so keep an eye on them in hot weather.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03251","question":"Is canned tuna an oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03252","question":"When should I see a doctor about a cough?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03253","question":"Is a headache a sign I need water?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03254","question":"My cold is on day 8 - is that normal?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03255","question":"How long does a stomach bug last?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03256","question":"Why do I get headaches at my desk?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03257","question":"Why do I crave junk food when I am tired?","answer":"Short sleep disturbs appetite hormones: ghrelin (hunger) rises and leptin (fullness) falls, so you feel hungrier the next day. Tired brains also reach for quick, sugary energy and have weaker brakes on impulse snacking. Studies consistently link chronic short sleep with higher rates of weight gain, though sleep alone is not the whole story.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03258","question":"How should I warm up before exercise?","answer":"Start the same activity slower and lighter, then ramp up over 5-10 minutes - that is the whole warm-up. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03259","question":"Does the sugar limit include fruit and milk?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03260","question":"Can I drink juice instead of eating fruit?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03261","question":"What is the best treatment for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03262","question":"How long before bed should I stop drinking coffee?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03263","question":"Should I eat with diarrhea and vomiting?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03264","question":"I got the worst headache of my life very suddenly - what should I do?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03265","question":"Is it dangerous to drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03266","question":"What should I do when my child has a nightmare?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03267","question":"What strength exercises can I do at home for beginners?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03268","question":"How do I teach a child to wash hands properly?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03269","question":"Do crash diets work?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03270","question":"What are the signs of sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03271","question":"What are the signs of low iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03272","question":"Why is oily fish good for you?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03273","question":"Does sleep affect your immune system?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. Short sleep dents immune defenses, which is one reason the run-down-and-sick pattern is so common. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03274","question":"Can I exercise every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness. Sleep is the highest-yield recovery tool you have - guard it like part of the program.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03275","question":"How do I stop a bee sting from itching?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03276","question":"Why can I not stick to an exercise routine?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03277","question":"Does vinegar help jellyfish stings?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03278","question":"What is the difference between a normal allergic reaction and anaphylaxis?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. The rule is simple: adrenaline first, emergency call always - even after successful adrenaline use. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03279","question":"What is CBT for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03280","question":"My cut is still bleeding after 20 minutes of pressure - what now?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Redness, swelling, warmth or pus in the days after a cut mean it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03281","question":"How do I know my inhaler technique is right?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03282","question":"Is honey healthier than sugar?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03283","question":"I am out of shape - where do I begin?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03284","question":"Why is vitamin D important?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03285","question":"What fever temperature is dangerous for kids?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. A rash that does not blanch under pressure plus fever needs emergency assessment. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03286","question":"My elderly mother will not drink and seems confused - is that dehydration?","answer":"Urgent signs include no urination for 8 or more hours, sunken eyes, extreme thirst with inability to keep fluids down, marked lethargy or confusion, and cold, mottled hands and feet. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03287","question":"How do I know if a head injury is serious?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Feeling fine right away does not rule out a delayed problem, which is why the 48-hour watch matters. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03288","question":"Why does exercise make me wheeze?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03289","question":"Are energy drinks worse than coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03290","question":"Why can I not keep my legs still when trying to sleep?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Low iron stores are sometimes involved, so it is worth seeing a doctor - several treatments work well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03291","question":"Should I drink tea with my meals?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03292","question":"Should I take antihistamines every day during allergy season?","answer":"They block the histamine signal rather than treating the underlying sensitivity. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03293","question":"Is 400 mg of caffeine a hard limit?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03294","question":"What should I look for in a milk alternative?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Choose drinks fortified with calcium - and ideally vitamin D and B12 - and check the label for added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03295","question":"How do new parents cope with no sleep?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Night waking in young babies is developmental, not a problem to fix - feeding needs and immature body clocks drive it. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03296","question":"What are night terrors?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. In a night terror the child is stuck between sleep stages, so waking them usually prolongs the confusion rather than helping. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03297","question":"Is a glass of wine before bed a good idea?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. In the second half of the night, as alcohol wears off, sleep becomes shallow and broken - that is the rebound. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03298","question":"How do I treat a minor burn?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03299","question":"What triggers migraines?","answer":"One-sided throbbing pain with nausea and light sensitivity is the classic migraine pattern. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. See a doctor if migraines are frequent (more than a few a month), disabling, or do not respond to simple measures - effective preventive treatments exist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03300","question":"Is my teen lazy or just tired?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Meanwhile school often starts before 8:30 am, meaning chronic sleep loss across the school week. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03301","question":"How do night shift workers sleep better?","answer":"Shift work fights your body clock, so the goal is defending one solid sleep block: blackout curtains, a cool quiet room, phones off, and telling household members you are off-limits. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03302","question":"How do I start exercising after years of being sedentary?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03303","question":"What makes asthma worse in winter?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03304","question":"Why not cover coughs with your hand?","answer":"The elbow works because you rarely shake hands with it or rub your eyes with it. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03305","question":"Do I need an expensive mattress to sleep well?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. Replace a mattress when it sags, lumps, or you wake stiff and sleep better elsewhere, which is often around 7 to 8 years. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03306","question":"Why should I not scratch a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03307","question":"How do you do CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03308","question":"When should a rash go to the emergency room?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Rashes that are painful rather than itchy, rapidly spreading, blistering, or accompanied by fever, confusion or breathing difficulty all need urgent assessment. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03309","question":"Do nails hold germs?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Keep nails trimmed and scrub your fingertips against your palms while washing so the nails get attention too. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03310","question":"Should I splash water on someone who fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03311","question":"How do I know if a rash is something serious?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03312","question":"What are night terrors?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. Unlike nightmares, the child usually has no memory of it and settles back to sleep; they are harmless and typically fade with age. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03313","question":"What time should kids go to bed?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Children who regularly sleep less than their range are more likely to be irritable, struggle with learning, and behave as if hyperactive rather than sleepy. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03314","question":"My toddler has a fever but is playing - should I worry?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. For older children, how they behave matters more than the number: a feverish child who drinks, responds and perks up between doses of medicine is usually less worrying than a quiet, floppy one with a lower reading. A rash that does not blanch under pressure plus fever needs emergency assessment. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03315","question":"What should I drink during a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Blood, severe pain, or dehydration signs move this from home care to a doctor. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03316","question":"Is it bad to eat late at night?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. A good rule of thumb is to finish a big meal 2-3 hours before you lie down, mainly to protect sleep and digestion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03317","question":"Does snoring mean sleep apnea?","answer":"Plain snoring is common and usually harmless, though it can strain relationships. It gets louder with alcohol, sleeping on your back, nasal blockage, excess weight and age. It matters when it is loud and paired with pauses, gasping or choking during sleep and heavy daytime sleepiness - that pattern suggests sleep apnea and deserves a doctor's attention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03318","question":"Is bacon bad for you?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. The bowel cancer link is why experts treat processed meat as an occasional food rather than an everyday staple. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03319","question":"How much water is too much in one day?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03320","question":"How long do sore throats last?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03321","question":"How do I avoid germs at the gym?","answer":"Staph bacteria live happily on shared mats and benches; the wipe-and-wash routine is what keeps them away from your skin. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03322","question":"How do you do the Heimlich maneuver?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03323","question":"When should a headache after hitting my head worry me?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03324","question":"Does vitamin C cure a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03325","question":"Should I raise the cut above my heart?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03326","question":"How long does conjunctivitis last?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03327","question":"How can my teenager get enough sleep before school?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. During puberty the internal clock drifts later, so teens genuinely are not tired at bedtime - biology, not laziness. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03328","question":"Why is ORS better than water for a stomach bug?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Small, frequent sips are the technique that works when the stomach is irritated. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03329","question":"What is hands-only CPR?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03330","question":"How short can an exercise session be and still count?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03331","question":"What are examples of muscle-strengthening activities?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03332","question":"Which allergy medicine will not make me sleepy?","answer":"They block the histamine signal rather than treating the underlying sensitivity. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03333","question":"How much sleep do children need?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Children who regularly sleep less than their range are more likely to be irritable, struggle with learning, and behave as if hyperactive rather than sleepy. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03334","question":"Which foods are high in protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Plant proteins arrive with fiber and less saturated fat, so mixing beans, lentils, nuts and whole grains through the day works very well. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03335","question":"Does vitamin C help absorb iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03336","question":"Why is tilting your head back during a nosebleed wrong?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Deep-packed tissues tear the clot away when removed - keep it to external pressure only. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03337","question":"Is dark urine a sign that I need more water?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03338","question":"Can exercise improve my mood?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03339","question":"What is CBT for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03340","question":"What counts as strength exercise?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03341","question":"Should I make someone throw up after swallowing a poison?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03342","question":"What can I do at 2 am instead of staring at the ceiling?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Lying there frustrated teaches your brain that bed equals stress, which deepens the problem. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03343","question":"Does white noise help you sleep?","answer":"White noise works by masking sudden sounds - traffic, snoring, household noise - that would otherwise punch through light sleep. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03344","question":"How do I fix my sleep schedule?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. A fixed wake time is the strongest lever you have - it sets the pressure for sleep that evening more than anything else. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03345","question":"My child has a fever and white patches on her throat - should I get it checked?","answer":"High fever plus pus spots plus tender glands plus no cough is the strep pattern. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03346","question":"Why wash hands after handling raw meat or pets?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. Germs reach your hands mainly at these moments, which is why targeting them beats constant frantic washing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03347","question":"Why is ultra-processed food so easy to overeat?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03348","question":"What should I do if someone is choking?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. A silent cough or total inability to speak means the airway is truly blocked - act now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03349","question":"How do I clean after norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03350","question":"How do I stop hitting snooze?","answer":"Snoozing chops the last stretch of sleep into 9-minute scraps of the lightest, least refreshing kind. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03351","question":"When is vertigo an emergency?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03352","question":"How much salad counts as a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03353","question":"Are sleeping pills from the pharmacy safe?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. Tolerance builds within days - the sleepiness fades while the grogginess the next morning does not. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03354","question":"When can I go back to work after a stomach bug?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03355","question":"How quickly do you need to take Tamiflu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For most healthy people the small benefit rarely outweighs side effects; for high-risk groups it can matter a lot. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03356","question":"Should I remove contact lenses after a chemical splash?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03357","question":"My blister burst on its own - what now?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03358","question":"When should I worry about dizziness?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03359","question":"What does a severe asthma attack look like?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. Speech in single words, lips tinged blue, or no response to 10 puffs all mean an emergency call now. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03360","question":"How much sleep do toddlers and preschoolers need?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Overtired children often look wired rather than worn out, so bedtime battles can be a sign of too little sleep. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03361","question":"Do masks stop you spreading colds?","answer":"A face mask on a person who is coughing or sneezing catches a large share of the droplets they would otherwise release. It is most useful when you cannot avoid being near others while ill - the commute home, a pharmacy, a crowded waiting room. The mask's main job when you are sick is source control - protecting everyone else - not protecting you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03362","question":"Why does trying harder to sleep make it worse?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Lying there frustrated teaches your brain that bed equals stress, which deepens the problem. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03363","question":"Do beans really have protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Plant proteins arrive with fiber and less saturated fat, so mixing beans, lentils, nuts and whole grains through the day works very well. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03364","question":"Can my baby sleep on their tummy?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03365","question":"Can daylight exposure fix insomnia?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Morning daylight tells the clock 'morning is here', which times evening melatonin release properly that night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03366","question":"Should I keep my bedroom warm or cold?","answer":"Falling asleep is tied to a small drop in core body temperature, which a cool room makes easier. Around 18 to 20 °C (65-68 °F) suits most people, with warmer bedding rather than a warmer room. A hot, stuffy bedroom causes more night waking, while a slightly cool one usually goes unnoticed once you are under the covers.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03367","question":"How is heat exhaustion different from heatstroke?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. Confusion, hot dry skin or collapse means heatstroke - an emergency that needs an immediate call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03368","question":"What is the difference between a reliever and preventer inhaler?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03369","question":"How often should I wash my towels?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Bedding is comfortable to wash every 1-2 weeks - more often if you sweat heavily, share a bed with pets, or have allergies. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03370","question":"What is norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03371","question":"How do I know if my cut needs stitches?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Pad around anything stuck in a wound and leave it for the professionals to remove. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03372","question":"Constipation with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Bleeding, weight loss or alternating constipation and diarrhea shift this from pharmacy to clinic. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03373","question":"Is walking really good exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03374","question":"Why do doctors recommend honey over cough syrup?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. The honest evidence review: honey performs about as well as the syrups and costs far less. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03375","question":"My dad has chest pain and is sweating - what should I do?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03376","question":"Why do I wake up at night after drinking?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. In the second half of the night, as alcohol wears off, sleep becomes shallow and broken - that is the rebound. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03377","question":"Does flu come with a fever?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03378","question":"Should you squeeze a stinger out?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03379","question":"Why is my asthma inhaler blue?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03380","question":"Do eggs cause heart disease?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. For most people, cholesterol from food (like egg yolks) matters less for blood cholesterol than the saturated fat in butter, fatty meat and pastry. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03381","question":"Why is my phone dirtier than a toilet seat?","answer":"Phones meet your face and hands dozens of times a day, which makes them a highway for whatever germs you picked up last. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03382","question":"How much alcohol is safe to drink?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03383","question":"What headache symptoms should I never ignore?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03384","question":"Should I put a burn under water right away?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03385","question":"When should my cold be getting better?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03386","question":"What alcohol percentage should hand sanitizer be?","answer":"Hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol is a good backup when soap and water are not available. It works well against many germs but is weaker than washing when hands are visibly dirty or greasy, and some bugs (like norovirus) resist it. After the toilet, after handling food, or whenever hands look dirty, soap and water is the better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03387","question":"How dirty is my phone?","answer":"Phones meet your face and hands dozens of times a day, which makes them a highway for whatever germs you picked up last. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03388","question":"What is the daily salt limit for adults?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03389","question":"My friend is choking on a piece of steak but can still talk a little - what now?","answer":"Coughing is the body's own first aid for choking, so let it work while it is working. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03390","question":"How long does an ear infection last?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03391","question":"How do I stop heartburn without medicine?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment. Frequent reflux over years can change the esophageal lining, which is the reason persistent symptoms deserve review rather than a lifetime of antacids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03392","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I have not had enough to drink?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03393","question":"Should I use a white noise machine?","answer":"White noise works by masking sudden sounds - traffic, snoring, household noise - that would otherwise punch through light sleep. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03394","question":"What should I eat and drink with a cold?","answer":"Rest, fluids and simple painkillers are the evidence-backed core of cold care. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03395","question":"What is a good cool-down?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03396","question":"What foods have the most fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03397","question":"Should I scrub my nails when washing hands?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Fingertips and nail beds are among the dirtiest spots on the hand, which is why the 20-second routine includes them. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03398","question":"How does the flu vaccine work?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03399","question":"How often should I wash bedding when someone is ill?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. Most respiratory viruses spread by hands and close contact, so these small habits cut the chain better than disinfecting every surface blindly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03400","question":"My daughter was stung and her lip is swelling - what should I do?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03401","question":"What is segmented sleep?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. It becomes a problem when you notice the waking, check the time, and start worrying, which re-activates the brain. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03402","question":"Why is frozen spinach cheaper but still good for me?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03403","question":"Why do I need to take antibiotics exactly as prescribed?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03404","question":"What if I am alone when someone collapses?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03405","question":"What are the signs of tonsillitis?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. White tonsil patches alone are not conclusive, but combined with fever and no cough they warrant a check. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03406","question":"Is 400 mg of caffeine a hard limit?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03407","question":"Why do coughs last so long after a cold?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03408","question":"Someone swallowed pills - when is it an emergency?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03409","question":"Do fevers always need medicine?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03410","question":"Are nightmares normal for children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. Reassure, keep lights low, and avoid long discussions mid-night - children take their cue from your calm. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03411","question":"Is blood in my stool from constipation serious?","answer":"See a doctor if constipation has lasted more than about 3 weeks despite fiber and fluids, or if it keeps returning. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03412","question":"When should I go to urgent care instead of the ER?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03413","question":"Should pregnant women eat fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03414","question":"Is my child getting enough sleep?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Overtired children often look wired rather than worn out, so bedtime battles can be a sign of too little sleep. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03415","question":"What causes the common cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03416","question":"What is vertigo exactly?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03417","question":"What fever temperature is dangerous for kids?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. Trust the pattern: drinking, crying appropriately and responding normally are reassuring; floppiness, refusal to drink or a shrill cry are not. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03418","question":"How is heat exhaustion different from heatstroke?","answer":"Sweating plus dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps in a hot setting is the classic heat exhaustion picture. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03419","question":"Is brown rice better than white rice?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03420","question":"Why are nuts recommended if they are so high in calories?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03421","question":"Why do my eyes and nose itch when I am near cats?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03422","question":"What should I do if a cut gapes open?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. Cuts on the face, puncture wounds, and any animal or human bite should be checked - infection and scarring risks are higher there. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03423","question":"When is the most important time to brush?","answer":"The most important brush of the day is last thing at night; the second can be before breakfast or at another convenient time. Acidic food and drink soften enamel for a while, so brushing immediately after juice, soda or fruit can scrub the softened layer away. After anything acidic, wait about 30 to 60 minutes - or brush before eating instead.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03424","question":"Is it normal for one spot to hurt much more after exercise?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Pain that changes your stride or form is the clearest dividing line between soreness and injury. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03425","question":"Why do I get heartburn when I lie down?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03426","question":"How do I stop grinding my teeth in my sleep?","answer":"Nighttime grinding (bruxism) often comes with stress, and can also travel with sleep apnea, alcohol and some medications. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03427","question":"What counts as one portion of fruit or vegetables?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03428","question":"What is the best way to build an exercise habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Plan for missed days in advance - the rule is never miss twice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03429","question":"What counts as strength exercise?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03430","question":"Why do I grind my teeth at night?","answer":"Nighttime grinding (bruxism) often comes with stress, and can also travel with sleep apnea, alcohol and some medications. Many people only learn about it from a partner's complaints, morning jaw ache, headaches, or a dentist spotting worn enamel. A dentist can fit a night guard to protect the teeth, and treating stress or a suspected sleep problem addresses the root cause.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03431","question":"How many calories are in alcohol?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Cutting back on alcohol is one of the easiest ways to trim 'invisible' calories.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03432","question":"How do I treat an earache at home?","answer":"Comfort care plus 3 days of patience resolves the majority of earaches. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Do not put oil, drops or cotton buds into a painful ear unless a doctor has advised it - cotton buds mainly push wax deeper. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03433","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I stand up?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03434","question":"Is instant noodle soup bad for me?","answer":"Canned and instant soups can be surprisingly salty - some contain close to half a day's salt limit in a single bowl. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03435","question":"What causes skin rashes?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03436","question":"Can exercise cancel out a sedentary job?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03437","question":"I got shampoo in my baby's eyes - is that an emergency?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03438","question":"How long do you rinse an eye after a chemical splash?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03439","question":"What is the difference between saturated and unsaturated fat?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fats - butter, ghee, coconut oil, fatty processed meat and pastry - raise cholesterol, so keep them to a smaller share of your diet. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03440","question":"Do nails hold germs?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Fingertips and nail beds are among the dirtiest spots on the hand, which is why the 20-second routine includes them. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03441","question":"How much fiber do I need for regular bowel movements?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03442","question":"Does blue light really affect sleep?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. The stimulation matters as much as the blue light: a tense show or an endless feed keeps your nervous system alert. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03443","question":"What helps a headache from staring at screens?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03444","question":"Can older adults still build muscle?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03445","question":"Can I eat as much honey as I want?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03446","question":"Why do dispatchers ask you to put the phone on speaker during CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03447","question":"Is instant noodle soup bad for me?","answer":"Salt is the catch with store-bought soup: many brands pack a large share of your daily limit into one serving. Compare labels per 100 grams and pick lower-salt versions, or make your own soup where you control the salt. Bulk soup out with vegetables, beans and whole grains and it becomes a genuinely filling meal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03448","question":"When does flu treatment with antivirals work best?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. The 48-hour window is the key fact: antivirals lose most of their benefit after it closes. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03449","question":"What is the first thing to do for a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03450","question":"How long before bed should I stop drinking coffee?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03451","question":"Do you need to stretch after a workout?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03452","question":"Will a glass of water before dinner stop me overeating?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. It is a harmless habit with modest benefits: helpful, but not a magic weight-loss trick.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03453","question":"Why do I feel dizzy if I stop exercising suddenly?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03454","question":"How many fruits and vegetables should I eat a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03455","question":"Why does one eye turn into two with pink eye?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03456","question":"Is peanut butter as healthy as whole nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03457","question":"What causes sudden dizziness?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03458","question":"Do potatoes count towards five a day?","answer":"White potatoes, yams, cassava and plantain do not count towards your five a day, because they are starchy foods that sit with bread, rice and pasta. Think of potatoes as your carb for the meal, while sweet potatoes, carrots and beets are genuine vegetables. Sweet potatoes do count, and so do other roots like carrots, beets and parsnips.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03459","question":"What does dehydrated pee look like?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03460","question":"Is it lazy or actually fine to rely on frozen veg?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03461","question":"Can a cold knock you out like the flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03462","question":"Is it okay to take someone else's antibiotics?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03463","question":"What are the risks of sitting too long?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03464","question":"I went from fine to flattened in one morning - is that flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03465","question":"What is the healthiest way to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03466","question":"Can a wearable diagnose sleep apnea?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. A wristwatch guesses at stages from movement and heart rate; only a clinic study with brain sensors can measure sleep properly. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03467","question":"How many grams of added sugar is too much?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03468","question":"My toddler fell and bumped her head - what should I do?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03469","question":"My friend is having an asthma attack and the inhaler is not working - what now?","answer":"Upright posture, one puff every 30-60 seconds up to 10, then reassess - that is the core sequence. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03470","question":"Is coffee as hydrating as water?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. Caffeine has a mild diuretic effect, but habitual drinkers build tolerance, so the overall effect is still hydration. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03471","question":"My child spilled boiling water on their arm - should I take them in?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. White, leathery or charred-looking skin means a deep burn, and chemical or electrical burns always need professional assessment. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03472","question":"How often should I stretch to get more flexible?","answer":"Hold, breathe, release - 15 to 30 seconds per stretch on warm muscles. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. Evidence that stretching before exercise prevents injury is weak, so treat it as a flexibility and comfort practice rather than armor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03473","question":"Is pink eye serious?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03474","question":"When is the latest I can drink coffee?","answer":"Caffeine lingers: about half of it is still in your body roughly 5 hours after you drink it. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03475","question":"Should I wash raw chicken before cooking?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. Washing does not remove bacteria anyway - only proper cooking does. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03476","question":"Do I need antibiotics for pink eye?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03477","question":"How do I know if I am dehydrated?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03478","question":"What should I do when I can't fall asleep?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Staying in bed awake and annoyed builds a learned link between bed and frustration, so a brief calm reset elsewhere works better. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03479","question":"What is the 48-hour rule for vomiting bugs?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03480","question":"What are the danger signs of dehydration in babies?","answer":"No urine in 8 hours, sunken eyes or new confusion are the stop-waiting signs. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03481","question":"What is in OTC sleep medicine?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. Tolerance builds within days - the sleepiness fades while the grogginess the next morning does not. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03482","question":"Why is my kid so sedentary and what do I do?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03483","question":"What is the first thing to do after burning yourself?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03484","question":"Why is ultra-processed food so easy to overeat?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03485","question":"Why is drowsy driving compared to drunk driving?","answer":"Sleep deprivation slows reaction time and attention much like alcohol, without the smell that warns anyone. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03486","question":"I never know when to call the doctor - any rules of thumb?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03487","question":"Does sleeping in repay my sleep debt?","answer":"A weekend lie-in feels great and repays a little of the debt, but only part of it - and at the cost of shifting your body clock later. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03488","question":"Is dizziness a sign of a stroke?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03489","question":"How much exercise do children need?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03490","question":"Is toothpaste good for burns?","answer":"No - ice, butter, toothpaste and ointments can all make a burn worse or trap heat in the skin. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03491","question":"Does skipping breakfast make you gain weight?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03492","question":"Is being sore after exercise a good sign?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03493","question":"What actually helps when you have a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03494","question":"How many eggs is a day's worth of protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03495","question":"Is a handful of grapes really a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03496","question":"Is the flu vaccine worth it?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03497","question":"Why do dispatchers ask you to put the phone on speaker during CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03498","question":"How is heat exhaustion different from heatstroke?","answer":"Heat exhaustion shows as heavy sweating, dizziness, headache, nausea and cramps after exertion in the heat. Move the person somewhere cool, have them lie down, loosen clothing, and cool the skin with wet cloths or a fan while they sip water or a drink with salts (like a sports drink). Most people improve within 30 minutes with these steps. If there is no improvement in 30 minutes, or they become confused, stop sweating, have very hot dry skin, or faint, treat it as heatstroke and call emergency services.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03499","question":"How do I clean a graze properly?","answer":"Rinse the wound under clean running water and wash around it with mild soap; pat dry with a clean cloth. Skip hydrogen peroxide and undiluted alcohol on the wound itself - they damage healthy tissue and can slow healing. The old advice to let wounds 'breathe' has been overturned: a clean, moist, covered wound heals measurably faster.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03500","question":"Is a handful of grapes really a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat. Juice and smoothies can only ever count as one portion a day, however much you drink.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03501","question":"How do I stay motivated to exercise?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03502","question":"My mom's face is drooping on one side - what should I do?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03503","question":"What are the benefits of walking daily?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03504","question":"When should I see a doctor for a burn?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Young children, babies and older adults should have most burns other than tiny ones assessed by a professional. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03505","question":"What is norovirus?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03506","question":"Why is drinking called 'drinking your calories'?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Calories you drink barely register as food to your appetite, which makes alcohol calories easy to overdo.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03507","question":"How do I use an EpiPen?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03508","question":"Why does my skin itch after a hot shower?","answer":"Very hot water dissolves the skin's protective oil layer, leaving it tight, dry and itchy. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03509","question":"My child has a fever and white patches on her throat - should I get it checked?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03510","question":"Should I bundle up when I have a fever?","answer":"Light clothing, steady fluids and rest are the comfort basics; medicine is optional if they feel fine. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03511","question":"Should I get antivirals for the flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03512","question":"Are sleeping pills from the pharmacy safe?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. After a few nights, antihistamine-based aids stop helping sleep but keep causing heavy-headed mornings. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03513","question":"Is tongue scraping safe?","answer":"The tongue's rough surface traps bacteria and food debris, and that coating is a major source of bad breath. Brushing your tongue gently with your toothbrush, or using a scraper from back to front, removes most of it. It is optional but cheap and quick; a little gagging at first is normal and fades with practice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03514","question":"How many baby carrots make one portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. That means one medium apple or banana, two plums, a cupful of grapes, three heaped tablespoons of vegetables, or a cereal bowl of salad. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03515","question":"Is it bad to exercise at night?","answer":"Consistent daytime activity deepens sleep for most people within a few weeks. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03516","question":"How often should I wash my towels?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Weekly-hot washing of bedding also kills the dust mites that allergy sufferers react to. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03517","question":"What counts as insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03518","question":"Do step counts even matter?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03519","question":"Someone is choking but still coughing - what should I do?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. A silent cough or total inability to speak means the airway is truly blocked - act now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03520","question":"Does honey count as added sugar?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03521","question":"My child poked her eye - when do we see a doctor?","answer":"Rubbing can turn a speck into a corneal scratch, which is far worse than the speck. If blinking does not work, rinse the eye with clean lukewarm water or saline - tilt the head so the water runs from the inner corner outward. Never try to remove anything embedded in the eye: cover it loosely (a paper cup works) and get emergency help. See a doctor urgently for pain that does not settle, vision changes, or sensitivity to light after any eye injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03522","question":"How do I start strength training with no equipment?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03523","question":"When should I call 911 instead of driving to the hospital?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03524","question":"Do I need to use my preventer inhaler when I feel fine?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03525","question":"What do I do during an asthma attack?","answer":"Upright posture, one puff every 30-60 seconds up to 10, then reassess - that is the core sequence. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03526","question":"What are the warning signs after a head bump?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03527","question":"How do I stay motivated to exercise?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03528","question":"Is pink eye contagious?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03529","question":"How do I stay hydrated with a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03530","question":"Can mouthwash replace brushing?","answer":"Mouthwash cannot replace brushing and interdental cleaning, because it cannot remove plaque - only mechanical cleaning does that. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03531","question":"Why am I wide awake after evening workouts?","answer":"Consistent daytime activity deepens sleep for most people within a few weeks. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03532","question":"Can sleep apnea be treated?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03533","question":"Is my teen lazy or just tired?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Later start times give back the hours the late-shifted teen clock steals, and studies show teens use them to sleep more. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03534","question":"If I only drink tea and coffee all day, am I still hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03535","question":"Should I raise the cut above my heart?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03536","question":"Should I replace my toothbrush after being sick?","answer":"A splayed toothbrush is a sign it has done its job; past that point it scrubs less and scratches more. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03537","question":"I felt dizzy and sweaty and nearly blacked out - what happened?","answer":"Heat, pallor, tunnel vision and a cold sweat are the classic pre-faint signs. The moment you feel it, lie down or sit with your head between your knees - fainting standing up is what causes head injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03538","question":"What helps constipation fast?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03539","question":"How active should my 8-year-old be?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03540","question":"Why do people say caffeine dehydrates you?","answer":"Yes - for people who drink them regularly, tea and coffee count towards your daily fluid total. Caffeine has a mild diuretic effect, but habitual drinkers build tolerance, so the overall effect is still hydration. Only very large doses on top of an empty stomach or a hot day might leave you needing a bit of extra water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03541","question":"How do I get rid of a stress headache?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03542","question":"Does a standing desk count as being active?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. Set a loose timer - the movement matters more than the specific exercise you pick. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03543","question":"Does drinking water really help constipation?","answer":"Fiber, water and walking are the evidence-backed trio for ordinary constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03544","question":"Do I need mouthwash?","answer":"Think of mouthwash as a supplement to the brush, never a substitute for it. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03545","question":"Is it safe to eat food past the use-by date?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. Your nose can detect spoilage, but it cannot detect the bugs that actually poison you, so the use-by date is the one to trust.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03546","question":"How long does conjunctivitis last?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03547","question":"How do I start cooking more at home?","answer":"When you cook at home you control the salt, sugar, fat and portion size, which is why people who cook more tend to eat better overall. It is usually cheaper per portion than takeout, especially for staples like soups, stews, curries and pasta dishes. Batch cooking on the weekend makes the habit stick: cook once, eat three times.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03548","question":"How do I spot sugar in an ingredients list?","answer":"Sugar hides under many names: glucose, dextrose, fructose, maltose, corn syrup, rice syrup, honey, agave and fruit juice concentrate. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03549","question":"What temperature kills food poisoning bacteria?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Leftovers should be reheated until steaming hot all the way through, and only reheated once. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03550","question":"How do I care for a burn blister?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03551","question":"Can my child sleep after a head bump?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03552","question":"How do night shift workers sleep better?","answer":"Daytime sleep is lighter and shorter by nature, so blackout darkness, cool air and ear protection are doing real work, not just comfort. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03553","question":"Why is drying between my toes important?","answer":"Wash feet daily and dry carefully between the toes, where trapped moisture lets fungus grow. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03554","question":"How do I know chicken is fully cooked?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Leftovers should be reheated until steaming hot all the way through, and only reheated once. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03555","question":"How much fiber do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03556","question":"Does spicy food damage your stomach?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Hot peppers irritate an existing ulcer or bad heartburn, but they do not create ulcers - bacteria and anti-inflammatory painkillers do that. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03557","question":"What headache symptoms should I never ignore?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. Thunderclap onset, or any headache with neurological signs like weakness or confusion, means emergency services. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03558","question":"Will a glass of water before dinner stop me overeating?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. Water with food is harmless and can add to fullness; the 'bad for digestion' claim is a myth.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03559","question":"What is a tension headache?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03560","question":"Which electric toothbrush should I buy?","answer":"The brush is the tool; the 2 minutes of coverage is the treatment. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03561","question":"Which plant milk has the most protein?","answer":"Plant drinks vary a lot: many almond milks contain very little almond, and the unsweetened versions are the ones to pick. Choose drinks fortified with calcium - and ideally vitamin D and B12 - and check the label for added sugar. Soy milk is the closest match to cow's milk for protein; oat and almond are much lower.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03562","question":"Should I drink water before meals?","answer":"A glass of water before a meal adds a little stretch to your stomach, and some studies find people eat slightly less afterwards. Drinking with meals is fine and does not interfere with digestion - the 'water washes away stomach acid' idea is a myth. Water with food is harmless and can add to fullness; the 'bad for digestion' claim is a myth.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03563","question":"What exercise is best for over 70s?","answer":"Muscle, bone and balance respond to training at essentially any age - studies show strength gains even in people in their 80s and 90s. Strength plus balance practice is the fall-prevention combination worth prioritizing later in life. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03564","question":"How much protein does the average person need?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03565","question":"Should I worry if my child cries a lot after hitting their head?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03566","question":"Is napping normal for seniors?","answer":"The need for sleep barely changes with age - what changes is how easily the brain holds deep sleep. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03567","question":"Do over-the-counter sleep aids work?","answer":"Most non-prescription sleep aids rely on sedating antihistamines, the same drowsy ingredient as in allergy medicine. After a few nights, antihistamine-based aids stop helping sleep but keep causing heavy-headed mornings. They are acceptable occasionally, but for ongoing insomnia the evidence-backed route is CBT-I and sleep habits rather than nightly pills.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03568","question":"How does drinking affect REM sleep?","answer":"Alcohol can make you fall asleep faster, which is why it feels like a sleep aid. But as your body processes it, sleep fragments: more awakenings in the second half of the night, less REM, and often a bathroom trip. You wake feeling less restored than the hours suggest, so regular nightcaps quietly worsen sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03569","question":"Is 6 hours of sleep enough?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03570","question":"Do kids need structured exercise or just play?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03571","question":"What is histamine?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03572","question":"Is popcorn a whole grain?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Look for oats, whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley and popcorn - they all count as whole grains. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03573","question":"Why does deodorant stop working?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Neither has been shown to cause cancer or health harm in the decades they have been studied; 'aluminum causes breast cancer' is a myth without evidence. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03574","question":"What is the best treatment for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03575","question":"How much sleep do teenagers need?","answer":"Teenagers need 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night - more than adults. During puberty the internal clock drifts later, so teens genuinely are not tired at bedtime - biology, not laziness. Consistent wind-down times, dimmer evenings and phones out of the bedroom help them land closer to what they need.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03576","question":"What causes the common cold?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03577","question":"My cut is still bleeding after 20 minutes of pressure - what now?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03578","question":"How do I remove a bee stinger?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. Wash the area with soap and water, then hold a cold pack (wrapped in cloth) on it for about 10 minutes to limit swelling and pain. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03579","question":"How do I know if my infection is bacterial?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Every unnecessary antibiotic course trains bacteria to survive the next real one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03580","question":"What helps constipation fast?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03581","question":"When should I see a doctor about sleep?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. The first-choice treatment is a structured talking therapy called CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia) - it outperforms sleeping pills long-term and has no hangover effect. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03582","question":"My cold is on day 8 - is that normal?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03583","question":"What is norovirus?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03584","question":"Can my newborn sleep with a pillow or blanket?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Tummy sleeping and side sleeping both raise the risk of SIDS, so back is the answer for every sleep until babies can roll both ways themselves. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03585","question":"How long should I cool down for?","answer":"Taper the intensity over a few minutes rather than stopping mid-stride. This keeps blood moving back to the heart while demand drops, which reduces the light-headedness of an abrupt stop. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper. If you felt dizzy after workouts before, a real cool-down usually fixes it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03586","question":"What is social jet lag?","answer":"Your body clock runs on consistency: going to bed and - most importantly - waking at the same time every day trains it to make you sleepy and alert on schedule. A fixed wake time is the strongest lever you have - it sets the pressure for sleep that evening more than anything else. If you must shift, moving bedtime and wake time by 15-30 minutes at a time works far better than one dramatic reset.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03587","question":"Why do gyms ask you to bring a towel?","answer":"Staph bacteria live happily on shared mats and benches; the wipe-and-wash routine is what keeps them away from your skin. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03588","question":"I sliced my finger on a knife and it will not stop bleeding - what do I do?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03589","question":"How do I start exercising after years of being sedentary?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03590","question":"Why does exercise make me wheeze?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03591","question":"What is a unit of alcohol?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A standard pint of lager carries roughly 2.5 units, so the 14-unit weekly limit is about 5-6 pints spread across the week. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03592","question":"Can I share a toothbrush with my partner?","answer":"Razor nicks and gum bleeding make those two the most important items to keep personal. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03593","question":"How do I know if I should see a doctor or wait it out?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03594","question":"Why is my eye red and sticky?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03595","question":"How do I stop germs spreading at home when someone is sick?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. The high-traffic touchpoints matter more than doorknobs nobody touches - focus cleaning where hands actually go.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03596","question":"Should I wrap a swollen ankle?","answer":"Rest, wrapped ice, a compression bandage and elevation are the four first-day moves for a sprain. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Avoid heat, alcohol, running and deep massage in the first 48 to 72 hours - they can increase swelling. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03597","question":"Is 150 minutes of exercise really necessary?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03598","question":"My cough has lasted a month - is that normal?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03599","question":"What should I do for chest pain?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03600","question":"Why does my ear feel blocked?","answer":"Earwax is protective - it traps dust, moisturizes the canal and has antibacterial properties - and ears clean themselves as old wax works out naturally. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03601","question":"I feel dizzy when I stand up - could it be dehydration?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03602","question":"What is REM sleep for?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. The first half of the night is deep-sleep-heavy for body repair; the second half is REM-heavy for dreams and emotional sorting. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03603","question":"Should I make someone throw up after swallowing a poison?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03604","question":"I never took a CPR class - what should I do if someone collapses and is not breathing?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03605","question":"Does a standing desk count as being active?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03606","question":"Do antivirals help with flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For most healthy people the small benefit rarely outweighs side effects; for high-risk groups it can matter a lot. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03607","question":"What do I do if my burn blisters?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Growing redness, swelling or pus means it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03608","question":"What is the difference between a dry and chesty cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03609","question":"Should I eat breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03610","question":"I touched a hot pan and my fingers hurt - what should I do first?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03611","question":"How much caffeine is safe during pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03612","question":"When can I go back to work after a stomach bug?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03613","question":"Why does my newborn wake up so much?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Waking often is normal and healthy for the first months; 'sleeping through the night' arrives gradually, often somewhere after 4-6 months. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03614","question":"How deep do compressions need to be?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03615","question":"Can exercise cancel out a sedentary job?","answer":"Exercise does not fully offset 10 motionless hours; the breaks between sitting do real work. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems. Meetings and calls are easy anchors: walk during calls, stand during meetings, move between tasks.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03616","question":"What do I do if my burn blisters?","answer":"Leave the blister intact; breaking it opens the door to infection. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03617","question":"Is it dangerous to swallow blood from a nosebleed?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. Blood thinners turn a routine nosebleed into one that deserves prompt medical help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03618","question":"My head feels like a tight band - what is that?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03619","question":"Should I suppress my cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03620","question":"Do eggs cause heart disease?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you. Eggs bring protein, vitamins and minerals for relatively few calories, so they earn their place on the plate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03621","question":"Why can't babies have honey?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03622","question":"Do kids need structured exercise or just play?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03623","question":"Can my body fight a bacterial infection without antibiotics?","answer":"Viruses cause most everyday infections - colds, most sore throats, most coughs, flu, norovirus - and they clear as your immune system does the work. Bacterial infections (strep throat, many urinary infections, some chest infections) are the ones antibiotics treat. Even then, many minor bacterial infections are handled by the immune system; antibiotics speed recovery and prevent spread in specific cases a doctor can judge. Taking antibiotics for viral illness adds side effects, wipes out helpful gut bacteria, and fuels resistance that makes future infections harder to treat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03624","question":"How many grams of salt is too much?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03625","question":"Does drinking water really help constipation?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03626","question":"What is heartburn?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Cardiac chest pain and reflux pain can feel similar - when unsure, treat it as urgent.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03627","question":"How do I know if I am dehydrated?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03628","question":"Is plain water the only drink that keeps me hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03629","question":"Fever with a stiff neck - is that serious?","answer":"Seek medical advice for a fever above about 39.4C (103F), any fever lasting more than 3 days, or one that returns after you seemed to recover. Fever with a stiff neck or an unexplained rash can signal meningitis - that is a same-hour call. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03630","question":"Why does flu make your whole body ache?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03631","question":"What are healthy snacks?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. If afternoon cravings hit, check the basics first: a proper lunch with protein and enough water prevents many 'hunger' episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03632","question":"How much sleep do toddlers and preschoolers need?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Children who regularly sleep less than their range are more likely to be irritable, struggle with learning, and behave as if hyperactive rather than sleepy. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03633","question":"Should I pick my scabs?","answer":"A scab is your body's natural bandage, and picking it reopens the wound and invites infection and scars. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03634","question":"How many back blows do you give a choking person?","answer":"Five firm back blows between the shoulder blades come first, leaning the person forward. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Call emergency services if the blockage does not clear, and never do a blind finger sweep in the mouth - you may push the object deeper. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03635","question":"How should I brush my child's teeth?","answer":"Children need adult help with brushing until about age 7 or 8, when their hand control is good enough to do it alone under supervision. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03636","question":"My head feels like a tight band - what is that?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03637","question":"Can exercise improve my mood?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03638","question":"Are nuts good for you?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03639","question":"What does it feel like right before you faint?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. Getting horizontal - or at least head-down seated - before you hit the floor prevents most faint-related injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03640","question":"Why do I keep catching colds?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. Most colds travel by droplets and contaminated hands, which is why handwashing works. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold and cause side effects and resistance for nothing. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03641","question":"I keep getting dizzy every day - should I see a doctor?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03642","question":"What temperature should my fridge be?","answer":"Keep the fridge at 5 °C (41 °F) or below, and cook poultry through to 74 °C (165 °F) with no pink meat and juices running clear. Pink can linger in safely cooked chicken and white-looking meat can still be unsafe, which is why the 74 °C number matters. A cheap food thermometer removes the guessing - color alone is unreliable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03643","question":"What is the first thing to do after burning yourself?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03644","question":"What are whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03645","question":"Why am I constipated all the time?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03646","question":"What is the 48-hour rule for vomiting bugs?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03647","question":"Should I remove contact lenses after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03648","question":"I sliced my finger on a knife and it will not stop bleeding - what do I do?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03649","question":"How should a baby sleep safely?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03650","question":"Can I drink juice instead of eating fruit?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03651","question":"Why should I eat five portions of fruit and veg every day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Five a day is a minimum rather than a ceiling - more is generally better, and variety counts. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03652","question":"How can I tell a craving from real hunger?","answer":"Mild thirst can masquerade as hunger - both can feel like an empty, restless stomach. A useful test: drink a glass of water and wait 10 to 15 minutes; if the feeling fades, it was probably thirst. True hunger is gradual and open to any food; a sudden longing for one specific snack is usually a craving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03653","question":"Why are nuts recommended if they are so high in calories?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03654","question":"Is 150 minutes of exercise really necessary?","answer":"150 minutes moderate (or 75 vigorous) a week is the widely adopted adult target. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03655","question":"How can I lose weight without a fad diet?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03656","question":"Should I wake a sleepwalker?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Do not try to wake the person; gently guide them back to bed. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03657","question":"Is a fever bad for you?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03658","question":"Do I really need 8 hours of sleep?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03659","question":"How do I know if I am dehydrated?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03660","question":"What cough signs mean urgent care?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still. A change in a long-standing 'usual' cough counts as a new cough - mention it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03661","question":"How much exercise do I need for better sleep?","answer":"Consistent daytime activity deepens sleep for most people within a few weeks. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03662","question":"How much melatonin should I take?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. It is not a classic sleeping pill - it nudges timing rather than forcing sedation - and evidence for ordinary insomnia is mixed. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03663","question":"Is being sore after exercise a good sign?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Soreness is not the measure of a good workout; progress and comfort over weeks are. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03664","question":"Is a cold caused by bacteria?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03665","question":"Can the flu shot give you the flu?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03666","question":"My kid has a nosebleed at bedtime - what should I do?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03667","question":"Why do I catch colds when I am run down?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. During deep sleep the immune system ramps up defensive activity, which is one reason rest is prescribed for illness. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03668","question":"What are good sources of protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and whole grains add up to plenty of protein over a day, with extra fiber as a bonus. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03669","question":"I have fever and body aches - is this the flu?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Comfort measures plus time are the treatment; the tiredness can linger longest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03670","question":"Are trans fats banned?","answer":"Artificial trans fats are made when liquid oils are hardened into solids, and they raise heart disease risk more than any other kind of fat. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03671","question":"What does a spacer do?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer turns a puffed cloud into delivered dose; technique checks at the pharmacy take minutes. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03672","question":"Is walking really good exercise?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03673","question":"Why is my throat worse in the morning?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03674","question":"What is the first thing to do for a bleeding cut?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03675","question":"What is the right way to stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Sit down and lean the head forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose, just below the bony bridge, for 10 minutes without releasing to peek. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03676","question":"How much exercise do children need?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03677","question":"Should I take medicine to bring a fever down?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03678","question":"Can cotton buds damage your ears?","answer":"Earwax is protective - it traps dust, moisturizes the canal and has antibacterial properties - and ears clean themselves as old wax works out naturally. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03679","question":"Why do doctors recommend walking so much?","answer":"Walking briskly is real, guideline-qualifying exercise - not a consolation prize. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03680","question":"What do I do if my burn blisters?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03681","question":"How do I reset my body clock?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Morning daylight tells the clock 'morning is here', which times evening melatonin release properly that night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03682","question":"How do I know I have influenza and not a cold?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03683","question":"How long do you rinse an eye after a chemical splash?","answer":"Twenty minutes of steady flushing is the goal - time matters more than anything else you could do. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03684","question":"What should I do if someone faints in front of me?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03685","question":"Do antibiotics interfere with the pill?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03686","question":"What are the benefits of walking daily?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03687","question":"Is carrying heavy shopping bags exercise?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03688","question":"How does sleep deprivation affect the brain?","answer":"Sleep loss amplifies emotional reactions: the brain's alarm centers run hotter and the calming, rational control from the prefrontal cortex weakens. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03689","question":"When should I worry about my child's nightmares?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03690","question":"How much water should I drink every day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03691","question":"How long does conjunctivitis last?","answer":"Red, gritty, sticky eyes that still see clearly and feel more irritating than painful are typical conjunctivitis. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03692","question":"What are the signs that need an ambulance immediately?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03693","question":"Is cold water better for your skin?","answer":"Very hot water dissolves the skin's protective oil layer, leaving it tight, dry and itchy. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03694","question":"Who gets sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03695","question":"What is the fastest way to heal a blister?","answer":"Leave an unbroken blister alone - the roof of skin is a natural sterile dressing and popping it invites infection. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. Pus, spreading redness or heat around a blister means infection - and foot blisters with diabetes always need review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03696","question":"My dad has chest pain and is sweating - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03697","question":"Does exercise still help if you start late in life?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03698","question":"How many puffs can I take in an asthma attack?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. Speech in single words, lips tinged blue, or no response to 10 puffs all mean an emergency call now. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03699","question":"Does a higher fever mean a worse illness?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03700","question":"How much exercise do I need for mental health benefits?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03701","question":"Can the flu shot give you the flu?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03702","question":"Can you smell food poisoning bacteria?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. The dangerous bacteria are the quiet ones: no smell, no taste, no visible change - which is why dates, not sniffing, keep you safe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03703","question":"Can low iron make me tired?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03704","question":"Does caffeine affect sleep?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03705","question":"How do you do CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03706","question":"What are the signs of sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03707","question":"My head feels like a tight band - what is that?","answer":"A tight band feeling across both sides of the head is the tension headache signature. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Painkillers more than about 10 days a month can themselves cause rebound headaches, so use them sparingly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03708","question":"Is showering every day bad for your skin?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. If you do shower daily, keep it short and lukewarm, and moisturize afterwards to replace what the water washes away. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03709","question":"What are microsleeps?","answer":"Sleep deprivation slows reaction time and attention much like alcohol, without the smell that warns anyone. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03710","question":"Why do my shoes smell?","answer":"Fungus thrives in warm, damp, dark places - exactly what sweaty, enclosed toes provide when left damp. Change socks daily - more often if they get damp - and rotate shoes so each pair dries out for a day or two between wears. In shared showers and locker rooms, flip-flops cut the risk of picking up athlete's foot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03711","question":"How long should a home strength workout be?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03712","question":"What should I do if someone sleepwalks?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Waking a sleepwalker is hard and can cause confusion and distress - steering them calmly back to bed is the better move. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03713","question":"My son cannot put weight on his ankle after a fall - should I take him in?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03714","question":"Who gets sleep apnea?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. Untreated apnea strains the heart and blood pressure system, but treatments like CPAP and weight loss transform it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03715","question":"When should I go to the doctor for a minor illness?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03716","question":"How do antihistamines work?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. Nasal steroid sprays added to antihistamines control stubborn seasonal allergies better than either alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03717","question":"Does caffeine count from tea and chocolate in pregnancy?","answer":"Pregnant women are advised to keep caffeine under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee or one strong filter coffee. Too much caffeine during pregnancy is linked with low birth weight, which is why the 200 mg cap exists. Remember to count tea, cola, energy drinks and chocolate towards the daily total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03718","question":"Why doesn't hand sanitizer work on norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03719","question":"Do I really need to warm up?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03720","question":"What is restless legs syndrome?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Because iron deficiency is one known contributor, a simple blood test is part of the standard work-up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03721","question":"My mom's face is drooping on one side - what should I do?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03722","question":"Should I exercise through sharp pain?","answer":"DOMS feels dull and achy on both sides (both legs, both arms), peaks a day or two after exercise, and improves steadily. Injury announces itself differently: sharp or stabbing pain in one spot, swelling, pain inside a joint, pain that alters how you move, or pain that worsens instead of fading. Pain that changes your stride or form is the clearest dividing line between soreness and injury. If pain is severe, causes limping, or has not clearly improved after several days, get it assessed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03723","question":"Can the flu shot give you the flu?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. A sore arm and a day of mild achiness is your immune system responding - it is not influenza. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03724","question":"Why is fiber good for you?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. People who eat more fiber tend to have lower rates of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03725","question":"How do I stop a stomach bug spreading at home?","answer":"Norovirus and similar stomach bugs spread mostly by hands and surfaces, and alcohol gel does not reliably kill them - soap and water does. Stay off work, school and visits until 48 hours after the last episode of vomiting or diarrhea, because that is how long you keep shedding the virus. Clean bathrooms and touchpoints with a bleach-based cleaner, wash laundry hot, and keep the sick person's towels separate.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03726","question":"Does hot water kill more germs when washing hands?","answer":"Studies comparing water temperatures find no meaningful difference in germs removed - the soap and scrubbing do the job. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03727","question":"Are nuts fattening?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. Even though nuts are calorie-dense, a daily handful does not tend to cause weight gain - they satisfy hunger efficiently. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03728","question":"What is a fever, actually?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03729","question":"Why use a preventer inhaler every day?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03730","question":"Can cold weather set off asthma?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03731","question":"How do antihistamines work?","answer":"Antihistamines block the histamine that causes sneezing, itching, runny nose and hives, so they ease symptoms rather than cure the allergy. Newer formulations (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine style) cause little drowsiness for most people, while older ones do - which is why the older type is now mostly a bedtime option. Starting them before or at the very start of allergy season usually works better than chasing symptoms. If antihistamines alone are not enough, steroid nasal sprays are the standard next step - a pharmacist can walk you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03732","question":"My fever has lasted 4 days - is that okay?","answer":"For adults: above 39.4C, beyond 3 days, or plus stiff neck, rash or confusion are the thresholds for care. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03733","question":"My toddler fell and bumped her head - what should I do?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03734","question":"What is the best cough medicine?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. The honest evidence review: honey performs about as well as the syrups and costs far less. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03735","question":"Is a nosebleed while on blood thinners an emergency?","answer":"Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat, where it can cause choking, nausea and vomiting - lean forward instead. Do not stuff tissues deep into the nostril; when you pull them out, the clot comes with them. Sitting forward, pinching the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes, is the reliable method. If someone takes blood thinners (like warfarin) and the nosebleed will not stop quickly, they need medical care.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03736","question":"How much water do adults need daily?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03737","question":"How often should I change my bedding?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Weekly-hot washing of bedding also kills the dust mites that allergy sufferers react to. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03738","question":"I got the worst headache of my life very suddenly - what should I do?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03739","question":"Why is eating an orange better than drinking orange juice?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03740","question":"Should I stop using my phone in bed?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. People who use phones in bed tend to fall asleep later and sleep less overall. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03741","question":"Why do women need more iron than men?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03742","question":"What should I do if I feel like I am going to faint?","answer":"Most faints give a warning: sudden heat, a grey or pale face, ringing ears, dim vision and a cold sweat. Getting horizontal - or at least head-down seated - before you hit the floor prevents most faint-related injuries. The feeling usually passes in under a minute once blood returns to the brain. Tell a doctor if it happens repeatedly, happens with chest pain or a racing heart, or occurs during exercise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03743","question":"When should I go to the doctor for a minor illness?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03744","question":"When should a child first see a dentist?","answer":"Most young children cannot brush their own teeth well - an adult's hand finishing the job nightly is the standard advice. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03745","question":"How much caffeine is safe per day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03746","question":"What is the right way to stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury. For the next day, avoid blowing the nose, bending over and nose-picking so the clot stays put.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03747","question":"How long do you rinse an eye after a chemical splash?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Everyday shampoo stings but is not a chemical emergency; industrial cleaners, drain products and acids are. Bring the product container with you so the treating team knows exactly what they are dealing with.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03748","question":"How often should I change my bedding?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Bedding is comfortable to wash every 1-2 weeks - more often if you sweat heavily, share a bed with pets, or have allergies. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03749","question":"Can exercise cancel out a sedentary job?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03750","question":"How do I soothe a sore throat?","answer":"The vast majority of sore throats are viral and clear within about a week on their own. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Honey in a warm drink is cheap, safe (over age 1) and genuinely soothing. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03751","question":"Why is it hard to breathe out when you have asthma?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03752","question":"Why do I wake up at 3 am every night?","answer":"Waking briefly between sleep cycles is completely normal - everyone does it, several times a night, and usually falls straight back asleep without noticing. The waking itself is normal sleep architecture; the second wake-up is anxiety switching the lights on upstairs. Stay relaxed and still, or use the get-up-if-restless rule; knowing that brief waking is normal defuses much of it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03753","question":"How do I clean my phone safely?","answer":"Phones meet your face and hands dozens of times a day, which makes them a highway for whatever germs you picked up last. Wipe it regularly with a soft cloth slightly dampened with appropriate screen cleaner (check the manufacturer's guidance), especially after illness in the house. And wash your hands - a clean phone in dirty hands does not stay clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03754","question":"What is the poison control number?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03755","question":"Do babies and children need less salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03756","question":"Does freezing destroy vitamins in vegetables?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03757","question":"Do you need to stretch after a workout?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03758","question":"How do you help a person who has fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03759","question":"Do I need dairy for calcium?","answer":"Dairy is an easy calcium-and-protein package, but you can get the same nutrients from fortified plant drinks, leafy greens, tofu and fish with soft bones. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03760","question":"Why do I feel worse after a huge lie-in?","answer":"A weekend lie-in feels great and repays a little of the debt, but only part of it - and at the cost of shifting your body clock later. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03761","question":"What is vertigo exactly?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03762","question":"How should I warm up before exercise?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Warm muscles, not cold stretched ones, are what protect you before a session. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03763","question":"Is a handful of grapes really a portion?","answer":"One portion is about 80 grams, which is roughly what fits in your hand. A medium apple or banana, three heaped spoons of veg, or a dessert bowl of salad each count as one portion. Dried fruit counts too, but only 30 grams - about a tablespoon of raisins - because it is sticky, sugary and easy to overeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03764","question":"Why can't I sleep after a night shift?","answer":"Daytime sleep is lighter and shorter by nature, so blackout darkness, cool air and ear protection are doing real work, not just comfort. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03765","question":"I am out of shape - where do I begin?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03766","question":"Is there treatment for norovirus?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03767","question":"Should I bundle up when I have a fever?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03768","question":"Should I pick my scabs?","answer":"Every time you pick a scab you reset part of the healing clock. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03769","question":"How do I stop a nosebleed?","answer":"Firm, uninterrupted pressure on the soft part of the nose for 10 minutes stops most nosebleeds. Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, which can cause nausea. Breathe through the mouth while you pinch, and you can hold a cool pack on the bridge of the nose to help. If it is still bleeding after another 10-minute pinch, seek medical care - and get help sooner if the bleed is heavy or after a head injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03770","question":"How do I treat a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Multi-symptom products can quietly double you up on paracetamol - one active ingredient per symptom is safer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03771","question":"Why can't my teenager fall asleep before midnight?","answer":"At puberty the internal clock shifts about two hours later, so many teenagers cannot fall asleep before 11 pm-midnight no matter how tired their parents want them to be. Meanwhile school often starts before 8:30 am, meaning chronic sleep loss across the school week. Schools that moved starts later report more sleep, better attendance and better mood - evidence that the biology is real, not laziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03772","question":"How active should my 8-year-old be?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03773","question":"How long is too long to be constipated?","answer":"Three weeks, blood, weight loss or severe pain are the standard thresholds for a GP visit. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, severe abdominal pain, vomiting, or constipation alternating with diarrhea need assessment rather than self-treatment. A new, persistent change in bowel habit - especially over 50 - is something doctors always want to hear about. Bright red streaks on paper from a straining-related fissure are common and usually minor, but any bleeding deserves at least a mention at an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03774","question":"What foods have the most fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03775","question":"How do I stop heartburn without medicine?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03776","question":"Is it better to stay in bed or get up when I can't sleep?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Lying there frustrated teaches your brain that bed equals stress, which deepens the problem. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03777","question":"How do I get over jet lag faster?","answer":"Jet lag is your body clock still running on the time zone you left, so sleep and wake signals arrive at the wrong local hours. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03778","question":"Can you be allergic to antibiotic ointment?","answer":"Petroleum jelly alone keeps minor wounds moist just as well for most people, without the allergy risk. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03779","question":"Should I drink tea with my meals?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03780","question":"Why do I need a flu shot every year?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing. Vaccination matters most for older adults, pregnant people, young children and anyone with chronic conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03781","question":"Do canned and frozen vegetables count towards five a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03782","question":"Do I really need 10,000 steps a day?","answer":"Ten thousand is a nice round marketing number; the science says most of the benefit arrives well before it. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03783","question":"Why do I regain weight after dieting?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03784","question":"What foods should I avoid with reflux?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raise the whole head of the bed rather than adding pillows - the incline is what matters. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03785","question":"How do I know if my sore throat is strep?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. White tonsil patches alone are not conclusive, but combined with fever and no cough they warrant a check. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03786","question":"Do ear infections need antibiotics?","answer":"Most earaches, especially with a cold, are viral middle-ear infections that improve within about 3 days without antibiotics. Paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain, and holding something comfortably warm against the ear, are the mainstay of care. Nothing goes into an aching ear without advice; drops in a perforated eardrum can cause damage. Doctors increasingly delay antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours to see if the body clears it, precisely because most do.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03787","question":"How can I prevent nightmares in children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03788","question":"Can a wearable diagnose sleep apnea?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. They are useful for spotting trends - short nights, late bedtimes - but not for diagnosing anything; that needs a medical sleep study. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03789","question":"Why is the second day after a workout worse than the first?","answer":"Soreness peaking on day 2 after new exercise is DOMS - adaptation at work, not injury. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03790","question":"What is the difference between moderate and vigorous activity?","answer":"If you can talk but not sing, you are in the moderate zone; if you cannot speak in full sentences, you are vigorous. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03791","question":"Cough with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03792","question":"Is peanut butter as healthy as whole nuts?","answer":"Nuts are packed with unsaturated fats, protein, fiber, vitamin E and minerals. A small handful a day - about 30 grams - is a sensible portion, and studies find nut eaters do not tend to gain weight from them, probably because they are so filling. Choose unsalted versions, and plain over honey-coated or heavily roasted. Nut butters count too, as long as the label says 100% nuts with no added sugar or palm oil.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03793","question":"How long before bed should I stop eating?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. A good rule of thumb is to finish a big meal 2-3 hours before you lie down, mainly to protect sleep and digestion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03794","question":"My child spilled boiling water on their arm - should I take them in?","answer":"Get medical help for any burn larger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands, feet, genitals, or over a joint. Also seek care for burns that look white, leathery or charred (a sign all skin layers are affected), and for all chemical and electrical burns. Anything bigger than the person's palm belongs in front of a clinician. While you wait, keep up the cool running water and cover the area loosely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03795","question":"My child cannot keep fluids down with a stomach bug - what now?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03796","question":"What should I look for when buying canned fruit?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer. Rinsing canned vegetables under the tap washes away some of the added salt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03797","question":"Do antacids help heartburn?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03798","question":"Is a manual toothbrush enough?","answer":"A manual brush used properly for 2 minutes cleans just as well as many electric ones. Electric brushes help people who brush too hard, too fast, or with limited hand control, and oscillating heads show a small extra benefit in studies. Whichever you pick, technique and the full 2 minutes matter more than the price tag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03799","question":"Is sea salt healthier than table salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03800","question":"How is a migraine different from a normal headache?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. Frequent or disabling migraines are very treatable now - no one should simply endure them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03801","question":"What helps a sore throat fast?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03802","question":"How many units are in a pint of beer?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A standard pint of lager carries roughly 2.5 units, so the 14-unit weekly limit is about 5-6 pints spread across the week. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03803","question":"What should I do if I cannot fall asleep after 20 minutes?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. If you have been awake for what feels like 20 minutes, get up, do something calm in dim light, and return when sleepy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03804","question":"Is bed-sharing safe with a newborn?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03805","question":"How do I start strength training with no equipment?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03806","question":"Do I need to use my preventer inhaler when I feel fine?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Skipping the preventer while using the reliever more and more is the classic pattern that ends in an asthma attack. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03807","question":"Is heartburn the same as a heart problem?","answer":"Acid escaping upward onto the unprotected esophageal lining is the entire mechanism of heartburn. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03808","question":"How long should I hold a stretch?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. The injury-prevention case for pre-exercise stretching is not well supported; the flexibility case for regular stretching is.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03809","question":"What is the difference between a dry and chesty cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. The post-cold cough can run 2 to 3 weeks without meaning anything new is wrong. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03810","question":"How do I avoid cross-contamination in the kitchen?","answer":"Keep raw meat and its juices away from food that is eaten raw: use separate cutting boards and utensils, or wash them thoroughly between uses. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03811","question":"Why do I need to take antibiotics exactly as prescribed?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Some antibiotics interact with other medications, including oral contraceptives - read the leaflet. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03812","question":"What helps with shift work sleep problems?","answer":"Daytime sleep is lighter and shorter by nature, so blackout darkness, cool air and ear protection are doing real work, not just comfort. Keeping the same sleep block even on days off - or shifting it only slightly - beats flip-flopping between schedules. A nap before a night shift and bright light during the shift help alertness; a doctor or pharmacist can discuss melatonin timing if problems persist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03813","question":"Why is my kid so sedentary and what do I do?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03814","question":"What does a spacer do?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03815","question":"Is cheese unhealthy?","answer":"You do not strictly need dairy, but milk, yogurt and cheese are convenient sources of calcium, protein and iodine. Guidance suggests some dairy each day - for example a glass of milk, a pot of yogurt and a matchbox-sized piece of cheese. Lower-fat and lower-sugar versions deliver the same calcium with less saturated fat, and fortified plant drinks work for those who prefer them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03816","question":"Why should I eat five portions of fruit and veg every day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03817","question":"Can I use the same cutting board for meat and vegetables?","answer":"Cross-contamination is the most common way kitchen germs reach people: raw meat touching a salad board, then lunch. Store raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge so drips cannot land on ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands with soap after touching raw meat, poultry, fish or eggs - every time, before you touch anything else.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03818","question":"When should I worry about dizziness?","answer":"Dizziness plus any neurological or cardiac companion symptom is an emergency call. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03819","question":"Why did advice about eggs change?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03820","question":"When should I see a doctor about a cough?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03821","question":"Is coughing good for you?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03822","question":"Is a nightcap a good idea?","answer":"A drink may knock you out faster, but it fragments your sleep later in the night and cuts REM. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03823","question":"Does exercise help you sleep better?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03824","question":"What happens if I sleep 5 hours a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03825","question":"Can I do strength exercises at home without equipment?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03826","question":"Do I need to lift weights to be healthy?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03827","question":"How many units of alcohol a week is OK?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03828","question":"When should I call an ambulance for asthma?","answer":"Sit the person upright - do not lie them down - keep them calm, and help them take one puff of their reliever inhaler every 30 to 60 seconds, up to a maximum of 10 puffs. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03829","question":"What does FAST stand for?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Facial droop on one side is a presumptive stroke; make the call and note the time. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03830","question":"Is it bad to exercise at night?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Evening hard sessions keep some people wired; others sleep fine - your own response is the test. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03831","question":"What causes sudden dizziness?","answer":"Stand-up dizziness is usually postural: gravity plus low fluid volume, not a brain problem. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Most everyday dizziness is brief and harmless: sit or lie down, drink some water, and it passes. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03832","question":"Dizziness with chest pain - what should I do?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness. New severe dizziness in someone over 65 or with cardiovascular risk factors warrants a lower threshold for checking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03833","question":"I never took a CPR class - what should I do if someone collapses and is not breathing?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03834","question":"Is it okay to take someone else's antibiotics?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03835","question":"Do I need to wash hands after touching my phone?","answer":"Wash your hands before preparing or eating food, after using the toilet, after coughing, sneezing or blowing your nose, and after touching pets or their waste. Also wash after handling garbage, after commuting on public transport, and before and around anyone who is ill. These moments are where germs actually travel onto your hands and then to your mouth, eyes and food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03836","question":"Is canned tuna an oily fish?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03837","question":"Is there a medicine that treats the flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. The 48-hour window is the key fact: antivirals lose most of their benefit after it closes. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03838","question":"How long does it take for exercise to become a habit?","answer":"The research-backed answer is to lower the friction: choose activity you actually enjoy, schedule it at a consistent time, and make the first step tiny. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03839","question":"Should I sponge someone with a fever with cold water?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works. Never give aspirin to children or teenagers with fever because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03840","question":"Should I avoid all processed food?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03841","question":"Why does handwashing take 20 seconds?","answer":"Twenty seconds is roughly the time it takes for soap plus friction to lift grime and germs from every nook of your hands. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this. Singing 'Happy Birthday' twice is the classic 20-second timer for children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03842","question":"When should I go to the doctor for a minor illness?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03843","question":"Is it lazy or actually fine to rely on frozen veg?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03844","question":"Why does my chest burn after eating?","answer":"Acid escaping upward onto the unprotected esophageal lining is the entire mechanism of heartburn. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03845","question":"Do white spots on tonsils mean I need antibiotics?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. A cough, runny nose and hoarse voice point instead to a viral cause, which antibiotics will not help. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03846","question":"How do I start strength training with no equipment?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03847","question":"When should I go to the doctor for a minor illness?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03848","question":"Is cold water as good as hot for handwashing?","answer":"The water temperature barely matters for germ removal - comfortable water works as well as scalding hot. Hot water strong enough to kill germs would burn your skin first; what actually removes microbes is soap plus friction plus time. Pick whatever temperature you can comfortably wash in for 20 seconds, and you are covered.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03849","question":"Do kids grow out of night terrors?","answer":"Night terrors are episodes early in the night where a child sits up, cries or thrashes, eyes open, but is still asleep and unresponsive. Unlike nightmares, the child usually has no memory of it and settles back to sleep; they are harmless and typically fade with age. Do not try to wake or restrain them - keep the surroundings safe, stay nearby, and let it pass.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03850","question":"Is fruit juice as healthy as whole fruit?","answer":"Eating whole fruit beats drinking juice because the fiber slows the sugar down and actually fills you up. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03851","question":"Does caffeine affect sleep?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03852","question":"Can I spread a cold before I have symptoms?","answer":"You are contagious from about a day before symptoms start, and most contagious in the first 2-3 days of the cold. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03853","question":"Should I snack between meals?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. Many afternoon cravings are really thirst, boredom or a low-protein lunch in disguise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03854","question":"When is bleeding heavy enough to call for help?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. With chest pain or heavy bleeding, wait for the ambulance rather than driving yourself or the patient.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03855","question":"Should I splash water on someone who fainted?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03856","question":"How do I clean under my nails?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Keep nails trimmed and scrub your fingertips against your palms while washing so the nails get attention too. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03857","question":"Is pink eye serious?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Stop contact lens wear until the eye has fully settled, and do not share towels or pillows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03858","question":"Are frozen vegetables as healthy as fresh?","answer":"Nutritionally, frozen vegetables are usually just as good as fresh, because freezing locks in nutrients at their peak. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03859","question":"My baby fell off the bed onto the carpet - how do I know if he is okay?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03860","question":"Are granola bars a healthy snack?","answer":"Good snack choices pair protein or fiber: fruit with nuts, plain yogurt, hummus with vegetables, or wholegrain crackers with cheese. Constant sugary snacking keeps teeth bathed in acid and makes energy swing through the day. Many afternoon cravings are really thirst, boredom or a low-protein lunch in disguise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03861","question":"I never know when to call the doctor - any rules of thumb?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03862","question":"Should I take my child to the doctor for a fever?","answer":"A baby under 3 months with a temperature of 38C (100.4F) or more needs urgent medical assessment the same day - this is a firm rule. Trust the pattern: drinking, crying appropriately and responding normally are reassuring; floppiness, refusal to drink or a shrill cry are not. Get urgent care for fever with a stiff neck, an unexplained rash that does not fade under a glass pressed on it, trouble breathing, persistent vomiting, or signs of dehydration. Fevers from routine viruses can run high; that alone is not the emergency - the other signs are.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03863","question":"Is dizziness a sign of a stroke?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. See a doctor promptly for dizziness that keeps recurring, lasts continuously for hours, or comes with new hearing loss in one ear. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03864","question":"How fast should I increase my exercise?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Expect some muscle soreness at first; sharp pain, chest discomfort, unusual breathlessness or dizziness are stop signs that deserve medical review. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting. Consistency at a modest level beats heroic starts that end in injury by week three.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03865","question":"Is 30 minutes of activity enough for a child?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03866","question":"What does dehydration feel like?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03867","question":"Should I call 911 before starting CPR?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03868","question":"How do I clean after a stomach bug?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. The high-traffic touchpoints matter more than doorknobs nobody touches - focus cleaning where hands actually go.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03869","question":"When is a headache an emergency?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03870","question":"When is bleeding heavy enough to call for help?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03871","question":"How do I remove earwax safely?","answer":"The only thing you should ever clean with a bud is the outer ear; inside the canal, the bud is the problem. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03872","question":"Can cold weather set off asthma?","answer":"Infections, allergens, smoke, cold air and exercise top the trigger list. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03873","question":"Does alcohol help you sleep?","answer":"A drink may knock you out faster, but it fragments your sleep later in the night and cuts REM. It suppresses REM sleep, and as it wears off you wake more often, often needing the bathroom. Regular nightcaps therefore tend to leave you less refreshed, not more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03874","question":"How long should a nap be?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Naps past about 30 minutes tip you into deep sleep, so you wake groggy - and late-afternoon or evening naps eat into the sleep pressure you need at bedtime. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03875","question":"My cold is on day 8 - is that normal?","answer":"Seven to ten days is the standard cold timeline, and the tail-end cough often outstays the rest. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03876","question":"How long does caffeine stay in your body?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03877","question":"Does drinking water really help constipation?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Going when the urge comes rather than postponing helps too, as the colon reabsorbs water from stool that waits. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol. Prunes and kiwifruit have decent evidence as natural options if you would rather start with food.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03878","question":"Is it safe to take melatonin every night?","answer":"Melatonin is the hormone your brain releases in darkness to signal night; supplements can modestly help with jet lag and shifted schedules. Melatonin is a timing signal, not a knockout drop, so 'more' does not mean 'more asleep'. Availability and dosing differ by country (prescription-only in some, over-the-counter in others), so check with a pharmacist or doctor, especially before giving it to children.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03879","question":"How many cups of fluid should I have each day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03880","question":"Why is eating an orange better than drinking orange juice?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03881","question":"How much exercise do children need?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03882","question":"How do I treat conjunctivitis at home?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies. Seek care the same day for pain in the eye, sensitivity to light, blurred vision that does not clear with blinking, or a newborn with red eyes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03883","question":"Is rinsing poultry an old habit I should drop?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. Washing does not remove bacteria anyway - only proper cooking does. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03884","question":"How long does a cold last?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Symptoms that keep worsening after a week, or a fever that returns after you had improved, suggest something else is going on. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold. Recovery slows if you skip sleep and hydration, so rest is not optional advice - it is the treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03885","question":"What is a healthy amount of red meat?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Cutting back is easier than it sounds: smaller portions, chicken or fish on some days, and a few meat-free meals a week all help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03886","question":"What is the daily limit for added sugar?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03887","question":"The whole family is vomiting - what is going around?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03888","question":"What is the 150 minutes a week guideline?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03889","question":"When should I go to urgent care instead of the ER?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03890","question":"How do new parents cope with no sleep?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Waking often is normal and healthy for the first months; 'sleeping through the night' arrives gradually, often somewhere after 4-6 months. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03891","question":"Should you hit a choking person on the back?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. A silent cough or total inability to speak means the airway is truly blocked - act now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03892","question":"When should I see a doctor for a sprain?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03893","question":"Do naps count towards my nightly sleep?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Long naps pull you into deep sleep stages, and waking from those produces that heavy, hungover feeling. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03894","question":"How do I stay hydrated with a stomach bug?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03895","question":"How long does a cold last?","answer":"Most colds run their course in 7 to 10 days, with symptoms peaking around days 2 to 3. A lingering cough after the stuffy nose clears is common and can last 2 to 3 weeks; it does not mean you need antibiotics. Getting worse after day 7, or a second fever spike, is the pattern that deserves a doctor's visit. Rest and time are genuinely the main treatment - there is no cure for the common cold.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03896","question":"Fever with a stiff neck - is that serious?","answer":"For adults: above 39.4C, beyond 3 days, or plus stiff neck, rash or confusion are the thresholds for care. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03897","question":"Why compare foods per 100 grams?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03898","question":"How often should I replace my mattress?","answer":"There is no single best mattress - comfort and support are personal, and studies find medium-firm suits most backs but not all. Replace a mattress when it sags, lumps, or you wake stiff and sleep better elsewhere, which is often around 7 to 8 years. A trial period is worth more than marketing claims: your spine will tell you in a week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03899","question":"What is an allergic reaction doing in my body?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. Histamine is the main chemical messenger of allergy, which is why blocking it relieves symptoms. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03900","question":"Is everything processed unhealthy?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. The concern is not processing itself but the formula: many ultra-processed foods are engineered to be easy to overeat and are low in fiber. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03901","question":"What should I do if cleaning fluid gets in my eye?","answer":"Hold the eyelids open and flush the eye with clean lukewarm running water for a full 15 to 20 minutes, letting it run from the inner corner outward. Remove contact lenses as soon as flushing starts if they do not come out on their own. Call emergency services or Poison Control while or after flushing - some chemicals keep damaging tissue even after rinsing. Harsh household chemicals like drain cleaner or bleach are true eye emergencies; mild soap stings but rinses clean.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03902","question":"Why do I panic when I see it's 3 am?","answer":"Every glance at the clock triggers mental arithmetic and worry, both of which are wake-up signals to the brain. Each calculation raises arousal, making the next sleep attempt harder. Turn clocks away, silence phone notifications, and let the night flow without a scoreboard.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03903","question":"What counts as insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03904","question":"Is a glass of wine a day good for me?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03905","question":"How do you do CPR?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03906","question":"Can cold weather set off asthma?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03907","question":"How many grams of salt is too much?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Sea salt, pink salt and table salt are all salt: the health effect is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03908","question":"How do I treat a mild rash at home?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Note when it appeared, where it spread, and anything new (soap, detergent, food, medication) - that history is exactly what a doctor will ask. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03909","question":"How much caffeine is safe per day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03910","question":"Is being sore after exercise a good sign?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03911","question":"Is it bad to skip breakfast?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03912","question":"I feel lightheaded - is it serious?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03913","question":"Is walking really good exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. The 'brisk' bar is simple: noticeably faster breathing, still able to chat. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03914","question":"How much salmon counts as a portion?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. The omega-3 fats in oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel support your heart and brain. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03915","question":"Is the flu vaccine worth it?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03916","question":"What strength exercises can I do at home for beginners?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03917","question":"Why do I wake up exhausted despite 8 hours in bed?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03918","question":"What is hands-only CPR?","answer":"Chest compressions in the middle of the chest, hard and fast, are the core of modern bystander CPR. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03919","question":"Cough with weight loss - what does that mean?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Blood in coughed-up mucus always merits assessment; a large amount with breathlessness is an emergency. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03920","question":"Do antivirals help with flu?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For otherwise healthy adults with mild flu, doctors often judge rest and fluids the better trade-off. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03921","question":"How do I know if I am not drinking enough?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. If your pee is dark yellow or strong-smelling, that is a clear sign to drink more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03922","question":"I always quit exercising after a few weeks - what am I doing wrong?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03923","question":"How do I know I have influenza and not a cold?","answer":"Flu typically brings a sudden fever, chills, aching muscles and joints, headache, dry cough and profound tiredness. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course. Seek care if you improve and then worsen, have trouble breathing, or are in a high-risk group with worsening symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03924","question":"What is the best cough medicine?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03925","question":"What should I do for chest pain?","answer":"Call emergency services immediately for chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03926","question":"What should I do when my child has a nightmare?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03927","question":"What should I do during a migraine?","answer":"Migraines are usually a throbbing or pulsing pain, often on one side, and come with nausea, and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people get an 'aura' first - flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling. A dark, quiet room, fluids, and medication taken early in the attack work best; common triggers include poor sleep, stress, skipped meals, and hormonal changes. Frequent or disabling migraines are very treatable now - no one should simply endure them.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03928","question":"What are head injury warning signs in children?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03929","question":"How long does conjunctivitis last?","answer":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the clear membrane over the eye, making it red, gritty, itchy and sometimes sticky with discharge. Most cases are viral or allergic, and they settle within 1 to 2 weeks without treatment; antibiotics rarely change the course of viral cases. Gently bathe crusted lashes with cooled boiled water or saline, and bathe allergic eyes with a cool flannel. Viral pink eye clears on its own; drops only matter for bacterial or allergic variants a clinician identifies.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03930","question":"What is the difference between deodorant and antiperspirant?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Neither has been shown to cause cancer or health harm in the decades they have been studied; 'aluminum causes breast cancer' is a myth without evidence. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03931","question":"What is the healthiest amount of alcohol?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03932","question":"Is there a link between sleep and getting colds?","answer":"Sleep and immunity are tightly linked: in studies, people sleeping under 6-7 hours are noticeably more likely to catch colds after virus exposure. Short sleep dents immune defenses, which is one reason the run-down-and-sick pattern is so common. Sleeping well also appears to improve how well vaccines train your immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03933","question":"How do I clean under my nails?","answer":"The space under fingernails traps dirt and germs that survive a casual wash. Fingertips and nail beds are among the dirtiest spots on the hand, which is why the 20-second routine includes them. Nail biting also moves whatever is under the nails straight into your mouth - one more reason to quit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03934","question":"How should a baby sleep safely?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03935","question":"Why does the room spin when I lie down or turn over?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03936","question":"Is it possible to eat too much fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Swap white bread for wholegrain, add beans to soups and stews, and leave the skin on fruit and veg for an easy fiber boost. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03937","question":"When should I worry about dizziness?","answer":"Call emergency services if dizziness comes with chest pain, palpitations, new weakness or numbness, slurred speech, severe headache, or double vision - these can signal heart or brain problems. Daily recurrence or hours-long episodes deserve an appointment, though not usually an ambulance. Dizziness from standing too fast in a warm room is usually benign; dizziness that arrives with neurological signs never gets the benign assumption. If dizziness has already caused faints, describe those events to a doctor rather than only the dizziness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03938","question":"What is orthosomnia?","answer":"Consumer sleep trackers estimate sleep and wake reasonably well, but their 'deep sleep' and 'REM' numbers are rough guesses, not measurements. A wristwatch guesses at stages from movement and heart rate; only a clinic study with brain sensors can measure sleep properly. Some people become anxious chasing perfect sleep scores, a phenomenon called orthosomnia - if the app is stressing you, ignore the scores.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03939","question":"How do I start strength training with no equipment?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03940","question":"What counts as activity for children?","answer":"Children and teens need around 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, and unlike adults they do not need it in one block - the day can be stitched together from play, sport, walking and chores. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. For young kids, active play is the exercise prescription - structure is optional. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03941","question":"Why do I feel so much better after a walk?","answer":"Movement is one of the best-evidenced self-help tools for mood; it works alongside professional care, not instead of it. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. It does not require intensity: 20 to 30 minutes of brisk walking already moves the needle, and benefits show up within weeks. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03942","question":"When should I see a doctor for a sprain?","answer":"Seek medical care if you cannot bear weight on the limb, the joint looks crooked or deformed, there is numbness, or pain is severe. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. A joint that will not take weight may be fractured - get it looked at. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03943","question":"Are protein bars and cereals ultra-processed?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03944","question":"What are the warning signs after a head bump?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Watch the person for two days: any repeated vomiting, growing confusion or unusual sleepiness means emergency care. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03945","question":"What are whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn. Check the label carefully: 'made with whole grain' can mean just a sprinkle, so 'whole grain' should be high on the ingredients list.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03946","question":"Why is oily fish good for you?","answer":"Aim for two portions of fish a week, and make one of them oily - each portion about 140 grams. Oily fish includes salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring and trout; these are rich in omega-3 fats that support heart and brain health. Pregnant women should keep oily fish to no more than two portions a week and avoid shark, swordfish and marlin because of mercury. Canned sardines and salmon eaten with their soft bones add calcium as a bonus. (Canned tuna is not an oily fish once canned.)","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03947","question":"Someone is not breathing - what comes first, calling or compressions?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Doing something imperfectly is far better than doing nothing while waiting for a trained responder.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03948","question":"What does FAST stand for?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03949","question":"How long should you run a burn under cold water?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03950","question":"What does dehydration feel like?","answer":"Early signs of dehydration include thirst, a dry mouth, and passing darker, smellier pee less often. You may also feel tired, headachy or lightheaded, and find it harder to concentrate. A quick check is pee colour: dark yellow means your body is asking for more fluid.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03951","question":"Why do doctors keep telling me to eat more fiber?","answer":"Fiber keeps your digestion regular and helps prevent constipation. It also feeds the healthy bacteria in your gut, helps you feel full after meals, and can lower cholesterol. People who eat more fiber tend to have lower rates of heart disease and bowel cancer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03952","question":"How long should a home strength workout be?","answer":"Chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows are the low-barrier trio most beginners can start this week. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03953","question":"How do I know if I am dehydrated?","answer":"Thirst is the first signal, followed by a dry mouth, dark yellow urine, tiredness, headache and lightheadedness on standing. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Dark amber urine is the single most practical daily hydration gauge. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness. By the time you feel thirsty you are already mildly behind - regular sips beat catch-up gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03954","question":"Is strength training really that important?","answer":"Two strength days a week is the guideline; the moves, not the gym membership, are what matter. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03955","question":"How much exercise do I need for better sleep?","answer":"Regular physical activity is one of the better-supported natural sleep improvers - people who move more fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply. Evening hard sessions keep some people wired; others sleep fine - your own response is the test. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03956","question":"How long should you run a burn under cold water?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03957","question":"Why do I wake up exhausted despite 8 hours in bed?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03958","question":"Does vitamin C cure a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing. Stay home while feverish, both to recover and to keep the virus to yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03959","question":"Is a fever bad for you?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03960","question":"Is decaf a good evening substitute?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03961","question":"Should I go to the doctor for a deep cut?","answer":"A cut may need professional care if it is deep, has edges that gape apart, if you can see fat or deeper tissue, or if bleeding does not stop after 10 minutes of firm pressure. The gaping test is simple: if the edges will not fall together on their own, the wound usually needs closing. When in doubt, get it looked at; stitches work best within hours of the injury.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03962","question":"How do I set up my bedroom for better sleep?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. If you have been awake for what feels like 20 minutes, get up, do something calm in dim light, and return when sleepy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03963","question":"Why am I tired all the time if I sleep 6 hours?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. The small number of true short sleepers is tiny; most people cutting sleep to 6 hours are building a deficit they pay for in focus and mood. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03964","question":"Why do my legs feel creepy and crawly at night?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Because iron deficiency is one known contributor, a simple blood test is part of the standard work-up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03965","question":"Why do my eyes and nose itch when I am near cats?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03966","question":"When does flu treatment with antivirals work best?","answer":"Antiviral medicines such as oseltamivir can shorten flu by about a day and reduce complications, but only if started within about 48 hours of the first symptoms. They are mainly recommended for people at higher risk of complications - older adults, pregnant people, and those with asthma, diabetes or heart disease. For most healthy people the small benefit rarely outweighs side effects; for high-risk groups it can matter a lot. If you think you have flu and fall in a risk group, call a doctor early rather than waiting to see how it develops.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03967","question":"How can I quickly judge if a food is healthy?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03968","question":"Why do I feel worse after a huge lie-in?","answer":"Extra weekend sleep helps somewhat, but you cannot fully bank sleep on Saturday for a week of 5-hour nights. That is why marathon lie-ins often end with grogginess and a rough Monday. Better tools: a short early-afternoon nap on the weekend, an earlier bedtime, and holding your weekday wake time within about an hour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03969","question":"What are examples of muscle-strengthening activities?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Effort near the last few repetitions is the signal that the muscle is being challenged enough. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too. Progress by adding reps, sets or load gradually - never by jumping to heavy weights on day one.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03970","question":"Why do I feel weaker if I train hard every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03971","question":"I got stung by a bee on my arm - what now?","answer":"Scrape the stinger out sideways with the edge of a card or a fingernail - a quick sideways swipe is better than squeezing, which can pump more venom in. After the stinger is out, soap and water plus a wrapped cold pack are all most stings need. Raise the limb if the sting is on an arm or leg. Wasps usually leave no stinger behind, so a quick check of the site tells you which culprit you had.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03972","question":"What if I am alone when someone collapses?","answer":"For an unresponsive adult who is not breathing, make the emergency call first and put the phone on speaker so your hands stay free. The dispatcher stays on the line, confirms the steps, and can tell you where the nearest public defibrillator (AED) is. Then start chest compressions and keep going until the ambulance crew takes over, an AED is attached, or the person starts breathing normally. Continue until help arrives, an AED is ready, or the person clearly starts breathing. First aid courses teach the full sequence and are worth taking - this guidance is awareness, not training.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03973","question":"When is fainting an emergency?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance. Once recovered, getting up slowly and having a drink and a snack helps prevent a repeat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03974","question":"How long does the flu last?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03975","question":"What is the difference between use-by and best-before?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. The dangerous bacteria are the quiet ones: no smell, no taste, no visible change - which is why dates, not sniffing, keep you safe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03976","question":"Can I eat too much protein?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Protein beyond what you need is not stored as extra muscle; it is burned for energy or stored as fat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03977","question":"What are the signs of a stroke?","answer":"Face, Arms, Speech - if any is abnormal, call emergency services at once. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03978","question":"Is it lazy or actually fine to rely on frozen veg?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03979","question":"What should I do if my child swallowed something harmful?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03980","question":"What should I do if someone faints in front of me?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03981","question":"What lowers the risk of SIDS?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03982","question":"What is the fastest way to heal a blister?","answer":"Resist popping it; the fluid is sterile and the skin over it does the job of a dressing. Cover it with a soft plaster or a donut-shaped pad that takes pressure off the blister itself. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, keep the flap of skin in place, and cover with a clean dressing. See a doctor if it fills with yellow-green pus, the surrounding skin turns hot and spreading-red, or you have diabetes and blisters on your feet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03983","question":"How do I treat a mild rash at home?","answer":"Everyday rashes are most often contact irritation, dry skin, heat, or a mild viral rash, and they settle with simple care. Keep the area cool, avoid soaps and scrubbing, moisturize with a plain fragrance-free emollient, and take an oral antihistamine if it itches badly. Do not cover rashes with ointments or home remedies you have not identified - the cleaner the skin stays, the easier the rash is to assess if it worsens. Photograph a spreading rash daily - the timeline is diagnostically valuable. Moisturizing within a couple of minutes of bathing locks in the most water.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03984","question":"How fast does walking need to be to count as exercise?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03985","question":"Are energy drinks worse than coffee?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Overdo the caffeine and you may feel shaky, on edge, or notice your heart racing. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03986","question":"How often should you wash your hair?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. Daily showers are fine too, but very hot water and harsh scrubbing strip the skin's oils and can trigger dryness and itch. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03987","question":"Do I really need 8 hours of sleep?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03988","question":"Is heartburn dangerous?","answer":"Acid escaping upward onto the unprotected esophageal lining is the entire mechanism of heartburn. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03989","question":"Should I take medicine to bring a fever down?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03990","question":"Is it possible to eat too much fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03991","question":"Should I make someone throw up after swallowing a poison?","answer":"Call Poison Control (in the US, 1-800-222-1222) right away, or emergency services if the person is drowsy, struggling to breathe, or having seizures. Inducing vomiting is the classic mistake: it re-exposes the gullet and is wrong for most poisons. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03992","question":"Is it possible to eat too much fiber?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated. Sudden huge fiber jumps can cause gas and cramps, so pace yourself.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03993","question":"What happens if I do not get enough vitamin D?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. From about October to March in countries far from the equator the sun is too weak, so guidance recommends everyone take a 10 microgram (400 IU) vitamin D supplement through autumn and winter. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03994","question":"How do I clean after norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03995","question":"Do I need protein shakes or supplements?","answer":"Most adults need roughly 0.75 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight a day - about 45 grams for the average woman and 55 grams for the average man. That works out to a couple of palm-sized portions of protein foods spread across the day, and most people already eat this much without trying. Your body cannot bank spare protein as muscle - the surplus is used for energy or stored as fat. People who train hard, are pregnant, or are older may benefit from a little more, and a dietitian can advise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03996","question":"Is numbness after a sprain serious?","answer":"Inability to bear weight, visible deformity or numbness all mean it needs an examination, not home care. The same goes if swelling and pain are not clearly improving after 2 to 3 days, or if the joint feels unstable. Those signs can mean a fracture or a torn ligament rather than a simple sprain, which changes the treatment completely. Most simple sprains improve steadily within a week or two.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03997","question":"Should I cool down after exercise?","answer":"Finish hard sessions with a few minutes of progressively lighter movement - jogging becomes walking becomes easy strolling. Stopping dead after intense effort can pool blood in the legs - that is the faint, wobbly feeling. A cool-down is a good window for static stretching, when muscles are warm and pliable. Five minutes is plenty for most moderate sessions; the harder the session, the longer the taper.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03998","question":"Are sugary drinks bad for me?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-03999","question":"Does red wine protect the heart?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04000","question":"How do I know if someone is allergic to a bee sting?","answer":"Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading over the whole body mean a severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04001","question":"How do I stay hydrated with a stomach bug?","answer":"Fluid is the whole game: sip small amounts every few minutes - water, diluted juice or oral rehydration solution rather than large gulps that come back up. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04002","question":"Does walking count as moderate exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04003","question":"How many steps a day is actually enough?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. Roughly 7,000-8,000 steps captures a large share of the health benefit for most adults. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04004","question":"Should I raise the cut above my heart?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the dressing every few seconds restarts clotting - resist the urge. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04005","question":"When is fainting an emergency?","answer":"Flat on the back with legs raised is the position that restores blood flow to the brain fastest. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04006","question":"How much sugar can I eat a day?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04007","question":"Are sugary drinks bad for me?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04008","question":"What is restless legs syndrome?","answer":"Restless legs syndrome is an irresistible urge to move the legs, often with crawling or tingling feelings, that appears when resting and worsens in the evening. Moving the legs relieves it briefly, which makes settling to sleep a nightly fight. Low iron stores are sometimes involved, so it is worth seeing a doctor - several treatments work well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04009","question":"What should I do on rest days?","answer":"The workout is the stimulus; the rest day is where the improvement is manufactured. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04010","question":"Is one soda a day really a problem?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04011","question":"Can my child sleep after a head bump?","answer":"A child who cries loudly and then settles, plays normally and shows no other signs has usually had a minor bump. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Sleep after a bump is fine; what matters is that they wake and behave normally when roused. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04012","question":"What does a spacer do?","answer":"Reliever inhalers (usually blue) contain fast-acting medicine that opens the airways within minutes - they are the emergency tool. Preventer inhalers contain a low steroid dose taken every day whether or not you feel symptoms, because they work by calming the underlying inflammation. Needing the blue reliever three or more times a week is a sign the preventer routine needs adjusting. A spacer tube makes inhalers more effective - much more medicine reaches the lungs, especially in children - and a pharmacist or nurse can check your technique anytime. Rinse the mouth after using a steroid preventer to avoid throat irritation and thrush.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04013","question":"What should I do if someone faints in front of me?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Slow recovery, confusion, chest pain, exercise-related faints or a head strike all mean an ambulance.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04014","question":"Why should I swap soda for water?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04015","question":"Should I stretch before or after exercise?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. The injury-prevention case for pre-exercise stretching is not well supported; the flexibility case for regular stretching is.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04016","question":"Can I eat as much honey as I want?","answer":"Honey is natural, but nutritionally it is still sugar: your body treats honey and table sugar almost identically. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04017","question":"How do I know if I stop breathing in my sleep?","answer":"Sleep apnea is when the airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, pausing breathing for seconds at a time, all night. Classic signs: loud snoring, gasping or choking awakenings (often reported by a partner), morning headaches and overwhelming daytime sleepiness despite enough hours in bed. It is common, serious if untreated - linked with high blood pressure and heart problems - and very treatable, so ask your doctor for a sleep assessment. Excess weight, a large neck, alcohol and sleeping on the back all make apnea worse.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04018","question":"Why do women need more iron than men?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04019","question":"Is a glass of wine a day good for me?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. The World Health Organization now states that no amount of alcohol is completely safe for health. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04020","question":"What are the signs a cut is infected?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04021","question":"Why does my body react to pollen?","answer":"An allergy is the immune system treating a harmless substance - pollen, pet dander, certain foods - as a threat. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04022","question":"How does the flu vaccine work?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Annual vaccination also keeps your immunity topped up, which fades over months. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04023","question":"Is it too late to start exercising at 60?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04024","question":"What should I do with leftover antibiotics?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take. Return unused antibiotics to a pharmacy for safe disposal rather than keeping them in the cabinet.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04025","question":"Why does my asthma get worse at night?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Good control is the norm with modern treatment - frequent symptoms mean the plan needs review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04026","question":"How long should a warm-up take?","answer":"A warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of gentle movement that gradually raises your heart rate and body temperature - walking before jogging, easy pedaling before intervals. It shifts blood to the muscles, loosens joints, and lets effort feel smoother from the first hard minute. A proper warm-up lowers the strain of sudden intense effort on heart and muscles, which matters most as you get older or less trained. Save static stretching for after; warm-up time is better spent moving. If the first minutes of exercise feel unexpectedly hard, that is usually an under-warmed body talking.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04027","question":"My head feels like a tight band - what is that?","answer":"Tension headaches feel like a dull, pressing band around the head, usually on both sides, and they are the most common headache type. Stress, poor posture, screen time, skipped meals and dehydration are the usual triggers. Water, a break from screens, stretching neck and shoulders, and a simple painkiller like paracetamol or ibuprofen usually settle one. Frequent painkiller use can create medication-overuse headaches - a trap worth knowing. Regular water intake, posture breaks and sleep often reduce how often they come.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04028","question":"What should I do instead of washing chicken?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. The splash zone from rinsing chicken is a classic source of food poisoning outbreaks traced back to home kitchens. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04029","question":"Does lack of sleep affect your mood?","answer":"Sleep loss amplifies emotional reactions: the brain's alarm centers run hotter and the calming, rational control from the prefrontal cortex weakens. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04030","question":"What is the first thing to do after burning yourself?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell. Cool the burn, not the whole person - keep the rest of them warm so they do not get chilled.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04031","question":"Do antibiotics interfere with the pill?","answer":"Take antibiotics exactly as prescribed - the dose, the times, and the duration your prescriber gave - and ask them directly if you are unsure about the course length. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04032","question":"What should I do with leftover antibiotics?","answer":"Follow the prescription exactly and check with the prescriber rather than the internet when in doubt. Never share antibiotics or self-treat from leftovers: the drug may be wrong for the infection, and partial courses breed resistance. Report rashes, severe diarrhea or allergic symptoms to your doctor promptly. Many antibiotics interact with other medicines (some affect contraceptive pills), so mention everything you take.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04033","question":"What counts as strength exercise?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Bodyweight exercises, bands and lifting everyday heavy things all qualify as strengthening. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04034","question":"How do I set up my bedroom for better sleep?","answer":"Sleep hygiene is the set of habits that make good sleep likely: a cool, dark, quiet bedroom, a regular schedule, and a wind-down routine instead of screens. Keep your bed for sleep (and intimacy) only, so your brain links the bed with sleep rather than scrolling or worrying. Lying in bed frustrated trains your brain to associate the bed with wakefulness - getting up briefly breaks that link.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04035","question":"When should I use mouthwash?","answer":"Think of mouthwash as a supplement to the brush, never a substitute for it. A fluoride mouthwash is useful at a different time of day from brushing (for example after lunch) to add protection without washing off your toothpaste fluoride. Children should use alcohol-free versions, and only if recommended for their age.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04036","question":"Do I need to drink eight glasses of water a day?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour. On hot days, or when you are sweating through exercise, you need more than usual.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04037","question":"How often should I change my toothbrush?","answer":"Replace your toothbrush - or electric brush head - about every 3 months. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04038","question":"Which alcoholic drinks have the fewest calories?","answer":"Alcohol carries 7 calories per gram - almost as dense as fat - and mixers and sugars often add more on top. A pint of regular lager has around 180 to 230 calories, and a large glass of wine can top 200. Cutting back on alcohol is one of the easiest ways to trim 'invisible' calories.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04039","question":"How many teaspoons of sugar are in a can of cola?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort. Diet drinks cut the sugar and calories, but plain water remains the simplest healthy habit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04040","question":"What symptoms always need medical attention?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. Escalate when symptoms are severe or getting worse rather than better, when a fever or symptoms last much longer than expected, or when any red-flag sign appears (breathing difficulty, chest pain, severe pain, confusion, non-fading rash). People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Pharmacists triage minor illness well and cost nothing - use them as the first step. In the US, 911 or the local emergency number is for emergencies; urgent care or a same-day appointment covers most other worrying situations.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04041","question":"How can I reduce scarring?","answer":"Every time you pick a scab you reset part of the healing clock. Modern wound care keeps wounds clean, moist and covered, which heals them faster and with less scarring than letting them dry and crack. Itching during healing is normal - tapping or pressing the area beats scratching it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04042","question":"How do I stop heartburn without medicine?","answer":"Smaller portions, upright after eating, and known-trigger reduction are the evidence-backed basics. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04043","question":"Is spicy food bad for you?","answer":"For most people spicy food is perfectly healthy - chili peppers even bring vitamin C and antioxidants. Hot peppers irritate an existing ulcer or bad heartburn, but they do not create ulcers - bacteria and anti-inflammatory painkillers do that. The burning feeling comes from capsaicin, which trips heat sensors in your mouth but does not burn or damage the tissue.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04044","question":"How should I dress a minor wound at home?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. Change the dressing daily and keep the cut clean and dry while it heals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04045","question":"Do animal bites need a doctor?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Pad around anything stuck in a wound and leave it for the professionals to remove. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus or red streaks toward the heart in the following days are signs of infection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04046","question":"Why is my skin worse in winter?","answer":"Very hot water dissolves the skin's protective oil layer, leaving it tight, dry and itchy. Choose lukewarm water, keep showers short, and pat skin almost dry before applying moisturizer within a few minutes. That after-shower moisturizer is the step most people skip, and it makes the biggest difference in winter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04047","question":"Which fats are healthy?","answer":"Your body needs some fat, and the type matters: unsaturated fats - olive and rapeseed oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish - support heart health. Saturated fat pushes LDL cholesterol up, so trading butter for olive or rapeseed oil is a heart-friendly deal. The smartest move is usually to swap rather than add: replace butter with olive oil instead of pouring oil over everything.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04048","question":"What happens if I sleep 5 hours a night?","answer":"Most adults need between 7 and 9 hours of sleep a night to function well. A few rare people thrive on less, but studies show most people who sleep 6 hours or less are measurably impaired - and often don't notice it. A useful test: if you need an alarm plus caffeine to drag yourself up, and you crash on free weekends, you are running a sleep debt. Regularly sleeping under 6-7 hours is linked with worse immunity, blood pressure, mood and memory.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04049","question":"When can I go back to work after a stomach bug?","answer":"Soap and water is the only reliable defense; alcohol gel gives false comfort against norovirus. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. Follow the 48-hour rule: no work, school, or handling food for others until 48 hours after the last symptoms. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical. Anyone cleaning up vomit should open windows, wear gloves, and use paper towels disposed of in a sealed bag.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04050","question":"Is a headache a sign I need water?","answer":"Thirst, dark pee, headache and dizziness are the standard dehydration checklist. Pee color is the quickest check: pale straw is well hydrated, dark amber means you are behind. Most mild dehydration fixes itself with steady drinking over an hour or two - do not gulp a liter at once, sip regularly. Older adults and young children feel thirst later, so they dehydrate faster and need watching in hot weather or during illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04051","question":"Do I need to lift weights to be healthy?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04052","question":"Is bacon bad for you?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04053","question":"Is it bad to bounce while stretching?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. The injury-prevention case for pre-exercise stretching is not well supported; the flexibility case for regular stretching is.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04054","question":"What happens if I drink litres of water very quickly?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can dilute the salt in your blood to dangerous levels, a condition called hyponatremia. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04055","question":"Is pink eye contagious?","answer":"Very contagious by touch - hygiene beats any drops for containment. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Many schools ask for children to stay home until the discharge settles; policies vary, so check locally.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04056","question":"Should I worry if my child cries a lot after hitting their head?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor. If anything about their behavior seems wrong to you, trust that instinct and get them checked.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04057","question":"What are the symptoms of the flu?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04058","question":"How much sugar can I eat a day?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count. Most free sugars hide in soda, candy, cookies, sweetened cereal and sauces rather than the sugar bowl.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04059","question":"What makes meat 'processed'?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04060","question":"Someone collapsed and will not wake up - what do I do?","answer":"Trouble breathing, chest pain, unstoppable bleeding or an unresponsive person always justify an emergency call. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04061","question":"What should I drink during a stomach bug?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course. Sips of fluid even between vomits still count toward rehydration - persistence wins.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04062","question":"Is heartburn dangerous?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Cardiac chest pain and reflux pain can feel similar - when unsure, treat it as urgent.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04063","question":"Why do doctors recommend honey over cough syrup?","answer":"For adults and children over 1, studies suggest honey is about as effective as - and often better than - over-the-counter cough syrups at easing cough, particularly night-time symptoms. A spoonful of honey, or honey stirred into a warm (not boiling) drink, is cheap and low-risk; never give honey to babies under 1 because of infant botulism risk. Many OTC syrups have thin evidence for benefit, and combination products carry dosing-confusion risks. If a cough needs treating, treating the whole person - fluids, humidity, elevated sleeping position - usually beats any single bottle.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04064","question":"How should I blow my nose?","answer":"Blow gently, one nostril at a time, with the other closed - hard blowing pushes mucus into the sinuses and can hurt your ears. Saline rinses wash out mucus and allergens and genuinely help with congestion and hay fever; use sterile, distilled, or previously boiled and cooled water, never straight tap water. Straight tap water in nasal rinses carries a tiny but real risk of rare amoebic infections, which is why sterile or boiled water is the rule.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"train"},
  {"id":"hq-04065","question":"Which foods count as whole grains?","answer":"Whole grains keep all three parts of the grain - the bran, the germ and the starchy middle - while white flour keeps only the middle. That leaves more fiber, B vitamins, iron and magnesium in what you eat. Whole grains include oats, wholemeal or whole-wheat bread, brown rice, barley, bulgur and even popcorn.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04066","question":"How hard should I be breathing during exercise?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically. If you can sing easily, you are probably below moderate and can pick up the pace.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04067","question":"Why do I feel dizzy when I stand up?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable. Stand up slowly after lying down, keep fluids up, and eat regularly - those three habits prevent most episodes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04068","question":"How much do newborns sleep?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Waking often is normal and healthy for the first months; 'sleeping through the night' arrives gradually, often somewhere after 4-6 months. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04069","question":"My grandma says put butter on burns - is that right?","answer":"Ice and folk remedies like butter can damage the skin further or seal heat in - plain running water is the only first step that helps. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04070","question":"Is it better to stay in bed or get up when I can't sleep?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Staying in bed awake and annoyed builds a learned link between bed and frustration, so a brief calm reset elsewhere works better. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04071","question":"Do I need a vitamin D supplement?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04072","question":"What counts as activity for children?","answer":"Sixty active minutes a day, accumulated in any mix of play and sport, is the childhood guideline. Several days a week should include activities that strengthen muscle and bone: climbing, jumping, running games, ball sports. Unstructured play counts fully, and for young children it is the primary recommendation. The most effective lever is adult modeling - kids move more in families that move, and limiting recreational screen time frees the hours to do it. Short active bursts all day are exactly what children's bodies are built for.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04073","question":"How can I eat smaller portions?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Eat slowly and pause partway through - your brain needs about 20 minutes to register fullness. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04074","question":"Should I wipe down gym equipment?","answer":"Gym equipment is touched by hundreds of hands a day, so wiping machines and mats before and after use is the polite and healthy move. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04075","question":"I bumped my head and feel fine - should I still watch for something?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Urgent warning signs include being knocked out even briefly, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, unusual drowsiness or confusion, slurred speech, unequal pupil sizes, seizure, or blood or clear fluid from the ears or nose. Feeling fine right away does not rule out a delayed problem, which is why the 48-hour watch matters. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04076","question":"Why do women need more iron than men?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04077","question":"Can low iron make me tired?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04078","question":"Is bed-sharing safe with a newborn?","answer":"Babies should always be placed on their back for every sleep, day and night, on a firm, flat surface. Keep the sleep space clear: no pillows, quilts, bumpers or soft toys, and avoid overheating. Sharing a room (not a bed) with parents for the first 6 months is associated with a lower risk of SIDS. Smoking in the home and sleeping with a baby on a sofa or armchair are major risk factors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04079","question":"Do babies and children need less salt?","answer":"Adults should have no more than about 6 grams of salt a day - around one teaspoon - and the World Health Organization suggests even less, 5 grams. Children need less, and babies under 1 should have no added salt at all. Fancy salts are still salt - the crystals may look nicer, but the sodium inside is identical. Around three quarters of the salt people eat is already inside everyday foods like bread, cereal, cheese, sauces and ready meals.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04080","question":"Is heartburn dangerous?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04081","question":"Is coughing good for you?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus. Steam, warm fluids and honey (over age 1) soothe the irritated airway better than most bottles on the shelf.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04082","question":"Why does late gym keep me awake?","answer":"Exercise raises sleep pressure and lowers stress hormones, which is why active people generally sleep deeper. Timing matters for a minority: vigorous exercise within an hour or two of bed can leave some people too activated to settle. If evening sessions bother your sleep, shift them earlier or keep late workouts light - a walk rarely hurts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04083","question":"Why is it hard to breathe out when you have asthma?","answer":"Asthma is inflamed airways that narrow further with triggers, producing wheeze and breathlessness. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04084","question":"Is showering every day bad for your skin?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. Daily showers are fine too, but very hot water and harsh scrubbing strip the skin's oils and can trigger dryness and itch. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04085","question":"Is one soda a day really a problem?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Because drink sugar arrives as liquid, it is easy to drink a lot of it without feeling any less hungry. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04086","question":"What makes meat 'processed'?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04087","question":"Does vitamin C cure a cold?","answer":"There is no cure, so treatment is about comfort: rest, plenty of fluids, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches, fever and sore throat. Saline rinses, steamy showers and honey in warm water (adults and children over 1) each ease specific symptoms. Vitamin C has not been shown to shorten a cold once it has started. Combination cold products often mix the same ingredients, so check labels to avoid double-dosing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04088","question":"Why do I feel so much better after a walk?","answer":"Exercise has strong evidence behind it for lifting mood and reducing anxiety, and it is recommended alongside - not instead of - treatment for mild to moderate depression. The effect comes from a mix of brain chemistry, body temperature, accomplishment, and often being outdoors or around people. The mental health dose is modest: most benefit arrives with regular moderate activity, not heroic workouts. The catch is consistency - occasional hard sessions do little, while regular moderate movement does a lot. On low-motivation days, a 10-minute walk counts and usually begets more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04089","question":"How do I know I have influenza and not a cold?","answer":"Sudden fever plus body-wide aches and exhaustion is the flu signature. Most people feel genuinely ill for 3 to 5 days, with fatigue and cough hanging on for a week or two. The body aches come from your immune system's response to the virus, not from being 'more blocked up'. Rest, fluids and fever-reducing medication for comfort are the mainstay while it runs its course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04090","question":"Why do we get fevers when we are sick?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04091","question":"Does the sugar limit include fruit and milk?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. It is 'free sugars' that count against the limit: the kind added to products, plus honey, syrups and juice. Whole fruit and plain milk do not count.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04092","question":"What does asthma feel like?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04093","question":"Is heartburn the same as a heart problem?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04094","question":"The blister on my burn popped on its own - now what?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Growing redness, swelling or pus means it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04095","question":"Why does athlete's foot keep coming back?","answer":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection that makes the skin between the toes itchy, scaly, red and sometimes cracked. Over-the-counter antifungal creams work well if you keep using them for the full course - usually 1 to 2 weeks after the skin looks better - or it returns. It spreads through damp floors and shared towels, so dry feet well, change socks daily, and don't share footwear.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04096","question":"Why doesn't hand sanitizer work on norovirus?","answer":"Alcohol hand gel does not kill norovirus well - washing with soap and warm water is the effective method, especially after the bathroom and before food. Clean surfaces (especially the bathroom, door handles and anywhere vomit landed) with a bleach-based cleaner, and wash laundry hot. The 48-hour rule exists because people shed virus for days after feeling better. Keep the sick person's towels and utensils separate where practical.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04097","question":"I got the worst headache of my life very suddenly - what should I do?","answer":"A headache that explodes to 'worst of my life' intensity within seconds needs emergency assessment - it can signal bleeding around the brain. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04098","question":"What does acid reflux feel like?","answer":"Heartburn is a burning sensation behind the breastbone caused by stomach acid refluxing up into the esophagus, whose lining is not built for acid. It typically worsens after big meals, with bending or lying down, and with triggers like coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty food, and smoking. Occasional heartburn is common and mostly harmless, though unpleasant; frequent heartburn (several times a week for weeks) deserves a doctor's review. Note that heartburn is not a heart problem - but chest pain you cannot confidently attribute to reflux should be checked urgently.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04099","question":"What are the signs of low iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption. If you are constantly exhausted, ask a doctor for a blood test rather than guessing with supplements.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04100","question":"Which foods are high in iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Vitamin C - from orange juice, peppers or tomatoes - helps your gut absorb plant iron, while tea and coffee with meals can block it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04101","question":"Should I replace my toothbrush after being sick?","answer":"Replace your toothbrush - or electric brush head - about every 3 months. Change it sooner if the bristles splay or fray, because a worn brush cleans far worse. Many people also swap it after an illness like strep throat, as a cheap precaution.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04102","question":"Is coughing up blood serious?","answer":"Any cough lasting more than 3 weeks deserves a medical review, even if it started as an ordinary cold. Get checked promptly for coughing up blood, breathlessness, chest pain when breathing, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, or a barking cough with a whoop. Sudden severe breathlessness or coughing blood heavily is an emergency, not an appointment situation. For smokers and anyone with existing lung disease, thresholds should be lower still.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04103","question":"Do short bursts of exercise count?","answer":"Minutes accumulate - three brisk 10-minute walks in a day count the same as one 30-minute walk. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. This reframing matters most for people who currently do nothing: the first minutes carry the largest health payoff.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04104","question":"Do animal bites need a doctor?","answer":"Get medical help for cuts that are deep, gaping, longer than a couple of centimeters, or that keep bleeding after 10 to 20 minutes of firm pressure. Never pull out anything embedded in a wound - pad around it and go to urgent care, because the object may be plugging a blood vessel. Animal and human bites, dirty rusty wounds (for a tetanus check), and cuts with numbness beyond them also deserve professional care. Redness, swelling, warmth or pus in the days after a cut mean it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04105","question":"Why do doctors recommend walking so much?","answer":"Brisk walking counts fully toward moderate activity guidelines and meaningfully lowers the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and depression. It is the most accessible form of exercise: no equipment, no skill, low injury risk, and it can be chopped into small pieces across the day. Brisk means you feel warmer and breathe harder but can still talk - roughly 100 steps a minute is a common benchmark. Because it is sustainable, walking is the exercise most people actually keep doing, which matters more than any theoretical better option. A 10-minute walk after meals is a genuinely evidence-backed habit for blood sugar and mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04106","question":"Can napping at 7 pm ruin my night?","answer":"A 20-to-30-minute nap early in the afternoon refreshes most people without wrecking the night. Naps past about 30 minutes tip you into deep sleep, so you wake groggy - and late-afternoon or evening naps eat into the sleep pressure you need at bedtime. If you have trouble sleeping at night, keeping naps short and early is one of the first fixes to try.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04107","question":"How often should I wash bedding when someone is ill?","answer":"When someone at home is ill, give them their own towel, ventilate rooms regularly, and clean the shared touchpoints: door handles, light switches, remote controls, phones and taps. Wash their bedding and towels on a hot cycle, and keep their cups and cutlery separate until they recover. The high-traffic touchpoints matter more than doorknobs nobody touches - focus cleaning where hands actually go.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04108","question":"How often should I stretch to get more flexible?","answer":"Stretch when muscles are warm - after activity or a warm shower - and hold each stretch comfortably for 15 to 30 seconds without bouncing. Bouncing triggers a protective reflex that tightens the muscle you are trying to lengthen. Regular stretching several times a week does improve flexibility and range of motion over weeks. The injury-prevention case for pre-exercise stretching is not well supported; the flexibility case for regular stretching is.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04109","question":"How fast should you do chest compressions?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04110","question":"I sliced my finger on a knife and it will not stop bleeding - what do I do?","answer":"Firm, continuous pressure with something clean is the single most effective way to stop bleeding. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04111","question":"How do I stop eating when I am full?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Because fullness signals lag roughly 20 minutes behind your plate, slowing down genuinely helps you eat less. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04112","question":"How does the flu vaccine work?","answer":"Flu viruses mutate constantly, so the vaccine is reformulated each year to match the strains expected to circulate. Protection fades and the strains change, which is the honest reason for the yearly repeat. It cannot give you the flu - the injected vaccine contains inactivated virus - though mild soreness or a low fever for a day or two is common. Even when it does not prevent infection entirely, it reduces severity, complications and spread; autumn is the usual best timing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04113","question":"Is dextrose the same as sugar?","answer":"Manufacturers often spread sugar across several names - glucose, dextrose, maltose, syrups, honey - so no single one tops the ingredients list. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04114","question":"Does skipping breakfast make you gain weight?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. Research on breakfast and adult weight is genuinely mixed, so the best choice is whichever pattern keeps your energy level through the morning. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04115","question":"How long should you run a burn under cold water?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04116","question":"What happens if I drink too much caffeine?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04117","question":"How do I stop eating when I am full?","answer":"Simple tricks help: use a smaller plate, serve food in the kitchen instead of from the table, and stash leftovers before you sit down. Eat slowly and pause partway through - your brain needs about 20 minutes to register fullness. Protein and fiber make meals more filling, so you are less likely to go back for seconds.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04118","question":"Is it dangerous to drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat. For everyday life, drinking to thirst is safe; during long sweaty events, sip steadily instead of gulping.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04119","question":"Should I clean my ears with cotton buds?","answer":"Earwax is protective - it traps dust, moisturizes the canal and has antibacterial properties - and ears clean themselves as old wax works out naturally. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04120","question":"Should I put ice or butter on a burn?","answer":"Ice and folk remedies like butter can damage the skin further or seal heat in - plain running water is the only first step that helps. Ice can actually cause frostbite-type damage on already injured tissue. Once the burn has had its 20 minutes of running water, cover it loosely with cling film laid lengthways or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Do not wrap it tightly, and leave any blisters alone.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04121","question":"Is fruit juice as healthy as whole fruit?","answer":"Whole fruit is better: it brings fiber, takes longer to eat, and releases its sugar slowly. Juicing squeezes several fruits' worth of sugar into one fast-drinking glass, so blood sugar jumps instead of creeping up. Guidance caps juice at about 150 ml - one small glass - a day, and it can only count once towards your five a day. Smoothies behave the same way and are even easier to overdo, so keep them small as well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04122","question":"Does a higher fever mean a worse illness?","answer":"Fever is a defense mechanism doing its job; the illness causing it is what needs watching. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. How unwell someone seems - alertness, drinking, breathing - tells you more than the reading. A temperature of about 38C (100.4F) or higher counts as a fever when measured properly.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04123","question":"Why does afternoon coffee keep me awake?","answer":"Caffeine sticks around: roughly half of what you drank is still in your body about 5 hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon. If you want a warm evening drink, try caffeine-free tea or warm milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04124","question":"How often should I wash gym clothes?","answer":"Staph bacteria live happily on shared mats and benches; the wipe-and-wash routine is what keeps them away from your skin. Use your own towel to sit on, avoid touching your face mid-workout, and wash your hands when you finish. Re-wear sweaty clothes and you invite skin problems like folliculitis and fungal infections - wash kit after every session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04125","question":"Where do abdominal thrusts go?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04126","question":"How many hours should a 5 year old sleep?","answer":"Sleep needs fall slowly with age: preschoolers (3-5) need about 10 to 13 hours including naps, school-age children (6-12) about 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Children who regularly sleep less than their range are more likely to be irritable, struggle with learning, and behave as if hyperactive rather than sleepy. A consistent bedtime routine matters more for kids than any exact clock time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04127","question":"What symptoms always need medical attention?","answer":"Most minor illnesses follow a predictable arc - worst in the first 2 to 3 days, clearly better by the end of the week - so it is reasonable to manage them with rest, fluids and simple remedies first. The escalation triggers are: worse-not-better, lasting too long, severity out of proportion, or any red flag. People with chronic conditions, weakened immune systems, pregnancy, or very young children should set their personal threshold lower. Trust your judgment: feeling that something is genuinely wrong is itself a reason to call, and pharmacies and nurse lines are good middle steps before an appointment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04128","question":"What is vertigo exactly?","answer":"Brief lightheadedness on standing is usually a short blood-pressure dip, made more likely by dehydration, heat, hunger or certain medications. Vertigo - the sensation that you or the room is spinning - points to the inner ear, where the balance organs live. Sitting or lying down promptly prevents the dizziness from becoming a faint. Recurrent episodes, or dizziness on turning over in bed with brief spins, are worth describing to a doctor - the inner-ear causes are often very treatable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04129","question":"What makes asthma worse in winter?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. Exercise is still encouraged for people with asthma - warming up gradually and having the reliever handy usually prevents exercise-induced symptoms. Share your trigger list with your doctor - it shapes the medication plan more than you might expect.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04130","question":"My child has a fever and white patches on her throat - should I get it checked?","answer":"Features that suggest a bacterial (strep) throat are a sudden high fever, swollen and tender glands in the neck, white or pus-coated tonsils, and notably no cough. White tonsil patches alone are not conclusive, but combined with fever and no cough they warrant a check. Doctors use these signs (sometimes with a rapid swab) to decide about antibiotics rather than handing them out for every red throat. Untreated strep can occasionally lead to complications, which is why a bacterial-pattern throat deserves a real assessment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04131","question":"How quickly do you need to act on a stroke?","answer":"Check the face for drooping, ask the person to raise both arms (does one drift down?), and listen for slurred or strange speech. Any one of those failing means it is Time to call emergency services immediately, even if the symptoms seem to fade. Note the time symptoms started and share it with the paramedics - clot-busting treatment depends heavily on how long ago it began. Symptoms that vanish within minutes (a mini-stroke) still need urgent same-day assessment. Never wait to see if stroke signs pass - the treatments only work in a narrow window.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04132","question":"How often should I shower?","answer":"Most people do not need a full shower every day; washing every 2 to 3 days keeps most skin healthy, with a daily wash of armpits, groin, feet and hands. If you do shower daily, keep it short and lukewarm, and moisturize afterwards to replace what the water washes away. Wash hair when it feels greasy or looks it - for most people that is every few days, not every day.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04133","question":"Is rinsing poultry an old habit I should drop?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. The splash zone from rinsing chicken is a classic source of food poisoning outbreaks traced back to home kitchens. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04134","question":"The whole family is vomiting - what is going around?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04135","question":"Why should I not scratch a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04136","question":"Should I nap when I land after a long flight?","answer":"Jet lag is your body clock still running on the time zone you left, so sleep and wake signals arrive at the wrong local hours. It fades at roughly one day per time zone crossed; bright light at the right local times, plus shifting meals and bedtime toward the destination schedule, speeds it up. Melatonin taken in the destination's evening can help re-time the clock, and short naps (under 30 minutes) take the edge off without wrecking the first night.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04137","question":"Do I need to exercise for 10 minutes straight for it to matter?","answer":"Current guidance counts every minute of movement; the old requirement that activity come in 10-minute blocks has been dropped. Short bursts - stairs, a 5-minute walk to the store, carrying laundry - add up across the day toward the weekly target. Studies suggest accumulated short bouts deliver much of the same health benefit as continuous sessions. The jump from zero to a little movement is where the biggest health gains live. Sneaking movement into routines you already have (stairs, walking calls, getting off transit early) beats relying on motivation.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04138","question":"Is watching TV before bed bad?","answer":"Screens make sleep harder in two ways: the light tells your brain it is daytime, and whatever you watch or scroll keeps your mind switched on. People who use phones in bed tend to fall asleep later and sleep less overall. Powering down about an hour before bed - or at least switching to night mode, low brightness and calmer content - helps most people drift off sooner.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04139","question":"How long before bed should I stop drinking caffeine?","answer":"Caffeine lingers: about half of it is still in your body roughly 5 hours after you drink it. A mid-afternoon coffee can therefore still be working at lights-out, making it harder to fall asleep and lighter once you do. Most people sleep noticeably better if they finish caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04140","question":"What should I eat for breakfast to stay full until lunch?","answer":"Build breakfast around fiber plus protein - for example oatmeal with berries and nuts, or eggs with wholegrain toast. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04141","question":"Is sitting all day bad for you even if I exercise?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04142","question":"Do fried foods contain trans fats?","answer":"Trans fats are the unhealthiest fats there are - worse than saturated fat - which is why most countries have banned artificial ones. Many countries have now banned or restricted them, so they are far less common than they used to be. Check labels for 'partially hydrogenated oil', and go easy on hard margarines, deep-fried takeout and packaged baked goods.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04143","question":"How long does it take for exercise to become a habit?","answer":"Enjoyable, scheduled and small is the recipe that makes exercise stick. Anchoring movement to existing routines - walking after lunch, stretching while the coffee brews - survives motivation dips far better than willpower plans. Track consistency rather than performance; a 15-minute walk kept on a streak beats a skipped perfect workout. Missing a day is normal and harmless; missing the re-start is what actually ends routines. Identity helps more than goals: you are someone who moves daily, and the workouts are just evidence.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04144","question":"How should I dress a minor wound at home?","answer":"Once bleeding has stopped, rinse the cut under clean running water to wash out dirt - this does more good than any antiseptic. Pat the skin around it dry, leave the wound itself alone, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Alcohol and undiluted antiseptic sting and can damage healthy tissue, so keep them for the skin around the wound if at all. A fresh dressing once a day keeps the wound clean while it closes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04145","question":"How much caffeine is safe per day?","answer":"For most adults, up to 400 mg of caffeine a day is fine - roughly 4 mugs of instant coffee or 2 to 3 mugs of filter coffee. A mug of tea has around 75 mg, a can of cola about 40 mg, and a 250 ml energy drink about 80 mg. Too much caffeine can leave you jittery, anxious, with a racing heart and poor sleep. Pregnant women are advised to stay under 200 mg a day - about 2 mugs of instant coffee.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04146","question":"How long do I press on a bleeding cut?","answer":"Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze and keep it there for a solid 10 minutes without peeking. If you can, raise the injured part above the level of the heart to slow the blood flow. Lifting the cloth early is the most common reason bleeding seems to never stop. Once it has stopped, keep a clean dressing on it for support and protection. If blood soaks through, add another cloth on top rather than peeling the first one off.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04147","question":"My dad has chest pain and is sweating - what should I do?","answer":"Chest pressure with sweating, nausea or pain radiating to arm or jaw is a presumed heart attack until proven otherwise. Wait for the ambulance rather than driving - paramedics can start treatment on the spot. Have the person sit down, stay calm and stop all exertion while help is coming. Heart attack pain is not always severe or classic; pressure, tightness or indigestion-like discomfort in a high-risk person deserves the same emergency call. Emergency services may advise chewing an aspirin while help is on the way - only follow their instruction.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04148","question":"When should I use an adrenaline auto-injector?","answer":"Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, faintness and widespread hives together mean anaphylaxis. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04149","question":"What is the healthiest way to lose weight?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. Around half a kilogram (about a pound) a week is a realistic pace, and slower losses are the ones that stick. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04150","question":"What should I look for when buying canned fruit?","answer":"Frozen fruit and vegetables are picked ripe and frozen within hours, so they hold on to their vitamins very well. Canned is fine too - choose fruit canned in juice rather than syrup, and vegetables with no added salt. Frozen options are often cheaper, cut food waste, and mean there is always a vegetable ready in the freezer.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04151","question":"Does a standing desk count as being active?","answer":"Long uninterrupted sitting carries its own health risks that a single daily workout does not fully erase. Standing or moving briefly every 30 to 60 minutes - a lap of the room, a few stairs, a 2-minute stretch - measurably improves blood sugar and circulation. A standing desk helps, but alternating positions beats standing motionless for hours. The practical pattern is exercise plus frequent breaks: the two protect different systems.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04152","question":"How can I tell if I have a cold or the flu?","answer":"A cold builds over a day or two: sore throat, sneezing, runny nose, mild tiredness, usually no high fever. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04153","question":"How much water do adults need daily?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. You do not need every drink to be plain water - milk, tea and foods like soup and fruit add to your fluid total too. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04154","question":"Why do I sleep better after a day outdoors?","answer":"Bright light soon after waking is the strongest signal that sets your internal clock for the day. Morning daylight tells the clock 'morning is here', which times evening melatonin release properly that night. Indoor light is usually far weaker than a cloudy sky, so open the curtains or step outside rather than relying on lamps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04155","question":"How many puffs can I take in an asthma attack?","answer":"Upright posture, one puff every 30-60 seconds up to 10, then reassess - that is the core sequence. If there is no improvement after 10 puffs, or the person is getting worse, too breathless to speak in sentences, or turning pale or blue, call emergency services immediately. You can repeat the 10-puff cycle while waiting for the ambulance. An attack that needs more reliever than usual, or reliever that stops working as well as it used to, is a warning to get the asthma plan reviewed.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04156","question":"Do step counts even matter?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. Roughly 7,000-8,000 steps captures a large share of the health benefit for most adults. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04157","question":"Is Vaseline good for wounds?","answer":"For minor cuts and scrapes, keeping the wound moist is the goal - a thin layer of petroleum jelly does that fine. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04158","question":"What is the poison control number?","answer":"The poison specialists will tell you within minutes what is needed - faster and safer than guessing at home. Do NOT make them vomit - it can burn the gullet twice if the substance is corrosive and does not help for most swallowed substances. Have the container or plant in hand so you can read the name and amount to the specialist. Do not give food, milk or water unless the specialist tells you to, and keep the person sitting up and talking if they are drowsy. Prevention beats treatment: store medications and cleaning products locked and out of children's reach.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04159","question":"Are protein bars and cereals ultra-processed?","answer":"Ultra-processed foods are industrial products built from refined ingredients plus additives you would not find in a home kitchen - think sodas, candy, packaged cookies, instant noodles and many ready meals. Studies link diets high in them with weight gain and higher rates of several diseases, though the research is still developing. 'Processed' is not automatically bad - frozen vegetables, canned beans, wholegrain bread and plain yogurt are processed too, and they are healthy staples. A practical rule: cook from basic ingredients most of the time and treat ultra-processed food as an occasional convenience.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04160","question":"Can I exercise every day?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Signs you need more recovery include stalling performance, persistent fatigue, poor sleep, and irritability.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04161","question":"How do I wash my hands properly?","answer":"Wet your hands, apply soap, and lather every surface - palms, backs, between fingers, thumbs, fingertips and under nails - for about 20 seconds. Rinse well under running water, then dry thoroughly, because wet hands pick up and transfer germs more easily than dry ones. Soap matters: it lifts dirt and microbes off the skin so the rinse carries them away; water alone does not do this.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04162","question":"What is DOMS?","answer":"Delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is the achiness that peaks 24 to 72 hours after exercise your body is not used to, especially lengthening movements like downhill walking or lowering weights. It comes from microscopic muscle-fiber disruption as the muscle adapts - it is a normal training response, not a sign of damage or of a workout being 'better'. Gentle movement, light stretching, warmth and sleep all ease it; complete stillness usually makes it stiffen worse. It fades in a few days, and the same exercise rarely causes the same soreness again until you raise the difficulty. Ease back in with light activity during DOMS rather than resting completely or repeating the same hard session.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04163","question":"How long will a sting stay swollen?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04164","question":"If I only drink tea and coffee all day, am I still hydrated?","answer":"For most adults, about 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day is a sensible target. Water is the best everyday choice, but milk, tea, coffee and moist foods like soup and fruit all count towards the total. Your body is the best guide: drink when you are thirsty, and aim for pee that is a pale straw colour.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04165","question":"How much exercise should I get each week?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. The weekly target has two halves: aerobic minutes and 2 strength days - both count toward health. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04166","question":"What are sleep cycles?","answer":"Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, moving through light sleep, deep sleep and REM (dream) sleep. The first half of the night is deep-sleep-heavy for body repair; the second half is REM-heavy for dreams and emotional sorting. Waking mid-cycle leaves you groggier, which is one reason a consistent schedule beats constantly shifting bedtimes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04167","question":"Why does my heartburn get worse at night?","answer":"The biggest levers are smaller meals, cutting your personal triggers (commonly coffee, alcohol, fatty and spicy food), and staying upright for 2 to 3 hours after eating. Raising the head of the bed 10 to 20 centimeters, or using a wedge pillow, reduces night-time reflux - stacking extra pillows alone just kinks the neck. Losing excess weight around the middle takes direct pressure off the stomach and often reduces reflux noticeably. Antacids neutralize acid on contact and work fast for episodes; if you need them most days for weeks, see a doctor about ongoing treatment.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04168","question":"Should I pee on a jellyfish sting?","answer":"Rinse the area with seawater - not fresh water, which can make the stinging cells fire even more - and definitely skip the urine folklore. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Whether vinegar helps depends on the species, which is why local advice beats folklore.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04169","question":"Is a midnight snack unhealthy?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. Leaving about 2 to 3 hours between a large meal and bed is a comfortable rule of thumb.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04170","question":"Should I bundle up when I have a fever?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Offer fluids regularly; fever increases fluid loss through sweat. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04171","question":"How does an EpiPen work?","answer":"Anaphylaxis signs are breathing difficulty, swollen lips or tongue, faintness and widespread hives. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04172","question":"What is more important for weight loss - diet or exercise?","answer":"Lasting weight loss comes from a small, steady calorie gap: smaller portions, more fiber and protein, fewer sugary drinks and less alcohol, plus more daily movement. The diets that keep weight off are ones you could still be following in five years; extreme rules almost always bounce back. Fad diets work briefly because they cut calories, but most people regain the weight when they stop - changes you can live with win. If you have a lot of weight to lose or other health conditions, a doctor or dietitian can tailor a plan.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04173","question":"Do canned and frozen vegetables count towards five a day?","answer":"The standard advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and vegetables a day, roughly 400 grams in total. Any mix of fruit and veg works, and five portions is the floor, not the target to stop at. Fresh, frozen, canned and dried all count towards the total. Different colours carry different vitamins, so eat a rainbow across the week.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04174","question":"What is RICE for a sprain?","answer":"For the first couple of days follow the basics: rest the joint, apply a cloth-wrapped ice pack for 15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours, use a snug compression bandage, and keep the foot raised above hip height when sitting. Ice straight on bare skin can cause cold injury, so always wrap it. Heat, alcohol, running and massage are the four things to skip in the first couple of days. Gentle movement as pain allows usually speeds recovery more than total rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04175","question":"How long should a home strength workout be?","answer":"A beginner home session can be as simple as squats to a chair, wall or knee push-ups, step-ups on a stair, and rows with a resistance band. Two rounds of 8 to 12 repetitions each, taking the sets close to fatigue, covers the guideline for that day. Ten to fifteen focused minutes is genuinely enough when done twice a week with effort. Everyday load-bearing - carrying groceries, hauling a vacuum upstairs, digging in the garden - counts as resistance work too.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04176","question":"When does dehydration need a hospital?","answer":"No urine in 8 hours, sunken eyes or new confusion are the stop-waiting signs. Babies and older adults dehydrate faster and can deteriorate quickly, so they cross the urgent threshold sooner than healthy adults. A baby with a sunken fontanelle (soft spot on the head), no wet diapers for hours, or crying without tears needs same-day care. Confusion in an older person during a vomiting illness is dehydration until a doctor says otherwise.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04177","question":"Why am I constipated all the time?","answer":"The three levers are fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, prunes), fluids, and daily movement - together they fix most constipation. Fiber works by holding water in the stool, which is exactly why fiber without water can make things worse. Holding it in dries the stool further - respond to the urge promptly. If lifestyle change is not enough after a couple of weeks, a pharmacist can suggest a gentle laxative such as a fiber supplement or macrogol.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04178","question":"Does caffeine affect sleep?","answer":"Caffeine lingers - about half the amount you drank is still circulating five hours later. An afternoon coffee can easily still be working at bedtime, making it harder to fall asleep and shaving quality off the sleep you do get. Most people sleep better if they finish their caffeine by early-to-mid afternoon.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04179","question":"Why does the stomach flu hit so fast?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics target bacteria, so they are useless here; fluids do the real work. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04180","question":"How much sleep does a 70 year old need?","answer":"The need for sleep barely changes with age - what changes is how easily the brain holds deep sleep. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04181","question":"How can I prevent nightmares in children?","answer":"Nightmares are a normal part of childhood - most kids have them, especially between ages 3 and 8, often in the second half of the night. The best response is calm comfort: a hug, reassurance that it was a dream, and back to bed; keeping the room dim and settling quietly avoids fully waking them. Stressful events, scary shows and poor sleep raise nightmare frequency, so a steady routine helps. If nightmares are near-nightly, involve injuries, or leave a child afraid to sleep, bring it up with a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04182","question":"Is raw honey better for you?","answer":"Once honey reaches your bloodstream it is sugar like any other, so treat it as an occasional sweetener rather than a health food. It does carry trace antioxidants, but you would need to eat far too much honey for them to matter. Never give honey to babies under 1 year old, because it can carry spores that cause infant botulism. Raw honey is not safer than regular honey for babies - the botulism risk is the same.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04183","question":"Why is processed meat worse than fresh meat?","answer":"Processed meat is meat preserved by smoking, curing or salting, or with added preservatives - bacon, ham, salami, hot dogs and many sausages. Eating processed meat is linked to a higher risk of bowel cancer, which is why guidance says eat as little of it as you can. Fresh red meat is less of a concern than processed meat, but both are worth moderating. Swapping bacon for eggs, beans or fish a few mornings a week is one of the easiest upgrades.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04184","question":"Do I need calcium supplements?","answer":"Aim for about 700 mg daily - roughly three portions of dairy or fortified alternatives spread across the day. Dairy is the classic source, but calcium-fortified plant drinks, tofu set with calcium, sardines or salmon with soft bones, tahini, almonds and leafy greens like kale all help. Vitamin D helps your body absorb calcium, so the two work as a team.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04185","question":"Does exercise help you sleep better?","answer":"Consistent daytime activity deepens sleep for most people within a few weeks. Timing matters for some people: very vigorous exercise right before bed can leave you alert, so finish it about 1 to 2 hours before lights out. Gentle evening movement - stretching, an easy walk - does not cause the same problem and can be calming. Better sleep in turn makes exercise easier, so the two habits reinforce each other.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04186","question":"How many steps a day is actually enough?","answer":"The 10,000-step figure traces back to a 1960s Japanese pedometer campaign, not to physiology. Large studies find steep health benefits up to around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, with further but smaller gains beyond. The honest framing: more is generally better up to a point, the jump from 2,000 to 5,000 daily steps is enormous, and 10,000 is a fine aspiration rather than a cliff edge. Step trackers motivate, but treat their goals as tools, not verdicts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04187","question":"What is CBT for insomnia?","answer":"Insomnia means regularly struggling to fall asleep, stay asleep, or waking far too early despite having the chance to sleep. It is called chronic when it happens at least 3 nights a week for 3 months or more and drags down your daytime functioning. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended before medication, because it fixes the habits and worry loops that keep insomnia alive. See a doctor if poor sleep is wrecking your days - you do not have to white-knuckle it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04188","question":"Is antiperspirant bad for you?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Claims linking antiperspirant ingredients to cancer have been tested and do not hold up; the safety record is long and clean. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04189","question":"How do I use an EpiPen?","answer":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction: swollen lips, tongue or throat, difficulty breathing or noisy wheeze, dizziness or collapse, and hives spreading across the body. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. The rule is simple: adrenaline first, emergency call always - even after successful adrenaline use. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04190","question":"Why do we cough?","answer":"Coughing is housekeeping for the lungs - it exists to move things out. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04191","question":"What should I drink during a stomach bug?","answer":"Mouthfuls every few minutes beat big drinks; rehydration solution is the ideal fluid when losses are heavy. Eat as tolerated once vomiting settles; the old 'brat diet only' advice has given way to just eating what you can keep down. Seek medical care for signs of dehydration (dizziness, no urine for 8 hours, sunken eyes), blood in vomit or stool, severe abdominal pain, or symptoms lasting beyond a few days. Babies, elderly relatives and pregnant women should be checked earlier in the course.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04192","question":"Can I take an antihistamine for a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Scratching turns a clean sting into an infected one - keep nails short and hands busy. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04193","question":"Someone cannot breathe or speak because they are choking - what do I do?","answer":"Lean them forward and give up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If that has not cleared it, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts: fists just above the belly button, pulled sharply inward and upward. Alternate 5 back blows and 5 thrusts until the object comes out or the person collapses. Do not fish blindly in the mouth with fingers; if thrusts fail, emergency services take over. If the person collapses, call emergency services (on speaker) and start CPR.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04194","question":"Why is balance training important for older adults?","answer":"Adaptation does not retire: muscle and bone respond to load well past 80. For older adults, the highest-value additions are strength work twice a week plus balance practice (standing on one foot, heel-to-toe walking), which directly cut fall risk. Starting later still reduces heart disease, diabetes, depression and disability risk - the payoff begins within weeks of starting. The right starting point is gentle and progressive: chair exercises, water aerobics and walking are common and effective entry points. A doctor or physiotherapist can tailor a safe starting plan around existing conditions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04195","question":"Why should I not scratch a sting?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Swelling that stays local for 24 to 48 hours is a routine reaction; rapid spreading or breathing trouble is not. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable. If swelling keeps creeping outward over several hours, show it to a doctor or pharmacist.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04196","question":"Why am I so irritable when tired?","answer":"After a sleepless night, the emotional centers of the brain overreact and the rational brakes work less well. That is why a bad night turns small annoyances into big ones and makes worries loom larger. One or two good nights usually restore most of it, which is why sleep is the first lever for mood.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04197","question":"How do I know if I am working hard enough?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04198","question":"Does honey help a sore throat?","answer":"Viral sore throats need comfort measures and time, not antibiotics. Warm drinks with honey, rest, and paracetamol or ibuprofen for the pain do more than most sprays and lozenges. Dissolving half a teaspoon of salt in a glass of warm water and gargling can ease the raw feeling. Dry indoor air and mouth-breathing at night make mornings worse - a humidifier or simply more fluids often helps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04199","question":"How do I avoid injury when starting a new sport?","answer":"Start below what feels like your limit and increase gradually - roughly 10 percent more per week is a safe pace for most people. Walking is the best re-entry activity: self-paced, joint-friendly, and easy to measure. Chest pain, dizziness or pain that alters your gait are all stop-and-check signals, not badges of effort. Talk to a doctor first if you have heart disease symptoms, uncontrolled blood pressure, diabetes complications, or joint problems - for most people, starting gentle exercise is safer than not starting.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04200","question":"What should I do after using a tissue?","answer":"The elbow works because you rarely shake hands with it or rub your eyes with it. Your hands are the perfect vehicle for germs: they touch door handles, phones and other people all day, which is why coughing into them spreads illness. A sneeze can launch droplets several meters, so covering one is protecting everyone downwind.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04201","question":"Why do I keep catching colds?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04202","question":"Why is washing chicken bad?","answer":"Do not wash raw chicken: running water splashes campylobacter and other bacteria up to half a meter around the sink, onto surfaces and ready-to-eat food. The splash zone from rinsing chicken is a classic source of food poisoning outbreaks traced back to home kitchens. Pat the chicken dry with paper towel if needed, cook it thoroughly, and wash your hands and anything the raw juices touched.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04203","question":"Is breakfast the most important meal of the day?","answer":"Breakfast is a useful meal for many people - it is a chance for fiber and protein, and children tend to concentrate better at school when they have eaten. For adults, the evidence on weight is mixed: some studies favor breakfast eaters, others find little difference. If you skip breakfast and feel fine, that is a reasonable choice; if you raid the snack drawer mid-morning, a balanced breakfast may steady you. If you do eat breakfast, make it fiber plus protein - like oatmeal with nuts or eggs on wholegrain toast - rather than sugary cereal.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04204","question":"How do I know if a head injury is serious?","answer":"Put a wrapped cold pack on the bump for 15 to 20 minutes, then keep watch for the next 48 hours. Watch the person for two days: any repeated vomiting, growing confusion or unusual sleepiness means emergency care. Any of those needs emergency assessment, even if they appear hours later. Mild headache and a tender lump that slowly improve are common after minor bumps.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04205","question":"What is the difference between a dry and chesty cough?","answer":"A cough is the airways' clearing mechanism: it expels mucus, dust and irritants, protecting the lungs. A chesty (productive) cough moves phlegm up and out, while a dry cough is irritation without much to clear - which is why the two are treated differently. After a cold, airways stay twitchy for days to weeks, which is why a lingering cough is usually harmless residue rather than ongoing infection. Suppressants can help a dry cough disturb sleep less, but stopping a productive cough entirely can trap mucus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04206","question":"My doctor refused antibiotics for my cold - why?","answer":"Colds are caused by viruses - more than 200 different ones, with rhinoviruses the most common. They spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces then your nose or eyes. Antibiotics will not shorten a cold by a day; taking them when unneeded fuels antibiotic resistance. Frequent handwashing and keeping hands away from your face are the best prevention. Adults average 2 to 3 colds a year, and children more - it is normal, not a weak immune system.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04207","question":"How much fiber do I need a day?","answer":"Most adults need about 30 grams of fiber a day, but the average person gets only around 20. Easy ways to add fiber: choose wholegrain bread and cereal, stir beans or lentils into dishes, keep the skin on fruit and vegetables, and snack on nuts. Increase fiber gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water, otherwise you may feel bloated.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04208","question":"How long should you run a burn under cold water?","answer":"Hold the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Running water carries heat out of the skin and limits how deep the burn goes, so it is the single most useful first step. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04209","question":"Will my food taste bland if I cut salt?","answer":"Salt makes your body retain water, and that extra fluid raises the pressure inside your blood vessels. High blood pressure strains the heart and blood vessels and raises the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Your taste buds adjust within a few weeks of cutting back, and herbs, spices, lemon and pepper bring flavor without sodium.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04210","question":"How can I tell if I have a cold or the flu?","answer":"Gradual sniffles point to a cold; sudden fever and aches point to flu. Flu arrives like a switch being flipped - sudden fever, chills, deep body aches, headache and exhaustion that makes even getting up feel hard. Both are viruses and both get better on their own, but flu is more likely to develop complications and keep you in bed for days. If you are truly flattened rather than just sniffly, flu is the likelier culprit.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04211","question":"Do deodorants cause cancer?","answer":"Deodorant covers odor with fragrance and antibacterials; antiperspirant actually reduces sweating by briefly plugging sweat ducts with aluminum salts. Claims linking antiperspirant ingredients to cancer have been tested and do not hold up; the safety record is long and clean. Apply either to clean, dry skin at night for many products works best, since you sweat less then and the active ingredients hold better.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04212","question":"Should I worry if my child cries a lot after hitting their head?","answer":"Big cry, then back to normal play, is reassuring - but keep checking in over the next two days. Comfort them, offer a wrapped cold pack, and let them sleep if they settle - sleeping is fine as long as they rouse normally. Watch for 48 hours for repeated vomiting, unusual drowsiness or floppiness, irritability you cannot settle, unequal pupils, or any loss of consciousness. Babies under 1, falls from a significant height, or any of those warning signs mean the child should be seen by a doctor.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04213","question":"How do I rehydrate after vomiting?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04214","question":"How often should I do strength training?","answer":"Guidelines call for muscle-strengthening activity on at least 2 days a week, working all the major muscle groups: legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms. Weights are one option, but bodyweight moves (squats, push-ups, lunges), resistance bands, and heavy gardening all count. Strength work protects bone density, keeps metabolism steadier with age, and prevents the frailty that makes later life smaller. It is the half of the exercise guidelines most people skip - and the one with the steepest benefits once you start. Leave a rest day between strength sessions for the same muscles to recover.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04215","question":"How sleepy is too sleepy to drive?","answer":"Being awake for 17-19 hours impairs driving about as much as being over the legal alcohol limit in many countries. Very sleepy drivers experience microsleeps - 2-to-10-second blackouts they often don't notice - which at motorway speed means traveling blind for hundreds of meters. If you notice heavy eyelids, drifting, or missed signs, stop and rest; caffeine helps briefly but is not a substitute for sleep.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04216","question":"How do I help a choking adult at dinner?","answer":"Coughing is the body's own first aid for choking, so let it work while it is working. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. If the cough becomes silent or ineffective, or they cannot speak at all, move straight to back blows and abdominal thrusts.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04217","question":"Can a fan help me sleep better?","answer":"White noise works by masking sudden sounds - traffic, snoring, household noise - that would otherwise punch through light sleep. A fan, an app, or a dedicated machine all do the job; keep the volume moderate rather than blasting. For babies, place the machine away from the cot and at low volume, since loud sound close to small ears is not advised.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04218","question":"Is it bad to eat too fast?","answer":"Eating very fast tends to mean eating more, because you finish the plate before your body has signaled that it is full. Fullness signals take around 20 minutes to catch up, so fast eaters overshoot easily. Put the fork down between bites, chew properly, and let a meal last 20 minutes or more.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04219","question":"Someone collapsed after a wasp sting - is this an emergency?","answer":"Anaphylaxis signs are breathing difficulty, swollen lips or tongue, faintness and widespread hives. Call emergency services immediately - this can become life-threatening within minutes. If the person carries an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style), use it into the outer thigh right away and hold it in place as instructed. Lay them down with legs raised if dizzy, and stay with them until help arrives; a second pen dose may be given if there is no improvement after 5 minutes and the instructions allow it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04220","question":"What is the difference between use-by and best-before?","answer":"'Use by' is a safety date: food past it can be dangerous even if it looks and smells fine, because the bacteria that cause food poisoning often produce no smell. 'Best before' is about quality - bread, canned goods and dry foods are usually still safe after it, just past their peak. Your nose can detect spoilage, but it cannot detect the bugs that actually poison you, so the use-by date is the one to trust.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04221","question":"When should a headache after hitting my head worry me?","answer":"Sudden maximum-intensity headache is the single most urgent headache pattern there is. Headache with fever and a stiff neck, headache with new weakness, slurred speech, confusion or vision loss, and headache after a head injury are also emergencies. A new, different headache in someone over 50, or one that wakes you nightly, deserves prompt medical review rather than self-care. Everything else - band-like, gradual, with clear stress or dehydration triggers - is usually manageable with the basics. Trust a change in pattern: a headache that is genuinely different from your usual deserves a closer look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04222","question":"What triggers asthma attacks?","answer":"The most common triggers are respiratory infections, pollen and dust mites, pet dander, cold air, smoke, and exercise. Identifying your personal triggers lets you prevent flare-ups instead of just treating them - an asthma diary or app helps spot the pattern. Smoke (including vaping and secondhand) is one of the most damaging triggers and should be avoided completely. People with asthma should exercise; a warm-up and reliever nearby manage most exercise symptoms.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04223","question":"Is fragmented morning sleep restful?","answer":"Snoozing chops the last stretch of sleep into 9-minute scraps of the lightest, least refreshing kind. Those fragments are too short to be restorative but long enough to deepen grogginess - the 'broken' feeling after snoozing is real. Put the alarm across the room, keep one fixed wake time, and let morning light do the rest.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04224","question":"Is asthma a permanent condition?","answer":"In asthma, the airways are chronically inflamed and tighten further during flare-ups, narrowing the passage for air. That shows up as wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and coughing - classically worse at night or early morning, and triggered by exercise, allergens, cold air or infections. Asthma is a long-term condition that flares and settles, but with the right inhaler routine most people live completely normal lives. Untreated, it can become dangerous, so symptoms that are frequent or worsening always deserve medical review.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04225","question":"How much exercise should I get each week?","answer":"The standard guideline is at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity activity a week, or 75 minutes of vigorous activity, or a mix of the two. Spreading it over most days works better than one giant session - even 20 to 30 minutes on 5 days clears the bar. Weekend-warrior patterns still beat nothing, but spreading activity lowers injury risk and keeps energy steadier. Adults should add muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week on top of the minutes. Any amount is better than none; the guideline is a target, not a pass/fail exam.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04226","question":"How fast should you do chest compressions?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Push to the beat of 'Stayin' Alive' - roughly 100-120 compressions per minute. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04227","question":"Can I make my own rehydration drink?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04228","question":"What should I look for on a nutrition label?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Compare foods per 100 grams rather than per serving, because serving sizes are chosen to flatter the product. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04229","question":"Is a blister on a burn a bad sign?","answer":"Leave the blister intact; breaking it opens the door to infection. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. See a doctor if the area becomes increasingly red, swollen, painful, or oozes yellow-green pus.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04230","question":"How many units are in a pint of beer?","answer":"One unit is 10 ml of pure alcohol; a single 25 ml measure of spirits is 1 unit. A pint of average-strength beer (4-5%) holds about 2.3 to 2.8 units, and a 175 ml glass of wine is about 2.3 units. Because glass sizes and drink strengths vary, checking the label or a unit calculator beats guessing.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04231","question":"What should I do if someone faints in front of me?","answer":"Lay the person down and raise their legs above heart level to send blood back to the brain, and loosen tight collars or belts. Most people wake within a minute or two and feel washed out but otherwise fine. Do not crowd them, splash water or prop them sitting up straight away. Call emergency services if they do not recover quickly, are confused afterward, fainted during exercise or with chest pain, are pregnant or older with other conditions, or if they hit their head falling.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04232","question":"I touched a hot pan and my fingers hurt - what should I do first?","answer":"Cool the burn under cool or lukewarm running water for 20 full minutes, starting as soon as you can. The water draws the heat out of the skin, which is what limits the damage. It still helps even if you start a few minutes late. Take off rings or watches near the area early, before the skin starts to swell.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04233","question":"How much red meat should I eat a week?","answer":"Red meat such as beef, lamb and pork supplies protein and iron, but many people eat more of it than they need. If you eat more than about 90 grams of red or processed meat a day, guidance suggests cutting down towards 70 grams. Simple swaps make that easy: a couple of meat-free days a week, chicken or fish some nights, or smaller portions.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04234","question":"What can I eat for breakfast when I am in a hurry?","answer":"Build breakfast around fiber plus protein - for example oatmeal with berries and nuts, or eggs with wholegrain toast. Sugary cereal or pastries spike your blood sugar and leave you hungry again within a couple of hours. Short on time? Overnight oats made the night before, a banana with peanut butter, or wholegrain toast all travel well.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04235","question":"How much toothpaste should a toddler use?","answer":"Children need adult help with brushing until about age 7 or 8, when their hand control is good enough to do it alone under supervision. Use a smear of fluoride toothpaste (at least 1000 ppm) under age 3, and a pea-sized amount from 3 to 6. Take a child to the dentist by their first tooth or first birthday, whichever comes first, so visits are normal rather than scary.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04236","question":"What are the signs of anaphylaxis?","answer":"Breathing difficulty, throat or tongue swelling, faintness and widespread hives together mean anaphylaxis. It is a life-threatening emergency - call emergency services immediately, even if symptoms then seem to improve. Use an adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen style) into the outer thigh without delay if one is available; adrenaline is the only effective treatment. Lay the person flat with legs raised if dizzy (or sit them up if breathing is hard), and give a second dose after 5 minutes if there is no improvement and the device allows.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04237","question":"What do I do if a jellyfish stings me?","answer":"Seawater rinse, gentle tentacle removal and a hot-water soak are the mainstream steps. Remove any tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, without rubbing the skin. Soaking in hot (not scalding) water for 20 to 45 minutes eases the pain for most stings. Vinegar helps certain tropical species like box jellyfish but can worsen others, so follow local beach guidance where you swim.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04238","question":"Does red wine protect the heart?","answer":"Guidance in many countries caps drinking at about 14 units a week - roughly 6 glasses of wine or 6 pints of average-strength beer - spread over 3 or more days with several drink-free days. The old idea that a daily drink protects the heart has weakened as research methods improved; the less you drink, the lower your risk. Major health bodies now say no level of alcohol is completely risk-free - less is better, and none is best of all. If you drink, keeping some weeks entirely alcohol-free is one of the easiest ways to cut down.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04239","question":"What is oral rehydration solution?","answer":"ORS packets rehydrate fastest and restore the salts lost with diarrhea and vomiting. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Sip small amounts frequently - a mouthful every few minutes survives a queasy stomach better than big gulps. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04240","question":"How much do newborns sleep?","answer":"Newborns sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, but in short stretches of 2 to 4 hours because their tiny stomachs need frequent feeding. Night waking in young babies is developmental, not a problem to fix - feeding needs and immature body clocks drive it. Parents cope best by sleeping when the baby sleeps, taking shifts, and asking for help.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04241","question":"What is the difference between moderate and vigorous activity?","answer":"The simplest gauge is the talk test: during moderate activity you can hold a conversation but would struggle to sing. During vigorous activity you can manage only a few words at a time - think running, fast cycling, intervals, or swimming laps. Brisk walking, easy cycling on flat ground, doubles tennis and heavy cleaning are solidly moderate for most people. Intensity is relative to fitness, so a hill that is moderate for one person is vigorous for another - the talk test adapts automatically.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04242","question":"Do beans really have protein?","answer":"Good protein sources include eggs, fish, chicken, lean meat, milk, yogurt, cheese, beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and seeds. Beans, lentils, tofu, nuts and whole grains add up to plenty of protein over a day, with extra fiber as a bonus. You do not need to combine plant proteins perfectly at every meal - a varied day adds up.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04243","question":"The sting site is still swollen the next day - is that normal?","answer":"For ordinary stings, a wrapped cold pack and an oral antihistamine take care of most of the itch and swelling. Some redness and swelling for a day or two is a normal local reaction, not a sign of allergy, as long as it is not spreading far beyond the site. Try not to scratch - breaking the skin invites infection on top of the sting. A doctor or pharmacist can suggest pain relief or stronger antihistamines if it is uncomfortable.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04244","question":"When does a fever become an emergency for adults?","answer":"For adults: above 39.4C, beyond 3 days, or plus stiff neck, rash or confusion are the thresholds for care. Fever together with a stiff neck, severe headache, an unusual rash, confusion, chest pain or trouble breathing is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation. Adults on immune-suppressing medication or with chronic conditions should call a doctor earlier. A mild fever with ordinary cold symptoms in a healthy adult rarely needs anything beyond rest and fluids.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04245","question":"Why does my body react to pollen?","answer":"Allergy symptoms are your immune system misidentifying something harmless as an invader. That triggers the release of histamine and other chemicals, which cause itching, sneezing, watery eyes, rashes and swelling. The symptoms are the immune overreaction, not damage done by the substance itself. This mechanism explains why antihistamines - medicines that block histamine - relieve so many allergy symptoms. Triggers can be airborne (pollen, dander), touched (latex), injected (stings) or eaten (foods), with reactions ranging from mild to severe.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04246","question":"What are the signs of low iron?","answer":"Iron-rich foods include red meat, beans, lentils, fortified breakfast cereals, nuts and dark leafy greens. Women who have periods lose iron each month and need more than men, and low iron is a very common cause of tiredness. Pair plant iron with vitamin C: a squeeze of lemon or a small glass of orange juice noticeably improves absorption.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04247","question":"Can I make my own rehydration drink?","answer":"When you lose fluid through vomiting or diarrhea, you lose salts as well as water, and oral rehydration solution (ORS) from a pharmacy replaces both in the right balance. Plain water rehydrates too, but more slowly in gut-upset situations, and it does not restore lost salts. Small, frequent sips are the technique that works when the stomach is irritated. Sports drinks are a reasonable substitute in a pinch but are sweeter and saltier-profiled for athletes, not illness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04248","question":"Why is vitamin D important?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04249","question":"How can I quickly judge if a food is healthy?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04250","question":"Why do we get fevers when we are sick?","answer":"A fever is your immune system turning up body temperature to help fight infection - it is a response, not a disease. The number on the thermometer matters less than how the person looks and feels. Treating the discomfort with paracetamol or ibuprofen is reasonable; forcing a normal temperature is not required, and mild fevers may even help the immune response. A lively child with a 39C fever usually worries doctors less than a floppy one with 38C.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04251","question":"How do colds spread?","answer":"You are contagious from about a day before symptoms start, and most contagious in the first 2-3 days of the cold. Colds travel in droplets from coughs and sneezes, and on hands that then touch faces - yours and other people's. Staying home while feverish and at your most contagious protects colleagues and classmates; after that, handwashing and covering coughs do the work.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04252","question":"Can adults sleepwalk?","answer":"Sleepwalking - sitting up, walking around, eyes open but asleep - is common in children and usually outgrown; stress, sleep debt and fevers can trigger it. Waking a sleepwalker is hard and can cause confusion and distress - steering them calmly back to bed is the better move. The main job is safety: lock windows, gate stairs, remove trip hazards, and don't let them sleep near balconies or sharp edges.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04253","question":"What is hands-only CPR?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04254","question":"What is the best way to bring a fever down?","answer":"Keep the room comfortable and dress lightly - do not pile on blankets, and do not use ice baths or cold sponging, which cause shivering that pushes temperature back up. Cold sponging backfires - shivering generates heat and makes the fever fight you. Paracetamol or ibuprofen are for comfort when the person is miserable, not for hitting a target number. Rest as much as the body asks for while the immune system works.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04255","question":"The whole family is vomiting - what is going around?","answer":"Sudden-onset vomiting plus diarrhea that resolves within about 48 hours is the norovirus pattern. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04256","question":"Why do the elderly wake up so early?","answer":"Older adults still need around 7 to 8 hours; what changes is that sleep becomes lighter, more fragmented, and shifted earlier. Early waking is driven by a body clock that drifts earlier with age, and daytime naps become normal rather than lazy. Heavy snoring, depression, pain, medications and less daylight exposure can all worsen sleep, so persistent problems deserve a doctor's visit rather than a shrug.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04257","question":"Can you drink too much water?","answer":"Yes, but it is rare outside endurance sport. In rare cases, flooding your body with water faster than your kidneys can cope dilutes blood salt to a dangerous level. It is mostly seen in long-distance athletes who drink enormous amounts while losing salt in sweat.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04258","question":"How fast should you do chest compressions?","answer":"If someone is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call emergency services on speaker and push hard and fast in the center of the chest. Aim for 100 to 120 pushes per minute - the tempo of the song 'Stayin' Alive' - and about 5 centimeters (2 inches) deep. Let the chest rise fully between pushes, and keep going with as few pauses as possible. Hands-only CPR is effective for adults in cardiac arrest, and the emergency dispatcher will coach you through it. Fatigue makes compressions shallow, so if another person is present, swap every 2 minutes.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04259","question":"What does a serious rash look like?","answer":"Press a clear glass firmly against the rash: if the spots stay visible through the glass rather than fading, that is a non-blanching rash, and with fever it is a medical emergency (possible meningitis or sepsis). Painful, blistering or fast-spreading rashes are urgent dermatology territory, not home-care territory. A rash that appears after starting a new medication also warrants prompt review. Do not wait for other symptoms to develop - with sepsis, hours matter.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04260","question":"Should I clean my ears with cotton buds?","answer":"The only thing you should ever clean with a bud is the outer ear; inside the canal, the bud is the problem. Cotton buds push most of the wax deeper, pack it into plugs, and can scratch the canal or even perforate the eardrum. If an ear feels blocked, softening drops from a pharmacy are the gentle first step; a doctor can remove stubborn wax safely.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04261","question":"Why does one eye turn into two with pink eye?","answer":"Yes - conjunctivitis is quite contagious, spreading on hands, towels, pillowcases and eye makeup. Frequent handwashing, personal towels, and discarding eye makeup are the main containment moves. Rubbing one eye then the other is how it spreads from eye to eye, so keep hands busy elsewhere. Rubbing is the self-spreading mechanism; washcloths and makeup are the household vectors.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04262","question":"Is Vaseline good for wounds?","answer":"Petroleum jelly alone keeps minor wounds moist just as well for most people, without the allergy risk. Over-the-counter antibiotic ointments are a reasonable alternative, but a small number of people develop contact rashes to ingredients like neomycin. Whichever you use: clean first, apply a thin layer, cover with a fresh dressing daily.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04263","question":"Should children have less sugar than adults?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04264","question":"Do I need rest days from exercise?","answer":"Exercise stresses tissue; the adaptation that makes you stronger and fitter happens during the recovery afterward. For moderate activity like walking, daily movement is fine; for hard strength or interval sessions, leave 24 to 48 hours before working the same muscles hard again. Rest days can be active - walking, stretching, easy cycling - which often reduces stiffness more than lying still. Light movement on rest days speeds recovery better than total stillness.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04265","question":"Is a blister on a burn a bad sign?","answer":"Do not burst a burn blister on purpose - the fluid inside is sterile and the skin on top protects the raw area underneath. Cover it loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing instead. If it bursts on its own, wash gently with clean water, leave the flap of skin in place, and cover it with a plaster or sterile dressing. Growing redness, swelling or pus means it needs a medical look.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04266","question":"Why does my towel smell after two days?","answer":"Wash bath towels after about 3 uses and hand towels more often; when someone is ill, give them their own and wash everything hot. Bedding is comfortable to wash every 1-2 weeks - more often if you sweat heavily, share a bed with pets, or have allergies. Damp towels turn smelly fast because bacteria multiply in the folds; hang them spread out to dry, not crumpled on a hook.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04267","question":"Why do I need vitamin D in winter?","answer":"Vitamin D keeps bones, teeth and muscles healthy, and most of us make it when sunlight lands on our skin. In winter, when sunlight is too weak for your skin to make vitamin D, a daily 10 microgram supplement covers the gap. Food helps a little - oily fish, eggs and fortified cereals - but rarely covers what you need on its own. People with darker skin, or who mostly stay indoors, benefit from taking it all year round.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04268","question":"What should I drink instead of soda?","answer":"A regular 12-ounce (330 ml) can of cola contains about 35 grams of sugar - roughly 9 teaspoons. Sugary drinks are the single biggest source of added sugar for many people, and liquid sugar does not make you feel any fuller. Swapping to water, sparkling water, or tea and coffee without sugar cuts a lot of sugar for almost no effort.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04269","question":"How can I tell if a product is full of sugar without a nutrition panel?","answer":"Sugar hides under many names: glucose, dextrose, fructose, maltose, corn syrup, rice syrup, honey, agave and fruit juice concentrate. A useful trick is that most chemical names for sugar end in '-ose'. If several sweet-sounding ingredients sit near the top of the list, the product is sugar-heavy even when plain 'sugar' is never mentioned.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04270","question":"Why does the stomach flu hit so fast?","answer":"Norovirus is the classic 'stomach bug': vomiting and diarrhea that arrive abruptly, often sweeping through households. It is extremely contagious - a tiny number of particles infects - and spreads on hands, surfaces and food. There is no treatment beyond fluids, rest and time; most people are better within 1 to 2 days. Antibiotics do nothing against it, and anti-diarrhea medication is usually unnecessary for a short illness. Keep hydrating even as symptoms continue - steady small sips matter more than eating.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04271","question":"Should I avoid egg yolks?","answer":"For most healthy people, eggs are a nutritious food and there is no set limit - an egg a day fits comfortably in a healthy diet. The cholesterol you eat affects blood cholesterol far less than saturated fat does, though a few 'high responders' see bigger rises. If you have familial high cholesterol, heart disease or diabetes, ask your doctor what suits you.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04272","question":"How can I quickly judge if a food is healthy?","answer":"Start with the ingredients list, which runs from the biggest component to the smallest - if sugar or fat appears near the top, the food is mostly that. Comparing per 100 g is the fair way to judge two products, since serving sizes vary by brand. Traffic-light labels give a quick read: lots of reds for fat, salt or sugar means go easy. Short ingredient lists with words you recognize are usually a good sign.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04273","question":"Why does trying harder to sleep make it worse?","answer":"If you have been awake for about 20 minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet and boring in dim light - then go back only when you feel sleepy. Staying in bed awake and annoyed builds a learned link between bed and frustration, so a brief calm reset elsewhere works better. Clock-watching is a no: turn the clock around so you cannot check the time.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04274","question":"Why shouldn't I share towels?","answer":"Razors, toothbrushes, towels and clippers can carry blood and skin germs, so sharing them can pass on infections including hepatitis B and C. Each household member should have their own toothbrush and razor, and towels should be personal - especially during illness. It is a small habit with a big hygiene return.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04275","question":"How do I help a choking adult at dinner?","answer":"If they can cough, speak or breathe at all, encourage them to keep coughing - a strong cough often clears it on its own. Do not slap their back or try to remove the food while they are still coughing effectively, as you can push it deeper. Stay with them and watch whether the cough loses force. A silent cough or total inability to speak means the airway is truly blocked - act now.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04276","question":"Someone collapsed and will not wake up - what do I do?","answer":"Call emergency services for anyone not breathing normally, with chest pain or pressure, with bleeding that will not stop despite pressure, or who is unresponsive. Also call for signs of stroke (face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech), severe allergic reactions, a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, and severe burns. Give the location first, then follow the dispatcher's instructions - they can talk you through next steps. Do not drive yourself to the hospital with chest pain or heavy bleeding; an ambulance starts treatment on the spot. If you are unsure whether it is an emergency, calling and describing the situation is always the safer choice.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04277","question":"How much sugar can I eat a day?","answer":"For adults, guidance is to keep added sugar below about 30 grams a day - roughly 7 sugar cubes. Children should have less: no more than about 6 cubes a day for ages 7 to 10, and less again for younger kids. The limit covers sugar added to food and drinks plus honey, syrups and fruit juice - not the natural sugar in whole fruit or plain milk.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"},
  {"id":"hq-04278","question":"Is a midnight snack unhealthy?","answer":"Total calories matter most for weight, so late eating is not automatically fattening. That said, big meals close to bedtime can trigger heartburn and disrupt sleep, and late-night eating tends to be extra snacking on top of dinner. A good rule of thumb is to finish a big meal 2-3 hours before you lie down, mainly to protect sleep and digestion.","sources":["Authored: mainstream public health guidance (NHS/WHO/Mayo style)"],"split":"val"}
  ]
};
